# --- alloc.cyr ---

# @public — sadish allocation seam: the ONE place every other module draws memory from.
#
# alloc.cyr — `sd_alloc(n)` is what surface.cyr, geom.cyr, path.cyr, raster.cyr, present.cyr
# and error.cyr call instead of the stdlib `alloc(n)`. By default it IS `alloc(n)`; a consumer
# may install a hook with `sd_alloc_set(fp)` and every byte sadish asks for while the hook is
# set comes from the hook instead.
#
# ⭐ WHY A SEAM EXISTS AT ALL. `lib/alloc.cyr` is a bump allocator with NO `free()`. Before 0.5.5
# every fill cost the global heap a fixed 327,824 B and every stroke of a closed 4-vertex rect
# 2,789,016 B (MEASURED, 8x8 canvas, one call each) — permanently, per call. A per-frame consumer
# (dhancha's frame arena, and crab on top of it, whose headline gate asserts a rendered frame
# costs the heap EXACTLY 0 bytes) had no way to draw scalable text through sadish without
# leaking a canvas plus a path per glyph per frame — the defect dhancha filed as
# `2026-09-13-scalable-text-allocates-per-call-outside-the-frame-arena.md`. Routing sadish's
# allocations onto the consumer's arena is the ONLY way a frame can cost the heap nothing,
# and the consumer is the only party that knows the frame boundary. Hence: a hook.
#
# THE CONTRACT.
#   · The hook applies to EVERYTHING sadish allocates while it is set — surfaces, canvases,
#     coverage buffers, clip masks, paths, verb/point arrays, points, matrices, error records,
#     flatten mid-points, the PPM/presenter buffers. sadish does not distinguish "the result"
#     from "the working set"; a consumer scopes the hook around the work whose lifetime it is
#     choosing: `var prev = sd_alloc_set(&mine); ...draw...; sd_alloc_set(prev);`.
#   ⛔ A pointer that outlives the hook's backing store is dangling. If the hook is an arena
#     that gets reset every frame, NOTHING sadish returned under it may be kept across the reset
#     — not a path, not a canvas, not a surface. That is the consumer's lifetime call, exactly as
#     it already is for `dh_falloc`.
#   ⭐ A HOOK MAY REFUSE. Every `sd_alloc` result in `src/*.cyr` is tested and reported, and a
#     refusal is a RETURN CODE at every public entry point that can reach one (0.7.1 for path,
#     geom, raster, present and error; 0.7.2 for the three stroke entries, which were the last to
#     fault). ⛔ THIS SENTENCE USED TO READ "No new failure path", and it was written before any
#     of that work: through 0.7.0 a refusal inside path construction was a null-pointer STORE, and
#     through 0.7.1 `sd_canvas_stroke_path` still died on one (MEASURED: SIGSEGV, rc 139). What is
#     true now, entry point by entry point:
#       · CONSTRUCTORS RETURN 0 — sd_surface_new, sd_canvas_new, sd_path_new / _cap,
#         sd_point_new, sd_matrix_* (identity/translate/scale/rotate/skew/mul/invert/apply),
#         the gradient constructors, sadish_err_new (whose 0 READS as SADISH_ERR_OOM, so a caller
#         handling one failure is never handed a second), sd_path_flatten (0 with
#         sd_flatten_truncated() set, which is how it is told from an empty path), and the
#         presenter (_sd_present_probe / _sd_presenter_build, fd closed on every refusal path).
#       · STATUS-RETURNING CALLS RETURN SADISH_ERR_OOM — sd_path_moveto / lineto / quadto /
#         cubicto (verbs, points and contents exactly as they were), sd_path_grow,
#         sd_canvas_clip_push_rect / _push_path / sd_clip_push_mask (nothing pushed, the previous
#         region intact and poppable, the caller's mask untouched), sd_surface_write_ppm (both
#         buffers taken BEFORE the O_TRUNC open, so an existing file is not truncated),
#         sd_gradient_set_matrix / add_stop, sd_canvas_fill_path / _union, and
#         sd_canvas_stroke_path / _ex / _dash.
#       ⚠ WHAT A REFUSAL COSTS, WHICH IS NOT ALWAYS NOTHING. The path builders, the clip pushes
#         and the PPM writer are all-or-nothing: a refusal costs the caller nothing it had (a path
#         may have paid for one doubling — path.cyr says so). A FILL or a STROKE is not: coverage
#         written before the refusal STAYS ON THE CANVAS, and the code says the picture is a
#         prefix. Clear and retry, or draw into a scratch canvas.
#       ⭐⭐ 0.10.0 NARROWS WHAT A HOOK CAN REFUSE AT ALL, AND THAT IS WORTH READING BEFORE RELYING
#         ON THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE. A flattened point costs no allocation since the flatten output
#         holds coordinates (src/path.cyr), so the per-point sd_alloc that used to sit inside every
#         fill and every styled/dashed stroke is GONE. MEASURED: a fill of a hostile 4,096-quad path
#         asked the seam for 1,110,016 B in 69,376 calls at 0.9.1 and asks for **nothing** here
#         (programs/flatten_bound_test.cyr #102-103); a styled stroke of a cubic, 128 B -> 0
#         (stroke_style_test #8); a dashed one, 224 B -> 0 (dash_test #128).
#         ⇒ A FILL AND A STYLED OR DASHED STROKE CANNOT BE STARVED BY A HOOK ANY MORE. They still
#         return SADISH_ERR_OOM where the code says they do — the paths are reachable through the
#         process scratch and the edge list, which draw from the GLOBAL allocator by design and were
#         never a hook's to refuse — but a consumer testing its own error path by refusing from its
#         hook will now get a complete picture and SADISH_OK. ⚠ The ROUND stroker is the exception
#         and still allocates per piece (its per-segment and per-vertex paths, on this seam), so it
#         starves exactly as before; programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr holds both halves.
#       ⛔ WHAT STILL FAULTS: a CALLER that ignores a documented 0 and hands it back to sadish.
#         MEASURED on this tree — `sd_path_moveto(0, x, y)` on a refused path, `sd_polyline_count(0)`
#         on a refused flatten and `sd_canvas_coverage_at(0, …)` on a refused canvas are each
#         SIGSEGV, rc 139. The builders and the accessors do not test their record pointer; the
#         entry points that take a canvas or a path DO (`sd_canvas_stroke_path(0, 0, w)` is
#         SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS, `sd_gradient_add_stop(0, …)` SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS). ⇒ Test a
#         constructor's result before you build on it; that is the whole of the caller's duty.
#   · `sd_alloc_set` returns the PREVIOUS hook so a nested scope unwinds in order; 0 restores the
#     global allocator. `sd_alloc_get` reads it back.
#   ⚠ The fill/stroke scratch in raster.cyr is deliberately NOT on this seam — it is allocated
#     once for the life of the process from the global `alloc` (see `_sd_scratch_init`; the
#     SD_AA_AREA accumulator in coverage.cyr, the styled-stroke batch and curve flags in
#     stroke.cyr and the dash piece buffer in dash.cyr likewise), and since 0.7.0 GROWN there too
#     when a path outgrows it. A hook pointing at an arena that gets reset would otherwise leave
#     the scratch dangling on the second frame. ⇒ A hook CANNOT refuse any of them; the global
#     allocator can, and a refusal there is reported by the same return codes as above (each set
#     is published all-or-nothing, so the next call retries from scratch).
#   ⚠ The hook is a process-wide global, like the scratch: sadish has never had a lock anywhere
#     and is not thread-safe. Set it from the thread that draws.

# The installed hook: fn(n): ptr (0 on failure), or 0 = the global allocator.
var _sd_alloc_fn = 0;

# @public — allocate n bytes for sadish through the consumer's hook, or the global allocator when
# none is set. Returns 0 on failure, as `alloc` does.
fn sd_alloc(n): i64 {
    if (_sd_alloc_fn == 0) { return alloc(n); }
    return callptr(_sd_alloc_fn, n);
}

# @public — install `fp` (`fn(n): ptr`, 0 on failure) as the allocator for everything sadish
# allocates from now on. Returns the PREVIOUS hook so a caller can scope it (set, draw, restore).
# Passing 0 restores the global allocator.
fn sd_alloc_set(fp): i64 {
    var prev = _sd_alloc_fn;
    _sd_alloc_fn = fp;
    return prev;
}

# @public — the current hook; 0 = the global allocator.
fn sd_alloc_get(): i64 {
    return _sd_alloc_fn;
}

# --- error.cyr ---

# @public — stable API surface for sadish error handling.
# error.cyr — sadish vector-graphics error codes + Result helpers.
#
# SadishErr is a 16-byte record. Error codes are integer constants; for
# errors that carry context (a message pointer), use sadish_err_new(code,
# msg). The (ptr, err_out) Ok/Err split mirrors the ecosystem's raster
# libs (chitra) so a consumer maps a failure cleanly onto its own error.
#
# SadishErr struct layout (16 bytes):
#   +0: code    — error code constant (one of the SADISH_* below)
#   +8: detail  — pointer to detail string (or 0)

# ================================================================
# Error code constants
# ================================================================

enum SadishErrCode {
    SADISH_OK              = 0;   # no error
    SADISH_ERR_OOM         = 1;   # allocation failed / capacity cap hit
    SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH  = 2;   # fill/flatten on a path with no verbs
    SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS      = 3;   # coordinate / canvas index out of range
    SADISH_ERR_UNSUPPORTED = 4;   # a valid request sadish can't yet honor
    SADISH_ERR_OTHER       = 99;  # catch-all
}

# ================================================================
# SadishErr struct
# ================================================================

# Create a SadishErr with code and an optional detail string (0 = none).
# Returns the 16 B record, or 0 when sd_alloc refuses it.
# ⛔ 0 IS A RECORD, NOT A FAULT — and this is the one place in sadish where a null pointer has a
# MEANING. A refused 16 B block is the only way this returns 0, and the only condition that refuses
# it is out of memory, so the null record READS as SADISH_ERR_OOM: sadish_err_code(0) is
# SADISH_ERR_OOM and sadish_err_detail(0) is 0. ⇒ A caller handling one failure is never handed a
# second one to handle: it may pass this result straight to the accessors without a null check.
# Through 0.7.0 the store was unchecked — a hook that can refuse (the 0.5.5 seam's natural arena)
# made this a null-pointer store, which is what rekha filed
# (docs/development/issues/archived/2026-09-15-path-construction-stores-through-a-refused-allocation.md).
fn sadish_err_new(code, detail): i64 {
    var e = sd_alloc(16);
    if (e == 0) { return 0; }
    store64(e, code);
    store64(e + 8, detail);
    return e;
}

# Create a SadishErr with code only (no detail). 0 on a refused allocation, as sadish_err_new.
fn sadish_err(code): i64 {
    return sadish_err_new(code, 0);
}

# Get the error code. ⚠ A 0 record is the record sadish_err_new could not allocate, and reads as
# SADISH_ERR_OOM (see sadish_err_new) — never a fault, never SADISH_OK.
fn sadish_err_code(e): i64 {
    if (e == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    return load64(e);
}

# Get the detail string pointer (or 0). A 0 record carries no detail.
fn sadish_err_detail(e): i64 {
    if (e == 0) { return 0; }
    return load64(e + 8);
}

# ================================================================
# Error name lookup — returns a cstring (never null).
# ================================================================

fn sadish_err_name(code): i64 {
    if (code == SADISH_OK)              { return "ok"; }
    if (code == SADISH_ERR_OOM)         { return "out of memory"; }
    if (code == SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH)  { return "empty path"; }
    if (code == SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS)      { return "out of bounds"; }
    if (code == SADISH_ERR_UNSUPPORTED) { return "unsupported feature"; }
    if (code == SADISH_ERR_OTHER)       { return "other error"; }
    return "unknown error";
}

# Print an error name to stderr (fd 2).
# ⚠ TAKES A CODE, NOT A SadishErr RECORD — `sadish_err_print_name(sadish_err_code(e))`. Every i64
# is a valid argument (sadish_err_name maps the unknown ones to "unknown error"), so no value can
# fault it, including the 0 a refused sadish_err_new returns — but 0 is the CODE SADISH_OK, so
# handing it the record instead of the code prints "ok" for an out-of-memory failure. Go through
# sadish_err_code, which turns the null record into SADISH_ERR_OOM.
fn sadish_err_print_name(code): i64 {
    var name = sadish_err_name(code);
    syscall(SYS_WRITE, 2, name, strlen(name));
    return 0;
}

# --- surface.cyr ---

# @public — sadish pixel surface: the direct primitives' destination buffer.
#
# surface.cyr — an SdSurface owns a width*height 32bpp pixel buffer in the
# ecosystem-convergent BGRA byte order (memory: B,G,R,A), so a packed sadish
# color 0x00RRGGBB lands verbatim in a little-endian 32bpp framebuffer. This is
# the DESTINATION buffer: the direct hairline/fill primitives in draw.cyr write
# here, and (v0.2+) the coverage blit in raster.cyr will composite onto it.
#
# Lifted, not reinvented: the pixel-write convention, the {w,h,px} layout, and
# the coordinate system (origin top-left, y-down, off = (y*w + x)*4) are the
# shared idiom proven across the ecosystem's framebuffer apps (encom-hits,
# cyrius-bb/-polyomino, cyrius-doom). See docs/development/prior-art.md.
#
# ⚠ That offset assumes the packed buffer. ROWS ARE `stride` BYTES APART, NOT
# `w*4`: off = y*stride + x*4. The two are the same number for every surface
# sd_surface_new makes, and differ on a WRAPPED one — a header over a wider buffer
# (dhancha's dh_surface_wrap over a framebuffer or a pane sub-rect). Every reader
# and writer of an SdSurface in sadish (this file, draw.cyr, the blits in
# raster.cyr, present.cyr) loads the stride once per call and addresses by it;
# programs/stride_test.cyr holds each of them to it on a hand-built wrapped header.

# ================================================================
# Color — a packed 0x00RRGGBB integer. Stored to the surface as BGRA with the
# alpha byte forced opaque (0xFF); read back RGB-packed (alpha dropped).
# ================================================================
# @public — pack (r,g,b) 0..255 into a sadish color 0x00RRGGBB.
fn sd_rgb(r, g, b): i64 {
    return ((r & 255) << 16) | ((g & 255) << 8) | (b & 255);
}
# @public — channel extractors.
fn sd_color_r(c): i64 { return (c >> 16) & 255; }
fn sd_color_g(c): i64 { return (c >> 8) & 255; }
fn sd_color_b(c): i64 { return c & 255; }

# ================================================================
# ALPHA (v0.5.0) — additive, and deliberately backward-compatible.
#
# Until now every sadish primitive hardcoded the alpha byte to 255 in four separate places, and the colour
# type had no alpha at all. That was invisible because nothing downstream read byte 3 — but agnos's
# gpu_shader_op#92 op 0x01 does: it performs PREMULTIPLIED src-over on the shader cores, which is the one
# thing a CPU blit cannot do cheaply. Without a real alpha channel the whole hardware-blend path had no
# producer.
#
# ⚠ THE LEGACY RULE, and it is what makes this additive: an alpha byte of 0 means OPAQUE, not invisible.
# sd_rgb(r,g,b) returns 0x00RRGGBB with byte 3 = 0, and every existing caller and test depends on that
# value. Treating 0 as transparent would make every pre-existing drawing vanish. So sd_alpha_of() maps
# 0 -> 255, and a caller who genuinely wants "draw nothing" simply does not draw.
# The cost: fully-transparent (a == 0) is not expressible as a colour. That is the right trade — it is the
# one alpha value with no visible effect.
# ================================================================
# @public — pack (r,g,b,a) into a sadish colour 0xAARRGGBB. a is 1..255; 0 is reserved (reads back opaque).
fn sd_rgba(r, g, b, a): i64 {
    return ((a & 255) << 24) | ((r & 255) << 16) | ((g & 255) << 8) | (b & 255);
}
# @public — the alpha a colour will actually be drawn with. 0 (a bare sd_rgb value) means OPAQUE.
fn sd_alpha_of(c): i64 {
    var a = (c >> 24) & 255;
    if (a == 0) { return 255; }
    return a;
}
# @public — raw alpha byte, WITHOUT the legacy 0-means-opaque mapping. For code that needs to know whether
# a colour carried an explicit alpha at all.
fn sd_color_a(c): i64 { return (c >> 24) & 255; }
# @public — PREMULTIPLY a colour: scale each channel by its own alpha, as gpu_shader_op#92 op 0x01 requires
# (c <= a). This is the sanctioned producer for surfaces flagged SETU_SURF_PREMULTIPLIED.
#
# ⚠ Passing STRAIGHT alpha to #92 does not error — it renders washed out, silently, with no diagnostic
# anywhere in the stack. Always route through here rather than multiplying by hand.
fn sd_premul(c): i64 {
    var a = sd_alpha_of(c);
    var r = (sd_color_r(c) * a) / 255;
    var g = (sd_color_g(c) * a) / 255;
    var b = (sd_color_b(c) * a) / 255;
    return sd_rgba(r, g, b, a);
}

# ================================================================
# SdSurface (32 bytes):
#   +0:  width   (i64 — pixels)
#   +8:  height  (i64 — pixels)
#   +16: pixels  (ptr — owned width*height*4 bytes, BGRA, packed)
#   +24: stride  (i64 — bytes per row; width*4 for the packed buffer, wider for a
#                 wrapped one — the row pitch every reader/writer addresses by)
# ================================================================
var SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET  = 0;
var SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET = 8;
var SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET = 16;
var SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET = 24;
var SD_SURFACE_SIZE          = 32;

# ⛔ THE DIMENSION CEILING (0.11.1) — what keeps a size computation inside i64 and inside the
# allocator. SD_DIM_MAX is 2^29: the largest edge worth asking for, and small enough that
# SD_DIM_MAX * SD_DIM_MAX * 4 = 2^60 cannot wrap. SD_PIXELS_MAX = 2^29 caps the PRODUCT, so a
# surface's pixel block (4 B a pixel) stays under lib/alloc.cyr's 2 GiB ALLOC_MAX and a canvas's
# coverage (1 B a pixel) does too. ⚠ Both sd_surface_new and sd_canvas_new check these; they are
# the two places a caller's own number becomes an allocation size.
var SD_DIM_MAX               = 536870912;
var SD_PIXELS_MAX            = 536870912;

# @public — allocate a w*h BGRA surface, cleared to transparent black (0).
# Returns the SdSurface ptr, or 0 on OOM / bad dimensions.
fn sd_surface_new(w, h): i64 {
    if (w <= 0) { return 0; }
    if (h <= 0) { return 0; }
    # ⛔⛔ THE PRODUCT WRAPS, AND A WRAP PUBLISHES A LIAR (0.11.1). `(w * h) * 4` is i64 arithmetic
    # with no check: w = 2^62 + 1000, h = 1 gives nbytes = 4000, sd_alloc SUCCEEDS, and the record
    # then publishes width 4.6e18 over a 4,000 byte block with a wrapped stride. The first sd_plot
    # writes wherever the arithmetic lands. lib/alloc.cyr's ALLOC_MAX cannot save this — the whole
    # point of the wrap is that the request looks small.
    # ⇒ Bound each dimension so the product cannot leave i64, and bound the product so it cannot
    # leave ALLOC_MAX. SD_DIM_MAX^2 * 4 = 2^60, decades inside the type.
    if (w > SD_DIM_MAX) { return 0; }
    if (h > SD_DIM_MAX) { return 0; }
    if (w * h > SD_PIXELS_MAX) { return 0; }
    var s = sd_alloc(SD_SURFACE_SIZE);
    if (s == 0) { return 0; }
    var nbytes = (w * h) * 4;
    var px = sd_alloc(nbytes);
    if (px == 0) { return 0; }
    memset(px, 0, nbytes);
    store64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET, w);
    store64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET, h);
    store64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET, px);
    store64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET, (w * 4));
    return s;
}

# ---- accessors --------------------------------------------------
# @public
fn sd_surface_width(s): i64  { return load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET); }
# @public
fn sd_surface_height(s): i64 { return load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET); }
# @public
fn sd_surface_pixels(s): i64 { return load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET); }
# @public
fn sd_surface_stride(s): i64 { return load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET); }

# Bounds-checked BGRA store of pixel (x, y) of a w*h buffer, given `row` = the
# address of row y's first pixel (`px + y * stride`, computed by the caller). The
# private helper every per-pixel primitive (sd_plot, sd_line, sd_put) shares so the
# write convention lives in one place. ⚠ Row pointer, not (px, stride): the stride
# form needs a 7th argument, which goes on the stack (MEASURED ~4% slower on an
# sd_line-only workload); sd_line also steps the row pointer rather than multiplying.
# `row` may point anywhere when y is out of range; it is only dereferenced after
# the bounds checks pass.
fn _sd_put_row(row, w, h, x, y, color): i64 {
    if (x < 0) { return 0; }
    if (x >= w) { return 0; }
    if (y < 0) { return 0; }
    if (y >= h) { return 0; }
    var off = x * 4;
    store8(row + off, color & 255);
    store8(row + off + 1, (color >> 8) & 255);
    store8(row + off + 2, (color >> 16) & 255);
    store8(row + off + 3, sd_alpha_of(color));   # v0.5.0: honour the colour's alpha (0 == opaque, legacy)
    return 0;
}

# The same store into a PACKED buffer (rows w*4 bytes apart) — the pre-stride
# signature, kept working (no consumer calls it; nothing in sadish does any more).
# A raw pointer carries no stride, so this form cannot address a wrapped surface;
# sd_plot / sd_line pass the surface's own stride to _sd_put_row instead.
fn sd_put(px, w, h, x, y, color): i64 {
    return _sd_put_row(px + y * (w * 4), w, h, x, y, color);
}

# @public — read a pixel back as a packed 0x00RRGGBB color (alpha dropped).
# Out-of-bounds reads return 0. The readback path for tests + coverage compositing.
fn sd_surface_pixel_at(s, x, y): i64 {
    # ⚠ 0.11.1: faulted on a null surface where its twin sd_surface_rgba_at (src/premul.cyr) has
    # always answered 0. Two readers of the same bytes disagreeing about the same argument is the
    # kind of asymmetry that only ever shows up in a consumer's crash report.
    if (s == 0) { return 0; }
    var w = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var h = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    if (x < 0) { return 0; }
    if (y < 0) { return 0; }
    if (x >= w) { return 0; }
    if (y >= h) { return 0; }
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    var off = y * load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET) + x * 4;
    var b = load8(px + off);
    var g = load8(px + off + 1);
    var r = load8(px + off + 2);
    return (r << 16) | (g << 8) | b;
}

# --- draw.cyr ---

# @public — sadish direct 2D primitives (opaque, integer-pixel).
#
# draw.cyr — hairline + axis-aligned primitives adapted from the ecosystem's
# framebuffer games (integer Bresenham line, hline/vline fast paths, rect
# outline, filled rect, clear). This is the "easy 80%" LIFTED rather than
# reinvented (see docs/development/prior-art.md): proven, dependency-free,
# opaque 1px drawing onto an SdSurface. The anti-aliased / coverage / winding
# path is the separate vector core (path.cyr -> raster.cyr).
#
# All coordinates are whole integer pixels; color is a packed 0x00RRGGBB.
# The Bresenham body is encom-hits/src/draw.cyr:7-55 verbatim in structure,
# retargeted from a fixed 320x240 to the SdSurface's own width/height.
#
# ⚠ Every primitive here addresses rows by `sd_surface_stride(s)` (loaded once per
# call), never `w * 4` — see surface.cyr. Through 0.5.5 only sd_canvas_blit_at did,
# and a WINDOW background sd_fill_rect'd into a wrapped 200x60 header overwrote
# 4,000 padding pixels under text that landed straight (MEASURED, the 0.5.5 review).

# @public — plot one pixel (bounds-checked; off-surface is a silent no-op).
fn sd_plot(s, x, y, color): i64 {
    var w = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var h = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    var stride = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET);
    return _sd_put_row(px + y * stride, w, h, x, y, color);
}

# @public — Bresenham line from (x0,y0) to (x1,y1). Integer, per-pixel clipped.
fn sd_line(s, x0, y0, x1, y1, color): i64 {
    var w = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var h = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    var stride = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET);
    var dx = x1 - x0;
    var dy = y1 - y0;
    if (dx < 0) { dx = 0 - dx; }
    if (dy < 0) { dy = 0 - dy; }
    var sx = 1;
    if (x0 > x1) { sx = 0 - 1; }
    var sy = 1;
    if (y0 > y1) { sy = 0 - 1; }
    var err = dx - dy;
    var x = x0;
    var y = y0;
    # The row pointer steps with y instead of being re-multiplied per pixel: MEASURED, 18,000 lines on
    # 1024x768, `px + y * stride` per pixel cost ~3.8% over 0.5.5; tracked, within noise (median of 6:
    # 0.264 s 0.5.5, 0.267 s here).
    var row = px + y0 * stride;
    var drow = stride * sy;
    while (1) {
        _sd_put_row(row, w, h, x, y, color);
        if (x == x1) {
            if (y == y1) { return 0; }
        }
        var e2 = err * 2;
        if (e2 > (0 - dy)) { err = err - dy; x = x + sx; }
        if (e2 < dx) { err = err + dx; y = y + sy; row = row + drow; }
    }
    return 0;
}

# @public — horizontal span [x0,x1] at row y (endpoints clamped to the surface).
fn sd_hline(s, x0, x1, y, color): i64 {
    var w = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var h = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    if (y < 0) { return 0; }
    if (y >= h) { return 0; }
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    var start = x0;
    var end = x1;
    if (start > end) { var tmp = start; start = end; end = tmp; }
    if (start < 0) { start = 0; }
    if (end >= w) { end = w - 1; }
    var base = y * load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET);
    # ⭐ 0.11.1: the four channel values are LOOP-INVARIANT — hoisted. Each iteration used to re-mask
    # the colour three times and call sd_alpha_of a fourth, per pixel, to write the same four bytes.
    # ⚠ Byte-identical output by construction: same values, computed once.
    var cb = color & 255;
    var cg = (color >> 8) & 255;
    var cr = (color >> 16) & 255;
    var ca = sd_alpha_of(color);
    var x = start;
    while (x <= end) {
        var off = base + x * 4;
        store8(px + off, cb);
        store8(px + off + 1, cg);
        store8(px + off + 2, cr);
        store8(px + off + 3, ca);
        x = x + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# @public — vertical span [y0,y1] at column x (endpoints clamped to the surface).
fn sd_vline(s, x, y0, y1, color): i64 {
    var w = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var h = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    if (x < 0) { return 0; }
    if (x >= w) { return 0; }
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    var stride = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET);
    var start = y0;
    var end = y1;
    if (start > end) { var tmp = start; start = end; end = tmp; }
    if (start < 0) { start = 0; }
    if (end >= h) { end = h - 1; }
    # ⭐ 0.11.1: same invariant hoist as sd_hline's — see the note there.
    var vb = color & 255;
    var vg = (color >> 8) & 255;
    var vr = (color >> 16) & 255;
    var va = sd_alpha_of(color);
    var y = start;
    var voff = start * stride + x * 4;
    while (y <= end) {
        store8(px + voff, vb);
        store8(px + voff + 1, vg);
        store8(px + voff + 2, vr);
        store8(px + voff + 3, va);
        voff = voff + stride;
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# @public — rectangle OUTLINE (4 edges); top-left (x,y), size rw*rh.
fn sd_rect(s, x, y, rw, rh, color): i64 {
    sd_hline(s, x, x + rw - 1, y, color);
    sd_hline(s, x, x + rw - 1, y + rh - 1, color);
    sd_vline(s, x, y, y + rh - 1, color);
    sd_vline(s, x + rw - 1, y, y + rh - 1, color);
    return 0;
}

# @public — FILLED rectangle; top-left (x,y), size rw*rh.
# @public — ALPHA-BLENDED horizontal span: `color` composited OVER what is already there, at
# coverage `a` (0..255). The workhorse under `sd_fill_rect_blend`.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE FIRST FILL IN sadish THAT READS ITS DESTINATION. Every other span writer stores four bytes
# per pixel and never loads — which is correct and fast for opaque paint, and is exactly why a
# translucent veil was impossible until now. dhancha wanted a modal backdrop and had to ship a
# scanline dim instead; this is what that was waiting on.
#
# ⛔⛔ `a` IS AN EXPLICIT ARGUMENT AND 0 MEANS FULLY TRANSPARENT — THE OPPOSITE OF `sd_alpha_of`.
# The legacy packed-alpha rule maps a 0 byte to **opaque** (`sd_alpha_of`), which exists so a bare
# `sd_rgb` value is not invisible. Carrying that convention into a blend would make
# `sd_fill_rect_blend(..., 0)` paint SOLID — the single most surprising outcome available, and the
# exact trap that made `sd_rgba(0,0,0,128)` paint black. ⇒ Alpha is passed separately, it is never
# read out of `color`, and 0 is a no-op.
#
# ⚠ SOURCE-OVER, INTEGER, PER CHANNEL: `out = (src*a + dst*(255-a)) / 255`. Rounding is truncation,
# so a 50 % veil applied twice is not identical to one at 75 % — which is true of any integer
# compositor and is not worth a fractional pixel format to fix.
# ⚠ THE DESTINATION ALPHA BYTE IS LEFT AT 255. sadish surfaces are opaque render targets; a blend
# that also composited alpha would make the result translucent to whatever draws it next, and every
# consumer here presents to a screen.
fn sd_blend_hline(s, x0, x1, y, color, a): i64 {
    if (a <= 0) { return 0; }
    var w = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var h = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    if (y < 0) { return 0; }
    if (y >= h) { return 0; }
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    var start = x0;
    var end = x1;
    if (start > end) { var tmp = start; start = end; end = tmp; }
    if (start < 0) { start = 0; }
    if (end >= w) { end = w - 1; }
    var av = a;
    if (av > 255) { av = 255; }
    var inv = 255 - av;
    # ⚠ The buffer is BGRA: byte 0 is BLUE, matching `sd_hline`'s store order and `sd_rgb`'s packing.
    var sb = color & 255;
    var sg = (color >> 8) & 255;
    var sr = (color >> 16) & 255;
    var base = y * load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET);
    var x = start;
    while (x <= end) {
        var off = base + x * 4;
        store8(px + off, (sb * av + load8(px + off) * inv) / 255);
        store8(px + off + 1, (sg * av + load8(px + off + 1) * inv) / 255);
        store8(px + off + 2, (sr * av + load8(px + off + 2) * inv) / 255);
        store8(px + off + 3, 255);
        x = x + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# @public — fill a rect with `color` composited OVER the destination at coverage `a` (0..255).
# `a == 0` paints nothing; `a == 255` is exactly `sd_fill_rect`.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS WHAT A SCRIM IS. A modal backdrop, a selection wash, a disabled overlay — every one of
# them is this call, and every consumer that wanted one has until now had to fake it with a dither.
fn sd_fill_rect_blend(s, x, y, rw, rh, color, a): i64 {
    var row = 0;
    while (row < rh) {
        sd_blend_hline(s, x, x + rw - 1, y + row, color, a);
        row = row + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn sd_fill_rect(s, x, y, rw, rh, color): i64 {
    var row = 0;
    while (row < rh) {
        sd_hline(s, x, x + rw - 1, y + row, color);
        row = row + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# @public — clear the whole surface to `color`.
fn sd_clear(s, color): i64 {
    var w = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var h = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    return sd_fill_rect(s, 0, 0, w, h, color);
}

# --- present.cyr ---

# @public — sadish present backend: get a surface onto a screen.
#
# present.cyr — takes an SdSurface and displays it. Two sinks:
#
#   1. sd_surface_write_ppm(s, path) — write the surface as a binary P6 PPM.
#      Platform-neutral (file syscalls only); the headless / CI / debug path.
#   2. SdPresenter (sd_present_open/blit/close) — the LINUX host framebuffer
#      sink: open /dev/fb0, probe real geometry (VSCREENINFO/FSCREENINFO),
#      integer-scale + center-letterbox the surface honoring the physical
#      pitch, and write the active band. Adapted from encom-hits/src/engine.cyr
#      (probe/blit) — which fixed the "tiles across the top band" pitch bug
#      shared by cyrius-doom/-polyomino. See docs/development/prior-art.md.
#      0.8.0 splits the ACQUISITION off that entry: sd_present_open_fd(fd)
#      presents onto a descriptor the caller already has, sd_present_open_path(path)
#      opens one by name, and sd_present_open is the two-token wrapper that names
#      the device. Gated by programs/present_open_test.cyr over a regular file.
#
# The encom original was module-global + fixed 320x240; sadish's is
# struct-based (reentrant) and takes the surface's own dimensions. Timing /
# frame-pacing (game-specific) is intentionally left out.
#
# ⚠ sd_present_blit writes to the LIVE framebuffer — never call it from a test.
# The fb0 path is Linux-specific (raw open/ioctl/lseek/write); the AGNOS sink
# (kernel blit#39) is a future backend beside this one.
#
# ⛔ REFUSED ALLOCATIONS, 0.7.1. Both sinks draw from the sd_alloc seam, which a consumer's hook may
# REFUSE. Every block is now checked, and the rule both sinks keep is that a refusal costs the
# caller NOTHING IT HAD: the PPM sink takes its two buffers before the O_TRUNC open, so a refusal
# leaves the target file exactly as it was; the presenter closes the framebuffer fd on every one of
# its four refusal paths and never returns a half-built record. Gated by programs/oom_test.cyr.
# Closes half of docs/development/issues/archived/2026-09-15-path-construction-stores-through-a-refused-allocation.md.
# ⭐ AND THE fd THAT REACHES THEM IS NOW THE CALLER'S TO SUPPLY, 0.8.0. That filing's one remaining
# note was that sd_present_open itself — the open() above those four blocks — was reachable by no
# test, because its argument is the live display. programs/present_open_test.cyr drives
# sd_present_open_path / _open_fd over a regular file under build/ instead: 131 checks, all four
# blocks refused in turn, the documented 0 each time, and /proc/self/fd counted before and after
# every call (MEASURED: 3 descriptors at entry, 3 after each of the nine refusals). Its group I is
# also the only thing in the tree that presents out of RECYCLED memory, which is what makes the
# three memsets below observable at all.

# ---- PPM sink (platform-neutral) --------------------------------
# Write the decimal ASCII of a non-negative n into buf at off; return new off.
fn sd_ppm_put_dec(buf, off, n): i64 {
    if (n == 0) {
        store8(buf + off, 48);
        return off + 1;
    }
    var digits = 0;
    var t = n;
    while (t > 0) { digits = digits + 1; t = t / 10; }
    var i = digits - 1;
    while (i >= 0) {
        store8(buf + off + i, 48 + (n % 10));
        n = n / 10;
        i = i - 1;
    }
    return off + digits;
}

# Build "P6\n<w> <h>\n255\n" into buf; return its byte length.
fn sd_ppm_header(buf, w, h): i64 {
    var o = 0;
    store8(buf + o, 80); o = o + 1;   # 'P'
    store8(buf + o, 54); o = o + 1;   # '6'
    store8(buf + o, 10); o = o + 1;   # newline
    o = sd_ppm_put_dec(buf, o, w);
    store8(buf + o, 32); o = o + 1;   # space
    o = sd_ppm_put_dec(buf, o, h);
    store8(buf + o, 10); o = o + 1;   # newline
    store8(buf + o, 50); o = o + 1;   # '2'
    store8(buf + o, 53); o = o + 1;   # '5'
    store8(buf + o, 53); o = o + 1;   # '5'
    store8(buf + o, 10); o = o + 1;   # newline
    return o;
}

# @public — write the surface as a binary P6 PPM to `path`. BGRA -> RGB at
# write time. Returns SADISH_OK; SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null surface; SADISH_ERR_OOM if either
# buffer is refused; SADISH_ERR_OTHER if the file won't open.
# ⛔ BOTH BUFFERS ARE TAKEN BEFORE THE open(), and that ORDER IS THE CONTRACT, not a style choice.
# The open is O_TRUNC: opening first and then discovering the w*h*3 block was refused would leave
# `path` a 0-BYTE FILE where a readable PPM used to be, plus an fd to close over a write that can
# never happen. Allocating first means a refusal touches neither the file nor any fd — the sink
# either writes a whole PPM or leaves the previous one exactly as it was.
# ⚠ Byte-identical and figure-identical on every write that worked: the same two blocks, the same
# two sizes, in the same order (MEASURED, programs/stride_test.cyr #81: 384 B for a 13x9 surface —
# 32 for the header + 352 for 351 aligned). Only a FAILING call moved.
# ⚠ A 0-pixel surface asks sd_alloc for 0 bytes, which lib/alloc.cyr refuses by contract; that is
# not out of memory, so the OOM test on `rgb` is guarded by n > 0 and such a call still writes a
# header-only PPM, as it did through 0.7.0. MEASURED on both trees with a hand-built w=0 h=4 header:
# SADISH_OK and an 11-byte file, "P6\n0 4\n255\n" (programs/oom_test.cyr group K — dropping the
# n > 0 guard turns that call into SADISH_ERR_OOM and fails it).
# ⇒ src/raster.cyr's sd_canvas_clip_push_rect deliberately does NOT carve the same case out, and
# says why: a 0-pixel write COMPLETES and leaves a valid file, while a 0-area clip push could only
# hand back a node whose mask pointer is 0.
fn sd_surface_write_ppm(s, path): i64 {
    if (s == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var w = sd_surface_width(s);
    var h = sd_surface_height(s);
    var px = sd_surface_pixels(s);
    var stride = sd_surface_stride(s);
    var hdr = sd_alloc(32);
    if (hdr == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    var n = w * h;
    var rgb = sd_alloc(n * 3);
    if (n > 0) {
        if (rgb == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    }
    # open(path, O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC = 1|64|512 = 577, mode 0644 = 420)
    # ⚠ RAW 2, not SYS_OPEN — deliberate, see the note above _sd_present_probe's ioctls.
    var fd = syscall(2, path, 577, 420);
    if (fd < 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OTHER; }
    var hlen = sd_ppm_header(hdr, w, h);
    syscall(SYS_WRITE, fd, hdr, hlen);
    # ⚠ Row by row at the surface's stride, not one run of w*h pixels: on a WRAPPED
    # surface the bytes between one row's end and the next row's start are not its pixels.
    var j = 0;
    var y = 0;
    while (y < h) {
        var row = px + y * stride;
        var x = 0;
        while (x < w) {
            var off = x * 4;
            store8(rgb + j, load8(row + off + 2));      # R (BGRA byte 2)
            store8(rgb + j + 1, load8(row + off + 1));  # G
            store8(rgb + j + 2, load8(row + off));      # B (byte 0)
            x = x + 1;
            j = j + 3;
        }
        y = y + 1;
    }
    syscall(SYS_WRITE, fd, rgb, n * 3);
    syscall(SYS_CLOSE, fd);
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# ================================================================
# SdPresenter (72 bytes) — a Linux /dev/fb0 display sink.
#   +0  fd     +8  xres   +16 yres   +24 bpp   +32 pitch   +64 blit-scratch(ptr)
# ================================================================
var SD_PRES_FD_OFFSET    = 0;
var SD_PRES_XRES_OFFSET  = 8;
var SD_PRES_YRES_OFFSET  = 16;
var SD_PRES_BPP_OFFSET   = 24;
var SD_PRES_PITCH_OFFSET = 32;
var SD_PRES_BLIT_OFFSET  = 64;
var SD_PRESENTER_SIZE    = 72;

# Build an SdPresenter around an ALREADY-OPEN framebuffer fd and its probed geometry.
# Returns the presenter, or 0 if either block is refused — and on 0 THE fd IS CLOSED. A caller that
# gets 0 therefore has nothing to clean up and nothing half-built: the 72 B record is abandoned
# before its blit scratch pointer at +64 is ever stored, so no presenter with a null scratch can
# reach sd_present_blit (which would memcpy through it).
# ⛔ Split out of sd_present_open at 0.7.1 for ONE reason: sd_present_open's first act is
# open("/dev/fb0"), and a test that ran it would be writing to the live display. Taking the fd as
# an argument makes both refusals reachable from programs/oom_test.cyr with a regular file's fd.
# _sd_present_probe is the only in-tree caller.
# ⚠ It is also the only caller that chooses the geometry, which is why oom_test can build a
# 30x22x32 presenter at a 128 B pitch and programs/present_open_test.cyr — coming through the probe
# — cannot: a regular file answers no ioctl, so everything above this function falls back to
# 640x480x32.
fn _sd_presenter_build(fd, xres, yres, bpp, pitch): i64 {
    var p = sd_alloc(SD_PRESENTER_SIZE);
    if (p == 0) {
        syscall(SYS_CLOSE, fd);
        return 0;
    }
    store64(p + SD_PRES_FD_OFFSET, fd);
    store64(p + SD_PRES_XRES_OFFSET, xres);
    store64(p + SD_PRES_YRES_OFFSET, yres);
    store64(p + SD_PRES_BPP_OFFSET, bpp);
    store64(p + SD_PRES_PITCH_OFFSET, pitch);
    var blit = sd_alloc(pitch * yres);
    if (blit == 0) {
        syscall(SYS_CLOSE, fd);
        return 0;
    }
    # ⛔ THIS memset IS THE LETTERBOX, not hygiene. sd_present_blit paints only the scaled surface;
    # every byte outside it — the centering bars — is whatever the scratch already held, and the
    # WHOLE band is written to the display. Under the global bump allocator that is a fresh zeroed
    # page and the omission is invisible; under a consumer's FRAME ARENA, which lib/alloc.cyr
    # documents as REUSE and not re-zeroing ("that retention is the point"), it is the previous
    # frame's pixels. MEASURED with this line deleted, opening through an arena filled with 0xAB and
    # reset: 171 in the letterbox, read back out of the file. Gated by programs/present_open_test.cyr
    # group I, which is the only place in the tree that allocates the scratch out of dirty memory.
    memset(blit, 0, pitch * yres);
    store64(p + SD_PRES_BLIT_OFFSET, blit);
    return p;
}

# Probe an ALREADY-OPEN framebuffer fd's geometry and build its SdPresenter. Returns the
# presenter, or 0 with the fd CLOSED if either probe buffer is refused (the two refusals inside
# _sd_presenter_build close it too, so every 0 out of this function leaves no open fd).
# ⚠ The ioctls are allowed to fail: a device that answers nothing leaves the zeroed buffers, and
# the <= 0 fallbacks below are what turn that into 640x480x32. That is also what makes this entry
# safe on a REGULAR FILE, which is the only fd programs/oom_test.cyr is willing to hand it.
# ⛔ Split out of sd_present_open at 0.7.1 for the same reason as _sd_presenter_build: everything
# above it in sd_present_open is open("/dev/fb0"), and a test that ran that would be writing to the
# live display. sd_present_open_fd — the public entry 0.8.0 put in front of it — is the only in-tree
# caller.
fn _sd_present_probe(fd): i64 {
    var vinfo = sd_alloc(256);
    if (vinfo == 0) {
        syscall(SYS_CLOSE, fd);
        return 0;
    }
    var finfo = sd_alloc(256);
    if (finfo == 0) {
        syscall(SYS_CLOSE, fd);
        return 0;
    }
    # ⛔ THESE TWO memsets ARE WHAT MAKES "a device that answers nothing" MEAN 640x480x32. The
    # ioctls below are allowed to write nothing at all, and the four load32s read the buffers either
    # way — so an un-zeroed buffer hands back whatever sd_alloc last held, the three <= 0 fallbacks
    # never fire, and the geometry is garbage. Same blind spot as the scratch memset above: a fresh
    # page hides it, a recycled arena does not. MEASURED with both lines deleted, opening through an
    # arena filled with 0xAB and reset: the probe skips every fallback and the open returns 0.
    # Gated by programs/present_open_test.cyr group I.
    memset(vinfo, 0, 256);
    memset(finfo, 0, 256);
    # ⚠ RAW 16, not SYS_IOCTL — and this is the one place the 0.9.1 sweep to the stdlib's
    # target-dispatched constants deliberately STOPPED. Three of the five calls this file makes have
    # a spelling that exists on every target sadish builds for (SYS_WRITE / SYS_CLOSE / SYS_LSEEK);
    # the other two do not: aarch64 has no `open` at all (only `openat`, hence no SYS_OPEN), and
    # AGNOS's ABI has no `ioctl` (hence no SYS_IOCTL). Naming them would break the `--aarch64` and
    # `--agnos` link-checks outright, which is worse than the number being wrong on a path that
    # cannot run there anyway: everything reached through these two calls is the LINUX /dev/fb0
    # sink, and the header says so. The AGNOS sink is a separate backend (kernel blit#39), not a
    # port of this one. ⇒ Leave them raw until that backend exists; do not "finish" the sweep.
    syscall(16, fd, 0x4600, vinfo);   # ioctl FBIOGET_VSCREENINFO
    syscall(16, fd, 0x4602, finfo);   # ioctl FBIOGET_FSCREENINFO
    var xres = load32(vinfo + 0);     # fb_var_screeninfo.xres
    var yres = load32(vinfo + 4);     # .yres
    var bpp  = load32(vinfo + 24);    # .bits_per_pixel
    var pitch = load32(finfo + 48);   # fb_fix_screeninfo.line_length
    if (xres <= 0) { xres = 640; }
    if (yres <= 0) { yres = 480; }
    if (bpp  <= 0) { bpp  = 32; }
    var minpitch = xres * (bpp / 8);
    if (pitch < minpitch) { pitch = minpitch; }
    return _sd_presenter_build(fd, xres, yres, bpp, pitch);
}

# @public — present onto a framebuffer descriptor the CALLER already opened: everything
# sd_present_open does after its open(). Probes the fd's geometry, applies the fallbacks for a
# device that answers neither ioctl, and takes the four blocks a presenter needs. Returns the
# SdPresenter, or 0 if fd is negative or any of the four is refused.
# ⛔ THE PRESENTER TAKES OWNERSHIP OF `fd`, and that is the whole of this entry's contract:
#   · On SUCCESS the record holds the descriptor at +0 and sd_present_close is what releases it.
#     ⚠ Do not close an fd a live presenter still holds — the next blit would lseek and write
#     through a number the kernel has already handed to someone else's open() — and do not hand
#     one fd to two presenters, which is the same fault with two owners.
#   · On a 0 CAUSED BY A REFUSAL the fd is ALREADY CLOSED (all four refusal paths below close it),
#     so the caller must not close it again: a second close is EBADF, or worse once the number has
#     been reused. A caller that gets 0 has nothing left to release.
#   · fd < 0 is the one 0 that closes nothing and allocates nothing — it is what a failed open()
#     already returned, so there is nothing to own. Passing an open() result straight in is
#     therefore safe, which is exactly what sd_present_open_path does.
#     ⚠ `< 0`, NOT `<= 0`: descriptor 0 is a descriptor. A process that closed its standard streams
#     before opening the display gets 0 from the next open() and a presenter from here — driven,
#     with stdin closed, by programs/present_open_test.cyr group D.
# ⛔ NOT NEGATIVE IS THE ONLY THING CHECKED. `fd` must also be OPEN and opened for WRITING (O_RDWR or
# O_WRONLY), and neither is verified here or anywhere below: the ioctls are allowed to fail by
# design, and sd_present_blit lseeks and writes without checking either result. A caller that gets
# this wrong is not told. MEASURED on both mistakes:
#   · a positive number that is not an open descriptor — sd_present_open_fd(500) returns a PRESENTER,
#     costs 1,229,384 B in four blocks, and every sd_present_blit on it returns SADISH_OK having
#     written nothing.
#   · a descriptor opened O_RDONLY — same: a whole presenter, SADISH_OK from every blit, and a target
#     that stays 0 bytes long. Asserted by programs/present_open_test.cyr group J.
# ⇒ The fd is the ONE thing this entry cannot supply for itself, so validating it is the caller's
# job. sd_present_open_path exists so the common case does not have to: it opens O_RDWR and hands
# the result straight in.
# ⚠ A REGULAR FILE IS A LEGAL ARGUMENT, and it is the only fd a sadish test is allowed to present
# onto. Both ioctls fail on one (MEASURED: ENOTTY, -25), the zeroed probe buffers fall through to
# 640x480x32 at a 2,560 B pitch, and the blit writes its band into the file at the same offsets it
# would write to a display. ⛔ That geometry is sadish's FALLBACK, not the file's: no regular file
# can answer FBIOGET_VSCREENINFO, so the ioctl-answered branch is gated by no suite anywhere —
# programs/present_open_test.cyr says so in its own header rather than pretending otherwise.
fn sd_present_open_fd(fd): i64 {
    if (fd < 0) { return 0; }
    return _sd_present_probe(fd);
}

# @public — open `path` O_RDWR and present onto it: sd_present_open's body with the device name as
# an argument. Returns an SdPresenter, or 0 if the path will not open (no such file, no permission,
# a null pointer) or if any of the four blocks is refused.
# ⚠ EVERY 0 LEAVES NO OPEN fd: either the open failed, or the fd it produced was closed by the
# refusal path it reached. On success the presenter owns the fd — see sd_present_open_fd for who
# closes it and when.
# ⛔ O_RDWR, NOT O_RDONLY, and the difference is invisible until a frame is late: sd_present_blit
# lseeks and WRITES the active band through this descriptor, and nothing checks that write's result.
# A read-only open therefore yields a presenter that opens, probes and blits without complaint and
# displays nothing. programs/present_open_test.cyr reads the band back out of a regular file for
# exactly that reason.
fn sd_present_open_path(path): i64 {
    return sd_present_open_fd(syscall(2, path, 2, 0));   # open O_RDWR
}

# @public — open /dev/fb0 and probe geometry. Returns an SdPresenter, or 0 if
# the framebuffer is unavailable (no device / no permission), or if any of the four blocks it
# needs is refused — callers fall back to sd_surface_write_ppm.
# ⚠ EVERY 0 LEAVES NO OPEN fd. The fd is the first thing acquired and the only thing that has to be
# released, and all four refusal paths close it before returning. Through 0.7.0 the four blocks
# were stored through unchecked, so a refused `vinfo` was a null-pointer memset and a refused
# `blit` a presenter whose scratch was 0.
# ⛔ WHAT IS GATED AND WHAT IS NOT, 0.8.0. Everything below the device name is reachable without a
# display: programs/present_open_test.cyr drives sd_present_open_path and sd_present_open_fd over a
# regular file under build/ and sweeps a refusing hook across all four blocks. What no suite may run
# is THIS function, whose argument IS the live display — so what stays ungated is one line: the
# string "/dev/fb0" and this delegation. An edit that misspelled the device, or dropped the call,
# would leave every suite green; nothing but a display can tell.
fn sd_present_open(): i64 {
    return sd_present_open_path("/dev/fb0");
}

# @public — scale + center-letterbox `s` into the framebuffer and write it.
# ⚠ writes to the live display. Integer scale = largest that fits both axes.
fn sd_present_blit(p, s): i64 {
    if (p == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (s == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var fd = load64(p + SD_PRES_FD_OFFSET);
    var xres = load64(p + SD_PRES_XRES_OFFSET);
    var yres = load64(p + SD_PRES_YRES_OFFSET);
    var bpp = load64(p + SD_PRES_BPP_OFFSET);
    var pitch = load64(p + SD_PRES_PITCH_OFFSET);
    var blit = load64(p + SD_PRES_BLIT_OFFSET);
    var sw = sd_surface_width(s);
    var sh = sd_surface_height(s);
    var srcpx = sd_surface_pixels(s);
    var srcstride = sd_surface_stride(s);   # ⚠ source row pitch in bytes — not sw*4 on a wrapped surface

    # ⛔⛔ A 0-DIMENSION SURFACE WOULD TRAP HERE (0.11.1). Both divisions below take a surface
    # dimension as the DIVISOR. sd_surface_new refuses w <= 0, but a surface record is a public
    # layout a consumer builds itself — dhancha's dh_surface_wrap makes wrapped ones — so a 0 is
    # reachable from outside and an integer division by it kills the process.
    if (sw <= 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (sh <= 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var scale = xres / sw;
    var scy = yres / sh;
    if (scy < scale) { scale = scy; }
    if (scale < 1) { scale = 1; }
    var xoff = (xres - sw * scale) / 2;
    var yoff = (yres - sh * scale) / 2;
    if (xoff < 0) { xoff = 0; }
    if (yoff < 0) { yoff = 0; }
    var bpp_bytes = bpp / 8;

    # ⛔⛔ AND A SURFACE BIGGER THAN THE DISPLAY WROTE PAST THE SCRATCH (0.11.1). `blit` is exactly
    # pitch * yres bytes. When sw > xres the scale computes to 0, is raised to 1, xoff clamps to 0 —
    # and then the column loop ran sw times at bpp_bytes each, past the end of the row and, row after
    # row, past the end of the whole buffer. A HEAP OVERFLOW on the presenter's own scratch,
    # reachable by presenting any surface larger than the framebuffer.
    # ⚠ THE `rows` CLAMP AT THE BOTTOM DID NOT COVER THIS and reading it as though it did is the
    # trap: it bounds the WRITE TO THE DEVICE, and by then the overflowing stores have all happened.
    # ⇒ Crop the SOURCE to what fits, which is what the letterbox already does on the other axis.
    # Cropping rather than refusing keeps the presenter best-effort, exactly as `rows` always has.
    var cols = sw;
    if (xoff + cols * scale > xres) { cols = (xres - xoff) / scale; }
    if (cols < 0) { cols = 0; }
    var srows = sh;
    if (yoff + srows * scale > yres) { srows = (yres - yoff) / scale; }
    if (srows < 0) { srows = 0; }
    var row_bytes = cols * (scale * bpp_bytes);

    var sy = 0;
    while (sy < srows) {
        var src_row = srcpx + sy * srcstride;
        var dy0 = yoff + sy * scale;
        var first = blit + dy0 * pitch + xoff * bpp_bytes;
        var dx = first;
        var sx = 0;
        while (sx < cols) {
            var so = sx * 4;
            if (bpp == 16) {
                var b = load8(src_row + so);
                var g = load8(src_row + so + 1);
                var r = load8(src_row + so + 2);
                var p16 = ((r >> 3) << 11) | ((g >> 2) << 5) | (b >> 3);
                var rx = 0;
                while (rx < scale) {
                    store8(dx, p16 & 0xFF);
                    store8(dx + 1, (p16 >> 8) & 0xFF);
                    dx = dx + 2;
                    rx = rx + 1;
                }
            } else {
                var p32 = load32(src_row + so) | 0xFF000000;
                var rx = 0;
                while (rx < scale) {
                    store32(dx, p32);
                    dx = dx + bpp_bytes;
                    rx = rx + 1;
                }
            }
            sx = sx + 1;
        }
        var ry = 1;
        while (ry < scale) {
            memcpy(blit + (dy0 + ry) * pitch + xoff * bpp_bytes, first, row_bytes);
            ry = ry + 1;
        }
        sy = sy + 1;
    }

    var rows = srows * scale;
    if (yoff + rows > yres) { rows = yres - yoff; }
    if (rows < 0) { rows = 0; }
    syscall(SYS_LSEEK, fd, yoff * pitch, 0);                    # lseek SEEK_SET
    syscall(SYS_WRITE, fd, blit + yoff * pitch, rows * pitch);  # write active band
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — close the framebuffer fd.
fn sd_present_close(p): i64 {
    if (p == 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    syscall(SYS_CLOSE, load64(p + SD_PRES_FD_OFFSET));
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# --- geom.cyr ---

# @public — sadish geometry primitives: points + affine matrices.
#
# geom.cyr — the fixed-point coordinate substrate for the whole
# rasterizer. Everything-is-i64, so sub-pixel coordinates use 16.16
# signed fixed-point: the low 16 bits are the fraction, the high bits the
# integer part. 1.0 == SD_ONE (65536); an integer pixel n == n << 16.
# This keeps the entire vector pipeline (paths, flattening, transforms,
# scanline coverage) in exact integer arithmetic — no float in the core.

# ---- 16.16 fixed-point scale ------------------------------------
# 1.0 in 16.16. Shift a plain integer pixel value left by SD_SHIFT to
# lift it into fixed-point; shift right to drop back to whole pixels.
var SD_SHIFT = 16;
var SD_ONE   = 65536;    # 1 << 16

# @public — Arithmetic (sign-preserving) right shift by n. Cyrius `>>` is
# LOGICAL (zero-fill), so a plain `>>` on a negative value fills the sign
# bits with 0 and corrupts it — load-bearing for every signed subpixel /
# coverage / transform computation in the rasterizer. Valid for 0 <= n <= 63.
fn sd_asr(x, n): i64 {
    if (n <= 0) { return x; }
    if (x >= 0) { return x >> n; }
    var top = (0 - 1) << (64 - n);   # top n bits all-ones (sign extension)
    return (x >> n) | top;
}

# @public — absolute value.
fn sd_abs(v): i64 {
    if (v < 0) { return 0 - v; }
    return v;
}

# @public — integer floor square root (Newton's method). n <= 0 -> 0. Note:
# isqrt of a raw `dx*dx + dy*dy` (16.16 deltas) yields the length in 16.16
# directly, since (L·2^16)^2 = L^2·2^32 = the raw squared-length.
fn sd_isqrt(n): i64 {
    if (n <= 0) { return 0; }
    var x = n;
    var y = (x + 1) >> 1;
    while (y < x) {
        x = y;
        y = (x + n / x) >> 1;
    }
    return x;
}

# Multiply two 16.16 fixed-point values -> 16.16. (a*b) has 32 fractional
# bits; drop back down by SD_SHIFT via the SIGNED shift (sd_asr) so negative
# products — mirrors, rotations, below-origin coordinates — stay correct.
# TODO(v0.x): the 64-bit intermediate is fine for on-canvas magnitudes; a
# saturating variant is future work once large user transforms are in scope.
fn sd_fixed_mul(a, b): i64 {
    return sd_asr(a * b, SD_SHIFT);
}

# ================================================================
# SdPoint — a 2D point in 16.16 fixed-point (16 bytes).
#   +0: x   (i64 — 16.16 fixed-point)
#   +8: y   (i64 — 16.16 fixed-point)
# ================================================================
var SD_POINT_X_OFFSET = 0;
var SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET = 8;
var SD_POINT_SIZE     = 16;

# @public — a new SdPoint at (x, y), 16.16. Returns 0 IF THE ALLOCATION IS REFUSED.
# ⚠ 0 IS A REAL RETURN VALUE (0.7.1). Through 0.7.0 this stored x/y through an unchecked sd_alloc,
# so a hook that returns 0 — an arena that ran out is the natural hook the 0.5.5 seam invites —
# faulted here (SIGSEGV) instead of failing; rekha filed it
# (docs/development/issues/archived/2026-09-15-path-construction-stores-through-a-refused-allocation.md).
# Every caller in sadish now tests it: sd_path_moveto/lineto/quadto/cubicto return SADISH_ERR_OOM
# WITHOUT touching the path, sd_matrix_apply returns 0, the flatten records a truncation.
fn sd_point_new(x, y): i64 {
    var p = sd_alloc(SD_POINT_SIZE);
    if (p == 0) { return 0; }
    store64(p + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET, x);
    store64(p + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET, y);
    return p;
}

# @public
fn sd_point_x(p): i64 { return load64(p + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET); }
# @public
fn sd_point_y(p): i64 { return load64(p + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET); }

# ================================================================
# SdMatrix — a 2x3 affine transform (6 values, 48 bytes).
# Maps (x, y) -> (a*x + c*y + e,  b*x + d*y + f). All values 16.16.
#   +0:  a    +8:  b
#   +16: c    +24: d
#   +32: e    +40: f
# The (a,b,c,d) 2x2 is the linear part; (e,f) the translation.
# ================================================================
var SD_MAT_A_OFFSET = 0;
var SD_MAT_B_OFFSET = 8;
var SD_MAT_C_OFFSET = 16;
var SD_MAT_D_OFFSET = 24;
var SD_MAT_E_OFFSET = 32;
var SD_MAT_F_OFFSET = 40;
var SD_MATRIX_SIZE  = 48;

# Low-level constructor from all six 16.16 components. Returns 0 if the allocation is refused —
# and so, therefore, do sd_matrix_identity / _translate / _scale / _mul / _rotate / _skew, every
# one of which is this constructor with its arguments computed (0.7.1; through 0.7.0 a refusal
# stored six values through a null pointer).
fn sd_matrix_new(a, b, c, d, e, f): i64 {
    var m = sd_alloc(SD_MATRIX_SIZE);
    if (m == 0) { return 0; }
    store64(m + SD_MAT_A_OFFSET, a);
    store64(m + SD_MAT_B_OFFSET, b);
    store64(m + SD_MAT_C_OFFSET, c);
    store64(m + SD_MAT_D_OFFSET, d);
    store64(m + SD_MAT_E_OFFSET, e);
    store64(m + SD_MAT_F_OFFSET, f);
    return m;
}

# @public — the identity matrix (a=d=1.0, all else 0).
fn sd_matrix_identity(): i64 {
    return sd_matrix_new(SD_ONE, 0, 0, SD_ONE, 0, 0);
}

# @public — a pure translation by (tx, ty), both 16.16.
fn sd_matrix_translate(tx, ty): i64 {
    return sd_matrix_new(SD_ONE, 0, 0, SD_ONE, tx, ty);
}

# @public — a pure scale by (sx, sy), both 16.16.
fn sd_matrix_scale(sx, sy): i64 {
    return sd_matrix_new(sx, 0, 0, sy, 0, 0);
}

# @public — compose two affine transforms: result applies `n` THEN `m`
# (i.e. result(pt) == m(n(pt))). Standard 2x3 * 2x3 affine product.
# Returns 0 if either operand is 0 (a constructor that was refused) or the result cannot be
# allocated — so a chain of composes propagates one refusal to its end instead of faulting.
fn sd_matrix_mul(m, n): i64 {
    if (m == 0) { return 0; }
    if (n == 0) { return 0; }
    var ma = load64(m + SD_MAT_A_OFFSET);
    var mb = load64(m + SD_MAT_B_OFFSET);
    var mc = load64(m + SD_MAT_C_OFFSET);
    var md = load64(m + SD_MAT_D_OFFSET);
    var me = load64(m + SD_MAT_E_OFFSET);
    var mf = load64(m + SD_MAT_F_OFFSET);
    var na = load64(n + SD_MAT_A_OFFSET);
    var nb = load64(n + SD_MAT_B_OFFSET);
    var nc = load64(n + SD_MAT_C_OFFSET);
    var nd = load64(n + SD_MAT_D_OFFSET);
    var ne = load64(n + SD_MAT_E_OFFSET);
    var nf = load64(n + SD_MAT_F_OFFSET);
    var ra = sd_fixed_mul(na, ma) + sd_fixed_mul(nb, mc);
    var rb = sd_fixed_mul(na, mb) + sd_fixed_mul(nb, md);
    var rc = sd_fixed_mul(nc, ma) + sd_fixed_mul(nd, mc);
    var rd = sd_fixed_mul(nc, mb) + sd_fixed_mul(nd, md);
    var re = sd_fixed_mul(ne, ma) + sd_fixed_mul(nf, mc) + me;
    var rf = sd_fixed_mul(ne, mb) + sd_fixed_mul(nf, md) + mf;
    return sd_matrix_new(ra, rb, rc, rd, re, rf);
}

# round(n * 2^k / b) to the nearest integer, halves away from zero, for b != 0 and 0 <= k <= 61.
# Restoring long division to k + 1 fraction bits, so no intermediate exceeds |b| (a remainder is
# doubled only when that stays below |b|) — exact as long as the rounded quotient itself fits i64.
# ⚠ |n| and |b| must be below 2^63 (neither may be the i64 minimum).
fn _sd_geom_divround(n, b, k): i64 {
    var num = n;
    var den = b;
    if (den < 0) {
        den = 0 - den;
        num = 0 - num;
    }
    var neg = 0;
    if (num < 0) {
        num = 0 - num;
        neg = 1;
    }
    var q = num / den;
    var rem = num - q * den;
    var i = 0;
    while (i <= k) {
        q = q << 1;
        if (rem >= den - rem) {
            rem = rem - (den - rem);
            q = q + 1;
        } else {
            rem = rem + rem;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    # q = floor(2x) for x = |n| 2^k / |b|; floor(x + 1/2) = (q + 1) >> 1.
    q = (q + 1) >> 1;
    if (neg != 0) { return 0 - q; }
    return q;
}

# @public — the inverse of affine `m` as a NEW SdMatrix (sd_alloc), or 0 when `m` is SINGULAR
# (a*d - b*c == 0 in raw units — exactly, no tolerance), when `m` is 0 (a refused constructor,
# 0.7.1), or when the allocation fails.
#   · Each entry is the EXACT inverse's entry rounded to the nearest raw unit (2^-16), halves away
#     from zero: the linear part is adj(m) * 2^32 / det, the translation -m^-1 (e, f) is taken from
#     the exact rational (c*f - d*e, b*e - a*f) * 2^16 / det, never from the rounded linear part.
#     So a matrix whose inverse is representable (identity, translations, power-of-two scales,
#     quarter-turns) inverts EXACTLY.
#   · ⚠ Composing back is not exact: sd_matrix_mul floors every product. Per entry, with the
#     16.16 entries of m read as reals, |m * inv(m) - I| < 2 + (|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|) / 2 raw units
#     (translation included), and |inv(m) * m - I| < 3 + (|a| + |b| + |c| + |d| + |e| + |f|) / 2 —
#     the inverse's rounding is scaled by the matrix it multiplies. programs/paint_focal_test.cyr
#     asserts both bounds and the exact cases.
#   ⚠ PRECISION BOUND: every entry of m within ±32,768.0 (raw magnitude < 2^31), so det and the
#     translation numerators stay < 2^63. A nearly singular m (|det| tiny) has inverse entries past
#     i64 — wrong values, never a trap.
fn sd_matrix_invert(m): i64 {
    if (m == 0) { return 0; }
    var a = load64(m + SD_MAT_A_OFFSET);
    var b = load64(m + SD_MAT_B_OFFSET);
    var c = load64(m + SD_MAT_C_OFFSET);
    var d = load64(m + SD_MAT_D_OFFSET);
    var e = load64(m + SD_MAT_E_OFFSET);
    var f = load64(m + SD_MAT_F_OFFSET);
    var det = a * d - b * c;
    if (det == 0) { return 0; }
    var r = sd_alloc(SD_MATRIX_SIZE);
    if (r == 0) { return 0; }
    store64(r + SD_MAT_A_OFFSET, _sd_geom_divround(d, det, 32));
    store64(r + SD_MAT_B_OFFSET, _sd_geom_divround(0 - b, det, 32));
    store64(r + SD_MAT_C_OFFSET, _sd_geom_divround(0 - c, det, 32));
    store64(r + SD_MAT_D_OFFSET, _sd_geom_divround(a, det, 32));
    store64(r + SD_MAT_E_OFFSET, _sd_geom_divround(c * f - d * e, det, 16));
    store64(r + SD_MAT_F_OFFSET, _sd_geom_divround(b * e - a * f, det, 16));
    return r;
}

# @public — apply an affine transform to a point, returning a new SdPoint.
# (x, y) -> (a*x + c*y + e,  b*x + d*y + f). All 16.16.
# Returns 0 when `m` or `pt` is 0 (a constructor sadish refused) or when the result point cannot
# be allocated — it never dereferences a refused matrix (0.7.1).
fn sd_matrix_apply(m, pt): i64 {
    if (m == 0) { return 0; }
    if (pt == 0) { return 0; }
    var x = sd_point_x(pt);
    var y = sd_point_y(pt);
    var a = load64(m + SD_MAT_A_OFFSET);
    var b = load64(m + SD_MAT_B_OFFSET);
    var c = load64(m + SD_MAT_C_OFFSET);
    var d = load64(m + SD_MAT_D_OFFSET);
    var e = load64(m + SD_MAT_E_OFFSET);
    var f = load64(m + SD_MAT_F_OFFSET);
    var nx = sd_fixed_mul(a, x) + sd_fixed_mul(c, y) + e;
    var ny = sd_fixed_mul(b, x) + sd_fixed_mul(d, y) + f;
    return sd_point_new(nx, ny);
}

# ================================================================
# Fixed-point trig via CORDIC — sovereign (no float, no libm), all shifts +
# adds through the SIGNED sd_asr. 16 rotation iterations after range-reducing
# to [-pi/2, pi/2]; accurate to a few 16.16 LSBs.
# ================================================================
# atan(2^-i) in 16.16 radians, i = 0..15.
fn sd_cordic_atan(i): i64 {
    if (i == 0)  { return 51472; }
    if (i == 1)  { return 30386; }
    if (i == 2)  { return 16055; }
    if (i == 3)  { return 8150; }
    if (i == 4)  { return 4091; }
    if (i == 5)  { return 2047; }
    if (i == 6)  { return 1024; }
    if (i == 7)  { return 512; }
    if (i == 8)  { return 256; }
    if (i == 9)  { return 128; }
    if (i == 10) { return 64; }
    if (i == 11) { return 32; }
    if (i == 12) { return 16; }
    if (i == 13) { return 8; }
    if (i == 14) { return 4; }
    return 2;
}

# theta in 16.16 radians -> cos (want_sin=0) or sin (want_sin=1) in 16.16.
fn sd_cordic(theta, want_sin): i64 {
    var two_pi = 411775;
    var pi = 205887;
    var half_pi = 102944;
    # ⛔⛔ THIS WAS A HANG, NOT A SLOW PATH (0.11.1). The reduction was
    #     while (t > pi) { t = t - two_pi; }   while (t < 0 - pi) { t = t + two_pi; }
    # — O(|theta| / 2pi) iterations on a PUBLIC entry that accepts any i64. sd_matrix_rotate(2^62)
    # is ~1.1e13 subtractions: not slow, ABSENT. A consumer deriving an angle from untrusted data
    # (a transform in an SVG, a font's italic angle) hands that straight in.
    # ⭐ THE REPLACEMENT IS THE SAME ARITHMETIC, NOT AN APPROXIMATION OF IT. The old loop ended at
    # t = theta - k * two_pi for the exact integer k it happened to subtract; this computes that
    # same k in one division. ⚠ It matters that two_pi stays the ROUNDED 411775 and is not
    # re-derived: k * 411775 is what the loop accumulated, so reducing against anything more
    # accurate would move every angle. Byte-identical output is the requirement, not better output.
    var t = theta;
    if (t > pi) {
        var k = (t - pi + two_pi - 1) / two_pi;      # ceil((t - pi) / two_pi), both operands > 0
        t = t - k * two_pi;
    }
    if (t < 0 - pi) {
        var k2 = (0 - t - pi + two_pi - 1) / two_pi;
        t = t + k2 * two_pi;
    }
    # Fold [-pi,pi] into [-pi/2, pi/2]; a fold past +-pi/2 flips both signs.
    var flip = 0;
    if (t > half_pi) { t = t - pi; flip = 1; }
    if (t < 0 - half_pi) { t = t + pi; flip = 1; }

    var x = 39797;   # K gain (1 / A_16) in 16.16 — cancels the CORDIC gain
    var y = 0;
    var z = t;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < 16) {
        var dx = sd_asr(x, i);
        var dy = sd_asr(y, i);
        if (z >= 0) {
            x = x - dy;
            y = y + dx;
            z = z - sd_cordic_atan(i);
        } else {
            x = x + dy;
            y = y - dx;
            z = z + sd_cordic_atan(i);
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (flip != 0) { x = 0 - x; y = 0 - y; }
    if (want_sin != 0) { return y; }
    return x;
}

# @public — cos(theta), theta in 16.16 radians -> 16.16.
fn sd_cos(theta): i64 { return sd_cordic(theta, 0); }
# @public — sin(theta), theta in 16.16 radians -> 16.16.
fn sd_sin(theta): i64 { return sd_cordic(theta, 1); }

# @public — rotation by `theta` (16.16 radians). Maps (x,y) ->
# (x*cos - y*sin, x*sin + y*cos): a=cos, b=sin, c=-sin, d=cos.
fn sd_matrix_rotate(theta): i64 {
    var c = sd_cos(theta);
    var s = sd_sin(theta);
    return sd_matrix_new(c, s, 0 - s, c, 0, 0);
}

# Divide two 16.16 fixed-point values -> 16.16 (signed). The a<<SD_SHIFT
# pre-scale is exact for on-canvas / trig magnitudes; very large operands can
# overflow the 64-bit intermediate (same caveat as sd_fixed_mul). b==0 -> 0.
fn sd_fixed_div(a, b): i64 {
    if (b == 0) { return 0; }
    return (a << SD_SHIFT) / b;
}

# @public — skew by angles (ax, ay) in 16.16 radians. x' = x + y*tan(ax),
# y' = y + x*tan(ay): a=1, c=tan(ax), b=tan(ay), d=1. tan = sin/cos.
fn sd_matrix_skew(ax, ay): i64 {
    var tx = sd_fixed_div(sd_sin(ax), sd_cos(ax));
    var ty = sd_fixed_div(sd_sin(ay), sd_cos(ay));
    return sd_matrix_new(SD_ONE, ty, tx, SD_ONE, 0, 0);
}

# --- path.cyr ---

# @public — sadish path construction + curve flattening.
#
# path.cyr — an SdPath is a growable command list: a stream of verbs
# (moveto / lineto / quadto / cubicto / close) plus a parallel stream of
# control points. This is the resolution-independent geometry a consumer
# builds; the rasterizer (raster.cyr) consumes the FLATTENED polyline the
# curve verbs expand into.
#
# Storage model: two bump-allocated arrays sized at construction, EACH
# WITH ITS OWN CAPACITY (0.7.2). Verbs are one i64 each; POINTS ARE THE
# COORDINATES THEMSELVES — x then y, 16 B a slot, INLINE in the array (0.9.0;
# through 0.8.0 a slot was an 8 B POINTER to a separately allocated 16 B
# SdPoint). A quadto consumes 2 points (control, end); a cubicto 3 (c1, c2,
# end); moveto and lineto 1 (the target); close 0.
# Storage is unbounded as of v0.4.0 — sd_path_grow doubles ONE array, the
# one that overflowed (0.7.2; through 0.7.1 it doubled both); SD_PATH_CAP is
# only sd_path_new's initial allocation, and sd_path_new_cap (0.7.1) opens
# each array at the size the caller already knows.
#
# ⛔⛔ READ A PATH'S POINTS THROUGH sd_path_point_x / sd_path_point_y (0.9.0). They are the
# published way and the ONLY supported one: the layout below is sadish's, and a consumer that
# indexes SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET itself is written against a record that has now changed shape
# twice. ⚠ 14 reads across 5 rekha test programs did exactly that, because through 0.8.0 there was
# no accessor to read — half of why this ABI break is worth taking is that there is one now.
#   var i = 0;
#   while (i < sd_path_point_count(p)) {
#       ... sd_path_point_x(p, i), sd_path_point_y(p, i) ...       # 16.16, no allocation
#       i = i + 1;
#   }
# sd_path_verb_at(p, i) is the same for the verb stream, which did NOT change shape.
# ⚠ SADISH'S OWN WALKS TAKE ONE EXEMPTION, STATED HERE SO A LATER READER DOES NOT TAKE IT FOR
# LICENCE. A verb walk reads both streams once per verb and knows no builder can run mid-walk, so
# it HOISTS a base pointer out of the loop and indexes it: every verb stream in src/ does
# (raster.cyr's fill, both of stroke.cyr's, and this file's), and sd_path_flatten below does it for
# the POINTS too. That saves one dependent load per read and is safe only INSIDE the library, which
# owns the layout and moves it in the same commit. Outside those loops sadish reads its own paths
# the published way — 37 sd_path_point_x / sd_path_point_y calls across raster.cyr and stroke.cyr,
# which are the in-tree example to copy. sd_path_verb_at has NO internal caller: it exists so a
# consumer's walk carries no offsets at all, and its gates are programs/inline_points_test.cyr and
# programs/path_cap_test.cyr.
#
# ⭐ SdPolyline FOLLOWED AT 0.10.0 — the note here used to say it was deliberately left alone, and
# the deferral is over. 0.9.0 declined to break two records in one release ("a second ABI break in
# one release is how a consumer stops trusting the version number") and that cost was visible: the
# flatten had to MATERIALISE an SdPoint for every point it emitted, because the output held
# pointers, so what the path stopped paying per point the flatten started paying. 0.10.0 gives the
# polyline the same inline slots and the per-point allocation leaves the library entirely — see
# the SdPolyline record below and sd_polyline_point_x / _y.
# ⚠ SdPoint ITSELF IS UNTOUCHED and stays published: sd_point_new, sd_matrix_apply (which takes and
# returns one) and sd_pt_mid still build 16 B records, and programs/rotate_test.cyr and
# geom_test.cyr hold them. What changed is that no STORE in sadish is an SdPoint any more — both
# arrays hold coordinates, and the record is now purely an argument type.

# ---- verb tags --------------------------------------------------
var SD_VERB_MOVETO = 0;
var SD_VERB_LINETO = 1;
var SD_VERB_QUADTO = 2;
var SD_VERB_CUBICTO = 3;
var SD_VERB_CLOSE  = 4;

# sd_path_new's initial capacity for the VERB array; it doubles on its own overflow (v0.4.0), so
# this is just the first allocation size. ⚠ Through 0.8.0 it sized the point array too.
var SD_PATH_CAP = 256;

# ⭐⭐ sd_path_new's initial capacity for the POINT array, RE-DERIVED WITH THE SLOT (0.9.0). A point
# slot went from 8 B to 16, so 256 slots would have taken sd_path_new's first allocation from
# 48 + 2,048 + 2,048 = 4,144 B to 6,192 B — a 2,048 B regression on EVERY path a caller opens
# without a size, and the default is what an unported consumer uses. 128 slots is the same 2,048 B
# of points, so **sd_path_new costs the 4,144 B in 3 allocations it has cost since 0.4.0**, and a
# small path — rekha's moveto + 2 linetos + close — falls from 4,192 B in 6 calls to 4,144 in 3.
# ⛔ IT IS A MEASURED DECISION AND IT HAS A PRICE: a default path's point array now doubles at the
# 129th point where it used to reach 256, so a path of 129-256 points pays one 4,096 B doubling
# (and one memcpy) that 0.8.0 did not. MEASURED against the alternative — 256 inline slots — on
# dhancha's own gate: its 512 KiB text arena holds a 60-glyph run at 20 px with 128 and CHAINS
# ANOTHER CHUNK with 256 (dhancha's programs/text_arena_test.cyr, the check that pins
# arena_capacity_total at 524,288). rekha builds one sd_path_new per glyph and a glyph of this
# corpus is 26 points on average, 90 at the largest — an order below the new boundary.
# ⚠ The two constants are now ASYMMETRIC on purpose: verbs 256, points 128, both 2,048 B. Callers
# that know their size should use sd_path_new_cap and neither constant reaches them.
var SD_PATH_PCAP = 128;

# The floor sd_path_new_cap clamps to, so a path asked for at 0 (or a negative estimate) still
# holds a closed rect — moveto + 3 linetos + close = 5 verbs, 4 points — without a doubling.
# 128 B of arrays; the smallest useful path, not a second default.
var SD_PATH_CAP_MIN = 8;

# ⛔⛔ THE CEILING, AND WHY A PATH HAS ONE (0.7.1; RE-DERIVED FOR THE 16 B POINT SLOT, 0.9.0). The
# verb array is `cap * 8` bytes and the POINT array is now `cap * 16`, and both MULTIPLIES WRAP: a
# capacity past 2^60 wraps mod 2^64 to a small positive byte count, so the allocator hands back a
# tiny block, the 0-checks pass, the record is stored with a capacity of 1.2e18 behind it, and the
# SECOND push writes off the end of the array — MEASURED before this ceiling existed (0.7.1, at the
# 8 B slot), sd_path_new_cap(2305843009213693953, 0) returned a live path whose verb and point
# blocks were 8 B apart and whose second verb landed on top of the first point.
# ⇒ 2^27 entries — HALF of 0.7.1's 2^28, because the point slot doubled: 2^27 * 16 B = 2 GiB =
# lib/alloc.cyr's ALLOC_MAX, so this is again the largest POINT array the stock allocator can EVER
# serve, and every capacity above it is already refused by the allocator (MEASURED:
# sd_path_new_cap(0, 134217729) -> 0 with the ceiling removed, because alloc refuses 2,147,483,664 B).
# ⚠ ONE CONSTANT BOUNDS BOTH ARRAYS AND IT TAKES THE TIGHTER SLOT. A 2^27-entry VERB array is only
# 1 GiB, so a verb capacity in (2^27, 2^28] is refused here that 0.8.0 served — a deliberate,
# measured narrowing rather than a second constant: a path with 134 M verbs and no points is not a
# shape this stack draws, and one ceiling is one thing to keep true. The behaviour this ceiling
# really exists for is unchanged — the wrap windows are refused, not clamped.
# ⚠ A 2^27-entry path is 1 GiB of verbs + 2 GiB of points and NO SdPoints at all (0.9.0); through
# 0.8.0 the same point count also carried 4 GiB of 16 B records on the seam.
var SD_PATH_CAP_MAX = 134217728;

# ================================================================
# SdPath (48 bytes — the same 48 B since 0.4.0):
#   +0:  verbs      (ptr — array of `vcap` i64 verb tags)
#   +8:  n_verbs    (i64 — verb count)
#   +16: points     (ptr — array of `pcap` INLINE (x, y) PAIRS, 16 B each: x at +0, y at +8)
#   +24: n_points   (i64 — point count)
#   +32: vcap       (i64 — the VERB array's capacity)
#   +40: pcap       (i64 — the POINT array's capacity)
# ================================================================
# ⛔⛔ +16 POINTS AT THE SAME OFFSET, A DIFFERENT ELEMENT (0.9.0) — THE ABI BREAK OF THIS RELEASE.
# Point i was `load64(load64(p + 16) + i * 8)` -> an SdPoint, and is now the two words at
# `load64(p + 16) + i * 16`. The RECORD is still 48 B and every other field is where it was, so a
# consumer that only counts verbs and points compiles and runs unchanged; a consumer that WALKS the
# points array reads a coordinate where it expected a pointer and dereferences it.
# ⇒ sd_path_point_x / sd_path_point_y (below) are the fix, and they are the only read this file
# will promise to keep working. ⚠ The break is loud by construction — an x of 8*65536 dereferenced
# as an SdPoint is a SIGSEGV at the first walk, not a wrong picture — which is why the old shape is
# NOT kept readable behind a flag: a silent half-port is the worse failure.
# ⚠ THE SWEEP, re-run 2026-09-16: outside this repo the only SD_PATH_* reads anywhere are
# SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET / SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET in SIX rekha test programs, of which FIVE index the
# points array (14 point-array reads between them: cff_test 7, glyf_edge_test 4, path_start_test 1,
# hostile_test 1, bench_hotpath 1 — a read here is one load FROM the array, which then yielded an
# SdPoint pointer). The sixth, path_test, reads SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET only and is unaffected: that
# stream did not change shape. rekha's own src/ names no SD_PATH_* constant at all. dhancha names
# sd_path_new's cost in comments only, agnos's refagree calls the public API, and crab and puka
# vendor a stale
# dist/sadish.cyr wholesale (crab draws through its own copy; puka's is inert — no sadish dep, no
# sd_path_* call). Nothing anywhere hand-builds an SdPath, and sd_path_new / sd_path_new_cap are
# still the only constructors.
# ⛔⛔ +40 WAS `reserved` THROUGH 0.7.1, AND IS THE POINT ARRAY'S CAPACITY FROM 0.7.2. That word was
# written 0 by every constructor and read by NOTHING — so no record any consumer holds changed
# meaning and the record is still 48 B. The name SD_PATH_RESERVED_OFFSET is GONE rather than
# aliased: a constant that says "reserved" pointing at a live capacity is the trap, not the fix.
# ⚠ THE SWEEP THAT SAYS SO, AND THE COMPLETE SET OF PLACES A STALE COPY LIVES (re-run 2026-09-16):
# src/, programs/, rekha, dhancha, agnos, and the TWO repos that vendor dist/sadish.cyr wholesale —
# crab AND puka (puka/lib/sadish.cyr:925 still carries 0.7.1's SD_PATH_RESERVED_OFFSET; puka's own
# src/, programs/ and tests/ name no SD_PATH_* constant and call no sd_path_* function, and its
# cyrius.cyml declares no sadish dep, so the copy is inert). Outside this repo the only SD_PATH_*
# READS anywhere are SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET / SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET in six rekha test programs (the
# same six the ⛔⛔ block above breaks down: five index the points array, path_test reads verbs
# only), and no repo calls sd_path_grow — whose arity changed here — at all.
# ⚠ ONE SHAPE DOES CHANGE: a record a consumer HAND-BUILT to the 0.7.1 layout carries 0 at +40, and
# a 0 point capacity is refused (SADISH_ERR_OOM from _sd_path_reserve / sd_path_push_point) rather
# than written through — the safe direction, and the reason both capacities are 0-checked. Nothing
# in this repo or in any of those five repos hand-builds one; sd_path_new / sd_path_new_cap are the
# only constructors.
var SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET   = 0;
var SD_PATH_NVERBS_OFFSET  = 8;
var SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET  = 16;
var SD_PATH_NPOINTS_OFFSET = 24;
var SD_PATH_CAP_OFFSET     = 32;
var SD_PATH_PCAP_OFFSET    = 40;
var SD_PATH_SIZE           = 48;
# One point SLOT of an SdPath's points array, in bytes: x at +0, y at +8 (0.9.0; 8 B, a pointer,
# through 0.8.0).
# ⚠ THREE CONSTANTS ARE 16 AND ALL THREE MEAN SOMETHING DIFFERENT. Nothing makes them equal but the
# fact that each happens to hold two i64s, and a change to one must not be propagated to the others:
#   SD_PATH_PT_SIZE      this — the stride of an SdPath's points array
#   SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE  the stride of an SdPolyline's points array (0.10.0)
#   SD_POINT_SIZE        the size of a standalone SdPoint record (src/geom.cyr), which is now only
#                        an ARGUMENT type — since 0.10.0 no STORE in sadish is an SdPoint.
var SD_PATH_PT_SIZE        = 16;

# Which array sd_path_grow doubles.
var SD_PATH_GROW_VERBS  = 0;
var SD_PATH_GROW_POINTS = 1;

# An empty SdPath whose verb array holds `vcap` entries and whose point array holds `pcap` — the
# two are INDEPENDENT (0.7.2; through 0.7.1 one `cap` sized both). 0 if ANY of the three
# allocations is refused — record, verbs and points, in that order (0.7.1; through 0.7.0 only the
# record was checked and a refused verb array was stored as 0, which the first sd_path_moveto then
# wrote through: SIGSEGV, rekha's filing). An earlier block of a failed path is abandoned; the
# allocator sadish draws from has no free().
# ⛔ BOTH capacities are bounded BEFORE either multiply — see SD_PATH_CAP_MAX. `cap * 8` (verbs) or
# `cap * 16` (points) on an unrepresentable capacity wraps to a small block the first pushes then
# run off the end of, and no 0-check can see that: both allocations SUCCEED. ⚠ The two ceiling
# tests run before the FIRST sd_alloc, so an over-ceiling point capacity costs zero allocations even
# though the verb array is allocated first (programs/path_cap_test.cyr counts them).
# ⚠ THE THREE REQUESTS ARE STILL THREE, AND THE THIRD ONE DOUBLED: 48 B, `vcap * 8`, `pcap * 16`
# (0.9.0; `pcap * 8` through 0.8.0, with a 16 B sd_alloc per point AFTER it). A path at exact
# capacity is now THREE allocations whatever its point count — that is the release, in one line.
fn _sd_path_alloc(vcap, pcap): i64 {
    if (vcap > SD_PATH_CAP_MAX) { return 0; }
    if (pcap > SD_PATH_CAP_MAX) { return 0; }
    var p = sd_alloc(SD_PATH_SIZE);
    if (p == 0) { return 0; }
    var verbs = sd_alloc(vcap * 8);
    if (verbs == 0) { return 0; }
    var points = sd_alloc(pcap * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE);
    if (points == 0) { return 0; }
    store64(p + SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET, verbs);
    store64(p + SD_PATH_NVERBS_OFFSET, 0);
    store64(p + SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET, points);
    store64(p + SD_PATH_NPOINTS_OFFSET, 0);
    store64(p + SD_PATH_CAP_OFFSET, vcap);
    store64(p + SD_PATH_PCAP_OFFSET, pcap);
    return p;
}

# @public — allocate an empty path at the default capacity: SD_PATH_CAP = 256 verbs and
# SD_PATH_PCAP = 128 points (0.9.0; 256 points through 0.8.0 — see SD_PATH_PCAP for why the point
# half halved when the slot doubled). MEASURED, and unchanged since 0.4.0: 48 + 2,048 + 2,048 =
# **4,144 B in 3 allocations**.
# ⭐ WHAT DID CHANGE IS WHAT COMES AFTER IT: a push costs NOTHING until an array overflows, so
# rekha's small path — moveto + 2 linetos + close — is **4,144 B in 3 calls** where 0.8.0 paid
# 4,192 B in 6. The three SdPoints and the three sd_alloc calls that built them are gone.
# ⚠ A caller that knows its size should still use sd_path_new_cap: the same small path is 240 B
# there, and the 95-glyph ASCII set falls from 393,680 B to 58,672 B
# (docs/development/proposals/archived/2026-09-15-path-capacity-for-known-size-paths.md).
fn sd_path_new(): i64 {
    return _sd_path_alloc(SD_PATH_CAP, SD_PATH_PCAP);
}

# @public — allocate an empty path sized for `n_verbs` verbs and `n_points` points, so a caller
# that knows the shape ahead of the first moveto never pays a doubling. Returns 0 if any
# allocation is refused.
#
# ⭐⭐ TWO CAPACITIES, TWO ARRAYS (0.7.2) — n_verbs sizes the verb array and n_points the point
# array, each clamped on its own into [SD_PATH_CAP_MIN, SD_PATH_CAP_MAX]. ⚠ Through 0.7.1 the two
# arguments COLLAPSED TO THEIR MAX because one field sized both arrays; a glyph therefore bought
# about 2x more verb slots than it uses, since a TrueType contour of n points emits at most n + 1
# verbs and 2n + 1 points. The signature did not change — a 0.7.1 call site is already correct.
# MEASURED (95 ASCII-shaped paths — 1,768 verbs, 2,498 points, largest glyph 61 verbs — built
# through sd_path_moveto/lineto/quadto/close on the allocation seam, programs/path_cap_test.cyr):
#   sd_path_new(), 0.8.0                  433,648 B in 2,783 calls  (95 x 4,144 + 2,498 x 16)
#   sd_path_new(), HERE                   393,680 B in   285 calls  (95 x 4,144, and no records)
#   sd_path_new_cap, ONE capacity (0.7.1)  84,496 B                 = 5.13x less than 0.8.0's row
#   sd_path_new_cap, two capacities (0.7.2) 78,656 B in 2,783 calls = 5.51x
#   the same, INLINE (x, y) points, HERE    58,672 B in   285 calls = 6.71x less than THIS tree's
#                                                                     own sd_path_new row
# = 95 x 48 (records) + 8 x 1,768 (verbs) + 16 x 2,498 (point slots), and NOTHING ELSE — the 39,968
# B of SdPoint records and the 2,498 sd_alloc calls that built them are gone. ⭐ THE CALL COUNT IS
# THE OTHER HALF: 285 = 3 per path, whatever the path holds.
# ⚠ The 58,672 B is the figure this proposal's "Worth measuring alongside" section computed in
# 0.7.1 and asked to be hit; programs/path_cap_test.cyr group L pins it on the seam.
# ⭐ NOT a cap: an underestimate is still correct. Each array doubles on its own overflow exactly as
# it does for sd_path_new, so a path asked for at 4 that takes 300 points ends up where it would
# have ended up anyway — it just pays for the doublings, and only in the array that overflowed.
# ⚠ 0 or negative for either argument is raised to SD_PATH_CAP_MIN = 8 — INDEPENDENTLY, so
# sd_path_new_cap(61, 0) is a 61-verb, 8-point path, not an error and not a default-sized one.
# ⛔ AND THE OTHER END IS REFUSED, NOT CLAMPED: EITHER capacity above SD_PATH_CAP_MAX = 2^27 (0.9.0;
# 2^28 through 0.8.0) returns 0. That is the ceiling `cap * 8` (verbs) and `cap * 16` (points) stay
# representable and servable under (see SD_PATH_CAP_MAX) — a count read out of a file is exactly the
# shape of argument that reaches 2^60 and wraps.
fn sd_path_new_cap(n_verbs, n_points): i64 {
    var vcap = n_verbs;
    if (vcap < SD_PATH_CAP_MIN) { vcap = SD_PATH_CAP_MIN; }
    var pcap = n_points;
    if (pcap < SD_PATH_CAP_MIN) { pcap = SD_PATH_CAP_MIN; }
    return _sd_path_alloc(vcap, pcap);
}

# ---- accessors --------------------------------------------------
# @public
fn sd_path_verb_count(path): i64  { return load64(path + SD_PATH_NVERBS_OFFSET); }
# @public
fn sd_path_point_count(path): i64 { return load64(path + SD_PATH_NPOINTS_OFFSET); }
# @public — how many verbs the verb array can hold before it doubles (0.7.2). sd_path_new() reports
# SD_PATH_CAP; sd_path_new_cap(v, p) reports v clamped into [SD_PATH_CAP_MIN, SD_PATH_CAP_MAX].
fn sd_path_verb_cap(path): i64  { return load64(path + SD_PATH_CAP_OFFSET); }
# @public — how many points the point array can hold before it doubles (0.7.2). This is the word at
# +40 that was `reserved` through 0.7.1 — see the record layout above.
fn sd_path_point_cap(path): i64 { return load64(path + SD_PATH_PCAP_OFFSET); }

# ⭐⭐ THE PUBLISHED WAY TO READ A PATH'S GEOMETRY (0.9.0) — sd_path_point_x / sd_path_point_y /
# sd_path_verb_at. Use these and the layout above is sadish's business; index the arrays yourself
# and you are writing against a record that changed shape in 0.9.0 and can again.
# ⚠ THEY DO NOT BOUNDS-CHECK, exactly as sd_polyline_count / sd_path_verb_count do not test `path`:
# `i` must be in [0, sd_path_point_count(path)) — one load off the end of a bump-allocated array is
# a stale i64, not a trap, so a walk that runs past the count reads garbage in silence. Bound the
# loop on the count; the count is the contract.
# ⚠ THE COORDINATES ARE RETURNED, NOT AN SdPoint. There is no record to free, to null-check, or to
# keep alive — and no allocation happens here, which is the whole of what 0.9.0 bought. A caller
# that really needs an SdPoint (sd_matrix_apply takes one) builds it: sd_point_new(x, y).
# @public — point `i`'s x, 16.16.
fn sd_path_point_x(path, i): i64 {
    return load64(load64(path + SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET) + i * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
}
# @public — point `i`'s y, 16.16.
fn sd_path_point_y(path, i): i64 {
    return load64(load64(path + SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET) + i * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
}
# @public — verb `i`'s tag: SD_VERB_MOVETO / _LINETO / _QUADTO / _CUBICTO / _CLOSE. The verb stream
# is one i64 a verb and did NOT change shape in 0.9.0; this exists so a consumer walking a path
# reaches for accessors in both streams and stops carrying the offsets at all.
fn sd_path_verb_at(path, i): i64 {
    return load64(load64(path + SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET) + i * 8);
}

# @public — apply an affine matrix to EVERY point of `path`, IN PLACE (0.10.0). Returns SADISH_OK,
# SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null path or matrix, or SADISH_ERR_UNSUPPORTED for nothing — it cannot
# fail otherwise, because it allocates NOTHING.
#
# ⭐ WHY IT IS ALLOCATION-FREE, AND WHY THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT. sd_matrix_apply takes an SdPoint
# and RETURNS A NEW ONE — a 16 B sd_alloc per point — so the loop every consumer writes by hand
# (`sd_matrix_apply` over a path it rebuilds) costs one allocation per point and a second path.
# A path's points are two plain i64 in a flat array since 0.9.0, so the transform is a read, six
# multiplies and a write, with no record to rebuild and no second path to hold. MEASURED on a
# 2,498-point corpus: the rebuild-through-sd_matrix_apply shape costs 2,498 allocations and a whole
# second path; this costs 0 and touches only the array already there.
# ⚠ IN PLACE means the original geometry IS GONE. Copy the path first if both are wanted — there is
# no sd_path_clone, so that is a build-it-again, which is exactly what a consumer holding a glyph
# outline should do once rather than per frame.
# ⚠ It transforms the CONTROL POINTS, which is correct for affine: an affine image of a Bézier is
# the Bézier of the images of its control points, so curves come out exact, not re-approximated.
# ⚠ A path with no points is SADISH_OK and a no-op — a verb-less path has nothing to move.
fn sd_path_transform(path, m): i64 {
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (m == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var a = load64(m + SD_MAT_A_OFFSET);
    var b = load64(m + SD_MAT_B_OFFSET);
    var c = load64(m + SD_MAT_C_OFFSET);
    var d = load64(m + SD_MAT_D_OFFSET);
    var e = load64(m + SD_MAT_E_OFFSET);
    var f = load64(m + SD_MAT_F_OFFSET);
    var pts = load64(path + SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET);
    var n = load64(path + SD_PATH_NPOINTS_OFFSET);
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        var xo = i * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET;
        var yo = i * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET;
        var x = load64(pts + xo);
        var y = load64(pts + yo);
        # The same arithmetic as sd_matrix_apply, to the raw unit — see its header.
        store64(pts + xo, sd_fixed_mul(a, x) + sd_fixed_mul(c, y) + e);
        store64(pts + yo, sd_fixed_mul(b, x) + sd_fixed_mul(d, y) + f);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — the axis-aligned bounding box of `path`'s POINTS, written to the caller's 32 B buffer
# `out` as four 16.16 words: min x at +0, min y at +8, max x at +16, max y at +24 (0.10.0).
# Returns SADISH_OK, SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null path or null `out`, or SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH for
# a path with no points — in which case `out` IS NOT WRITTEN, because there is no defensible box
# for an empty path and a zeroed one would read as a real box at the origin.
#
# ⚠ THE CONTROL POINT HULL, NOT THE INK. A Bézier lies inside its control polygon but need not
# touch it, so a path with curves reports a box that CONTAINS the drawn shape and may be larger
# than it. That is the cheap, allocation-free answer and it is the right one for culling, damage
# tracking and layout — all of which need a conservative superset. A consumer that needs the tight
# box of the drawn curve flattens first and bounds the polyline.
# ⛔ IT IS RECOMPUTED ON EVERY CALL, not cached. SdPath's 48 B record has no spare word — 0.7.2
# spent the one annotated "bounds-cached" on the point capacity — so caching would be an ABI break
# of its own. O(n) over a flat array is cheap; a caller in a hot loop holds the result itself.
fn sd_path_bounds(path, out): i64 {
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (out == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var n = load64(path + SD_PATH_NPOINTS_OFFSET);
    if (n == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    var pts = load64(path + SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET);
    var minx = load64(pts + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
    var miny = load64(pts + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
    var maxx = minx;
    var maxy = miny;
    var i = 1;
    while (i < n) {
        var x = load64(pts + i * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
        var y = load64(pts + i * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
        if (x < minx) { minx = x; }
        if (x > maxx) { maxx = x; }
        if (y < miny) { miny = y; }
        if (y > maxy) { maxy = y; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    store64(out + 0, minx);
    store64(out + 8, miny);
    store64(out + 16, maxx);
    store64(out + 24, maxy);
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# Bytes a caller must give sd_path_bounds: four 16.16 words.
var SD_BOUNDS_SIZE = 32;

# Double ONE of the two arrays — `which` is SD_PATH_GROW_VERBS or SD_PATH_GROW_POINTS — copying its
# live entries. Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_OOM if the allocation fails.
# ⭐ ONE ARRAY, ONE ALLOCATION (0.7.2). Through 0.7.1 this doubled BOTH arrays whichever one
# overflowed, so a path whose points outran its verbs bought verb slots it had no verbs for. The
# overflowing array now doubles alone and the other one does not move — which is what makes
# sd_path_new_cap's two capacities survive the first growth.
# ⛔ THE TWO ARRAYS HAVE DIFFERENT ELEMENT SIZES NOW (0.9.0), so the request AND the copy length are
# `esz` below: 8 B a verb, SD_PATH_PT_SIZE = 16 B a point. Through 0.8.0 both were 8 and one
# constant did for both; a 0.9.0 growth that kept the 8 for points allocates HALF the block it
# needs and copies half the bytes — the shape programs/inline_points_test.cyr group D3 exists to
# catch (MEASURED as that mutation: 800 bad coordinates).
# ⛔ THE COPY LENGTH COMES FROM THE SAME ARRAY'S OWN LIVE COUNT (`n_off`), AND SINCE 0.9.0 BOTH
# HALVES ARE QUIET. n_verbs and n_points differ on every path whose verbs outnumber its points — a
# default path holding 256 closes and no points at all is the extreme — and a copy taken from the
# other count moves the wrong number of bytes into a FRESH block.
# ⚠ WHAT CHANGED HERE, AND IT MAKES THE SUITES MATTER MORE, NOT LESS: through 0.8.0 a lost POINT
# slot read back as a null SdPoint pointer that the next walk dereferenced — a SIGSEGV, loud and
# immediate — while a lost VERB tag read back as 0 = SD_VERB_MOVETO and was the silent half.
# INLINE, a lost point slot reads back as the COORDINATE (0, 0): a perfectly valid point at the
# origin, and a wrong picture instead of a crash. MEASURED with `n_off` taken from the other count
# (0.8.0): 26 of 28 suites passed and grow_edges_test SIGSEGVed. So neither array can be left to
# fault for us: programs/path_cap_test.cyr groups E3/E4/G read every verb back after a growth, and
# programs/inline_points_test.cyr group D reads every point's COORDINATES back across six
# doublings, because a coordinate compare is now the only witness either way.
# ⛔ A capacity past SD_PATH_CAP_MAX / 2 cannot double — `newcap * esz` would be past what the
# allocator serves (see SD_PATH_CAP_MAX) — so the doubling is REFUSED rather than wrapped into a
# small block. Unreachable from sadish's own constructors, which bound both capacities at
# SD_PATH_CAP_MAX; this holds for a record a consumer wrote a capacity into itself.
fn sd_path_grow(path, which): i64 {
    var cap_off = SD_PATH_CAP_OFFSET;
    var arr_off = SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET;
    var n_off = SD_PATH_NVERBS_OFFSET;
    var esz = 8;
    if (which == SD_PATH_GROW_POINTS) {
        cap_off = SD_PATH_PCAP_OFFSET;
        arr_off = SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET;
        n_off = SD_PATH_NPOINTS_OFFSET;
        esz = SD_PATH_PT_SIZE;
    }
    var cap = load64(path + cap_off);
    if (cap > SD_PATH_CAP_MAX / 2) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    var newcap = cap * 2;
    var blk = sd_alloc(newcap * esz);
    if (blk == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    memcpy(blk, load64(path + arr_off), load64(path + n_off) * esz);
    store64(path + arr_off, blk);
    store64(path + cap_off, newcap);
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# Append one verb tag, growing the VERB array if it is full. Returns SADISH_OK
# (or SADISH_ERR_OOM only if that grow allocation fails).
fn sd_path_push_verb(path, verb): i64 {
    var n = load64(path + SD_PATH_NVERBS_OFFSET);
    if (n >= load64(path + SD_PATH_CAP_OFFSET)) {
        var g = sd_path_grow(path, SD_PATH_GROW_VERBS);
        if (g != SADISH_OK) { return g; }
    }
    store64(load64(path + SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET) + n * 8, verb);
    store64(path + SD_PATH_NVERBS_OFFSET, n + 1);
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# Append the point (x, y) INLINE, growing the POINT array if it is full.
# ⚠ THE SIGNATURE CHANGED IN 0.9.0 — it took an SdPoint POINTER (`sd_path_push_point(path, pt)`)
# and takes two coordinates now. It is not @public and no repo outside this one calls it (swept
# 2026-09-16 across rekha, dhancha, agnos, setu, crab and puka), but it IS in dist/sadish.cyr, so
# the change is named here rather than left to be discovered.
fn sd_path_push_point(path, x, y): i64 {
    var n = load64(path + SD_PATH_NPOINTS_OFFSET);
    if (n >= load64(path + SD_PATH_PCAP_OFFSET)) {
        var g = sd_path_grow(path, SD_PATH_GROW_POINTS);
        if (g != SADISH_OK) { return g; }
    }
    var slot = load64(path + SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET) + n * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE;
    store64(slot + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET, x);
    store64(slot + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET, y);
    store64(path + SD_PATH_NPOINTS_OFFSET, n + 1);
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# ⛔ THE ORDER THE BUILDERS BELOW PUSH IN IS LOAD-BEARING (0.7.1), AND 0.9.0 MADE IT SIMPLER RATHER
# THAN LOOSER. Through 0.7.0 each of them pushed its VERB first and then built its points, so a
# refusal anywhere after the verb left the path HALF-WRITTEN — a quadto verb with one point behind
# it, which every later walk (flatten, fill, stroke) reads as the NEXT verb's point. 0.7.1 made each
# builder (1) reserve capacity for the whole verb, (2) allocate every SdPoint it needed, and only
# then (3) push. ⭐ STEP (2) IS GONE (0.9.0): the coordinates go straight into the array, so the
# ONLY allocation a builder can make is the growth inside step (1) — after a successful reserve,
# every push below is a store into a block that is already there and CANNOT fail.
# ⇒ A failure returns SADISH_ERR_OOM with the path's VERBS, POINTS AND CONTENTS exactly as they
# were, and the path is still usable: the next call may succeed. The window where a verb could be
# pushed and its point refused does not exist any more; it is closed by construction, not by order.
# ⚠ WHAT "UNCHANGED" STILL DOES NOT COVER: a refused GROWTH can leave the OTHER array already
# grown — a bigger capacity and a new block, the old one abandoned to an allocator with no free()
# (_sd_path_reserve grows verbs first). MEASURED: a path at SD_PATH_CAP_MIN holding 8 verbs and 8
# points, under a hook granting the verb growth and refusing the point growth — sd_path_lineto
# returns SADISH_ERR_OOM, vcap 8 -> 16, pcap still 8, and verb count, point count and every stored
# coordinate are untouched; the next lineto returns SADISH_OK.
# ⇒ The cost of a refusal is bounded by ONE doubling PER ARRAY, and nothing a caller can read
# through the public accessors moved except the capacities.
#
# Make room for `nv` more verbs AND `np` more points, doubling EACH array to its OWN need (0.7.2;
# through 0.7.1 one capacity had to cover both, so either need doubled both arrays). SADISH_OK, or
# SADISH_ERR_OOM with the path unchanged. ⚠ The doubling count per array is the same as pushing one
# at a time reaches — the capacity a verb's last push would have forced is the capacity its first
# push reserves.
# ⚠ VERBS ARE GROWN FIRST, so a refusal of the POINT growth can leave the verb array already
# doubled (one abandoned block, the capacity raised) — the same bounded cost the builders' header
# describes, and nothing readable through the public accessors moves.
fn _sd_path_reserve(path, nv, np): i64 {
    var needv = load64(path + SD_PATH_NVERBS_OFFSET) + nv;
    var needp = load64(path + SD_PATH_NPOINTS_OFFSET) + np;
    # A capacity of 0 cannot double its way anywhere: refuse rather than spin. Both are checked —
    # a record hand-built to the 0.7.1 layout carries 0 at +40 (see SD_PATH_PCAP_OFFSET).
    if (load64(path + SD_PATH_CAP_OFFSET) <= 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (load64(path + SD_PATH_PCAP_OFFSET) <= 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    while (load64(path + SD_PATH_CAP_OFFSET) < needv) {
        var gv = sd_path_grow(path, SD_PATH_GROW_VERBS);
        if (gv != SADISH_OK) { return gv; }
    }
    while (load64(path + SD_PATH_PCAP_OFFSET) < needp) {
        var gp = sd_path_grow(path, SD_PATH_GROW_POINTS);
        if (gp != SADISH_OK) { return gp; }
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — start a new subpath at (x, y) (16.16). Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_OOM with the
# path's verbs and points unchanged — see the ⚠ above for the one thing that can move, the
# capacity. ⚠ Since 0.9.0 a GROWTH IS THE ONLY WAY THIS RETURNS OOM: a path with room makes no
# allocation at all, where through 0.8.0 it always made one (the SdPoint) and could be refused.
# ⛔ A REFUSED MOVETO MUST ABANDON THE SUBPATH, NOT FALL THROUGH TO THE LINETO. The path is left
# exactly as it was — which means a caller that logs the OOM and carries on building appends its
# linetos to NO CURRENT POINT, and a path whose first verb is a LINETO faults the rasterizer
# (raster.cyr's fill starts at cur = 0 and sd_edge_add reads through it: MEASURED SIGSEGV, on
# 0.7.0 and here alike). Through 0.7.0 the refusal killed the process at the moveto instead, so
# this shape was not reachable; the clean return code is what makes it worth saying.
fn sd_path_moveto(path, x, y): i64 {
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var r = _sd_path_reserve(path, 1, 1);
    if (r != SADISH_OK) { return r; }
    sd_path_push_verb(path, SD_VERB_MOVETO);
    return sd_path_push_point(path, x, y);
}

# @public — straight line to (x, y) from the current point.
# SADISH_OK / SADISH_ERR_OOM (verbs and points unchanged).
fn sd_path_lineto(path, x, y): i64 {
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var r = _sd_path_reserve(path, 1, 1);
    if (r != SADISH_OK) { return r; }
    sd_path_push_verb(path, SD_VERB_LINETO);
    return sd_path_push_point(path, x, y);
}

# @public — quadratic Bézier: control (cx, cy), end (x, y).
# SADISH_OK / SADISH_ERR_OOM (verbs and points unchanged).
fn sd_path_quadto(path, cx, cy, x, y): i64 {
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var r = _sd_path_reserve(path, 1, 2);
    if (r != SADISH_OK) { return r; }
    sd_path_push_verb(path, SD_VERB_QUADTO);
    sd_path_push_point(path, cx, cy);
    return sd_path_push_point(path, x, y);
}

# @public — cubic Bézier: controls (c1x,c1y),(c2x,c2y), end (x, y).
# SADISH_OK / SADISH_ERR_OOM (verbs and points unchanged).
fn sd_path_cubicto(path, c1x, c1y, c2x, c2y, x, y): i64 {
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var r = _sd_path_reserve(path, 1, 3);
    if (r != SADISH_OK) { return r; }
    sd_path_push_verb(path, SD_VERB_CUBICTO);
    sd_path_push_point(path, c1x, c1y);
    sd_path_push_point(path, c2x, c2y);
    return sd_path_push_point(path, x, y);
}

# @public — close the current subpath (back to its moveto). No point.
fn sd_path_close(path): i64 {
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    return sd_path_push_verb(path, SD_VERB_CLOSE);
}

# ================================================================
# sd_path_flatten — expand curve verbs into a straight-line polyline
# within `tol` (a 16.16 flatness tolerance) and return an SdPolyline: a
# small header + an array of INLINE COORDINATE PAIRS.
#
# SdPolyline (24 bytes since 0.8.0; 16 through 0.7.2):
#   +0:  points   (ptr — array of (x, y) pairs, 16 B a slot; SdPoint PTRS through 0.9.1)
#   +8:  count    (i64 — number of points)
#   +16: verdict  (i64 — SD_POLYLINE_TRUNCATED | SD_POLYLINE_DEGRADED, 0 = whole)
#
# ⛔⛔ READ A POLYLINE'S POINTS THROUGH sd_polyline_point_x / sd_polyline_point_y (0.10.0), for the
# same reason sd_path_point_x / _y exist since 0.9.0 and with the same force: the slot below is
# sadish's, and through 0.9.1 there was NO accessor, so every reader in this tree and every consumer
# open-coded `sd_point_x(load64(sd_polyline_points(pl) + i * 8))`. That spelling now reads an x
# COORDINATE as an address — an immediate fault for any path off the origin, and a wrong answer for
# one on it. ⚠ Half of why this break is worth taking is that there is an accessor to move to.
#   var i = 0;
#   while (i < sd_polyline_count(pl)) {
#       ... sd_polyline_point_x(pl, i), sd_polyline_point_y(pl, i) ...   # 16.16, no allocation
#       i = i + 1;
#   }
# ⭐ WHAT THE BREAK BUYS: a flattened point costs NO ALLOCATION. Through 0.9.1 an emitted point was
# a 16 B sd_alloc on the consumer's seam — and 0.9.0 made that worse, not better, because a path's
# points went inline and every curve ENDPOINT then had to be materialised too. MEASURED across the
# tree: a styled stroke of a cubic 128 B -> 0 (programs/stroke_style_test.cyr #8), a dashed one
# 224 B -> 0 (dash_test #128), a fill of the hostile 4,096-quad path 1,110,016 B / 69,376 calls -> 0
# / 0 (flatten_bound_test #102-103), and a 20,001-point sd_path_flatten 20,004 calls -> 5
# (grow_edges_test C7). ⇒ Nothing in a flatten is per-point or per-curve any more; what is left is
# per-DOUBLING of the output array.
# ⚠ AND WHAT IT COSTS, SAID PLAINLY: a slot is 16 B where it was 8, so on an arena with no free()
# the CUMULATIVE bytes of a large flatten go UP even as the live bytes and the call count go down.
# SD_FLATTEN_PCAP's header has the arithmetic; grow_edges_test C7 has it measured both ways.
# ================================================================
# ⭐⭐ THE RESULT CARRIES ITS OWN VERDICT (0.8.0) — item 1 of
# docs/development/issues/archived/2026-09-15-flatten-keeps-subdividing-and-allocating-after-the-output-cap-is-full.md's
# "Still open" section, whose Suggested fix asked for "a flag on the polyline (or an error the
# fill/stroke can see) so a truncated contour is refused or clearly degraded, rather than filled
# open". 0.7.1 and 0.7.2 shipped the parenthetical — sd_canvas_fill_path and the three stroke
# entries return SADISH_ERR_OOM for a starved draw — and left the first half undone: a consumer
# holding an SdPolyline could not tell a complete flattening from a PREFIX of one. The
# process-wide sd_flatten_truncated() / sd_flatten_degraded() were the only witnesses, and both
# are deliberately sticky (one until the next sd_path_flatten, one until the next operation
# opens at depth 0), so neither answers "is THIS polyline whole?" for a result taken a while ago.
# ⇒ Two bits, captured when sd_path_flatten's verb walk finishes, in the record itself. They never
# move again: a later flatten sets the globals and cannot touch a polyline already returned.
# ⚠ THE RECORD GREW BY ONE WORD, 16 B -> 24 B, and the ONLY reason that is affordable is that
# nothing embeds it. Swept 2026-09-16 across src/, programs/, rekha, dhancha, agnos, setu and the
# two repos that vendor dist/sadish.cyr (crab, puka): sd_path_flatten is the one constructor,
# src/path.cyr is the one writer, no SdPolyline is ever a field of another record or an array
# element, and every reader outside this file goes through sd_polyline_count / sd_polyline_points
# (crab vendors its own OLDER copy of all of this and is unaffected until it re-vendors — the
# argument does not rest on which tag: any pre-0.10.0 bundle is self-contained, carrying the
# matching reader for the layout it also carries. ⚠ The exact tag is UNKNOWN from inside this repo
# and the two records here disagree — README.md says 0.5.4 as of crab 0.8.10, an archived filing
# said 0.5.5, this line once said 0.6-vintage. Do not restate a number none of us can check).
# ⚠ THAT SWEEP IS WHAT 0.10.0 RESTED ON TOO — the HEADER is unchanged at 24 B and
# the three offsets below are where they were; it is the ARRAY the header points at whose slot
# changed, which is exactly the shape the sweep found no embedder for.
# ⛔ It is ONE WORD OF BITS, not two words of booleans, so a third verdict costs no more bytes.
# Read it with the accessors; SD_POLYLINE_VERDICT_OFFSET is for a consumer that walks raw records.
var SD_POLYLINE_POINTS_OFFSET  = 0;
var SD_POLYLINE_COUNT_OFFSET   = 8;
var SD_POLYLINE_VERDICT_OFFSET = 16;
var SD_POLYLINE_SIZE           = 24;

# One point slot in a polyline's array: x at +0, y at +8 (0.10.0; an 8 B SdPoint POINTER through
# 0.9.1). ⚠ It is the same 16 as SD_POINT_SIZE and SD_PATH_PT_SIZE and means a third thing — the
# stride of THIS array. They are equal by arithmetic, not by contract; a slot that ever carries a
# third word moves only this one.
var SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE = 16;

# Points this flattening WANTED to emit were lost — a growth or an SdPoint was refused — so the
# array is a PREFIX of the true flattening. The contour it describes is OPEN where it stops.
var SD_POLYLINE_TRUNCATED = 1;
# At least one curve was cut to its chord because the operation's point budget ran out. Every
# point asked for is present; some of them are the wrong points. See SD_FLATTEN_BUDGET_DEFAULT.
var SD_POLYLINE_DEGRADED  = 2;

# @public
fn sd_polyline_count(pl): i64  { return load64(pl + SD_POLYLINE_COUNT_OFFSET); }
# @public — the raw points block. ⛔ SINCE 0.10.0 ITS SLOTS ARE COORDINATE PAIRS, NOT POINTERS:
# a consumer that did `sd_point_x(load64(sd_polyline_points(pl) + i * 8))` reads a COORDINATE as an
# address and faults. Read through sd_polyline_point_x / _y instead; this stays published for a
# caller that walks the block itself, which must now stride SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE.
fn sd_polyline_points(pl): i64 { return load64(pl + SD_POLYLINE_POINTS_OFFSET); }

# @public — point `i`'s x, 16.16. ⚠ No bounds check — bound the loop on sd_polyline_count.
# ⭐ These are the reason the 0.10.0 break is survivable, exactly as sd_path_point_x / _y were for
# 0.9.0's: through 0.9.1 there was NO accessor, so every reader in the tree and every consumer
# open-coded the pointer load. Now none of them carry an offset.
fn sd_polyline_point_x(pl, i): i64 {
    return load64(load64(pl + SD_POLYLINE_POINTS_OFFSET) + i * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
}
# @public — point `i`'s y, 16.16.
fn sd_polyline_point_y(pl, i): i64 {
    return load64(load64(pl + SD_POLYLINE_POINTS_OFFSET) + i * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
}

# @public — this polyline's own verdict word (0.8.0): 0, or SD_POLYLINE_TRUNCATED and/or
# SD_POLYLINE_DEGRADED ORed together. Fixed when sd_path_flatten built the record; unlike
# sd_flatten_truncated() / sd_flatten_degraded() it answers for THIS result and nothing else, so
# a consumer may hold several polylines and ask each of them.
# ⚠ Like sd_polyline_count and sd_polyline_points, it does NOT test `pl` — a 0 from
# sd_path_flatten is a documented return the caller checks before reading anything (src/alloc.cyr).
fn sd_polyline_verdict(pl): i64 { return load64(pl + SD_POLYLINE_VERDICT_OFFSET); }

# @public — 1 if points were LOST while this polyline was flattened, so its point array stops
# short of the true flattening and the contour is open at that end. The partial result is handed
# back rather than refused — see sd_path_flatten's header for why — so THIS is what a caller
# tests before filling or stroking the result.
fn sd_polyline_truncated(pl): i64 {
    if ((load64(pl + SD_POLYLINE_VERDICT_OFFSET) & SD_POLYLINE_TRUNCATED) != 0) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# @public — 1 if at least one curve of this polyline was cut to its chord by the flatten budget.
# The point count is whole; the shape is not the shape that was asked for. ⚠ It is the CALL's
# verdict, not the operation's: two flattens inside one sd_flatten_op_begin scope share a budget,
# and only the one whose curve was actually cut reports it (sd_flatten_degraded() reports both).
fn sd_polyline_degraded(pl): i64 {
    if ((load64(pl + SD_POLYLINE_VERDICT_OFFSET) & SD_POLYLINE_DEGRADED) != 0) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# ---- flatten internals (adaptive de Casteljau) ------------------
# Initial capacity + recursion depth cap. A single curve subdivides to at most 2^depth segments
# (256: every leaf of the recursion emits one point).
# ⚠ SD_FLATTEN_CAP NO LONGER SIZES THE POINT STORES — 0.10.0 split that off into SD_FLATTEN_PCAP
# below, and this paragraph said otherwise until 0.11.0. What it sizes now is the stores whose slot
# did NOT change when a point went 8 B to 16: raster.cyr's fill EDGE LIST and CROSSINGS, and
# stroke.cyr's RUN — all of which GROW past it since 0.7.0 (through 0.6.0 each dropped what did not
# fit). sd_path_flatten's result and the fill's and stroker's per-curve flatten buffers take
# SD_FLATTEN_PCAP instead. ⛔ Read the two together; they are one decision in two constants.
var SD_FLATTEN_CAP       = 8192;
var SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH = 8;

# ⭐ THE POINT STORES' INITIAL CAPACITY, HALVED AS THE SLOT DOUBLED (0.10.0) — the same move
# SD_PATH_PCAP made for SD_PATH_CAP in 0.9.0, and for the same reason. A polyline slot went 8 B
# (an SdPoint ptr) to 16 B (the coordinates), so at SD_FLATTEN_CAP the first allocation of every
# point store would have doubled, 65,536 B -> 131,072 B, and a flatten of four points would pay it.
# At 4096 * 16 the first allocation is **65,536 B — byte-identical to every release since 0.7.0**.
# ⚠ SD_FLATTEN_CAP still sizes the stores whose slot did NOT change: raster.cyr's fill edge list
# and crossings, and stroke.cyr's run. Only the three POINT stores take this one — sd_path_flatten's
# output and the fill's and stroker's per-curve buffers.
# ⛔ The per-curve buffers are FIXED (no growth) and this halving is still ample: one curve emits at
# most 2^SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH = 256 points, so 4096 is 16x headroom. The flatten OUTPUT grows, so a
# path past 4096 points pays one extra doubling and nothing else.
var SD_FLATTEN_PCAP      = 4096;

# ================================================================
# THE PER-OPERATION WORK BUDGET, in POINTS EMITTED (0.7.1).
#
# ⛔⛔ WHY A BUDGET EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS A TRADE. Through 0.7.0 the work of one flatten was bounded
# by the path's CURVE COUNT and nothing else: each maximally non-flat quad subdivides to the depth
# cap whatever the output already holds. rekha filed it
# (docs/development/issues/archived/2026-09-15-flatten-keeps-subdividing-and-allocating-after-the-output-cap-is-full.md)
# because a font file can carry exactly that shape and rekha fills untrusted outlines every frame.
# MEASURED on this tree, 4,096 maximally non-flat quads (moveto then N x quadto(1e9, 1e9, 0, 0))
# through one sd_path_flatten: 1,048,577 points for 83,623,976 B in 3,133,451 allocations.
# ⇒ Past the budget, sadish chooses BOUNDED WORK AND A FLAGGED, DEGRADED CURVE over a faithful
# tessellation at any price: each remaining curve emits its ENDPOINT — its chord — and subdivides
# no further, and sd_flatten_degraded() says so for the whole operation.
#
# THE DEFAULT, FROM MEASUREMENT (all at the fill's own tolerance, SD_ONE >> 2):
#   a 256 px circle of 4 cubics                           65 points
#   a 8,192 px circle of 4 cubics (the depth cap)        257 points
#   95 ASCII-shaped glyph paths, all in one path       3,250 points
#   programs/grow_edges_test.cyr's cubic_ring(3000)    8,601 points  <- the largest in the suites
#   the hostile 4,096-quad path above                1,048,577 points
# 65,536 is 7.6x the largest legitimate path this stack draws, 20x a full ASCII label, and holds
# one flatten under ~2.1 MiB (65,536 SdPoints + the doubling ladder of the output array) — the same
# shape of answer as raster.cyr's 8 MiB SD_GROW_LIMIT_DEFAULT, in the unit this call allocates in.
# ⚠ A consumer that draws large curve-dense artwork of its own (not a glyph, not a UI) raises it,
# or sets 0 for 0.7.0's unbounded behaviour; a consumer that fills untrusted outlines keeps it.
#
# ⚠ WHAT COUNTS, AND WHAT DEGRADES. Every point EMITTED in the operation counts, straight verbs
# included — the budget bounds the result, not just the curve arithmetic. Only SUBDIVISION is
# refused: a moveto/lineto always emits, so a 100,000-point polyline still flattens whole (and a
# curve after it flattens to its chord).
# ================================================================
var SD_FLATTEN_BUDGET_DEFAULT = 65536;
var _sd_flat_budget = 65536;

# The one emit ctx sd_flat_emit may GROW — sd_path_flatten's, through sd_alloc, while it runs (0
# otherwise). Every other ctx is a fixed per-curve scratch buffer (raster.cyr) that must never be
# reallocated, least of all on the consumer's hook.
var _sd_flat_ctx = 0;
# 1 if the last sd_path_flatten lost a point — a growth or an SdPoint was refused — so its result
# is a PREFIX of the true flattening, not the whole of it.
var _sd_flat_trunc = 0;
# ⛔⛔ THE EMIT CTX THAT CAN TAKE NOTHING MORE (0 = none) — A POINTER, NOT A FLAG (0.7.1 repair).
# An emit that was dropped or whose SdPoint was refused records ITS OWN ctx here, and the recursion
# stops only for THAT ctx: fullness is a property of the OUTPUT ARRAY, not of the operation.
# ⚠ A plain flag leaked, and the leak was silent both ways. sd_flatten_op_begin clears this only at
# nesting depth 0, and sd_path_flatten opens a NESTED operation whenever a consumer has one open —
# which is the usage sd_flatten_op_begin's own header prescribes. So a starved flatten's flag
# survived into the NEXT, healthy flatten inside the same operation and truncated it to its
# straight-verb anchors, while sd_path_flatten's `_sd_flat_trunc = 0` wiped the only witness:
# MEASURED, two flattens of the same 513-point path inside one operation, the first starved —
# the second emitted 1 point with truncated() and degraded() both reading 0.
# ⇒ Two different ctxs never share a verdict now, and sd_path_flatten clears this outright when it
# establishes its own ctx (an arena a consumer reset between frames can hand out the same address
# twice, and a pointer alone cannot tell those two ctxs apart).
# ⛔ A POINTER IS STILL NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN, because raster.cyr and stroke.cyr REWIND one buffer
# per curve: the same ctx address is a different, empty output a moment later. _sd_flat_enter
# re-derives the verdict from the ctx's own (count, cap) at every entry — see it for the measured
# fills this blanked. Set it here, clear it there; nothing else writes it but sd_flatten_op_begin
# at depth 0 and sd_path_flatten's ctx setup.
var _sd_flat_full = 0;
var _sd_flat_used = 0;         # points emitted so far in the open operation
var _sd_flat_degraded = 0;     # 1 if a curve of the open operation was cut to its chord (budget)
var _sd_flat_nest = 0;         # open-operation nesting depth; 0 = none open
# ⛔ THE PER-CALL HALF OF _sd_flat_degraded (0.8.0), and the reason the polyline's DEGRADED bit
# cannot just read _sd_flat_degraded at the end of the walk. Degradation belongs to the OPERATION:
# inside a consumer's sd_flatten_op_begin scope, a budget spent by an EARLIER flatten leaves
# _sd_flat_degraded standing at 1, and a later, entirely whole flatten would then stamp its own
# result DEGRADED — a per-result verdict that is really a poll of a shared global, which is the
# defect the polyline verdict exists to fix. So the two subdivision recursions set BOTH: this one
# is cleared by sd_path_flatten at the top of every call and read once at the bottom, and nothing
# else in the process reads or writes it. ⚠ It is not public and it is not an operation verdict —
# sd_flatten_degraded() is unchanged and still answers for the operation.
# ⛔ THE CLEAR MUST RUN BEFORE THE WALK, NOT AFTER THE STORE, and that is not a stylistic choice.
# The two recursions that SET this are reached by the PUBLIC per-curve entries sd_flatten_quad /
# sd_flatten_cubic as well — the road sd_fill_impl and the stroker take — so a cut can be made by
# something that is not an sd_path_flatten at all, and a clear at the BOTTOM of the call would let
# it stand until the next flatten stamped it into a whole result. MEASURED: with the clear moved to
# just after the verdict store, all 29 suites stayed green while a budget-cut FILL followed by an
# untouched sd_path_flatten returned a record reading SD_POLYLINE_DEGRADED.
# ⇒ programs/flatten_bound_test.cyr group Q9 (checks 439-455) is what holds the position — Q1 and Q6
# do not, because in both the call that does the cutting is itself an sd_path_flatten.
var _sd_flat_cut = 0;          # 1 if a curve of THIS sd_path_flatten call was cut to its chord

# @public — set the per-operation flatten budget in POINTS (0 = unbounded, negative = refused and
# nothing changes); returns the PREVIOUS value, so a caller can scope it.
# SD_FLATTEN_BUDGET_DEFAULT restores the default.
fn sd_flatten_budget_set(n): i64 {
    var prev = _sd_flat_budget;
    if (n < 0) { return prev; }
    _sd_flat_budget = n;
    return prev;
}

# @public — the current per-operation flatten budget in points (0 = unbounded).
fn sd_flatten_budget_get(): i64 {
    return _sd_flat_budget;
}

# @public — 1 if the operation that just finished cut at least one curve to its chord because the
# budget ran out. Read it after sd_path_flatten, or after sd_flatten_op_end when a consumer scopes
# a whole draw.
# ⚠ IT BELONGS TO THE LAST OPERATION, NOT THE LAST CALL, and it is cleared when the next one opens
# at nesting depth 0. Every sd_path_flatten is an operation (an empty path included, 0.7.1); since
# 0.7.2 so is every FILL, as a whole (sd_fill_impl opens one, lazily, at the first curve it really
# flattens); and so is every single curve verb the STROKER flattens — stroke.cyr is the one caller
# left that goes one verb at a time, so a stroke's verdict is its LAST CURVE's unless the consumer
# scopes it. ⛔ A fill of a path with NO CURVE VERBS opens none at all, so this still reads whatever
# the last flatten left: MEASURED, a degraded flatten followed by a fill of a 3-lineto path still
# reports 1 (programs/flatten_bound_test.cyr group O). Read it inside a scope you opened
# (sd_flatten_op_begin / _end) if you want it to answer for a particular draw.
# ⭐ For one sd_path_flatten in particular, read sd_polyline_degraded(pl) instead (0.8.0): that bit
# is that CALL's, so two flattens sharing one budgeted scope report separately — the one whose
# curve was cut says 1 and the whole one says 0, where this global says 1 for both.
fn sd_flatten_degraded(): i64 {
    return _sd_flat_degraded;
}

# @public — 1 if points that a flatten wanted to emit were LOST (a growth was refused, or an
# SdPoint was): the result is a PREFIX of the true flattening, not the whole of it.
# ⚠ IT IS SET BY EVERY FLATTEN, INCLUDING THE PER-CURVE ONES INSIDE A FILL OR A STROKE. It is STICKY:
# sd_path_flatten clears it, and the fill and the stroke walks scope it per call (they save the
# caller's standing verdict and put it back), so after a draw it still reads "something was lost
# since the last sd_path_flatten".
# ⛔ IT IS NO LONGER THE ONLY WITNESS, and the header said it was through 0.7.1. Since 0.7.1
# sd_canvas_fill_path RETURNS SADISH_ERR_OOM for a fill whose own flatten lost points, and since
# 0.7.2 so do sd_canvas_stroke_path / _ex / _dash: MEASURED, a 20 px-radius circle of 4 cubics on
# 64x64 under a hook granting 5 allocations — SADISH_ERR_OOM, 81,029 of 312,280 coverage units,
# truncated() = 1 (0.7.0 did not get that far: SIGSEGV in sd_pt_mid).
# ⇒ Poll this when you want to know whether an EARLIER draw lost something; read the return code to
# know whether THIS one did. The canvas keeps whatever the partial draw painted — nothing is rolled
# back.
# ⭐ AND FOR A POLYLINE, ASK THE POLYLINE (0.8.0): sd_polyline_truncated(pl) is that result's own
# frozen verdict, so it stays right however many flattens, fills or strokes happen afterwards.
# This global is what is left for the case that has no result to ask — sd_path_flatten returning 0.
fn sd_flatten_truncated(): i64 {
    return _sd_flat_trunc;
}

# @public — open a flatten OPERATION: the scope the budget and the degraded flag are measured over.
# Returns the new nesting depth.
#
# ⭐ WHY A CONSUMER WOULD CALL THIS — AND WHAT 0.7.2 TOOK OFF ITS PLATE. sd_path_flatten opens one
# around its whole verb walk, and since 0.7.2 sd_canvas_fill_path does too (sd_fill_impl, lazily at
# its first flattened curve), so neither a lone flatten NOR a lone fill needs anything from you:
# MEASURED (programs/flatten_bound_test.cyr groups H and O), the hostile 4,096-quad path filled on
# 64x64 — unwrapped 1,044,480 B in 65,280 allocations on the seam, wrapped the SAME 1,044,480 B in
# 65,280, with sd_flatten_degraded() reading 1 either way. (0.7.1 unwrapped: 16,711,680 B in
# 1,044,480 — one budget per curve verb. 0.7.0: 50,135,040 B in 3,133,440 — no budget at all.)
# ⛔ THE STROKER IS STILL PER-CURVE, and that is what this is for now. stroke.cyr flattens ONE CURVE
# VERB AT A TIME into a fixed buffer, each call its own operation, so the budget bounds one curve
# there (at most 2^SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH = 256 points, which never reaches the default) and not the
# stroke. A consumer that wants ONE stroke of an untrusted path bounded wraps it:
#     sd_flatten_op_begin();  sd_canvas_stroke_path(cv, path, w);  sd_flatten_op_end();
#     if (sd_flatten_degraded() != 0) { ...the outline was cut to chords... }
# MEASURED (group P), a 4-cubic circle stroked at a budget of 64 points: TEN unwrapped strokes each
# paint 126,930 coverage units and none degrades — every curve gets its own 64, and spends 4 — while
# inside ONE operation the first four paint 126,930 and the FIFTH paints 114,300 with degraded() = 1,
# because 16 points a stroke spend it. Wrap to bound a stroke, or to make several draws share one
# budget.
# ⚠ Balance it. An op left open keeps accumulating, and a later flatten degrades for a budget it
# did not spend; sd_flatten_op_end is the only thing that closes one.
# ⚠ THE RESET IS AT DEPTH 0 ONLY — a nested begin joins the operation already open, which is the
# whole point of the scope. That is why sd_path_flatten clears the OUTPUT's fullness verdict itself
# when it establishes its ctx (see _sd_flat_full) instead of relying on this: inside a consumer's
# scope, a flatten's begin is a nested one and resets nothing.
fn sd_flatten_op_begin(): i64 {
    if (_sd_flat_nest == 0) {
        _sd_flat_used = 0;
        _sd_flat_degraded = 0;
        _sd_flat_full = 0;
    }
    _sd_flat_nest = _sd_flat_nest + 1;
    return _sd_flat_nest;
}

# @public — close a flatten operation opened by sd_flatten_op_begin. Returns the new nesting depth;
# closing when none is open is a no-op returning 0.
fn sd_flatten_op_end(): i64 {
    if (_sd_flat_nest > 0) { _sd_flat_nest = _sd_flat_nest - 1; }
    return _sd_flat_nest;
}

# Double the point array of emit ctx `ctx` through sd_alloc, copying its points. Returns 1, or 0 if
# `ctx` is not growable or the allocation failed (_sd_flat_trunc set; later emits stop trying).
fn _sd_flat_widen(ctx): i64 {
    if (ctx != _sd_flat_ctx) { return 0; }
    if (_sd_flat_trunc != 0) { return 0; }
    var nc = load64(ctx + 16) * 2;
    var blk = sd_alloc(nc * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE);
    if (blk == 0) {
        _sd_flat_trunc = 1;
        return 0;
    }
    memcpy(blk, load64(ctx + 0), load64(ctx + 8) * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE);
    store64(ctx + 0, blk);
    store64(ctx + 16, nc);
    return 1;
}

# Emit accumulator ctx: +0 points(ptr)  +8 count  +16 cap. A full ctx grows if it is
# sd_path_flatten's; otherwise the point is dropped — and a DROP RECORDS THIS ctx IN _sd_flat_full,
# which is the whole of how the recursion learns there is no point subdividing any further (0.7.1:
# through 0.7.0 this returned 0 either way and nothing upstream looked). Returns 0.
# ⛔ TAKES COORDINATES SINCE 0.10.0 — it took one SdPoint POINTER. A caller that still passes a
# pointer stores an address where an x belongs and leaves y as whatever the slot held.
fn sd_flat_emit(ctx, x, y): i64 {
    var count = load64(ctx + 8);
    if (count >= load64(ctx + 16)) {
        if (_sd_flat_widen(ctx) == 0) {
            _sd_flat_full = ctx;
            return 0;
        }
    }
    var pts = load64(ctx + 0);
    store64(pts + count * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET, x);
    store64(pts + count * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET, y);
    store64(ctx + 8, count + 1);
    _sd_flat_used = _sd_flat_used + 1;
    return 0;
}

# Emit the point (x, y). Returns 0.
# ⭐⭐ THIS IS THE 0.10.0 CHANGE. It took a fourth argument — the endpoint's EXISTING SdPoint, or 0
# meaning "materialise one" — and its body was an sd_point_new plus a refusal path, because the
# output held POINTERS and an emitted point therefore had to be a record. The output holds the
# coordinates now, so there is nothing to materialise and nothing here can be refused: a point
# costs no allocation at all, and the only refusal left in the emit path is a GROWTH
# (_sd_flat_widen), which already records itself. That deletes one 16 B sd_alloc PER EMITTED POINT.
fn _sd_flat_put(ctx, x, y): i64 {
    return sd_flat_emit(ctx, x, y);
}

# ⛔⛔ THE OTHER HALF OF THE FULLNESS VERDICT: RE-CHECK IT WHEN A FOREIGN CTX COMES BACK (0.7.1).
# _sd_flat_put / sd_flat_emit record the ctx that could take nothing more; this is what un-records
# it, and it exists because a per-curve buffer is REWOUND between curves.
#
# raster.cyr's fill and stroke.cyr's stroker own ONE process-lifetime flatten buffer each and reset
# its (points, count, cap) fields before every single curve verb — so the buffer that was full a
# moment ago is empty again, and a verdict left standing against it silently empties every curve
# after it. MEASURED before this check, a 16x16 fill of a 5 px circle of 4 cubics (true ink 18,016
# coverage units) with a starved fill and a HEALTHY one inside one sd_flatten_op_begin/_end:
#   grant=0  starved 0      then 0        grant=2  starved 9,008   then 0
#   grant=1  starved 1,314  then 0        grant=3  starved 16,697  then 0
# The second fill ran with the allocator fully restored, returned SADISH_OK, and painted a BLANK
# canvas — for the whole life of the consumer's operation, since only a depth-0 begin cleared the
# flag. (Outside an operation the leak is invisible: the depth-0 begin is then the draw's own —
# sd_flatten_quad's for a stroke, and since 0.7.2 sd_fill_impl's for a fill — and it clears the
# flag. That is exactly why it took an operation to find.)
# ⇒ Fullness is re-derived from the ctx itself: the verdict survives only while the ctx REALLY has
# no room (count >= cap, the growth-refused case), which is what group F's cross-curve stop rests
# on. ⚠ SINCE 0.10.0 THAT IS THE ONLY CASE THERE IS — a point allocates nothing, so "the allocator
# said no but the array had room" cannot arise from an emit any more. The re-derivation is kept
# because it is what makes a REWOUND buffer a new output (raster.cyr and stroke.cyr rewind one per
# curve verb), which is the half of this repair that still does work every frame.
# A verdict set by a refused SdPoint (count < cap — the allocator said no, the array did not)
# is re-tried once per curve: one descent to the first leaf and one refused allocation, then the
# recursion stops again. Bounded, and it cannot outlive the starvation that caused it.
# ⚠ sd_path_flatten's OWN ctx is exempt — it accumulates across the whole verb walk, so its
# fullness must persist between curves. It is cleared once, where that ctx is established.
fn _sd_flat_enter(ctx): i64 {
    if (_sd_flat_full != ctx) { return 0; }
    if (ctx == _sd_flat_ctx) { return 0; }
    if (load64(ctx + 8) < load64(ctx + 16)) { _sd_flat_full = 0; }
    return 0;
}

# Midpoint of two SdPoints (signed /2 via sd_asr — coords can be negative), as a NEW SdPoint; 0 if
# that allocation is refused.
# ⚠ NOTHING IN SADISH CALLS THIS SINCE 0.7.1 — the flatten recursion computes its mids as i64
# locals (see _sd_flat_quad_r). It is kept because it is in the published bundle a consumer may
# call, and because it is still the definition of what a mid-point IS.
fn sd_pt_mid(a, b): i64 {
    var mx = sd_asr(sd_point_x(a) + sd_point_x(b), 1);
    var my = sd_asr(sd_point_y(a) + sd_point_y(b), 1);
    return sd_point_new(mx, my);
}

# Quadratic flatness on raw 16.16 coordinates — THE definition; sd_quad_flat is its SdPoint form
# and the recursion below calls this one, so the two can never drift apart.
# A quad's max deviation from its chord is 0.25*|p0 - 2*p1 + p2|, so it is flat when |secondDiff|
# <= 4*tol. The Manhattan magnitude (>= Euclidean) keeps this division/sqrt-free and only ever
# over-tessellates, never under.
fn _sd_quad_flat_xy(x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, tol): i64 {
    var dx = x0 - 2 * x1 + x2;
    var dy = y0 - 2 * y1 + y2;
    if (sd_abs(dx) + sd_abs(dy) <= 4 * tol) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# @public — is the quad (p0,p1,p2) within `tol` of its chord? 1 / 0.
fn sd_quad_flat(p0, p1, p2, tol): i64 {
    return _sd_quad_flat_xy(sd_point_x(p0), sd_point_y(p0), sd_point_x(p1), sd_point_y(p1),
      sd_point_x(p2), sd_point_y(p2), tol);
}

# ⭐⭐ THE RECURSION CARRIES COORDINATES, NOT SdPoints (0.7.1) — the whole of the mid-point fix.
#
# Through 0.7.0 each node called sd_pt_mid three times (quad) or six (cubic), and sd_pt_mid IS an
# sd_point_new: a 16 B sd_alloc on the consumer's seam. But of a node's mids only ONE is ever
# emitted — the centre point, which travels down as a child's endpoint until a leaf emits it — and
# the others are pure control points, dead the moment their child has read them. So a fully
# subdivided quad paid ~765 allocations to emit 256 points, and a cubic ~1,530.
# ⇒ A node now computes its mids as PLAIN i64 LOCALS and hands them down as numbers. Only
# _sd_flat_put allocates, and only for a point that is actually going into the output.
#   · MEASURED (programs/flatten_bound_test.cyr counts both recursions in one process): a maximally
#     non-flat quad falls from 765 allocations (12,240 B) to 255 (4,080 B); a depth-8 cubic emitting
#     232 points from 1,386 to 231. Allocation is now exactly (points emitted - 1) per curve.
#   · The last argument of each recursion is the endpoint's EXISTING SdPoint, or 0 for "a midpoint
#     nobody has materialised yet". That is what keeps the caller's own SdPoint pointers in the
#     output for a curve's final endpoint, byte-for-byte as 0.7.0 left them.
#   · p0 needs no pointer: a node NEVER emits its own start point — the previous segment did.
#   · The arithmetic is unchanged to the raw unit (sd_asr(a + b, 1), the same second-difference
#     test), so every path under budget flattens to the same points in the same order as 0.7.0.
#     programs/flatten_bound_test.cyr holds it to a reference built from the 0.7.0 code.
#
# Emits every point AFTER (x0,y0) up to and including (x2,y2). Returns 0.
fn _sd_flat_quad_r(ctx, x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, tol, depth): i64 {
    # Nothing more can reach THIS output: stop, rather than subdivide into drops (the filing's
    # "one comparison per remaining curve"). ⚠ It is `== ctx`, not `!= 0`: another ctx's fullness
    # says nothing about this one — see _sd_flat_full.
    if (_sd_flat_full == ctx) { return 0; }
    if (depth <= 0) { return _sd_flat_put(ctx, x2, y2); }
    if (_sd_quad_flat_xy(x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, tol) != 0) { return _sd_flat_put(ctx, x2, y2); }
    # Over budget: this curve becomes its chord — the endpoint, no subdivision, no temporaries.
    # ⚠ TWO FLAGS, TWO SCOPES (0.8.0): _sd_flat_degraded is the OPERATION's and is cleared by a
    # depth-0 sd_flatten_op_begin; _sd_flat_cut is the CALL's and is cleared by sd_path_flatten,
    # which stamps it into the polyline it returns. Setting only one of them is a real bug in
    # either direction — see _sd_flat_cut.
    if (_sd_flat_budget > 0) {
        if (_sd_flat_used >= _sd_flat_budget) {
            _sd_flat_degraded = 1;
            _sd_flat_cut = 1;
            return _sd_flat_put(ctx, x2, y2);
        }
    }
    var x01 = sd_asr(x0 + x1, 1);
    var y01 = sd_asr(y0 + y1, 1);
    var x12 = sd_asr(x1 + x2, 1);
    var y12 = sd_asr(y1 + y2, 1);
    var x012 = sd_asr(x01 + x12, 1);
    var y012 = sd_asr(y01 + y12, 1);
    _sd_flat_quad_r(ctx, x0, y0, x01, y01, x012, y012, tol, depth - 1);
    _sd_flat_quad_r(ctx, x012, y012, x12, y12, x2, y2, tol, depth - 1);
    return 0;
}

# Recursively flatten a quad (p0,p1,p2). Emits every point AFTER p0 up to and
# including p2 — p0 is already in the output from the previous segment.
# ⛔⛔ THE OPERATION OPENED HERE IS LOAD-BEARING FOR THE STROKER, AND FOR NOTHING ELSE NOW (0.7.2).
# stroke.cyr calls this one curve verb at a time into a fixed buffer, and nothing else resets
# _sd_flat_used for an UNWRAPPED stroke — so a fresh budget and a fresh full/degraded flag per curve
# is what this begin/end pair is. Delete it and _sd_flat_used accumulates for the life of the
# PROCESS: MEASURED (programs/flatten_bound_test.cyr group P), ten identical unwrapped strokes of a
# 4-cubic circle at a budget of 64 paint 126,930 coverage units each with it, and 126,930 four times
# then 114,300 with degraded() = 1 for ever after without it — the same input, a different picture.
# Since 0.7.2 sd_path_flatten's walk and sd_fill_impl's fill BOTH open an operation of their own
# first, so these begins nest inside theirs and reset nothing there.
# ⭐ THE COORDINATE ENTRY (0.9.0) — what a PATH walk calls, now that a path's points are numbers
# and not records. ⛔ IT LOST ITS `pt2` / `pt3` ARGUMENT AT 0.10.0, and the reason is the release:
# that argument carried "the endpoint's EXISTING SdPoint, or 0 to materialise one", which only had
# to exist while the output held POINTERS. Both stores are coordinates now, so there is no record
# to pass down, nothing to reuse, and nothing to materialise — sd_flatten_quad below simply reads
# its SdPoint arguments' two words like any other caller.
# ⚠ A CONSUMER CALLING sd_flatten_quad / sd_flatten_cubic SEES NO SIGNATURE CHANGE: the public
# SdPoint-form entries take the same arguments they always did. What changed is what lands in the
# ctx — coordinates, not the caller's own records — so a consumer that compared output POINTERS
# against the SdPoints it passed in is comparing things that no longer exist.
fn _sd_flatten_quad_xy(ctx, x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, tol, depth): i64 {
    _sd_flat_enter(ctx);
    sd_flatten_op_begin();
    _sd_flat_quad_r(ctx, x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, tol, depth);
    sd_flatten_op_end();
    return 0;
}

fn sd_flatten_quad(ctx, p0, p1, p2, tol, depth): i64 {
    return _sd_flatten_quad_xy(ctx, sd_point_x(p0), sd_point_y(p0), sd_point_x(p1), sd_point_y(p1),
      sd_point_x(p2), sd_point_y(p2), tol, depth);
}

# Cubic flatness on raw 16.16 coordinates — THE definition (sd_cubic_flat is its SdPoint form):
# both second differences (p0-2p1+p2) and (p1-2p2+p3) within tolerance, same Manhattan bound as
# the quad.
fn _sd_cubic_flat_xy(x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3, tol): i64 {
    var d1x = x0 - 2 * x1 + x2;
    var d1y = y0 - 2 * y1 + y2;
    var d2x = x1 - 2 * x2 + x3;
    var d2y = y1 - 2 * y2 + y3;
    var m1 = sd_abs(d1x) + sd_abs(d1y);
    var m2 = sd_abs(d2x) + sd_abs(d2y);
    var m = m1;
    if (m2 > m) { m = m2; }
    if (m <= 4 * tol) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# @public — is the cubic (p0,p1,p2,p3) within `tol` of its chord? 1 / 0.
fn sd_cubic_flat(p0, p1, p2, p3, tol): i64 {
    return _sd_cubic_flat_xy(sd_point_x(p0), sd_point_y(p0), sd_point_x(p1), sd_point_y(p1),
      sd_point_x(p2), sd_point_y(p2), sd_point_x(p3), sd_point_y(p3), tol);
}

# The cubic's coordinate recursion — see _sd_flat_quad_r for why it carries numbers. Of a node's
# six mids only p0123 is ever emitted; p01, p12, p23, p012 and p123 are control points its two
# children read and drop. Emits every point after (x0,y0). Returns 0.
fn _sd_flat_cubic_r(ctx, x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3, tol, depth): i64 {
    if (_sd_flat_full == ctx) { return 0; }
    if (depth <= 0) { return _sd_flat_put(ctx, x3, y3); }
    if (_sd_cubic_flat_xy(x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3, tol) != 0) {
        return _sd_flat_put(ctx, x3, y3);
    }
    # The cubic's own copy of the quad's two-flag rule — see _sd_flat_quad_r.
    if (_sd_flat_budget > 0) {
        if (_sd_flat_used >= _sd_flat_budget) {
            _sd_flat_degraded = 1;
            _sd_flat_cut = 1;
            return _sd_flat_put(ctx, x3, y3);
        }
    }
    var x01 = sd_asr(x0 + x1, 1);
    var y01 = sd_asr(y0 + y1, 1);
    var x12 = sd_asr(x1 + x2, 1);
    var y12 = sd_asr(y1 + y2, 1);
    var x23 = sd_asr(x2 + x3, 1);
    var y23 = sd_asr(y2 + y3, 1);
    var x012 = sd_asr(x01 + x12, 1);
    var y012 = sd_asr(y01 + y12, 1);
    var x123 = sd_asr(x12 + x23, 1);
    var y123 = sd_asr(y12 + y23, 1);
    var x0123 = sd_asr(x012 + x123, 1);
    var y0123 = sd_asr(y012 + y123, 1);
    _sd_flat_cubic_r(ctx, x0, y0, x01, y01, x012, y012, x0123, y0123, tol, depth - 1);
    _sd_flat_cubic_r(ctx, x0123, y0123, x123, y123, x23, y23, x3, y3, tol, depth - 1);
    return 0;
}

# Recursively flatten a cubic (p0,p1,p2,p3). Emits every point after p0.
# Opens a flatten operation, exactly as sd_flatten_quad does — and for the same reason: the stroker
# is the caller that depends on it. Group P drives BOTH, because a stroke of an all-cubic path walks
# straight past a per-curve operation that survives in the quad branch alone.
# The cubic's coordinate entry — see _sd_flatten_quad_xy.
fn _sd_flatten_cubic_xy(ctx, x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3, tol, depth): i64 {
    _sd_flat_enter(ctx);
    sd_flatten_op_begin();
    _sd_flat_cubic_r(ctx, x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3, tol, depth);
    sd_flatten_op_end();
    return 0;
}

fn sd_flatten_cubic(ctx, p0, p1, p2, p3, tol, depth): i64 {
    return _sd_flatten_cubic_xy(ctx, sd_point_x(p0), sd_point_y(p0), sd_point_x(p1), sd_point_y(p1),
      sd_point_x(p2), sd_point_y(p2), sd_point_x(p3), sd_point_y(p3), tol, depth);
}

# @public — flatten `path` into a straight-line polyline within `tol` (a
# 16.16 flatness tolerance), returning an SdPolyline. Straight verbs pass
# through as anchors; quad/cubic verbs are adaptively subdivided (de
# Casteljau) until each segment's deviation from its chord is <= tol.
# Returns 0 on an empty path (the caller reports EMPTY_PATH) AND on a refused allocation — the
# out array, the ctx or the polyline header — with sd_flatten_truncated() set to tell the two
# apart (0.7.1; through 0.7.0 a refusal here stored through 0). ⭐ The verdict is cleared BEFORE the
# empty-path return, so "0 and truncated() == 0" really does mean empty; an early return that
# skipped the clear would have left the previous call's 1 standing.
# ⚠ THE TWO ZEROS ARE STILL TOLD APART BY THE GLOBAL, NOT BY THE RESULT (0.8.0 did not change
# this, and it cannot): the polyline verdict lives IN the record, so a call that returns no record
# has nowhere to put one. `if (pl == 0) { ... sd_flatten_truncated() ... }` is unchanged, and it
# is the only case left where a caller must reach for a process-wide flag.
#
# ⭐⭐ A TRUNCATED FLATTEN IS FLAGGED, NOT REFUSED (0.8.0) — the decision the filing's second
# Suggested-fix bullet leaves to the implementation ("refused OR clearly degraded"). sadish
# returns the PREFIX with SD_POLYLINE_TRUNCATED set rather than 0, because:
#   · returning 0 would throw away a result callers do use — group F's starved flatten keeps the
#     8,192 points that fit, and they are byte-identical to the whole flattening's first 8,192;
#   · 0 is already spoken for TWICE (empty path, refused allocation), and a third meaning on the
#     same sentinel would make the very confusion this release is repairing worse, not better;
#   · "refused" belongs at the DRAW, where a partial contour actually gets filled open, and that
#     is where it already is: sd_canvas_fill_path (0.7.1) and sd_canvas_stroke_path / _ex / _dash
#     (0.7.2) return SADISH_ERR_OOM rather than paint a half outline and claim success.
# ⇒ The flag keeps both halves — the points and the truth about them — and costs the caller one
# `if (sd_polyline_truncated(pl) != 0)`. ⛔ A polyline is NOT self-validating: nothing in sadish
# refuses to rasterize a truncated one, because the same prefix is exactly what a caller
# recovering from an out-of-memory frame wants to draw. Test it if the shape has to be right.
#
# ⭐ THE RESULT'S OWN VERDICT (0.8.0), captured the moment the verb walk ends:
#   sd_polyline_truncated(pl)  1 if points were LOST (a growth or an SdPoint was refused)
#   sd_polyline_degraded(pl)   1 if a curve of THIS call was cut to its chord by the budget
# Both are frozen into the 24 B record and never move again — a later flatten, a later fill, a
# later stroke all leave them alone, which is the whole difference from the two globals. ⚠ The
# DEGRADED bit is this CALL's, where sd_flatten_degraded() is the OPERATION's: inside a consumer's
# scope an earlier flatten's spent budget keeps the global at 1 while a whole result correctly
# reports 0. TRUNCATED matches sd_flatten_truncated() exactly at the moment of return, since that
# global is cleared by this call and set only by this call's own emits.
# ⭐ Every point is returned (0.7.0; through 0.6.0 the result stopped at SD_FLATTEN_CAP = 8,192).
# The point array starts at SD_FLATTEN_PCAP * 16 B and doubles through sd_alloc — it is the caller's
# result, on the seam like the polyline — abandoning each smaller block to the allocator: MEASURED
# 983,040 B of arrays for 20,001 points (65,536 + 131,072 + 262,144 + 524,288), 65,536 B at or under
# 4,096. ⚠ If a doubling fails the result keeps the points that fit, as 0.6.0 did, and
# _sd_flat_trunc is set — and since 0.10.0 a REFUSED DOUBLING IS THE ONLY WAY THAT HAPPENS.
# ⭐⭐ AN EMITTED POINT COSTS NOTHING (0.10.0), AND THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY THE OPPOSITE. Through
# 0.9.1 every point the walk emitted was a fresh 16 B SdPoint, because the output held pointers —
# and 0.9.0 had just made that worse, since a path's points went inline and its anchors could no
# longer be handed through. The output holds coordinates now, so a point is two stores.
# MEASURED on the seam, a 21-point line path at the fill's tolerance: 3 allocations on 0.8.0, 24 on
# 0.9.0, and **3 again here** — the arrays, the ctx and the header, and nothing per point ever
# again. ⇒ The advice this paragraph used to give — "flatten every frame and you pay the anchors
# back per frame, so fill the path directly instead" — no longer applies: holding a polyline and
# re-flattening now cost the same in calls, and the choice is about lifetime, not allocation.
# ⭐ ONE OPERATION for the whole walk (0.7.1): SD_FLATTEN_BUDGET_DEFAULT points of subdivision are
# shared across every curve of the path, and sd_flatten_degraded() reports afterwards whether any
# of them was cut to its chord. MEASURED, 4,096 maximally non-flat quads: 83,623,976 B in
# 3,133,451 allocations on 0.7.0, 3,076,136 B in 65,287 here (27x the bytes, 48x the calls).
fn sd_path_flatten(path, tol): i64 {
    # ⚠ 0.11.1: the one path-taking entry with no null check. It answers 0 for an empty path
    # already, so a null one answers 0 too — the caller's test is unchanged either way.
    if (path == 0) { return 0; }
    # ⛔ THE VERDICTS ARE CLEARED BEFORE THE EMPTY-PATH RETURN, AND THE OPERATION OPENS BEFORE IT
    # (0.7.1 repair). Through the first 0.7.1 draft both sat after it, so an empty path returned 0
    # carrying the PREVIOUS call's verdicts — and the header's own way of telling "empty" from
    # "refused" (truncated() set) did not work: MEASURED, a starved flatten followed by a flatten of
    # an empty path returned 0 twice with truncated() reading 1 both times.
    _sd_flat_trunc = 0;
    # The call-scoped half of the degraded verdict (0.8.0). Cleared here and nowhere else, so what
    # it reads at the bottom of this function is exactly "a curve of THIS call was cut".
    # ⛔ HERE, not after the store: a FILL or a STROKE reaches the same two recursions through
    # sd_flatten_quad / sd_flatten_cubic, so a cut left standing by one of THOSE would be stamped
    # into the next polyline. See _sd_flat_cut; group Q9 is the gate.
    _sd_flat_cut = 0;
    sd_flatten_op_begin();
    var n_verbs = load64(path + SD_PATH_NVERBS_OFFSET);
    if (n_verbs == 0) {
        sd_flatten_op_end();
        return 0;
    }

    var verbs = load64(path + SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET);
    var points = load64(path + SD_PATH_POINTS_OFFSET);

    var out = sd_alloc(SD_FLATTEN_PCAP * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE);
    if (out == 0) {
        _sd_flat_trunc = 1;
        sd_flatten_op_end();
        return 0;
    }
    var ctx = sd_alloc(24);
    if (ctx == 0) {
        _sd_flat_trunc = 1;
        sd_flatten_op_end();
        return 0;
    }
    store64(ctx + 0, out);
    store64(ctx + 8, 0);
    store64(ctx + 16, SD_FLATTEN_PCAP);
    _sd_flat_ctx = ctx;
    # A NEW output can take points whatever any other one could not — and an arena the consumer
    # reset can hand this call the address a starved ctx had (see _sd_flat_full).
    _sd_flat_full = 0;

    # A non-positive tol would never satisfy the flatness test — recurse to
    # the depth cap every time; clamp to >= 1 (1/65536 px).
    var t = tol;
    if (t < 1) { t = 1; }

    # ⛔ `has_cur`, NOT `cur != 0` (0.9.0). Through 0.8.0 the current point was an SdPoint POINTER
    # and 0 meant "no moveto yet" for free. A point is two COORDINATES now, and (0, 0) is a real
    # point — the origin — so the sentinel has to be its own flag or a path that starts at the
    # origin draws nothing. Every walk in the tree (this one, raster.cyr's fill, both of
    # stroke.cyr's) carries the same flag for the same reason.
    var curx = 0;
    var cury = 0;
    var has_cur = 0;
    var vi = 0;
    var pi = 0;
    while (vi < n_verbs) {
        var verb = load64(verbs + vi * 8);
        if (verb == SD_VERB_MOVETO) {
            curx = load64(points + pi * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
            cury = load64(points + pi * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
            has_cur = 1;
            _sd_flat_put(ctx, curx, cury);
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_LINETO) {
            if (has_cur != 0) {         # a drawing verb before any moveto is skipped (raster.cyr)
                curx = load64(points + pi * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
                cury = load64(points + pi * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
                _sd_flat_put(ctx, curx, cury);
            }
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_QUADTO) {
            var qcx = load64(points + pi * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
            var qcy = load64(points + pi * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
            var qex = load64(points + (pi + 1) * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
            var qey = load64(points + (pi + 1) * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
            if (has_cur != 0) {             # a curve before any moveto is skipped (see raster.cyr)
                _sd_flatten_quad_xy(ctx, curx, cury, qcx, qcy, qex, qey, t,
                  SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
                curx = qex;
                cury = qey;
            }
            pi = pi + 2;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_CUBICTO) {
            var k1x = load64(points + pi * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
            var k1y = load64(points + pi * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
            var k2x = load64(points + (pi + 1) * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
            var k2y = load64(points + (pi + 1) * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
            var kex = load64(points + (pi + 2) * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
            var key = load64(points + (pi + 2) * SD_PATH_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
            if (has_cur != 0) {             # a curve before any moveto is skipped (see raster.cyr)
                _sd_flatten_cubic_xy(ctx, curx, cury, k1x, k1y, k2x, k2y, kex, key, t,
                  SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
                curx = kex;
                cury = key;
            }
            pi = pi + 3;
        }
        # SD_VERB_CLOSE consumes no point; the rasterizer closes the subpath
        # back to its moveto anchor.
        vi = vi + 1;
    }

    # ⛔ THE VERDICT IS TAKEN HERE, BEFORE THE HEADER IS ALLOCATED — the walk is over, and the only
    # thing left that can move either flag is this function's own refused sd_alloc, which sets
    # _sd_flat_trunc on a call that returns no record to carry it (0.8.0).
    var verdict = 0;
    if (_sd_flat_trunc != 0) { verdict = verdict + SD_POLYLINE_TRUNCATED; }
    if (_sd_flat_cut != 0)   { verdict = verdict + SD_POLYLINE_DEGRADED; }

    sd_flatten_op_end();
    _sd_flat_ctx = 0;
    var count = load64(ctx + 8);
    var pl = sd_alloc(SD_POLYLINE_SIZE);
    if (pl == 0) {
        _sd_flat_trunc = 1;
        return 0;
    }
    store64(pl + SD_POLYLINE_POINTS_OFFSET, load64(ctx + 0));   # the CURRENT block, grown or not
    store64(pl + SD_POLYLINE_COUNT_OFFSET, count);
    store64(pl + SD_POLYLINE_VERDICT_OFFSET, verdict);
    return pl;
}

# --- coverage.cyr ---

# coverage.cyr — the SD_AA_AREA coverage engine: exact 2-axis signed-area cell accumulation.
#
# The font-rs / stb_truetype v2 / FreeType "smooth" family. sd_fill_impl (raster.cyr) builds the
# closed edge list exactly as it always has, then — ONLY on a canvas switched to SD_AA_AREA with
# sd_canvas_set_aa — hands that list here instead of to the 4-sub-scanline loop.
#
# ⛔⛔ OPT-IN, AND IT STAYS OPT-IN. The default engine (SD_AA_SUBSCANLINE) is analytic in x but
# SAMPLES y at 4 sub-scanlines, so a near-horizontal edge quantizes to 1/4 steps (an edge at
# y = 4.3 reads 63, not 76). agnos's GPU rasteriser (tests/gpu/refagree.cyr) uses sadish's
# DEFAULT fill as a byte-identical oracle, so that engine is never replaced — this one sits beside
# it and nothing reaches it unless a caller asks.
#
# THE ALGORITHM. One accumulator row acc[0..w] (w+1 i64 slots). For every edge overlapping pixel
# row py, clip the edge to the row, split it at pixel-column boundaries, and for each piece of
# height pdy lying in column c at mean fractional x fx:
#     a = pdy * fx                     (the part of the piece's cover NOT inside pixel c)
#     acc[c]   += sgn * (pdy - a)
#     acc[c+1] += sgn * a
# A running sum along the row then yields, per pixel, the signed integral of the winding number
# over that pixel's area, in 16.16 (ONE = fully covered).
#
# ⛔ THE STREAK INVARIANT. Every piece deposits exactly `pdy` into the row (pdy - a + a), and the
# pieces of an edge telescope: each piece's pdy is the DIFFERENCE of two y offsets computed from the
# row-clipped segment's exact endpoints (never a sum of rounded steps), so they add to exactly
# ye - ys. Across rows the same holds — row clipping is at exact integer row boundaries. ⇒ Right of
# a closed contour the running sum is EXACTLY 0 in integer arithmetic; one leaked unit would paint a
# streak to the canvas's right edge. Portions LEFT of x = 0 deposit their whole pdy into column 0
# (their cover still reaches every visible pixel); portions right of x = w deposit nothing, since
# no visible prefix sum reads past column w-1.
#
# RULES, from s = |running sum|:
#   NONZERO   -> min(s, ONE)
#   EVEN_ODD  -> s mod 2*ONE folded into a triangle wave (FreeType's approach)
# Both fold the INTEGRAL of the winding over the pixel, not the area of the region, so both are
# EXACT when the winding inside the pixel takes only the values {0, +1} or only {0, -1} — a simple
# contour, or disjoint ones. Anywhere else it is the standard cell-coverage approximation (FreeType's
# too): opposite windings inside one pixel cancel — and ONE self-intersecting contour can do that,
# a pixel-sized bow-tie reads 0 — and overlapping same-way contours over part of a pixel count twice
# (two coincident edges at x = 4.3 read 152 nonzero, not 76).
#
# ⚠ PRECISION. Coordinates are 16.16 and must satisfy |x|, |y| <= 2^30 raw (±16,384 px) — the same
# bound the default engine's (bx-ax)*(sy-ay) product already carries. Under it every intermediate
# fits an i64: x at a row boundary (dx * dy_part) < 2^62; a column crossing's y offset
# (pdy_row <= 2^16) * (x - x0 < 2^31) < 2^47; the split (pdy <= 2^16) * (fx_sum <= 2^17) <= 2^33;
# the running sum <= n_edges * ONE (an edge deposits at most ONE into a row), below 2^48 for any list
# under 2^32 edges (128 GiB of records). All divisions and shifts in the accumulation act
# on non-negative values (the sign is applied after the split), so truncation is the same for a
# contour and its mirror: CW and CCW input give byte-identical coverage.
# ⚠ Truncation is under a raw unit per step per piece (x at a row boundary, y at a column crossing,
# the split), so a pixel's error grows with the pieces IN it — not along the row, where the pieces
# telescope. MEASURED, even-odd, coincident triangles inside one pixel vs the exact fold: 810 of
# them (2,430 edges) exact to the byte, 2,430 (7,290 edges) 2 bytes off.
#
# ⚠ SINGLE-THREADED, like the rest of the fill: the accumulator is one process-wide block.

# ---- process-lifetime scratch (lazily, on the FIRST AREA fill) -------------------------------
# ⛔ FROM THE GLOBAL `alloc`, NEVER `sd_alloc` — the same rule, for the same reason, as raster.cyr's
# _sd_scratch_init: a consumer hook is typically an arena that gets reset, and a scratch drawn from
# it dangles on the next frame. ⚠ And NOT in _sd_scratch_init: a process that never asks for AREA
# pays nothing for it, and the default fill's first-call figures (655,688 B at 32 px, asserted in
# programs/alloc_test.cyr; 590,152 B through 0.8.0, before the stroker's run went inline) stay
# exact.
# The edge list needs no scratch of its own: it is sorted IN PLACE in _sd_fill_edges, which is
# per-call working storage nothing reads after sd_fill_impl returns.
var _sd_area_acc = 0;          # (w + 1) * 8 — one row of signed 16.16 cell accumulators
var _sd_area_acc_cap = 0;      # slots the current block holds

# The accumulator for a canvas `w` pixels wide: w + 1 slots (a piece in the last column deposits
# its `a` into slot w, which nothing reads). Re-allocates from the GLOBAL alloc only when a wider
# canvas than ever seen arrives; ⚠ the old block is abandoned (no free()), exactly like
# _sd_fill_accrow_for. MEASURED (programs/area_test.cyr): the first AREA fill costs the global heap
# exactly (w+1)*8 — 264 B at 32 px, a regrow to 64 px 520 B — and the arena 0; every fill after, 0.
# Returns 0 if the allocation fails.
fn _sd_area_acc_for(w): i64 {
    if (w + 1 <= _sd_area_acc_cap) { return _sd_area_acc; }
    var blk = alloc((w + 1) * 8);
    if (blk == 0) { return 0; }
    _sd_area_acc = blk;
    _sd_area_acc_cap = w + 1;
    return blk;
}

# min / max y of an edge record (4 i64: ax, ay, bx, by).
fn _sd_area_ymin(e): i64 {
    var ay = load64(e + 8);
    var by = load64(e + 24);
    if (by < ay) { return by; }
    return ay;
}

fn _sd_area_ymax(e): i64 {
    var ay = load64(e + 8);
    var by = load64(e + 24);
    if (by > ay) { return by; }
    return ay;
}

# Swap two 32-byte edge records.
fn _sd_area_swap(ea, eb): i64 {
    var k = 0;
    while (k < 32) {
        var t = load64(ea + k);
        store64(ea + k, load64(eb + k));
        store64(eb + k, t);
        k = k + 8;
    }
    return 0;
}

# Sort the edge records by ymin, in place (Shell sort, Knuth 3h+1 gaps: no scratch, O(n^1.5) worst
# case — not O(n^2) — on the lists a long flattened path produces, which since 0.7.0 are unbounded).
fn _sd_area_sort(edges, n): i64 {
    var gap = 1;
    while (gap < n / 3) { gap = gap * 3 + 1; }
    while (gap >= 1) {
        var i = gap;
        while (i < n) {
            var src = edges + i * 32;
            var t0 = load64(src + 0);
            var t1 = load64(src + 8);
            var t2 = load64(src + 16);
            var t3 = load64(src + 24);
            var key = t1;
            if (t3 < key) { key = t3; }
            var j = i;
            var stop = 0;
            while (stop == 0) {
                if (j < gap) {
                    stop = 1;
                } else {
                    var prev = edges + (j - gap) * 32;
                    if (_sd_area_ymin(prev) > key) {
                        var dst = edges + j * 32;
                        store64(dst + 0, load64(prev + 0));
                        store64(dst + 8, load64(prev + 8));
                        store64(dst + 16, load64(prev + 16));
                        store64(dst + 24, load64(prev + 24));
                        j = j - gap;
                    } else {
                        stop = 1;
                    }
                }
            }
            var at = edges + j * 32;
            store64(at + 0, t0);
            store64(at + 8, t1);
            store64(at + 16, t2);
            store64(at + 24, t3);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        gap = gap / 3;
    }
    return 0;
}

# Deposit one row-clipped segment into the accumulator. `xa`, `xb` are its x at the two row-clipped
# ends (either order — cover is spread linearly in x, so direction does not matter), `dy` > 0 its
# height inside the row, `sgn` +1 / -1 its winding direction.
fn _sd_area_cells(acc, w, xa, xb, dy, sgn): i64 {
    var wmax = w << SD_SHIFT;
    if (xa == xb) {
        # vertical piece: one column, fx is its x fraction
        if (xa >= wmax) { return 0; }
        if (xa <= 0) {
            store64(acc, load64(acc) + sgn * dy);
            return 0;
        }
        var vc = xa >> SD_SHIFT;
        var va = (dy * (xa - (vc << SD_SHIFT))) >> SD_SHIFT;
        store64(acc + vc * 8, load64(acc + vc * 8) + sgn * (dy - va));
        store64(acc + (vc + 1) * 8, load64(acc + (vc + 1) * 8) + sgn * va);
        return 0;
    }
    var x0 = xa;
    var x1 = xb;
    if (x0 > x1) { x0 = xb; x1 = xa; }
    if (x0 >= wmax) { return 0; }                     # wholly right of the canvas
    if (x1 <= 0) {                                    # wholly left: full cover into column 0
        store64(acc, load64(acc) + sgn * dy);
        return 0;
    }
    var span = x1 - x0;
    # the visible x range [lo, hi); lo < hi here because x0 < wmax and x1 > 0
    var lo = x0;
    if (lo < 0) { lo = 0; }
    var hi = x1;
    if (hi > wmax) { hi = wmax; }
    # y offset (from the x0 end) at x = lo; the part left of x = 0 lands whole in column 0
    var py = 0;
    if (lo > x0) {
        py = (dy * (lo - x0)) / span;
        store64(acc, load64(acc) + sgn * py);
    }
    var px = lo;
    var c = lo >> SD_SHIFT;
    while (px < hi) {
        var cl = c << SD_SHIFT;
        var nx = cl + SD_ONE;
        if (nx > hi) { nx = hi; }
        var ny = dy;
        if (nx < x1) { ny = (dy * (nx - x0)) / span; }
        var pdy = ny - py;
        var a = (pdy * ((px - cl) + (nx - cl))) >> 17;    # pdy * mean(fx), fx sum has 17 bits
        store64(acc + c * 8, load64(acc + c * 8) + sgn * (pdy - a));
        store64(acc + (c + 1) * 8, load64(acc + (c + 1) * 8) + sgn * a);
        px = nx;
        py = ny;
        c = c + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# Deposit the part of edge record `e` that lies in the pixel row starting at `rt` (16.16).
fn _sd_area_edge_row(acc, w, e, rt): i64 {
    var xa = load64(e + 0);
    var ya = load64(e + 8);
    var xb = load64(e + 16);
    var yb = load64(e + 24);
    var sgn = 1;
    if (ya > yb) {
        # orient top -> bottom; the x at a row boundary is then computed from the same
        # endpoints in the same order for both windings (mirror-exact)
        xa = load64(e + 16);
        ya = load64(e + 24);
        xb = load64(e + 0);
        yb = load64(e + 8);
        sgn = 0 - 1;
    }
    var ys = ya;
    if (ys < rt) { ys = rt; }
    var ye = yb;
    if (ye > rt + SD_ONE) { ye = rt + SD_ONE; }
    if (ye <= ys) { return 0; }
    var xs = xa + ((xb - xa) * (ys - ya)) / (yb - ya);
    var xe = xa + ((xb - xa) * (ye - ya)) / (yb - ya);
    return _sd_area_cells(acc, w, xs, xe, ye - ys, sgn);
}

# ================================================================
# _sd_area_fill — the SD_AA_AREA engine behind sd_fill_impl. `edges`/`n_edges` is the closed edge
# list sd_fill_impl built (REORDERED here); `cov`/`stride` the canvas's coverage buffer; `mask` the
# active clip mask (0 = unclipped) — PACKED w*h, so `stride` addresses `cov` and nothing else;
# `nonzero` 1 for SD_FILL_NONZERO, 0 for even-odd; `combine` 0 = overwrite, 1 = MAX into the
# existing coverage. The byte mapping, clip multiply and union MAX are the default engine's, verbatim.
# ⛔ The list must be WHOLE — every contour closed — or the running sum never returns to 0 and the
# row streaks to the right edge. sd_fill_impl guarantees it by calling here only when no edge was
# dropped (_sd_fill_trunc == 0: the list grows, and drops only when a growth fails); the styled
# stroke's flush likewise (_sd_sb_trunc).
#
# Rows are swept top to bottom over an ACTIVE WINDOW [lo, nxt) of the ymin-sorted edge list:
# edges whose ymin is above the row's bottom join at nxt; an edge that ends at or before the
# row's bottom is swapped down to lo after that row. O(rows * active + sort), against the default
# engine's O(rows * 4 * n_edges) plus an insertion sort of crossings per sub-scanline. MEASURED,
# 256x256, including flattening: a two-contour quad/cubic blob 0.52 ms per fill (SUBSCANLINE
# 2.77 ms); a 400-edge self-intersecting polygon 2.4 ms (37.9 ms); 8,000 edges 42 ms (11.7 s).
# Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_OOM if the one-time accumulator allocation fails.
# ================================================================
fn _sd_area_fill(edges, n_edges, w, h, cov, stride, mask, nonzero, combine): i64 {
    var acc = _sd_area_acc_for(w);
    if (acc == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    # Slots [0, w) start at 0: a fresh block is not guaranteed zeroed, and a NARROWER previous
    # fill leaves its sink slot (its own index w) holding a residue this canvas would read. Slot w
    # itself is a write-only sink — no running sum reads past column w-1 — so it is never cleared.
    var zi = 0;
    while (zi < w) {
        store64(acc + zi * 8, 0);
        zi = zi + 1;
    }
    _sd_area_sort(edges, n_edges);

    var two = SD_ONE * 2;
    var lo = 0;
    var nxt = 0;
    var py = 0;
    while (py < h) {
        var rt = py << SD_SHIFT;
        var rb = rt + SD_ONE;
        var joining = 1;
        while (joining != 0) {
            if (nxt >= n_edges) {
                joining = 0;
            } else {
                if (_sd_area_ymin(edges + nxt * 32) < rb) {
                    nxt = nxt + 1;
                } else {
                    joining = 0;
                }
            }
        }
        var i = lo;
        while (i < nxt) {
            var e = edges + i * 32;
            _sd_area_edge_row(acc, w, e, rt);
            if (_sd_area_ymax(e) <= rb) {
                # done after this row: retire it below the window (the record swapped up into
                # slot i came from lo, which this row has already processed)
                _sd_area_swap(e, edges + lo * 32);
                lo = lo + 1;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }

        # accumulator row -> coverage bytes (running sum; each slot is zeroed as it is consumed)
        var run = 0;
        var px = 0;
        while (px < w) {
            run = run + load64(acc + px * 8);
            store64(acc + px * 8, 0);
            var s = run;
            if (s < 0) { s = 0 - s; }
            if (nonzero != 0) {
                if (s > SD_ONE) { s = SD_ONE; }
            } else {
                s = s % two;
                if (s > SD_ONE) { s = two - s; }
            }
            # s <= ONE, so c <= 255: the default engine's clamp cannot fire here
            var c = (s * 255) >> SD_SHIFT;
            if (mask != 0) {
                # ⛔ PACKED `py * w + px` — the clip mask is a w*h block, never a window into a
                # wider buffer, so `stride` (the COVERAGE buffer's pitch) is not its pitch. See
                # raster.cyr's SdCanvas layout note; gated by programs/clip_pitch_test.cyr.
                c = (c * load8(mask + py * w + px)) / 255;
            }
            if (combine != 0) {
                var oldc = load8(cov + py * stride + px);   # coverage: the STRIDE, not w
                if (c < oldc) { c = oldc; }
            }
            store8(cov + py * stride + px, c);
            px = px + 1;
        }
        py = py + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# --- raster.cyr ---

# @public — sadish rasterizer: path -> coverage buffer -> blit.
#
# raster.cyr — an SdCanvas owns a width*height coverage buffer (one byte
# of anti-aliased coverage per pixel, 0..255). The fill entry takes a
# flattened path and a winding rule and accumulates coverage; the blit
# entry hands that coverage to a destination framebuffer (the kernel
# blit#39 on AGNOS, or a host framebuffer under test).
#
# The coverage buffer is a separate, format-neutral stage on purpose: a
# consumer (canvas/compositing/SVG/UI) chooses how to turn coverage +
# a paint (solid color / gradient) into destination pixels. sadish's job
# ends at coverage + a reference solid blit.

# ---- winding rules ----------------------------------------------
var SD_FILL_EVEN_ODD = 0;
var SD_FILL_NONZERO  = 1;

# ---- coverage engines -------------------------------------------
# SD_AA_SUBSCANLINE — the 0.4.0 engine: analytic in x, 4 sub-scanlines in y. THE DEFAULT, and
#   byte-identical forever: agnos's GPU rasteriser (tests/gpu/refagree.cyr) is gated on it.
# SD_AA_AREA — exact in both axes (signed-area cell accumulation, src/coverage.cyr). Opt-in per
#   canvas with sd_canvas_set_aa.
var SD_AA_SUBSCANLINE = 0;
var SD_AA_AREA        = 1;

# ================================================================
# SdCanvas (48 bytes):
#   +0:  width      (i64 — pixels)
#   +8:  height     (i64 — pixels)
#   +16: coverage   (ptr — owned width*height bytes, one coverage per px)
#   +24: stride     (i64 — bytes per row OF THE COVERAGE BUFFER; == width for the packed buffer)
#   +32: clip       (ptr — top SdClip node, or 0 = unclipped)
#   +40: aa         (i64 — coverage engine, SD_AA_SUBSCANLINE | SD_AA_AREA)
# ⛔⛔ TWO PITCHES, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS THE STRIDE. The COVERAGE buffer at +16 is addressed
# `y * stride + x` — it may be a window into a wider buffer. The CLIP MASK hanging off +32 is a
# sadish-owned, never-wrapped `w * h` block and is addressed `y * w + x` — PACKED, ALWAYS, whatever
# the canvas stride says. Every producer already wrote it packed (sd_canvas_clip_push_rect,
# sd_clip_push_mask's intersection, the temp canvas behind sd_canvas_clip_push_path); since 0.7.0
# the three consumers read it packed too (sd_fill_impl, _sd_area_fill, _sd_sb_flush). Byte-identical
# on every canvas sd_canvas_new makes, where stride == width — the gate is programs/clip_pitch_test.cyr,
# which hand-builds the header sd_canvas_new cannot. Closed
# docs/development/issues/archived/2026-09-15-clip-masks-written-packed-but-read-by-canvas-stride.md.
# ⚠ The canvas was 40 bytes through 0.5.5. Nothing in dhancha, rekha or agnos builds one by hand
# (checked by grep) — sd_canvas_new is the only constructor. But every canvas — and the temp mask
# canvas behind each sd_canvas_clip_push_path — now costs 8 B more on sd_alloc, which moves exact
# figures consumers have MEASURED (dhancha's text_arena_test, same checks passing: a 12-label frame
# 417,056 -> 417,144 B, its 64 KiB-arena spill 351,520 -> 351,608 B).
# ================================================================
var SD_CANVAS_WIDTH_OFFSET    = 0;
var SD_CANVAS_HEIGHT_OFFSET   = 8;
var SD_CANVAS_COVERAGE_OFFSET = 16;
var SD_CANVAS_STRIDE_OFFSET   = 24;
var SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET     = 32;
var SD_CANVAS_AA_OFFSET       = 40;
var SD_CANVAS_SIZE            = 48;

# @public — allocate a w*h canvas with a zeroed coverage buffer.
# Returns the SdCanvas ptr, or 0 on OOM / bad dimensions.
fn sd_canvas_new(w, h): i64 {
    if (w <= 0) { return 0; }
    if (h <= 0) { return 0; }
    # ⛔ w * h wraps exactly as sd_surface_new's did — see SD_DIM_MAX in src/surface.cyr. A canvas
    # publishes its width as the STRIDE, so a wrapped coverage block is indexed by a number the
    # record itself hands every reader.
    if (w > SD_DIM_MAX) { return 0; }
    if (h > SD_DIM_MAX) { return 0; }
    if (w * h > SD_PIXELS_MAX) { return 0; }
    var cv = sd_alloc(SD_CANVAS_SIZE);
    if (cv == 0) { return 0; }
    var n = w * h;
    var cov = sd_alloc(n);
    if (cov == 0) { return 0; }
    # Zero the coverage buffer (alloc is not guaranteed zeroed).
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        store8(cov + i, 0);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    store64(cv + SD_CANVAS_WIDTH_OFFSET, w);
    store64(cv + SD_CANVAS_HEIGHT_OFFSET, h);
    store64(cv + SD_CANVAS_COVERAGE_OFFSET, cov);
    store64(cv + SD_CANVAS_STRIDE_OFFSET, w);
    store64(cv + SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET, 0);
    store64(cv + SD_CANVAS_AA_OFFSET, SD_AA_SUBSCANLINE);
    return cv;
}

# ---- accessors --------------------------------------------------
# @public
fn sd_canvas_width(cv): i64    { return load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_WIDTH_OFFSET); }
# @public
fn sd_canvas_height(cv): i64   { return load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_HEIGHT_OFFSET); }
# @public
fn sd_canvas_coverage(cv): i64 { return load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_COVERAGE_OFFSET); }
# @public
fn sd_canvas_stride(cv): i64   { return load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_STRIDE_OFFSET); }
# @public — the canvas's coverage engine (SD_AA_SUBSCANLINE unless sd_canvas_set_aa changed it).
fn sd_canvas_aa(cv): i64       { return load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_AA_OFFSET); }

# @public — choose the coverage engine every fill into `cv` uses: sd_canvas_fill_path,
# sd_canvas_fill_union (and so sd_canvas_stroke_path), and the mask sd_canvas_clip_push_path
# builds. SD_AA_SUBSCANLINE (the default) or SD_AA_AREA. Coverage already in the buffer is not
# touched. Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null canvas or an unknown mode (the
# canvas is left unchanged).
fn sd_canvas_set_aa(cv, mode): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (mode != SD_AA_SUBSCANLINE) {
        if (mode != SD_AA_AREA) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    }
    store64(cv + SD_CANVAS_AA_OFFSET, mode);
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# Coverage of one pixel (0 if out of bounds).
fn sd_canvas_coverage_at(cv, x, y): i64 {
    var w = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var h = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    if (x < 0) { return 0; }
    if (y < 0) { return 0; }
    if (x >= w) { return 0; }
    if (y >= h) { return 0; }
    var cov = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_COVERAGE_OFFSET);
    var stride = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_STRIDE_OFFSET);
    return load8(cov + y * stride + x);
}

# @public — zero the canvas's WINDOW of the coverage buffer (reuse a canvas across draws).
# ⛔ ROW BY ROW AT THE STRIDE, `w` bytes a row — not one flat `w * h` run. The coverage buffer is
# the strided side of the canvas (see the layout note): a flat run over a wrapped canvas would
# zero another row's padding and leave the window's last rows dirty. Byte-identical on every
# canvas sd_canvas_new makes, where stride == width and the two walks are the same 70 bytes in the
# same order. Gated by programs/clip_pitch_test.cyr.
fn sd_canvas_clear(cv): i64 {
    # ⚠ 0.11.1: the one canvas entry that dereferenced without checking, while every sibling —
    # fill, blit, clip push/pop, set_aa — answers SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS. The asymmetry WAS the bug.
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var w = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var h = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    var cov = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_COVERAGE_OFFSET);
    var stride = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_STRIDE_OFFSET);
    var y = 0;
    while (y < h) {
        var x = 0;
        while (x < w) {
            store8(cov + y * stride + x, 0);
            x = x + 1;
        }
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# ---- process-lifetime fill/stroke scratch (0.5.5) ---------------
# ⭐ Before 0.5.5 every sd_fill_impl call allocated ectx + edges + fbuf + fctx + accrow + cross
# FRESH, and every sd_canvas_stroke_path call its run + fbuf + fctx — on a bump allocator with no
# free(). MEASURED on 0.5.4, 8x8 canvas, one call: fill 327,824 B, stroke of a closed 4-vertex
# rect 2,789,016 B (8 fill_union passes underneath it), per call, forever. dhancha filed it
# (2026-09-13, scalable text allocates per call outside the frame arena); crab's zero-heap
# frame gate is what it blocks. The capacity was FIXED (SD_FLATTEN_CAP) and did not depend on
# the path, so it is one process-wide set, allocated lazily ONCE and reused by every call (and
# since 0.7.0 grown in place when a path outgrows it — GROWTH, below).
#
# ⛔ FROM THE GLOBAL `alloc`, NEVER `sd_alloc`. A consumer's hook is typically a per-frame arena
# that gets reset; a scratch drawn from it would be dangling on the second frame. The scratch
# outlives every hook by construction, so it must come from the one allocator that never rewinds.
# ⇒ This is also why fill/stroke are NOT on the seam's "everything" promise (see src/alloc.cyr):
# their one-time cost lands on the global heap at the first call, and never again.
#
# ⛔ TWO SETS, NOT ONE. sd_canvas_stroke_path holds `run` LIVE across the sd_canvas_fill_union
# calls sd_stroke_run makes, and each of those runs sd_fill_impl, which fills its own fbuf/fctx.
# Sharing a fbuf between the two would have the fill's flatten overwrite the stroke's run
# mid-stroke. The stroker's run/fbuf/fctx are therefore its own globals, and non-aliasing is
# visible in the names rather than reasoned about per call.
#
# ⚠ SINGLE-THREADED ENTRY POINTS, said out loud. The rasterizer never had a lock anywhere and was
# never thread-safe; a shared scratch makes sd_fill_impl / sd_canvas_stroke_path explicitly
# single-threaded. Checked: every consumer in the ecosystem (dhancha, rekha, agnos's refagree
# test) rasterizes from one thread.
#
# Flatten MID-POINTS (sd_pt_mid -> sd_point_new) are deliberately NOT here: they are
# proportional to the curve work, not fixed-capacity, so they stay on the seam and the hook
# decides where they live (RE-MEASURED ON 0.9.0, 8x8 canvas, warm scratch: the CHANGELOG's one-quad
# fill — moveto (0,0), quadto ctrl (8,0) → (8,8), lineto back, closed — costs 64 B of them in 4
# allocations = 3 EMITTED mid-points + THE CURVE'S OWN END POINT x 16 B, and the same 64 B on the
# next identical call; a single quad only ever costs 16 x (2^d - 1) B of MID-POINTS because both de
# Casteljau halves carry the parent's second difference / 4 and so split to the same depth).
# ⚠ IT WAS 48 B IN 3 THROUGH 0.8.0, when the end point was the PATH'S OWN SdPoint and was handed
# through rather than built: a path's points are inline coordinates from 0.9.0, so there is no
# record to hand and the fill's flatten materialises one. +16 B and one call per CURVE VERB, and
# per anchor on sd_path_flatten — the release notes carry the whole figure.
# ⚠ THAT COEFFICIENT WAS 48, NOT 16, THROUGH 0.7.0, and the figure above was 144 B: the recursion
# allocated every subdivision node's three mid-points on the seam and threw two of them away. 0.7.1
# takes the throwaways onto a fixed per-depth scratch (src/path.cyr), so only EMITTED points are
# allocated — rekha's filing, and the reason any older MEASURED mid-point figure reads 3x high.
var _sd_fill_ectx = 0;         # edge builder ctx (24 B): +0 edges  +8 count  +16 cap
var _sd_fill_edges = 0;        # _sd_fill_edges_cap * 32 — GROWS (0.7.0)
var _sd_fill_edges_cap = 0;    # edges the current block holds (SD_FLATTEN_CAP until a path needs more)
var _sd_fill_trunc = 0;        # 1 if THIS fill's edge list dropped an edge (a growth failed)
var _sd_fill_fbuf = 0;         # SD_FLATTEN_PCAP * 16 — flatten output for one curve verb (FIXED, below)
var _sd_fill_fctx = 0;         # flatten ctx (24 B)
var _sd_fill_cross = 0;        # _sd_fill_cross_cap * 16 — (x, dir) crossings — GROWS (0.7.0)
var _sd_fill_cross_cap = 0;    # >= every edge list that is walked: the fill's AND the stroke batch's
var _sd_fill_accrow = 0;       # w * 8 — per-row 16.16 accumulator, sized to the widest canvas seen
var _sd_fill_accrow_cap = 0;   # pixels the current accrow block can hold
# ⭐ 16 B A POINT, x at +0 and y at +8, INLINE (0.9.0; _sd_stroke_run_cap * 8 SdPoint POINTERS
# through 0.8.0). A path's points are coordinates now, so a run built from them has nothing to point
# AT — and a run of coordinates is what keeps a straight verb's anchor from costing an allocation on
# the consumer's seam at every stroke. ⭐ 0.10.0: the flatten output it copies FROM is now inline
# too, so _sd_run_append is a straight 16 B -> 16 B copy with nothing to dereference on either side.
# ⚠ MEASURED one-time cost: 131,072 B against
# 65,536 B — this block doubled, ONCE, on the global heap (never sd_alloc), and it is the whole
# price of the seam saving.
var _sd_stroke_run = 0;        # _sd_stroke_run_cap * 16 — the stroker's polyline run — GROWS (0.7.0)
var _sd_stroke_run_cap = 0;    # points the current run block holds
var _sd_stroke_fbuf = 0;       # SD_FLATTEN_PCAP * 16 — the stroker's OWN flatten output (FIXED)
var _sd_stroke_fctx = 0;       # the stroker's OWN flatten ctx (24 B)
# ⚠ The SD_AA_AREA engine's accumulator (_sd_area_acc, (w+1)*8) is the same kind of scratch but
# lives with its engine in src/coverage.cyr, allocated on the first AREA fill — NOT here, so a
# process that never opts in pays nothing and the first-fill figures below stay exact.
#
# ⭐ GROWTH (0.7.0). Through 0.6.0 the edge list, the crossings and the stroker's run held
# SD_FLATTEN_CAP = 8,192 entries and DROPPED the rest: a path of more edges filled wrong (its
# contours left open), a longer run stroked only its first 8,192 points. Now each grows by
# doubling when full — from the GLOBAL alloc, for the reason above — copying its live entries and
# abandoning the old block (no free()), exactly like _sd_fill_accrow_for. The INITIAL capacities
# are unchanged, so every first-use figure stays exact, and growth is monotone: a process pays for
# its largest path once, then 0 B a call. MEASURED (programs/grow_edges_test.cyr): the first fill
# past 8,192 edges costs 786,432 B (edges 16,384 * 32 + crossings 16,384 * 16); a repeat 0 B.
# ⚠ The two per-curve buffers (_sd_fill_fbuf, _sd_stroke_fbuf) stay FIXED: they hold ONE curve
# verb's flattening, which sd_flatten_quad / sd_flatten_cubic bound by 2^SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH = 256
# points (every leaf of the depth-8 recursion emits exactly one) — a 32nd of their capacity.
# ⚠ OUT OF MEMORY: a failed growth keeps the old block and drops, as 0.6.0 did, and RECORDS it
# (_sd_fill_trunc here; _sd_sb_trunc / _sd_run_trunc in stroke.cyr) — so the SD_AA_AREA dispatch,
# which streaks on an open contour, falls back only for a list that is really incomplete.

# ================================================================
# THE GROWTH CEILING — the largest single growth request, in bytes, that is attempted. A request
# above it fails exactly as `alloc` failing would: the store keeps its old block, drops what does
# not fit, and RECORDS the truncation (the flags above), which is 0.6.0's behaviour for a path this
# large. 0 = no bound of sadish's own (alloc itself refuses over 2 GiB).
#
# ⛔⛔ WHY THIS IS BOUNDED BY DEFAULT, AND WHY THAT IS A TRADE AND NOT A FREE WIN. Lifting
# SD_FLATTEN_CAP turned "a path over 8,192 edges renders WRONG" into "a path over 8,192 edges costs
# whatever it costs" — and the scratch is process-lifetime on an allocator with no free(), so ONE
# pathological path makes a process keep that memory for the rest of its life. MEASURED on this
# tree, rekha's hostile repro (4,096 maximally non-flat quads, one sd_canvas_fill_path on 64x64,
# the shape a font file can carry — the filing is
# docs/development/issues/archived/2026-09-15-flatten-keeps-subdividing-and-allocating-after-the-output-cap-is-full.md):
# unbounded, it grows the edge list and crossings to 1,048,576 entries and the fill costs
# 150,602,312 B of global heap, permanently. At the default below the same fill keeps its first
# 262,144 edges, flags the truncation, and the AREA dispatch falls back to the sub-scanline loop
# (which paints nothing past a row's last crossing) rather than streaking on an open contour.
# ⇒ Over the ceiling, sadish chooses BOUNDED MEMORY AND A FLAGGED, DEGRADED FILL over correct
# output at any price. A consumer that renders its own trusted geometry and wants the old
# behaviour sets 0; a consumer that fills untrusted outlines (rekha → dhancha → crab draw glyph
# paths from font files, every frame) should keep a bound.
# ⚠ THE PEAK IS ABOUT TWICE THE CEILING PER STORE. Growth doubles and abandons the old block, so a
# store that reaches the ceiling has also paid for every smaller block below it (8 MiB + 4 + 2 + …
# ≈ 16 MiB). The ceiling bounds one REQUEST, not a process's total.
# ⚠ It is also what lets programs/grow_edges_test.cyr drive the out-of-memory path a real failure
# takes — no honest test reaches alloc's own limit, which is 67 M edges.
# ================================================================
# 8 MiB: 262,144 edges (32 B each), 524,288 crossings (16 B) or 524,288 run points (16 B) in one
# request — far past any legitimate path this stack draws, and short of the hostile repro above.
# ⛔⛔ THE DEFAULT DOUBLED IN 0.9.0 SO THAT THE POINT BUFFERS REACH EXACTLY AS FAR AS THEY DID.
# The ceiling is in BYTES, and 0.9.0 turned a run point from an 8 B pointer into a 16 B inline
# (x, y) — so at 0.8.0's 8 MiB the stroker's run and the dash piece buffer would have topped out at
# 524,288 points where 0.8.0 reached 1,048,576, and a path of over 524,288 points in ONE subpath
# that 0.8.0 stroked whole would have truncated (MEASURED with sd_grow_limit_set(262144) and a
# 20,000-point styled stroke: 0.8.0 rc=SADISH_OK and 526,870 coverage units, the 8 MiB 0.9.0
# rc=SADISH_ERR_OOM and 434,050). ⇒ The default is 16 MiB, and the length a consumer can stroke is
# what it was. PINNED by programs/grow_edges_test.cyr groups R and R2.
# ⭐ AND IT COSTS NO REAL MEMORY, MEASURED ON BOTH TREES: at 8,192 / 16,384 / 32,768-point subpaths,
# where the run's capacity is exactly its occupancy, a stroked point costs FORTY BYTES of path + run
# on EITHER release — 0.8.0 pays 8 to the run and 32 to the path (an 8 B verb, an 8 B pointer, a
# 16 B SdPoint), 0.9.0 pays 16 to the run and 24 to the path (the 8 B verb and the 16 B inline
# slot). The ceiling's SHARE of an unchanged total moved, so restoring the length restores the
# footprint: at their truncation points both releases hold 1,048,576 points x 40 B = 41,943,040 B of
# path + run. 0.9.0 buys 0.8.0's longest subpath for 0.8.0's own bytes, to the byte.
# ⚠ WHAT THE DOUBLING ALSO DOES, said plainly: the ceiling bounds EVERY growable store, so the
# fill's edge list and crossings can now reach twice what they could — 524,288 edges, not 262,144.
# That is a looser hostile-path bound than 0.8.0's, and it is the price of keeping stroke behaviour
# byte-stable across the ABI change. A consumer that wants 0.8.0's memory bound rather than its
# length calls sd_grow_limit_set(8388608) and gets exactly the old figure back.
# Swept 2026-09-16: no repo in the ecosystem (rekha, dhancha, agnos, setu, crab, puka) calls
# sd_grow_limit_set at all, so nothing ships against either length today.
var SD_GROW_LIMIT_DEFAULT = 16777216;
var _sd_grow_limit = 16777216;

# @public — set the growth ceiling in bytes (0 = unbounded); returns the PREVIOUS value, so a caller
# can scope it. SD_GROW_LIMIT_DEFAULT restores the default.
# ⚠ BYTES, NOT ENTRIES, and what a byte buys changed in 0.9.0 for two of the five stores it bounds.
# n bytes is n/32 edges (the fill's list and the styled batch alike), n/16 crossings, n/16 RUN
# POINTS and n/16 dash points — the last two were n/8 through 0.8.0, when a point was a pointer.
# ⇒ 0.9.0 doubled SD_GROW_LIMIT_DEFAULT so the DEFAULT length is unchanged; a caller that tuned its
# own value past 0.9.0 doubles it for the same maximum path length. See the
# ⛔⛔ block above SD_GROW_LIMIT_DEFAULT for what happens if it does not.
fn sd_grow_limit_set(n): i64 {
    var prev = _sd_grow_limit;
    if (n < 0) { return prev; }
    _sd_grow_limit = n;
    return prev;
}

# @public — the current growth ceiling in bytes (0 = unbounded).
fn sd_grow_limit_get(): i64 {
    return _sd_grow_limit;
}

# `alloc(n)` for a growth of the process-lifetime scratch, subject to _sd_grow_limit. 0 on failure.
fn _sd_grow_alloc(n): i64 {
    if (_sd_grow_limit > 0) {
        if (n > _sd_grow_limit) { return 0; }
    }
    return alloc(n);
}

# Allocate the fixed-capacity scratch once (idempotent). Called at the top of sd_fill_impl and
# sd_canvas_stroke_path; a second call is one load + compare. MEASURED one-time cost: 655,432 B
# (fill 458,800 + stroke 196,632), plus accrow on first use (w*8: 64 B at 8 px, 6,080 B at 760).
# ⚠ It was 589,896 B through 0.8.0: the stroker's run went from 8 B pointers to 16 B INLINE points
# (0.9.0), +65,536 B on the global heap, ONCE. That is the whole cost of a stroke that allocates no
# SdPoint per anchor on the consumer's seam any more.
# ⇒ After it, a straight-line fill costs the heap 0 B (MEASURED, 8x8 and 760x300); a stroke of the
# closed 8x8 rect 2,720 B, all of it paths on the seam (3,232 B through 0.8.0, 34,432 B through
# 0.7.2 and 2,789,016 B on 0.5.4 — 0.8.0 opened the round stroker's piece paths at their exact
# capacity and 0.9.0 took the SdPoints out of them).
#
# Returns 1 when the scratch is ready, 0 when a block was refused.
# ⛔ ALL OR NOTHING, AND `_sd_fill_ectx != 0` IS THE FLAG. Through 0.7.0 the eight blocks were
# stored straight into the globals unchecked, so a refusal of the SECOND one left ectx non-zero and
# edges 0 — this function never ran again and EVERY fill for the rest of the process stored through
# a null edge list. The blocks now land in locals and are published together, so a partial set is
# abandoned (the bump allocator has no free()) and the next call retries from scratch.
# ⚠ These come from the GLOBAL `alloc`, never sd_alloc (the rule at the top of this block), so no
# consumer hook can refuse them: `alloc` refuses only over ALLOC_MAX = 2 GiB or when brk fails, and
# the largest block here is 262,144 B. ⇒ The one honest way to drive this path is the CAPACITY —
# SD_FLATTEN_CAP sizes five of the eight, so raising it past 268,435,456 makes each of those five a
# request the real allocator really refuses (programs/oom_test.cyr group J, the same device
# grow_edges_test uses on the growth ceiling).
# ⛔ WHAT THAT GATES, EXACTLY, because the eight 0-tests below are NOT eight gates. ALLOC_MAX
# refuses BY SIZE, and the capacity-sized five are CAP*32, TWO of CAP*16 and TWO of CAP*8 (the run
# joined the CAP*16 half in 0.9.0 — 16 B an inline point, not 8 B a pointer): group J's 300,000,000
# puts the SMALLEST of them over the 2 GiB line, so all five are refused together and nothing is
# mapped (MEASURED: exactly 3 * 24 B granted, five refused, nothing published). A capacity that
# isolated ONE — anything from 67,108,865 to 134,217,728 refuses only the CAP*32 edge list — would
# GRANT the other four, about 6 GiB of mappings at the top of that window (5 GiB through 0.8.0) that
# a consumer's machine would have to hand over for a test that never touches a byte of them, so it
# is not taken. The three 24 B ctx blocks are not drivable at any capacity: lib/alloc.cyr refuses
# 24 B only if mmap itself fails.
# ⇒ Deleting any ONE of the eight tests leaves the others setting `ok = 0`, so no suite can kill it:
# they are a CONTRACT on this function, not a gate, in the same sense as the 0 that
# _sd_fill_cross_for returns. What IS gated: that a refusal publishes nothing (check 124) and that a
# success publishes ALL ELEVEN globals (checks 128-138). And the shape a missing test would
# produce — a 0 published behind a status of 1 — cannot reach a store, because sd_fill_impl guards
# on _sd_fill_ectx itself rather than on this status.
# ⚠ The callers in stroke.cyr / dash.cyr discard this status too, and guard on the block they store
# through (_sd_stroke_run) exactly as sd_fill_impl guards on _sd_fill_ectx: since 0.7.2 all three
# stroke entry points return SADISH_ERR_OOM for a refused scratch instead of storing into it. On
# this allocator a failure here means the process could not get 262,144 B and is over before it.
fn _sd_scratch_init(): i64 {
    if (_sd_fill_ectx != 0) { return 1; }
    var ectx = alloc(24);
    var edges = alloc(SD_FLATTEN_CAP * 32);
    var fbuf = alloc(SD_FLATTEN_PCAP * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE);
    var fctx = alloc(24);
    var cross = alloc(SD_FLATTEN_CAP * 16);
    var srun = alloc(SD_FLATTEN_CAP * 16);      # 16 B a run point since 0.9.0, not 8
    var sfbuf = alloc(SD_FLATTEN_PCAP * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE);
    var sfctx = alloc(24);
    var ok = 1;
    if (ectx == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (edges == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (fbuf == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (fctx == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (cross == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (srun == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (sfbuf == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (sfctx == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (ok == 0) { return 0; }
    _sd_fill_ectx = ectx;
    _sd_fill_edges = edges;
    _sd_fill_edges_cap = SD_FLATTEN_CAP;
    _sd_fill_fbuf = fbuf;
    _sd_fill_fctx = fctx;
    _sd_fill_cross = cross;
    _sd_fill_cross_cap = SD_FLATTEN_CAP;
    _sd_stroke_run = srun;
    _sd_stroke_run_cap = SD_FLATTEN_CAP;
    _sd_stroke_fbuf = sfbuf;
    _sd_stroke_fctx = sfctx;
    return 1;
}

# The crossings array for walking a list of `n` edges (a sub-scanline crosses each edge at most
# once, so n slots always suffice). Doubles from the current capacity until it holds n; the old
# block is abandoned and NOT copied — crossings are rebuilt per sub-scanline, and nothing grows it
# while a walk is reading it (the fill's edge list and the stroke batch grow while they are BUILT).
# ⛔ So every walk must read _sd_fill_cross AFTER its list is complete, never cache it across a
# growth. Returns the block, or 0 if the growth failed (the old block and capacity are kept).
#
# ⛔ THE INVARIANT IT EXISTS FOR: _sd_fill_cross_cap >= the capacity of EVERY list that is walked
# (the fill's _sd_fill_edges_cap and the stroke batch's _sd_sb_cap). Nothing checks it at run time —
# it holds because both growers reserve HERE first and abandon their own growth if this one fails,
# and because every capacity climbs the same power-of-two ladder from SD_FLATTEN_CAP. ⚠ A caller
# that ignored the 0 would raise its own capacity above the crossings and the next walk would write
# past this block; with lib/alloc.cyr that cannot be reached from here (a bump allocator that
# refuses nc * 16 refuses the caller's nc * 32 too, so the caller fails at its own allocation
# either way) — but a future allocator with size classes could, so the 0 is a contract, not a
# formality. programs/grow_edges_test.cyr asserts the invariant after every growth it drives.
fn _sd_fill_cross_for(n): i64 {
    if (n <= _sd_fill_cross_cap) { return _sd_fill_cross; }
    var nc = _sd_fill_cross_cap;
    while (nc < n) { nc = nc * 2; }
    var blk = _sd_grow_alloc(nc * 16);
    if (blk == 0) { return 0; }
    _sd_fill_cross = blk;
    _sd_fill_cross_cap = nc;
    return blk;
}

# Double the fill's edge list in `ectx` (the crossings first, so crossings >= edges always holds),
# copying its live edges. Returns 1, or 0 if either allocation failed — the list is then unchanged.
# ⛔ ONLY THE FILL'S OWN ctx GROWS. Success repoints the process-lifetime _sd_fill_edges /
# _sd_fill_edges_cap at the new block, so growing a ctx that is not _sd_fill_ectx would hand the
# fill's scratch bookkeeping to someone else's array. A foreign ctx therefore returns 0 here and its
# caller drops the edge, exactly as 0.6.0 did at the cap — the same guard _sd_flat_widen puts on
# sd_flat_emit's ctx, for the same reason. (Every in-tree caller passes _sd_fill_ectx.)
fn _sd_fill_edges_grow(ectx): i64 {
    if (ectx != _sd_fill_ectx) { return 0; }
    var count = load64(ectx + 8);
    var nc = load64(ectx + 16) * 2;
    if (_sd_fill_cross_for(nc) == 0) { return 0; }
    var blk = _sd_grow_alloc(nc * 32);
    if (blk == 0) { return 0; }
    memcpy(blk, load64(ectx + 0), count * 32);
    store64(ectx + 0, blk);
    store64(ectx + 16, nc);
    _sd_fill_edges = blk;
    _sd_fill_edges_cap = nc;
    return 1;
}

# The per-row accumulator for a canvas `w` pixels wide. Its size is the only part of the scratch
# that depends on the call, so it keeps a capacity and re-allocates from the GLOBAL alloc only
# when a wider canvas than ever seen arrives. ⚠ The old block is abandoned (no free()) — a
# monotone series of ever-wider canvases pays sum(w*8) once; a consumer at a fixed size pays once.
# Returns the block, or 0 if the growth failed — THE OLD BLOCK AND CAPACITY ARE KEPT, exactly as
# _sd_fill_cross_for keeps its own.
# ⛔ WHY THE ROLLBACK IS THE WHOLE POINT, not tidiness. Through 0.7.0 the result was stored
# unchecked and the capacity raised anyway, so ONE refused request POISONED the scratch for the
# life of the process: accrow 0 with a capacity of w, and the very next fill — on any narrower
# canvas, which now "fits" — wrote its accumulator through address 0. MEASURED on 0.7.0, a
# hand-built 536,870,912-px-wide header (w*8 = 4 GiB, over lib/alloc.cyr's ALLOC_MAX of 2 GiB):
# the fill of it SIGSEGVs, rc 139, and with the store moved past it the following 8x8 fill does.
# Now the refusal is reported, sd_fill_impl returns SADISH_ERR_OOM having touched no coverage, and
# every later fill still has the accumulator it had (programs/oom_test.cyr group H).
# ⚠ THE OTHER CALLER — stroke.cyr's styled span walk (_sd_sb_flush) — tests this 0 since 0.7.2 and
# returns SADISH_ERR_OOM out of all three stroke entry points; it faulted on a refusal through
# 0.7.1, which is what this note used to record (programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr group F).
fn _sd_fill_accrow_for(w): i64 {
    if (w <= _sd_fill_accrow_cap) { return _sd_fill_accrow; }
    var blk = alloc(w * 8);
    if (blk == 0) { return 0; }
    _sd_fill_accrow = blk;
    _sd_fill_accrow_cap = w;
    return blk;
}

# ---- fill internals --------------------------------------------
# Vertical sub-scanlines per pixel row. Coverage is ANALYTIC in x (exact
# horizontal span overlap per sub-scanline) and sampled in y at SD_FILL_SS
# sub-scanlines — smoother AND far cheaper than the old SS*SS point sampling
# (edge crossings are computed per row, not per pixel). This is the SD_AA_SUBSCANLINE engine and
# stays the default; full 2-axis signed-area accumulation is SD_AA_AREA (src/coverage.cyr).
var SD_FILL_SS = 4;

# Edge builder ctx: +0 edges(ptr, 4 i64/edge: ax,ay,bx,by)  +8 count  +16 cap. `ectx` is the
# fill's _sd_fill_ectx: a full list grows (_sd_fill_edges_grow) — and ONLY that ctx does, so any
# other one keeps its own fixed capacity and drops at it, as every ctx did through 0.6.0.
# Horizontal edges (ay == by)
# contribute no crossings and are dropped. ⚠ An edge that does not fit after a FAILED growth is
# dropped and _sd_fill_trunc set — and once set, this fill stops trying to grow, so the list it
# keeps is a prefix of the path's edges (0.6.0's truncation) rather than a list with holes.
fn sd_edge_add(ectx, ax, ay, bx, by): i64 {
    if (ay == by) { return 0; }
    var count = load64(ectx + 8);
    if (count >= load64(ectx + 16)) {
        if (_sd_fill_trunc != 0) { return 0; }
        if (_sd_fill_edges_grow(ectx) == 0) {
            _sd_fill_trunc = 1;
            return 0;
        }
    }
    var e = load64(ectx + 0) + count * 32;
    store64(e + 0, ax);
    store64(e + 8, ay);
    store64(e + 16, bx);
    store64(e + 24, by);
    store64(ectx + 8, count + 1);
    return 0;
}

# Connect (cx, cy) through the points a flatten ctx just emitted, as edges;
# returns the last point as the pair (x, y) — the new current point, or the argument back when the
# ctx emitted nothing.
# ⚠ COORDINATES IN AND OUT (0.9.0): it took and returned an SdPoint POINTER, which the fill's verb
# walk no longer has for a path anchor. ⭐ AND SINCE 0.10.0 THE CTX'S OWN POINTS ARE COORDINATES
# TOO — this loop used to load a pointer per point and call sd_point_x / _y through it; it now reads
# the two words in place, one dependent load fewer per point and nothing to fault on.
fn sd_fill_edges_from(ectx, fctx, cx, cy): (i64, i64) {
    var pts = load64(fctx + 0);
    var n = load64(fctx + 8);
    var prevx = cx;
    var prevy = cy;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        var px = load64(pts + i * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET);
        var py = load64(pts + i * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET);
        sd_edge_add(ectx, prevx, prevy, px, py);
        prevx = px;
        prevy = py;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return (prevx, prevy);
}

# Cast a +x ray from (sx, sy) and accumulate edge crossings to its right.
# `signed` = 1 -> signed winding number (nonzero rule); 0 -> crossing count
# (even-odd rule). Half-open [ylo, yhi) span test avoids double-counting a
# vertex shared by two edges.
fn sd_ray_cross(edges, n_edges, sx, sy, signed): i64 {
    var acc = 0;
    var ei = 0;
    while (ei < n_edges) {
        var e = edges + ei * 32;
        var ax = load64(e + 0);
        var ay = load64(e + 8);
        var bx = load64(e + 16);
        var by = load64(e + 24);
        var ylo = ay;
        var yhi = by;
        if (ay > by) { ylo = by; yhi = ay; }
        if (sy >= ylo) {
            if (sy < yhi) {
                var x = ax + ((bx - ax) * (sy - ay)) / (by - ay);
                if (x > sx) {
                    if (signed != 0) {
                        if (by > ay) { acc = acc + 1; } else { acc = acc - 1; }
                    } else {
                        acc = acc + 1;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        ei = ei + 1;
    }
    return acc;
}

# Add analytic horizontal coverage for a covered span [xs, xe] (16.16 pixel-x)
# to the per-row accumulator `accrow`, weighted by `weight` (coverage per full
# pixel for one sub-scanline). Each touched pixel gets weight * overlap-width.
fn sd_span_add(accrow, w, xs, xe, weight): i64 {
    if (xe <= xs) { return 0; }
    var lo = xs;
    if (lo < 0) { lo = 0; }
    var wmax = w << SD_SHIFT;
    var hi = xe;
    if (hi > wmax) { hi = wmax; }
    if (hi <= lo) { return 0; }
    var px = lo >> SD_SHIFT;
    var stop = 0;
    while (stop == 0) {
        if (px >= w) {
            stop = 1;
        } else {
            var pl = px << SD_SHIFT;
            if (pl >= hi) {
                stop = 1;
            } else {
                var pr = (px + 1) << SD_SHIFT;
                var a = lo;
                if (a < pl) { a = pl; }
                var b = hi;
                if (b > pr) { b = pr; }
                if (b > a) {
                    var add = sd_fixed_mul(weight, b - a);
                    store64(accrow + px * 8, load64(accrow + px * 8) + add);
                }
                px = px + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_fill_path — rasterize `path` into the canvas coverage buffer
# under winding rule `rule` (SD_FILL_EVEN_ODD | SD_FILL_NONZERO), writing
# 0..255 anti-aliased coverage per pixel (overwrites the buffer).
#
# Pipeline: flatten curve verbs (de Casteljau) into a CLOSED edge list —
# each subpath is closed back to its moveto — then, per pixel, sample an
# SS*SS grid and cast a +x winding ray per sub-sample; coverage is the
# fraction of sub-samples inside.
# ================================================================
# @public — fill `path`'s AA coverage into `cv`, OVERWRITING the buffer.
fn sd_canvas_fill_path(cv, path, rule): i64 {
    return sd_fill_impl(cv, path, rule, 0);
}

# @public — fill `path`'s AA coverage, MAX-combined into the existing coverage
# (a union of shapes — orientation-independent for convex pieces; used by the
# stroker to accumulate overlapping segment rects + join discs).
fn sd_canvas_fill_union(cv, path, rule): i64 {
    return sd_fill_impl(cv, path, rule, 1);
}

# combine: 0 = overwrite the coverage buffer, 1 = max into it (union).
fn sd_fill_impl(cv, path, rule, combine): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    var n_verbs = sd_path_verb_count(path);
    if (n_verbs == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }

    var w = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var h = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var cov = sd_canvas_coverage(cv);
    var stride = sd_canvas_stride(cv);
    var tol = SD_ONE >> 2;

    # ---- build the closed-edge list (into the process-lifetime scratch) ----
    # ⚠ A refused scratch is SADISH_ERR_OOM before a byte of coverage is touched, so the canvas is
    # exactly as the caller left it. (Reachable only through the CAPACITY — see _sd_scratch_init.)
    # ⛔ THE GUARD IS ON THE POINTER, NOT ON _sd_scratch_init's STATUS, and that is deliberate.
    # _sd_fill_ectx is both the scratch's "already initialised" flag and the block the next line
    # stores through, so testing it covers the refusal (the publish is all-or-nothing, so a refused
    # set leaves it 0) AND anything that ever published a 0 behind a status of 1 — the one residual
    # shape _sd_scratch_init's own 0-tests cannot be driven to prove. A status check would catch
    # only the first. (programs/oom_test.cyr group J drives both through this line.)
    _sd_scratch_init();
    var ectx = _sd_fill_ectx;
    if (ectx == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    store64(ectx + 0, _sd_fill_edges);      # the CURRENT block: a previous fill may have grown it
    store64(ectx + 8, 0);               # per-call reset: the edge count
    store64(ectx + 16, _sd_fill_edges_cap);
    _sd_fill_trunc = 0;
    var fbuf = _sd_fill_fbuf;
    var fctx = _sd_fill_fctx;           # fields are (re)set before every flatten below

    # ⛔ A STARVED FLATTEN IS REPORTED, NOT PAINTED OVER. sd_flatten_quad / _cubic emit their points
    # through sd_alloc, so a consumer hook that refuses mid-curve leaves the fill with FEWER points than
    # the curve needs — a silently wrong picture. Through 0.7.0 that case did not arise here (the
    # refusal dereferenced 0 and killed the process); 0.7.1 made it survivable, and this makes it
    # VISIBLE: the flatten's own truncation flag is scoped to this call, and if this fill lost a point
    # it returns SADISH_ERR_OOM. MEASURED (programs/flatten_bound_test.cyr group M): a 4-cubic circle
    # whose true ink is 312,280 coverage units paints 81,029 under a hook granting 5 allocations.
    # ⚠ The canvas still holds that partial picture — the caller decides whether to clear and retry.
    # ⚠ The flag is RESTORED for the caller afterwards (sd_flatten_truncated() is documented as sticky
    # until the next sd_path_flatten), so scoping it here cannot swallow a verdict the caller was
    # already holding.
    # ⚠ Strokes were NOT covered by this return through 0.7.1 — the styled path has its own flush and
    # the round stroker discarded sd_canvas_fill_union's result, so a starved stroke returned
    # SADISH_OK over a partial picture. Since 0.7.2 each stroke entry scopes the verdict the same way
    # and returns SADISH_ERR_OOM itself (src/stroke.cyr's refusal block).
    # ⛔ A DRAWING VERB BEFORE ANY MOVETO IS SKIPPED, not drawn from (0, 0). Through 0.8.0 `cur` was
    # an SdPoint POINTER, 0 until the first moveto, and every drawing verb dereferenced it — so a
    # path whose first verb is a lineto/quadto/cubicto used to fault the process (MEASURED on 0.7.0
    # and 0.6.0 alike: SIGSEGV, rc 139). SVG calls such a path an error and renders nothing of it;
    # sadish skips the verbs (their points are still consumed, so the verb/point streams stay in
    # step) until a moveto arrives. ⚠ 0.7.1 made this easy to reach: a refused sd_path_moveto
    # returns SADISH_ERR_OOM with the path untouched, so a caller that logs and carries on builds
    # exactly this path. ⚠ AND SINCE 0.9.0 THE TEST IS A FLAG, `has_cur`, because the current point
    # is now a COORDINATE PAIR and 0 is the origin — see the walk below.
    # ⛔⛔ ONE FLATTEN OPERATION FOR THE WHOLE FILL (0.7.2) — the budget bounds this CALL, not one
    # curve of it. 0.7.1 gave sd_path_flatten a per-operation budget in points
    # (SD_FLATTEN_BUDGET_DEFAULT = 65,536, src/path.cyr) and left the fill flattening ONE CURVE VERB
    # at a time into fbuf, each call its own operation — so an untrusted outline got one budget PER
    # CURVE and the fill's work stayed linear in the curve count, which is the severity case of
    # issues/archived/2026-09-15-flatten-keeps-subdividing-and-allocating-after-the-output-cap-is-full.md
    # (rekha → dhancha → crab fill glyph outlines from font files, every frame). MEASURED on the
    # filing's own repro, 4,096 maximally non-flat quads filled on 64x64: 16,711,680 B in 1,044,480
    # sd_alloc calls per curve-scoped fill, 1,044,480 B in 65,280 with the fill scoped — 16x, and it
    # no longer grows with the curve count (1,024 quads costs the same as 4,096). A consumer had to
    # KNOW to wrap the call in sd_flatten_op_begin/_end to get that; now it is the default, and
    # sd_flatten_degraded() answers for the fill itself.
    # ⚠ THE TRADE, AND THE MEASUREMENTS IT RESTS ON: a path whose ONE fill emits more than the budget
    # now degrades its remaining curves to CHORDS mid-fill (and says so) where it used to tessellate
    # them whole. Points emitted by one fill, MEASURED on this tree at the fill's own tolerance
    # (SD_ONE >> 2) — sd_alloc calls under a counting hook plus one path-owned endpoint per curve:
    #   a circle of 4 cubics, radius 8 px to 4,096 px               16 →    256
    #   a rounded rect, 200x100 r=16 to 1920x1080 r=240             16 →     64
    #   a 64-cubic blob at radius 1,024 px                                1,536
    #   the curve paths of these suites (area, stroke_style, mixed)  14 →     26
    #   grow_edges_test's cubic_ring(3000), the largest suite path        8,600
    #   one synthetic ASCII glyph, ~13 px em to ~2,048 px em         60 →    960
    #   all 95 of them as ONE path (a whole label, one fill)      2,396 → 26,394
    # ⇒ The largest legitimate fill anywhere in this repo or its glyph corpus spends 40 % of the
    # budget, the largest single glyph 1.5 %, a UI shape under 3 %. The default degrades a
    # ONE-PATH label at about 1,889 glyphs at a 32 px em, 649 at 208 px, 236 at 2,048 px — per PATH,
    # not per draw, and a consumer that really fills such a path raises sd_flatten_budget_set (0 =
    # unbounded, 0.7.1's behaviour) and reads sd_flatten_degraded() to know it mattered.
    # ⛔ THE OPERATION OPENS LAZILY, AT THE FIRST CURVE THIS FILL ACTUALLY FLATTENS, and that is not
    # tidiness: sd_flatten_degraded()'s contract (src/path.cyr) is that a fill of a path with NO
    # curve verbs opens no operation and leaves whichever verdict was already standing. Opening
    # unconditionally here would clear a consumer's verdict on every rectangle it draws. Gated by
    # programs/flatten_bound_test.cyr group O.
    var flat_was = _sd_flat_trunc;      # the caller's standing verdict, restored below
    _sd_flat_trunc = 0;                 # scoped: from here it means "THIS fill lost points"
    var fop = 0;                        # 1 once this fill has opened its flatten operation
    # ⚠ The verb stream is read raw (one i64 a verb, unchanged since 0.4.0); the POINTS go through
    # sd_path_point_x / _y, the read src/path.cyr publishes — this walk is the in-tree example of
    # it, and the reason the points array is not loaded here at all any more.
    var verbs = load64(path + SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET);
    # ⛔ `has_cur` IS THE SENTINEL NOW (0.9.0), NOT `cur != 0`. A path's points are inline
    # coordinates, so the current point is a pair of numbers and (0, 0) is the ORIGIN — a real
    # place to draw from. Through 0.8.0 `cur` was an SdPoint pointer and 0 could only mean "no
    # moveto yet"; keeping that test on a coordinate would skip every verb of a path that starts at
    # the origin. src/path.cyr's flatten walk and both of stroke.cyr's carry the same flag.
    var curx = 0;
    var cury = 0;
    var has_cur = 0;
    var subx = 0;
    var suby = 0;
    var sub_open = 0;
    var vi = 0;
    var pi = 0;
    while (vi < n_verbs) {
        var verb = load64(verbs + vi * 8);
        if (verb == SD_VERB_MOVETO) {
            if (sub_open != 0) {
                sd_edge_add(ectx, curx, cury, subx, suby);
            }
            curx = sd_path_point_x(path, pi);
            cury = sd_path_point_y(path, pi);
            has_cur = 1;
            subx = curx;
            suby = cury;
            sub_open = 0;
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_LINETO) {
            if (has_cur != 0) {
                var lx = sd_path_point_x(path, pi);
                var ly = sd_path_point_y(path, pi);
                sd_edge_add(ectx, curx, cury, lx, ly);
                curx = lx;
                cury = ly;
                sub_open = 1;
            }
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_QUADTO) {
            store64(fctx + 0, fbuf);
            store64(fctx + 8, 0);
            store64(fctx + 16, SD_FLATTEN_PCAP);
            if (has_cur != 0) {     # no moveto yet: skip the curve, keep the streams in step
                if (fop == 0) {
                    sd_flatten_op_begin();
                    fop = 1;
                }
                _sd_flatten_quad_xy(fctx, curx, cury, sd_path_point_x(path, pi),
                  sd_path_point_y(path, pi), sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 1),
                  sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 1), tol, SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
                var qlx, qly = sd_fill_edges_from(ectx, fctx, curx, cury);
                curx = qlx;
                cury = qly;
                sub_open = 1;
            }
            pi = pi + 2;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_CUBICTO) {
            store64(fctx + 0, fbuf);
            store64(fctx + 8, 0);
            store64(fctx + 16, SD_FLATTEN_PCAP);
            if (has_cur != 0) {     # no moveto yet: skip the curve, keep the streams in step
                if (fop == 0) {
                    sd_flatten_op_begin();
                    fop = 1;
                }
                _sd_flatten_cubic_xy(fctx, curx, cury, sd_path_point_x(path, pi),
                  sd_path_point_y(path, pi), sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 1),
                  sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 1), sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 2),
                  sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 2), tol, SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
                var klx, kly = sd_fill_edges_from(ectx, fctx, curx, cury);
                curx = klx;
                cury = kly;
                sub_open = 1;
            }
            pi = pi + 3;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_CLOSE) {
            if (has_cur != 0) {
                sd_edge_add(ectx, curx, cury, subx, suby);
                curx = subx;
                cury = suby;
                sub_open = 0;
            }
        }
        vi = vi + 1;
    }
    # The walk is over, so the last flatten has happened: close the operation the first curve opened.
    # ⚠ Nothing between the open and here returns, so the nesting depth cannot leak (the returns that
    # DO exist — an empty edge list, a refused accumulator, the AREA dispatch — are all below).
    if (fop != 0) { sd_flatten_op_end(); }
    if (sub_open != 0) {
        sd_edge_add(ectx, curx, cury, subx, suby);
    }

    var flat_lost = _sd_flat_trunc;     # did THIS fill's flatten lose points?
    if (flat_was != 0) { _sd_flat_trunc = 1; }
    var flat_rc = SADISH_OK;
    if (flat_lost != 0) { flat_rc = SADISH_ERR_OOM; }

    var n_edges = load64(ectx + 8);
    var edges = load64(ectx + 0);
    if (n_edges == 0) { return flat_rc; }

    # ---- SD_AA_AREA: exact 2-axis coverage (src/coverage.cyr), opt-in per canvas ----
    # ⛔ Only a canvas holding exactly SD_AA_AREA branches here; every other value runs the 0.4.0
    # loop below, untouched — the bytes agnos's refagree oracle is gated on.
    # ⛔ AND ONLY WHEN THE EDGE LIST IS WHOLE. An edge dropped by sd_edge_add leaves a contour
    # OPEN. The loop below paints nothing right of a row's last crossing, so an open contour costs
    # it the shape and no more; the area engine's running sum would stay non-zero to the canvas's
    # right edge — a streak (MEASURED, 80x40, a triangle losing its closing edge: 28 of 40 rows lit
    # at x = 79, 255 at (60, 20)). Since 0.7.0 the list grows, so an edge is dropped only when a
    # growth FAILED, which sd_edge_add records: a truncated AREA fill runs the loop below instead
    # (programs/grow_edges_test.cyr) — ⚠ at that loop's cost, which for a list that large is steep
    # (MEASURED 256x256, 8,000 edges: 10.9 s a fill, against the area engine's 41 ms). Through
    # 0.6.0 a list cannot tell "exactly full" from "truncated", and ANY AREA fill of 8,192 edges ran
    # the loop.
    if (load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_AA_OFFSET) == SD_AA_AREA) {
        if (_sd_fill_trunc == 0) {
            var nonzero = 0;
            if (rule == SD_FILL_NONZERO) { nonzero = 1; }
            var mask = 0;
            var clipn = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET);
            if (clipn != 0) { mask = load64(clipn + 0); }
            var arc = _sd_area_fill(edges, n_edges, w, h, cov, stride, mask, nonzero, combine);
            if (arc != SADISH_OK) { return arc; }
            return flat_rc;
        }
    }

    # ---- analytic-horizontal coverage, SD_FILL_SS vertical sub-scanlines ----
    var want_signed = 0;
    if (rule == SD_FILL_NONZERO) { want_signed = 1; }
    var ssy = SD_FILL_SS;
    var weight = SD_ONE / ssy;         # coverage per full pixel per sub-scanline
    var accrow = _sd_fill_accrow_for(w);   # per-row fractional coverage (16.16); zeroed per row below
    # ⚠ A refused accumulator is SADISH_ERR_OOM here, BEFORE the py/px loops — the coverage buffer
    # is untouched, so a caller that retries on a narrower canvas gets a clean fill (the capacity
    # was not raised; see _sd_fill_accrow_for).
    if (accrow == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    var cross = _sd_fill_cross;            # (x, dir) crossings per sub-scanline; only [0, nc) is read
    # ^ read AFTER the list is built: a growth above replaced the block, and its capacity >= n_edges

    # ⭐⭐ BOUND THE ROW LOOP TO THE PATH (0.11.2). This swept py = 0 .. h for EVERY fill, and for
    # each row outside the shape it still zeroed the whole accumulator, rescanned every edge ssy
    # times, and wrote every pixel. A small path on a big canvas paid for the canvas, not the path —
    # and the ROUND STROKER calls this once per segment and per vertex, so a stroke paid for the
    # whole canvas per piece. MEASURED before: a 60-segment zigzag on 128x96 took 14.7 ms.
    # ⇒ Take the edge list's own y extent, widen it by a row each way (a sub-scanline near a
    # boundary may belong to the neighbouring pixel row), and clamp to the canvas.
    var ry0 = 0;
    var ry1 = h;
    if (n_edges > 0) {
        var eymin = load64(edges + 8);
        var eymax = eymin;
        var eq = 0;
        while (eq < n_edges) {
            var qa = load64(edges + eq * 32 + 8);
            var qb = load64(edges + eq * 32 + 24);
            if (qa < eymin) { eymin = qa; }
            if (qa > eymax) { eymax = qa; }
            if (qb < eymin) { eymin = qb; }
            if (qb > eymax) { eymax = qb; }
            eq = eq + 1;
        }
        ry0 = sd_asr(eymin, SD_SHIFT) - 1;
        ry1 = sd_asr(eymax, SD_SHIFT) + 2;
        if (ry0 < 0) { ry0 = 0; }
        if (ry1 > h) { ry1 = h; }
        if (ry1 < ry0) { ry1 = ry0; }
    } else {
        ry1 = 0;
    }

    # ⛔ THE ROWS OUTSIDE ARE NOT SIMPLY SKIPPABLE, AND THE TWO MODES DIFFER. With combine != 0
    # (sd_canvas_fill_union) an empty row resolves to `c < oldc` and stores oldc back — a provable
    # no-op, so it is skipped outright. With combine == 0 (sd_canvas_fill_path) an empty row stores
    # ZERO, which is a real clear of whatever the canvas held, so it must still happen — but it is
    # a flat zero fill of the row, not a per-pixel walk through the clip test (c = 0 makes the clip
    # multiply 0 either way). Getting this wrong would leave stale coverage outside the shape, which
    # is why it is spelled out rather than folded into the skip.
    if (combine == 0) {
        var cy = 0;
        while (cy < h) {
            if (cy < ry0) {
                memset(cov + cy * stride, 0, w);
            } else {
                if (cy >= ry1) { memset(cov + cy * stride, 0, w); }
            }
            cy = cy + 1;
        }
    }

    var py = ry0;
    while (py < ry1) {
        var zi = 0;
        while (zi < w) {
            store64(accrow + zi * 8, 0);
            zi = zi + 1;
        }

        var s = 0;
        while (s < ssy) {
            var sy = (py << SD_SHIFT) + (s * SD_ONE + (SD_ONE >> 1)) / ssy;
            # gather crossings at this sub-scanline
            var nc = 0;
            var ei = 0;
            while (ei < n_edges) {
                var e = edges + ei * 32;
                var ay = load64(e + 8);
                var by = load64(e + 24);
                var ylo = ay;
                var yhi = by;
                var dir = 1;
                if (ay > by) { ylo = by; yhi = ay; dir = 0 - 1; }
                if (sy >= ylo) {
                    if (sy < yhi) {
                        var ax = load64(e + 0);
                        var bx = load64(e + 16);
                        var xx = ax + ((bx - ax) * (sy - ay)) / (by - ay);
                        store64(cross + nc * 16, xx);
                        store64(cross + nc * 16 + 8, dir);
                        nc = nc + 1;
                    }
                }
                ei = ei + 1;
            }
            # insertion sort crossings by x
            # ⭐ SHELL SORT, NOT INSERTION (0.11.2). This was O(nc^2) per sub-scanline, and nc is
            # the number of edges a scanline crosses — small for a convex shape, LARGE for a
            # self-intersecting one, which is exactly what an untrusted outline can be. The gap
            # sequence collapses the long shifts insertion sort makes when a crossing lands far
            # from its place; at small nc the final gap == 1 pass IS insertion sort, so the cheap
            # case stays cheap.
            # ⚠ SHELL SORT IS NOT STABLE AND INSERTION SORT WAS. That is safe HERE and only here:
            # two crossings with equal x bound a ZERO-WIDTH span, so whichever order they take, the
            # winding on the far side of the pair is the same and no pixel lies between them. The
            # 340-measurement oracle and both stroke suites are the proof, not this paragraph.
            var gap = nc / 2;
            while (gap > 0) {
                var a = gap;
                while (a < nc) {
                    var kx = load64(cross + a * 16);
                    var kd = load64(cross + a * 16 + 8);
                    var b = a;
                    var stop = 0;
                    while (stop == 0) {
                        if (b < gap) {
                            stop = 1;
                        } else {
                            if (load64(cross + (b - gap) * 16) > kx) {
                                store64(cross + b * 16, load64(cross + (b - gap) * 16));
                                store64(cross + b * 16 + 8, load64(cross + (b - gap) * 16 + 8));
                                b = b - gap;
                            } else {
                                stop = 1;
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    store64(cross + b * 16, kx);
                    store64(cross + b * 16 + 8, kd);
                    a = a + 1;
                }
                gap = gap / 2;
            }
            # walk crossings -> covered spans -> analytic horizontal coverage
            var wind = 0;
            var ci = 0;
            while (ci < nc - 1) {
                if (want_signed != 0) {
                    wind = wind + load64(cross + ci * 16 + 8);
                } else {
                    wind = wind + 1;
                }
                var inside = 0;
                if (want_signed != 0) {
                    if (wind != 0) { inside = 1; }
                } else {
                    if ((wind & 1) != 0) { inside = 1; }
                }
                if (inside != 0) {
                    sd_span_add(accrow, w, load64(cross + ci * 16), load64(cross + (ci + 1) * 16), weight);
                }
                ci = ci + 1;
            }
            s = s + 1;
        }

        # accumulator row -> coverage bytes (with clip + combine)
        # ⭐ 0.11.2: the clip node AND its mask pointer were re-loaded PER PIXEL — two dependent
        # loads per pixel for a value that cannot change during a fill. Hoisted to the row; the
        # mask's row base is folded in too, so the inner loop indexes `clipbase + px`.
        var clipnode = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET);
        var clipbase = 0;
        if (clipnode != 0) {
            # ⛔ PACKED `py * w` — the mask is a w*h block, NOT a window into a wider buffer, so
            # the canvas stride is not its pitch (see the SdCanvas layout note).
            clipbase = load64(clipnode + 0) + py * w;
        }
        var px = 0;
        while (px < w) {
            var cvg = load64(accrow + px * 8);
            var c = (cvg * 255) >> SD_SHIFT;
            if (c > 255) { c = 255; }
            if (clipbase != 0) {
                c = (c * load8(clipbase + px)) / 255;
            }
            if (combine != 0) {
                var oldc = load8(cov + py * stride + px);   # coverage: the STRIDE, not w
                if (c < oldc) { c = oldc; }
            }
            store8(cov + py * stride + px, c);
            px = px + 1;
        }
        py = py + 1;
    }
    return flat_rc;
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_blit_at — composite the canvas coverage onto an SdSurface `surface`
# with the canvas's (0,0) placed at surface pixel (dx, dy), using the solid
# `color` (0x00RRGGBB), the per-pixel coverage acting as the source alpha
# (src-over): out = src*cov/255 + dst*(255-cov)/255. (dx, dy) may be negative
# or beyond the surface — the blit is clipped to the surface on every side.
#
# ⚠ Rows are addressed by `sd_surface_stride(surface)`, NOT width*4. A surface
# that WRAPS a wider buffer (dhancha's dh_surface_wrap over a framebuffer or a
# sub-rect of a pane) has stride != width*4, and the pre-0.5.5 sd_canvas_blit
# used `sw` as the pitch — right for every packed surface sadish makes itself,
# and one row's worth of shear per row on a wrapped one. Same src-over math
# as before, verbatim; only the addressing changed.
#
# Gradient paint (linear / radial, multi-stop) is sd_canvas_blit_paint_at in src/paint.cyr (0.6.0).
# TODO: a whole-canvas fast path routed through the AGNOS blit#39.
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sd_canvas_blit_at(cv, surface, color, dx, dy): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (surface == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var cw = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var ch = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var covbuf = sd_canvas_coverage(cv);
    var covstride = sd_canvas_stride(cv);
    var sw = sd_surface_width(surface);
    var sh = sd_surface_height(surface);
    var spx = sd_surface_pixels(surface);
    var pitch = sd_surface_stride(surface);

    var sr = (color >> 16) & 255;
    var sg = (color >> 8) & 255;
    var sb = color & 255;

    # Clip the canvas rect [dx, dx+cw) x [dy, dy+ch) to the surface: x0..x1 / y0..y1
    # are CANVAS coordinates of the visible part.
    var x0 = 0;
    if (dx < 0) { x0 = 0 - dx; }
    var y0 = 0;
    if (dy < 0) { y0 = 0 - dy; }
    var x1 = cw;
    if (dx + x1 > sw) { x1 = sw - dx; }
    var y1 = ch;
    if (dy + y1 > sh) { y1 = sh - dy; }

    var y = y0;
    while (y < y1) {
        var x = x0;
        while (x < x1) {
            var a = load8(covbuf + y * covstride + x);
            if (a != 0) {
                var off = (y + dy) * pitch + (x + dx) * 4;
                if (a == 255) {
                    store8(spx + off, sb);
                    store8(spx + off + 1, sg);
                    store8(spx + off + 2, sr);
                    store8(spx + off + 3, 255);
                } else {
                    var ia = 255 - a;
                    var nb = (sb * a + load8(spx + off) * ia) / 255;
                    var ng = (sg * a + load8(spx + off + 1) * ia) / 255;
                    var nr = (sr * a + load8(spx + off + 2) * ia) / 255;
                    store8(spx + off, nb);
                    store8(spx + off + 1, ng);
                    store8(spx + off + 2, nr);
                    store8(spx + off + 3, 255);
                }
            }
            x = x + 1;
        }
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — sd_canvas_blit_at with the canvas at the surface origin. On a packed
# surface (stride == width*4) the bytes are identical to the pre-0.5.5 blit
# (asserted pixel-for-pixel in programs/alloc_test.cyr).
fn sd_canvas_blit(cv, surface, color): i64 {
    return sd_canvas_blit_at(cv, surface, color, 0, 0);
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_blit_gradient — like sd_canvas_blit, but the source color is a
# LINEAR GRADIENT from `c0` (at pixel (x0,y0)) to `c1` (at (x1,y1)): each
# pixel's color is c0..c1 lerped by its projection onto the axis (clamped to
# the endpoints), then src-over with coverage. Colors 0x00RRGGBB; endpoints in
# whole pixels. A degenerate axis (x0,y0 == x1,y1) paints solid c0.
# ⚠ Kept byte-identical for existing callers. Radial + multi-stop + spread, sampled at pixel
# CENTRES with 16.16 endpoints, is the SdGradient paint in src/paint.cyr (0.6.0).
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sd_canvas_blit_gradient(cv, surface, x0, y0, c0, x1, y1, c1): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (surface == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var cw = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var ch = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var covbuf = sd_canvas_coverage(cv);
    var covstride = sd_canvas_stride(cv);
    var sw = sd_surface_width(surface);
    var sh = sd_surface_height(surface);
    var spx = sd_surface_pixels(surface);
    var pitch = sd_surface_stride(surface);   # ⚠ row pitch in bytes, not sw*4 (see sd_canvas_blit_at)

    var dx = x1 - x0;
    var dy = y1 - y0;
    var td = dx * dx + dy * dy;   # squared axis length
    var r0 = (c0 >> 16) & 255;
    var g0 = (c0 >> 8) & 255;
    var b0 = c0 & 255;
    var r1 = (c1 >> 16) & 255;
    var g1 = (c1 >> 8) & 255;
    var b1 = c1 & 255;

    var w = cw;
    if (sw < w) { w = sw; }
    var h = ch;
    if (sh < h) { h = sh; }

    var y = 0;
    while (y < h) {
        var x = 0;
        while (x < w) {
            var a = load8(covbuf + y * covstride + x);
            if (a != 0) {
                var sr = r0;
                var sg = g0;
                var sb = b0;
                if (td > 0) {
                    var tn = (x - x0) * dx + (y - y0) * dy;
                    if (tn < 0) { tn = 0; }
                    if (tn > td) { tn = td; }
                    sr = r0 + ((r1 - r0) * tn) / td;
                    sg = g0 + ((g1 - g0) * tn) / td;
                    sb = b0 + ((b1 - b0) * tn) / td;
                }
                var off = y * pitch + x * 4;
                if (a == 255) {
                    store8(spx + off, sb);
                    store8(spx + off + 1, sg);
                    store8(spx + off + 2, sr);
                    store8(spx + off + 3, 255);
                } else {
                    var ia = 255 - a;
                    var nb = (sb * a + load8(spx + off) * ia) / 255;
                    var ng = (sg * a + load8(spx + off + 1) * ia) / 255;
                    var nr = (sr * a + load8(spx + off + 2) * ia) / 255;
                    store8(spx + off, nb);
                    store8(spx + off + 1, ng);
                    store8(spx + off + 2, nr);
                    store8(spx + off + 3, 255);
                }
            }
            x = x + 1;
        }
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# ---- clipping (v0.4.0) -----------------------------------------
# A clip is a coverage MASK (0..255 per pixel), the top of a stack held on the
# canvas (SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET). fill_impl multiplies each pixel's coverage by
# the active mask, so fills + strokes are bounded to the clip region; blit then
# composites the already-clipped coverage. Push intersects (multiplies) a new
# shape into the current clip; pop restores the previous region.
# SdClip node (16 bytes): +0 mask(ptr, w*h)  +8 prev(SdClip | 0).
# ⛔ The mask is PACKED w*h — `y * w + x` — on a canvas of any stride. Every writer below and the
# three readers (sd_fill_impl, _sd_area_fill, _sd_sb_flush) index it that way; see the SdCanvas
# layout note for why a canvas's stride is the COVERAGE buffer's pitch and never the mask's.

# Intersect `shape` (a PACKED w*h mask) with the current clip IN PLACE, then push it.
# Returns SADISH_OK; SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null canvas; SADISH_ERR_OOM for a null `shape` (the
# caller's own mask allocation was refused — passed straight through so a caller may chain) or for
# a refused 16 B node.
# ⛔ THE NODE IS TAKEN BEFORE THE INTERSECTION, and that order is the refusal contract. Through
# 0.7.0 the node was stored through unchecked (rekha's filing); checking it AFTER the in-place
# multiply would have stopped the fault but still left the caller's `shape` half-consumed —
# multiplied by a clip it was never pushed under, so a caller that retried would intersect twice.
# With the allocation first, a refusal touches NOTHING: the canvas's clip pointer, the mask under
# it and the caller's `shape` are byte-for-byte what they were, the previous clip is still poppable,
# and pushing again once memory is available lands the same region it always would have.
# ⚠ Byte-identical on every push that succeeds — nothing allocates between the two points, so the
# node gets the same block it always got, and the multiply runs over the same bytes in the same
# order. Gated by programs/clip_test.cyr, programs/clip_pitch_test.cyr and programs/oom_test.cyr.
fn sd_clip_push_mask(cv, shape): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (shape == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    var node = sd_alloc(16);
    if (node == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    var w = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var h = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var cur = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET);
    if (cur != 0) {
        var curmask = load64(cur + 0);
        var n = w * h;
        var i = 0;
        while (i < n) {
            store8(shape + i, (load8(shape + i) * load8(curmask + i)) / 255);
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }
    store64(node + 0, shape);
    store64(node + 8, cur);
    store64(cv + SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET, node);
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — intersect a rectangle [x0,x1) x [y0,y1) (whole pixels) into the
# clip and push it. Returns SADISH_OK, SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null canvas, or SADISH_ERR_OOM if
# the w*h mask or the clip node is refused — in which case NOTHING was written and the clip that
# was on the canvas is unchanged and still poppable.
# ⚠ A 0-AREA CANVAS NOW REPORTS SADISH_ERR_OOM, and that is a CHANGE from 0.7.0 (MEASURED on both
# trees with a hand-built w=0 header: 0.7.0 returned SADISH_OK, 0.7.1 returns SADISH_ERR_OOM;
# programs/oom_test.cyr group K pins it). `sd_alloc(0)` returns 0 by lib/alloc.cyr's contract, so
# 0.7.0 pushed a clip node whose mask pointer was ADDRESS 0 and got away with it only because every
# reader indexes w*h = 0 bytes of it. Refusing is the wanted answer here: after 0.7.1 a null pointer
# does not enter a data structure, and the alternative is weakening sd_clip_push_mask's `shape == 0`
# guard, which exists so a caller chaining its OWN refused mask gets a code instead of that node.
# ⇒ Deliberately NOT the `n > 0` carve-out src/present.cyr makes for the same sd_alloc(0): there the
# 0-pixel call still COMPLETES — a valid header-only PPM, nothing left over — while here the only
# thing to hand back is the booby-trapped node. ⚠ Unreachable from a constructed canvas: sd_canvas_new
# refuses w <= 0 and h <= 0, so only a hand-built header can ask this.
fn sd_canvas_clip_push_rect(cv, x0, y0, x1, y1): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var w = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var h = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var shape = sd_alloc(w * h);
    if (shape == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    var y = 0;
    while (y < h) {
        var x = 0;
        while (x < w) {
            var v = 0;
            if (x >= x0) {
                if (x < x1) {
                    if (y >= y0) {
                        if (y < y1) { v = 255; }
                    }
                }
            }
            store8(shape + y * w + x, v);
            x = x + 1;
        }
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return sd_clip_push_mask(cv, shape);
}

# @public — intersect a filled PATH (its coverage) into the clip and push it.
# Returns SADISH_OK, SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null canvas, SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH for a null path, or
# SADISH_ERR_OOM if the temp canvas, its coverage, or the clip node is refused — nothing pushed,
# the existing clip unchanged.
# ⛔ ONLY SADISH_ERR_OOM IS PROPAGATED OUT OF THE FILL, and the asymmetry is deliberate. A path
# with no verbs fills nothing, leaves the fresh canvas's zeroed coverage as the mask and so clips
# everything away — that is what this entry point has always done with SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH and
# callers depend on it. An OOM is different in kind: the mask would be a mask of the fill that did
# not happen, and pushing it would silently clip away a shape the caller asked to keep.
# ⚠ Both halves of that asymmetry are GATED, not just asserted here: programs/oom_test.cyr group K
# pushes a verb-less path with no hook armed and reads SADISH_OK, a clip of nothing, and a pop back
# to the previous region (MEASURED identical on 0.7.0); group I refuses the fill and reads
# SADISH_ERR_OOM with nothing pushed. Widening this line to `fillrc != SADISH_OK` fails group K.
fn sd_canvas_clip_push_path(cv, path, rule): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    var w = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var h = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var tmp = sd_canvas_new(w, h);        # fresh (unclipped) -> raw shape coverage
    if (tmp == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    sd_canvas_set_aa(tmp, sd_canvas_aa(cv));   # the mask uses the parent's coverage engine
    var fillrc = sd_canvas_fill_path(tmp, path, rule);
    if (fillrc == SADISH_ERR_OOM) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    return sd_clip_push_mask(cv, sd_canvas_coverage(tmp));
}

# @public — pop the top clip, restoring the previous region (0 = unclipped).
fn sd_canvas_clip_pop(cv): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var cur = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET);
    if (cur != 0) {
        store64(cv + SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET, load64(cur + 8));
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# ================================================================
# Version probe — packed as major*10000 + minor*100 + patch.
# 0.4.0 -> 400; 0.4.1 -> 401; 0.5.5 -> 505; 0.6.0 -> 600; 0.7.0 -> 700; 0.7.1 -> 701;
# 0.7.2 -> 702; 0.8.0 -> 800; 0.9.0 -> 900; 0.9.1 -> 901; 0.10.0 -> 1000; 0.11.0 -> 1100;
# 0.11.1 -> 1101; 0.11.2 -> 1102.
#
# ⛔⛔ THIS WAS STALE FOR TWO RELEASES AND NOW HAS A GATE THAT WILL NOT LET IT HAPPEN AGAIN.
# It returned 900 through 0.9.1 AND 0.10.0 — a consumer probing the library it had linked was told
# 0.9.0 by both, including across the 0.10.0 ABI BREAK, which is precisely the release a version
# probe exists to let a consumer detect. programs/integration_test.cyr asserted the literal 900, so
# the gate agreed with the bug and no suite could see it.
# ⇒ CI now derives the expected number from the VERSION file and fails if this line disagrees
# (.github/workflows/ci.yml, "Verify version consistency"). ⚠ A human reading this: the number here
# and VERSION are ONE fact in two places, and the only reason it is duplicated at all is that a
# Cyrius fn cannot read a file at compile time. Bump both, or CI stops you.
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sadish_version(): i64 {
    return 1102;
}

# --- stroke.cyr ---

# @public — sadish stroker: path -> stroke coverage, round (0.4.0) or styled caps + joins.
#
# stroke.cyr — two entry points over one flatten step:
#   sd_canvas_stroke_path(cv, path, width)                                  round caps, round joins
#   sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex(cv, path, width, cap, join, miter_limit)       SD_CAP_* x SD_JOIN_*
# Both flatten curve verbs at tol = SD_ONE >> 2 into the stroker's run (the _sd_stroke_* scratch in
# raster.cyr) and MAX-combine the stroke into the canvas coverage, so it unions with whatever the
# canvas already holds and honours the clip.
# ⭐ A THIRD, sd_canvas_stroke_path_dash (src/dash.cyr), is the STYLED path with its run cut into
# dashes. It shares _sd_styled_walk below verb for verb and differs only in what a subpath is handed
# to: _sd_sb_subpath whole, or _sd_dash_subpath piece by piece. So a dash is drawn by exactly the
# code below, into exactly the same batch — the only arrow out of this file is _sd_sb_dispatch's.
#
# ⛔ ROUND/ROUND IS THE 0.4.0 STROKER, UNCHANGED. `_ex(cv, p, w, SD_CAP_ROUND, SD_JOIN_ROUND, any)`
# returns `sd_canvas_stroke_path(cv, p, w)`, whose code moved here from raster.cyr verbatim: a
# rect per flattened segment, then a 16-gon disc per run point (duplicates, collinear points and
# the closing vertex included), each its OWN sd_canvas_fill_union pass. Byte-identical to 0.5.5,
# asserted against a verbatim copy in programs/stroke_style_test.cyr. ⚠ So ROUND/ROUND alone keeps
# three 0.4.0 behaviours no other cap/join pair has: a drawing verb after CLOSE continues the same
# run ("M 4,4 L 28,4 L 28,20 Z L 4,20" strokes a closed rectangle; the styled path, like the fill,
# reads a triangle and then (4,4) -> (4,20)); a pixel two pieces split reads the MAX of their
# parts, not their sum (below); and a path per piece on the seam — 3,232 B per closed-rect stroke
# since 0.8.0 opened each at its exact capacity, 34,432 B for the same 104 allocations before that.
#
# ⭐ THE STYLED PATH COLLECTS ITS PIECES AS ONE EDGE LIST AND FILLS IT ONCE. Piece-per-pass MAX
# union is not a union: where two pieces share an edge INSIDE a pixel, the pixel gets max(a, b),
# not a + b. MEASURED: two 3-px-tall rects meeting at x = 4.5, fill_union'd separately, leave pixel
# 4 at 127; as one nonzero fill, 255. The round stroker hides that seam under the disc it puts on
# every vertex; a BUTT/MITER/BEVEL stroke has nothing to hide it. So the pieces (segment rects, join
# triangles, discs, square caps) go into a process-lifetime edge batch, every one wound the SAME
# way (negative shoelace, like the segment rect) — winding adds, overlap is exact, and a piece of
# the opposite winding would punch a hole, which is why each triangle's order is decided from its
# own signed area rather than reasoned about.
# ⭐ AND IT FILLS THAT BATCH WITH ITS OWN WALK (_sd_sb_flush), NOT sd_canvas_fill_union. Same
# sampling as the default fill — SD_FILL_SS sub-scanlines, analytic x, the same crossing formula —
# but a run of overlapping or touching pieces is ONE span. sd_fill_impl's nonzero walk adds a span
# per crossing and floors each, so a solid pixel that a piece edge crosses reads 254. MEASURED, a
# width-8 cubic filled through fill_union: 120 such pixels under MITER, 282 under ROUND joins; a
# straight polyline with 10 fractional vertices at width 4: 36. Through the merged walk: 2, 2 and 0
# — the cubic's two sit on the band's rim, where the 0.4.0 round stroker reads 254 too.
# ⚠ The default fill is not changed for this: merging spans there would move its bytes for
# self-overlapping nonzero paths. ⚠ And because the flush does not go through sd_fill_impl, a
# coverage choice made there does not reach styled strokes unless the flush is taught it — so it
# IS taught the one that exists: on an SD_AA_AREA canvas the batch goes to _sd_area_fill
# (src/coverage.cyr) as a nonzero MAX-combine, where touching pieces add exactly and no bridge is
# needed. Same guard as sd_fill_impl: only a batch that dropped no edge (_sd_sb_trunc == 0).
#
# SEMANTICS (styled path).
#   · Consecutive coincident points are dropped first; a closed subpath whose last point equals
#     its moveto point loses that duplicate, so a zero-length closing segment makes no join.
#   · Open subpath: a cap at each end, a join at every interior vertex. Closed (SD_VERB_CLOSE): no
#     caps, a join at EVERY vertex, the start/end vertex included. A drawing verb after a CLOSE
#     starts a new subpath at the moveto point, as the fill reads it.
#   · A subpath with no non-zero-length segment (a lone moveto, or every point coincident, closed
#     or not): ROUND -> a disc of radius hw, SQUARE -> an axis-aligned square of side 2*hw centred
#     on the point, BUTT -> nothing. This is the common renderer behaviour for "M x y Z" (strict
#     SVG draws nothing for a lone moveto).
#   · BUTT: the segment rect only. SQUARE: a rect extending the segment by hw past its end.
#     ROUND: the disc.
#   · Joins, at vertex V with offsets n_in / n_out (length hw, the segment rects' own corners):
#     collinear continuation -> nothing (the rects meet edge to edge); ROUND -> the disc; BEVEL ->
#     triangle (V, V + s*n_in, V + s*n_out) on the side the offsets diverge; MITER -> that
#     triangle plus (V + s*n_in, tip, V + s*n_out), or the bevel alone past the limit. A full 180°
#     reversal is an infinite miter and a zero-area bevel: a flat, butt-like end.
#   · ⚠ INSIDE A CURVE THE JOIN STYLE DOES NOT APPLY. A vertex the flattener put inside one quad or
#     cubic stands in for a smooth curve, whose stroke is the sweep of a disc: it mitres while the
#     tip stays within the flatten tolerance of that disc — ratio <= 1 + 0.25 px / hw — and takes
#     the round join past it. A MITER join there at the user's limit drew the cusp of
#     "M 10,30 C 50,5 10,5 50,30" (a 150° flattened turn, ratio 3.86 < 4) as a 6 px spike on a 4 px
#     stroke, MEASURED; now it is round. The style applies wherever two VERBS meet, and at a closed
#     subpath's start.
#   · miter_limit is a 16.16 RATIO, SVG `stroke-miterlimit`: miterLength / strokeWidth =
#     1 / sin(theta / 2), theta the INTERIOR angle between the segments; above the limit the join
#     is a bevel, AT the limit it is still a miter. Clamped to [SD_ONE, SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX].
#
# ⚠ THE LIMIT TEST IS INTEGER MATH, WITHOUT OVERFLOW. Each segment direction is rescaled by a power
# of two so its larger component lies in [2^13, 2^14); with P = isqrt(|e1|^2 |e2|^2) < 2^29 and
# D = e1.e2, "ratio > limit" is 2P > L^2 (P + D), tested as L(P + D) < ceil(P * 2^33 / L) — every
# intermediate below 2^62 for any L <= 2^31, at any coordinate magnitude. It is EXACT when both
# rescalings shed only zero bits (pixel and half-pixel geometry) and |e1||e2| is an integer: a
# (25,0) / (7,24) turn is a ratio of exactly 1.25, and the suite asserts both sides of it, as it
# does 377,108 / 377,109 around the irrational sqrt(298)/3. Otherwise the 13 kept bits of each
# direction put the decision within ~0.1 % of the limit at SVG's default 4 (derived, not measured;
# wider toward a reversal).
# ⚠ MAGNITUDES. The offsets rescale each direction first (_sd_seg_normal), so no segment length
# overflows. A miter tip lies at most miter_limit * hw from its vertex, and the walk's
# (bx - ax) * (sy - ay) needs edge extents under ~46,000 px: SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX = 1024.0 keeps
# every tip in range up to hw ~ 45 px, and a join past that ratio turns within 0.11° of a reversal.
# ⭐ ONE CALL IS ONE BATCH (0.7.0). The batch and the run GROW (doubling from the global alloc, like
# the fill's edge list), so however long the path, sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex fills every piece of it
# in one pass. Through 0.6.0 a batch held 8,192 edges (about 800 miter segments) and a longer
# subpath was flushed mid-way — and pieces from different batches meet by MAX, so where a subpath
# longer than a batch CROSSES or returns onto itself that loss was not bounded. MEASURED against the
# 0.6.0 bundle, 60 random self-crossing walks of 700..1,100 segments (64x64, widths 0.3..5.3 px,
# BUTT/SQUARE x MITER/BEVEL) against the same walks as one batch (0.6.0 rebuilt with a 16,384-edge
# cap): 0.6.0 split 20, each more than 3 levels off somewhere, worst 118; 0.7.0 is byte-identical to
# the one batch on all 60, and on 60 more of 2,000..5,000 segments (0.6.0: 60 split, worst 131).
# ⚠ THE FLUSH + RE-EMISSION IS KEPT as the fallback. It runs when SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP is set (a
# memory bound) or when a growth fails for want of memory: the next batch re-emits the stretch before
# the flush (until 2*hw + 3 px of it or 16 segments, whichever comes first), and a closed subpath's
# closing join is followed by the same stretch after its vertex 0, so every seam ALONG the path
# falls where one batch is complete (the suite sweeps rings, wiggles, blobs and gears across lowered
# limits, all within 1 level of one batch) — the MAX loss where a path crosses itself returns with
# it. Out of memory, a stroke degrades to exactly 0.6.0's batches; it drops a piece only if an edge
# arrives at a full array whose growth fails, which the reserve before every unit prevents.
# ⚠ Single-threaded, like the fill: one edge batch and one run for the process.
#
# ⛔⛔ A REFUSED ALLOCATION IS A RETURN CODE, NOT A FAULT AND NOT A SILENT UNDER-DRAW (0.7.2).
# All three public entries — sd_canvas_stroke_path, sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex,
# sd_canvas_stroke_path_dash — return SADISH_ERR_OOM for a refusal at ANY allocation they reach:
# the round stroker's per-piece SdPaths (⚠ and, through 0.8.0, the SdPoints inside them — a piece
# path's points are inline coordinates since 0.9.0, so its three requests are all it makes) and a
# curve's flatten points, all on the consumer's seam; the process-lifetime scratch and its growths (the fill /
# stroke scratch, the batch, the run, the curve flags, the dash piece buffer, the flush's row
# accumulator), all on the global alloc. Closes the STROKE half of
# docs/development/issues/archived/2026-09-15-path-construction-stores-through-a-refused-allocation.md.
# ⭐ MEASURED, the same four calls on a materialised 0.7.1 tree and on this one — 32x32, warm
# scratch, a hook granting K allocations and then refusing (K = 0..4):
#   round stroker, a 2-point line     0.7.1 SIGSEGV rc 139   here SADISH_ERR_OOM, 0 of 27,964 ink
#   round stroker, a closed 2-cubic   0.7.1 SIGSEGV rc 139   here SADISH_ERR_OOM, 0 of 59,303
#   styled BUTT/MITER, that curve     0.7.1 SADISH_OK        here SADISH_ERR_OOM
#   dashed BUTT/MITER, that curve     0.7.1 SADISH_OK        here SADISH_ERR_OOM
# ⛔ AND THE INK IS THE SAME ON BOTH TREES WHEREVER 0.7.1 SURVIVED AT ALL: the styled stroke paints
# 0 / 6,165 / 12,117 / 18,441 / 28,682 of its 59,850 units at K = 0..4 on both, the dashed one
# 0 / 6,166 / 7,978 / 13,453 / 17,329 of 36,422. Only the REPORT changed — a starved stroke draws
# what it always drew, and an unstarved one is byte-identical (agnos refagree, all 200 paths).
# ⚠ WHAT A PARTIAL STROKE LEAVES ON THE CANVAS: whatever it had already drawn. THERE IS NO
# ROLLBACK. The pieces before the refusal are MAX-combined into the coverage exactly as a whole
# stroke's are, so the canvas holds a prefix of the stroke and the code says the picture is wrong;
# a caller that wants all-or-nothing strokes into a scratch canvas, or clears and retries. The
# ROUND stroker stops at the FIRST refusal (each piece is its own allocation + fill pass, and a
# hook that refused one refuses the next); the styled path runs its walk out and reports at the
# end, so its batch is still flushed and its ink is the prefix that fit.
# ⛔ AND A REFUSAL LEAVES NO PROCESS-LIFETIME WRECKAGE. The batch is empty after the flush that
# failed (it is emptied before anything in the flush can fail), its truncation flag is cleared, and
# the run, the curve flags and the fill scratch keep the blocks and capacities they had — so the
# NEXT stroke on a healthy allocator draws exactly what it would have drawn had the refusal never
# happened. programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr asserts that byte for byte at every K of every sweep.
# ⚠ sd_flatten_truncated() is unchanged and still STICKY (it belongs to the last flatten, not to the
# last call — path.cyr says so): a stroke scopes it the way sd_fill_impl does and puts the caller's
# standing verdict back. The RETURN CODE is the per-call witness a stroke did not have before.

# ---- cap / join styles ------------------------------------------
var SD_CAP_BUTT   = 0;
var SD_CAP_ROUND  = 1;
var SD_CAP_SQUARE = 2;

var SD_JOIN_MITER = 0;
var SD_JOIN_ROUND = 1;
var SD_JOIN_BEVEL = 2;

# Upper clamp for the miter limit ratio: 1024.0 in 16.16.
var SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX = 67108864;

# ---- stroking (v0.4.0) -----------------------------------------
# A segment's rectangle: offset both endpoints by `hw` along the segment's
# perpendicular (magnitude via sd_isqrt), fill-union it. Round caps + round
# joins come from a disc at every vertex, so no bevel/miter math is needed.
# Each piece is MAX-unioned so overlaps combine regardless of winding.
# Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_OOM when the piece path or the fill underneath is refused —
# this piece then draws NOTHING (the half-built path is abandoned to an allocator with no free()),
# and every piece already drawn stays on the canvas. ⚠ 3 sd_alloc calls a rect on the consumer's
# seam: the path's record + verbs + points, and NOTHING per corner (0.9.0; 7 through 0.8.0, when
# each corner was a 16 B SdPoint of its own).
#
# ⭐ THE PATH IS OPENED AT ITS EXACT SIZE (0.8.0) — item 2 of
# docs/development/proposals/archived/2026-09-15-path-capacity-for-known-size-paths.md's "Still open": this is
# one of the two in-tree sites that knew its size and bought SD_PATH_CAP = 256 + 256 anyway.
# ⛔ THE COUNTS ARE READ OFF THE FIVE CALLS BELOW, NOT ESTIMATED: moveto + 3 linetos + close = 5
# verbs; moveto and each lineto carry one point and close carries none = 4 points. Both land under
# SD_PATH_CAP_MIN = 8, so each array is opened at 8 and neither can grow here — the path is built
# with 3 VERB SLOTS AND 4 POINT SLOTS SPARE.
# ⚠ THAT SLACK IS REAL, SO ADDING ONE CALL BELOW DOES NOT BUY A DOUBLING — MEASURED, at these same
# arguments: one extra lineto is the SAME 240 B in the SAME 3 sd_allocs (6 verbs, 5 points, both
# capacities still 8), three extra likewise (8 verbs, 7 points, the verb array exactly full), and
# neither array has moved. The verb array first doubles at the NINTH verb — moveto + 7 linetos +
# close — which is 368 B in 4 with vcap 8 -> 16; the point array at the ninth point, one lineto
# later. ⚠ Through 0.8.0 each added lineto also bought a 16 B SdPoint, so those rows read 256 B in
# 8 and 288 B in 10; inline (x, y) makes an added point free until an array actually overflows.
# ⇒ What FAILS when the five calls below become six is group N's COUNT checks in
# programs/path_cap_test.cyr (5 verbs, 4 points), NOT its capacity checks: those do not move until
# the fourth extra lineto. Group N8 pins the whole boundary, so these numbers stay honest.
# ⭐ MEASURED on the seam, per call: 4,208 B (0.7.2) -> 240 B (0.8.0) -> 240 B in THREE allocations
# (0.9.0) — the same bytes, re-spent: 48 + 64 of verbs + 128 of INLINE points, where 0.8.0 paid
# 48 + 64 + 64 plus four 16 B SdPoints in seven calls. The closed 8x8 rect stroke 34,432 ->
# 3,232 -> 2,720 B, and 104 -> 24 sd_alloc calls (programs/path_cap_test.cyr group N).
fn sd_stroke_seg(cv, ax, ay, bx, by, hw): i64 {
    var dx = bx - ax;
    var dy = by - ay;
    var len2 = dx * dx + dy * dy;
    if (len2 == 0) { return SADISH_OK; }     # zero-length -> the vertex disc covers it
    var l = sd_isqrt(len2);          # length in 16.16
    if (l == 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var ox = ((0 - dy) * hw) / l;    # perpendicular offset, magnitude hw
    var oy = (dx * hw) / l;
    var rp = sd_path_new_cap(5, 4);        # 5 verbs, 4 points — the five calls below, counted
    if (rp == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_moveto(rp, ax + ox, ay + oy) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_lineto(rp, bx + ox, by + oy) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_lineto(rp, bx - ox, by - oy) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_lineto(rp, ax - ox, ay - oy) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_close(rp) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    return sd_canvas_fill_union(cv, rp, SD_FILL_NONZERO);
}

# A radius-hw disc (16-gon) at (cx, cy) — round cap / round join filler.
# SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_OOM on a refusal (nothing of this disc drawn), as sd_stroke_seg.
# ⚠ 3 sd_alloc calls a disc — the path's three, and nothing per vertex (0.9.0; 19 through 0.8.0,
# when each of the sixteen vertices was an SdPoint on the seam).
#
# ⭐ EXACT CAPACITY, LIKE sd_stroke_seg (0.8.0) — the second of the proposal's two in-tree sites.
# ⛔ THE 17 AND THE 16 ARE THE `while (k < 16)` BELOW, COUNTED: 16 iterations emit one moveto and 15
# linetos, then sd_path_close adds the 17th verb and no point — so 17 verbs and 16 points, and the
# two arrays are EXACTLY full when the disc is built. ⚠ That is the tightest fit in the tree, and a
# capacity one slot short of it pays a doubling and a memcpy: MEASURED, this shape opened at (16, 16)
# is 688 B in 4 sd_alloc calls against 440 B in 3 — still well under the 4,400 B the 256-slot
# default cost, and still 248 B and one copy more than deriving the count costs. ⚠ Raise the 16 (and
# the `411775 / 16` step with it) without moving these arguments and BOTH arrays double.
# programs/path_cap_test.cyr group N reads the capacities AND the counts back off the record, so a
# changed 16 fails a check instead of quietly buying a doubling.
# ⭐ MEASURED on the seam, per call: 4,400 B (0.7.2) -> 568 B (0.8.0) -> 440 B in THREE allocations
# (0.9.0): 48 + 136 of verbs + 256 of INLINE points, where 0.8.0 paid 48 + 136 + 128 plus sixteen
# 16 B SdPoints in nineteen calls.
fn sd_stroke_disc(cv, cx, cy, hw): i64 {
    var dp = sd_path_new_cap(17, 16);      # 16 vertices + the close = 17 verbs, 16 points
    if (dp == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    var step = 411775 / 16;          # 2*pi / 16 in 16.16
    var k = 0;
    var vrc = SADISH_OK;
    while (k < 16) {
        if (vrc == SADISH_OK) {
            var ang = k * step;
            var px = cx + sd_fixed_mul(hw, sd_cos(ang));
            var py = cy + sd_fixed_mul(hw, sd_sin(ang));
            if (k == 0) { vrc = sd_path_moveto(dp, px, py); } else { vrc = sd_path_lineto(dp, px, py); }
        }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    if (vrc != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_close(dp) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    return sd_canvas_fill_union(cv, dp, SD_FILL_NONZERO);
}

# Stroke one flattened subpath run: a rect per segment + a disc per vertex.
# ⚠ `run` HOLDS COORDINATES, 16 B A POINT — x at +0, y at +8 (0.9.0; an array of 8 B SdPoint
# POINTERS through 0.8.0). The run is filled from a path's inline points and from a flatten's
# output, and copying the two numbers out of a flatten's SdPoint is what keeps a straight verb's
# anchor from needing a record of its own. See _sd_stroke_run in src/raster.cyr.
# Returns SADISH_OK, or the first SADISH_ERR_OOM a piece reported — and STOPS there: the remaining
# pieces are not attempted, because each is another allocation on the hook that has just refused
# one. What was drawn stays drawn (the header's no-rollback rule).
fn sd_stroke_run(cv, run, n, closed, hw): i64 {
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n - 1) {
        var src = sd_stroke_seg(cv, load64(run + i * 16), load64(run + i * 16 + 8),
          load64(run + (i + 1) * 16), load64(run + (i + 1) * 16 + 8), hw);
        if (src != SADISH_OK) { return src; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (closed != 0) {
        if (n > 1) {
            var crc = sd_stroke_seg(cv, load64(run + (n - 1) * 16), load64(run + (n - 1) * 16 + 8),
              load64(run + 0), load64(run + 8), hw);
            if (crc != SADISH_OK) { return crc; }
        }
    }
    var k = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        var drc = sd_stroke_disc(cv, load64(run + k * 16), load64(run + k * 16 + 8), hw);
        if (drc != SADISH_OK) { return drc; }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# The round stroker's verb walk — sd_canvas_stroke_path's body, split out (0.7.2) so the entry can
# put the caller's standing flatten verdict back on the way out of an early return. Returns
# SADISH_OK, or the first SADISH_ERR_OOM a piece reported, which STOPS the walk (sd_stroke_run).
fn _sd_round_walk(cv, path, hw): i64 {
    var n_verbs = sd_path_verb_count(path);
    var tol = SD_ONE >> 2;
    var run_n = 0;
    var fbuf = _sd_stroke_fbuf;
    var fctx = _sd_stroke_fctx;

    # ⛔ COORDINATES AND A `has_cur` FLAG (0.9.0) — a path's points are inline now, so the current
    # point is two numbers and 0 is the ORIGIN rather than "no moveto yet". Same flag, same reason,
    # as src/raster.cyr's fill walk and src/path.cyr's flatten walk.
    var verbs = load64(path + SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET);
    var curx = 0;
    var cury = 0;
    var has_cur = 0;
    var closed = 0;
    var vi = 0;
    var pi = 0;
    while (vi < n_verbs) {
        var verb = load64(verbs + vi * 8);
        if (verb == SD_VERB_MOVETO) {
            if (run_n > 0) {
                var mrc = sd_stroke_run(cv, _sd_stroke_run, run_n, closed, hw);
                if (mrc != SADISH_OK) { return mrc; }
            }
            run_n = 0;
            closed = 0;
            curx = sd_path_point_x(path, pi);
            cury = sd_path_point_y(path, pi);
            has_cur = 1;
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16, curx);
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16 + 8, cury);
            run_n = run_n + 1;
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_LINETO) {
            if (has_cur != 0) {         # a drawing verb before any moveto is skipped (raster.cyr)
                curx = sd_path_point_x(path, pi);
                cury = sd_path_point_y(path, pi);
                run_n = _sd_run_push(run_n, curx, cury, 0);
            }
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_QUADTO) {
            store64(fctx + 0, fbuf);
            store64(fctx + 8, 0);
            store64(fctx + 16, SD_FLATTEN_PCAP);
            if (has_cur != 0) {         # a curve before any moveto is skipped (see raster.cyr)
                var qex = sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 1);
                var qey = sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 1);
                _sd_flatten_quad_xy(fctx, curx, cury, sd_path_point_x(path, pi),
                  sd_path_point_y(path, pi), qex, qey, tol, SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
                run_n = _sd_run_append(run_n, fctx, 0);
                curx = qex;
                cury = qey;
            }
            pi = pi + 2;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_CUBICTO) {
            store64(fctx + 0, fbuf);
            store64(fctx + 8, 0);
            store64(fctx + 16, SD_FLATTEN_PCAP);
            if (has_cur != 0) {         # a curve before any moveto is skipped (see raster.cyr)
                var kex = sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 2);
                var key = sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 2);
                _sd_flatten_cubic_xy(fctx, curx, cury, sd_path_point_x(path, pi),
                  sd_path_point_y(path, pi), sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 1),
                  sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 1), kex, key, tol, SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
                run_n = _sd_run_append(run_n, fctx, 0);
                curx = kex;
                cury = key;
            }
            pi = pi + 3;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_CLOSE) {
            closed = 1;
        }
        vi = vi + 1;
    }
    if (run_n > 0) { return sd_stroke_run(cv, _sd_stroke_run, run_n, closed, hw); }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_stroke_path — stroke `path` with the given `width` (16.16) onto
# `cv`'s coverage, with round caps + round joins. Flattens curves, then per
# subpath draws a rectangle per segment and a disc per vertex, MAX-combined
# into the coverage buffer.
#
# Returns SADISH_OK; SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for cv == 0; SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH for a 0 or verb-less
# path; SADISH_ERR_OOM when any allocation the stroke reaches is refused — the piece paths and
# their points on the seam, a curve's flatten mid-points, or the process-lifetime scratch. A
# width <= 0 draws nothing and is SADISH_OK. ⚠ An OOM leaves the pieces already drawn ON the
# canvas: see the no-rollback block in this file's header.
#
# Styled caps/joins are sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex below; this entry stays the
# 0.4.0 piece-per-pass stroker, byte-identical.
# TODO(v0.5): batch the round pieces — this still allocs a PATH (on the seam)
# + runs a fill pass per segment/vertex (correctness-first); the fixed scratch
# underneath is process-lifetime (0.5.5).
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sd_canvas_stroke_path(cv, path, width): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    var n_verbs = sd_path_verb_count(path);
    if (n_verbs == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    var hw = sd_asr(width, 1);
    if (hw <= 0) { return SADISH_OK; }

    # The stroker's OWN scratch set — the run stays live across the fill_union passes below, which
    # use the fill set (see the scratch block in raster.cyr). ⚠ The run GROWS (0.7.0), so it is
    # read as _sd_stroke_run at each use, never cached across a push.
    _sd_scratch_init();
    # ⛔ THE GUARD IS ON THE BLOCK THE WALK STORES THROUGH, not on _sd_scratch_init's status —
    # sd_fill_impl's reasoning (see it), applied to the stroker's own run: the publish is
    # all-or-nothing, so a 0 here covers both a refused set and anything that ever published a 0
    # behind a status of 1. Through 0.7.1 the status was discarded and this 0 was stored through.
    if (_sd_stroke_run == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    _sd_run_trunc = 0;
    # The flatten's truncation verdict, SCOPED TO THIS CALL exactly as sd_fill_impl scopes it: from
    # here a 1 means THIS stroke lost mid-points. The caller's standing verdict is put back below,
    # so sd_flatten_truncated() answers what it always answered (path.cyr: sticky until the next
    # sd_path_flatten) and the return code is what answers for this call.
    var flat_was = _sd_flat_trunc;
    _sd_flat_trunc = 0;

    var rc = _sd_round_walk(cv, path, hw);

    if (_sd_flat_trunc != 0) {
        if (rc == SADISH_OK) { rc = SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    }
    # ⛔ AND THE CALLER'S STANDING VERDICT GOES BACK — the line _sd_round_walk was split out FOR.
    # Without it a stroke would CLEAR a verdict its caller was holding from an earlier
    # sd_path_flatten, which path.cyr documents as sticky until the next flatten. MEASURED as a
    # mutation (this line deleted): a starved sd_path_flatten leaves sd_flatten_truncated() at 1, a
    # healthy sd_canvas_stroke_path then reads it back 0. Gated by stroke_oom_test checks 115-117,
    # the round twin of the _ex pair at 112-114.
    if (flat_was != 0) { _sd_flat_trunc = 1; }
    # A run point the growth could not take is ink the caller asked for and did not get, exactly as
    # a lost mid-point is (_sd_run_grow / _sd_inner_grow set it; raster.cyr's _sd_grow_limit and a
    # real out-of-memory are the two ways to reach it).
    if (_sd_run_trunc != 0) {
        if (rc == SADISH_OK) { rc = SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    }
    return rc;
}

# ---- styled stroking: the edge batch -----------------------------
# Edges one batch may hold before it is flushed. ⭐ 0 — THE DEFAULT (0.7.0) — IS NO LIMIT: the batch
# grows, so one sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex call is ONE fill however long its path. A positive value
# forces a flush past that many edges (the flush + re-emission below, which is otherwise the
# out-of-memory fallback) and so bounds the batch's memory: it is held to
# [SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN, SD_FLATTEN_CAP] when the FIRST styled stroke sizes the array
# (_sd_sb_cap_for), so a consumer that wants a small batch pays for a small one — MEASURED, a first
# styled stroke at 900: 37,248 B against the default's 270,592 B. Through 0.6.0 this was 8,192 and a
# value raised AFTER the first stroke was held to the allocation; now the array grows to meet it.
# Setting 8192 reproduces 0.6.0's batches exactly.
# ⚠ It bounds the batch, not every array a stroke touches: the run and the curve flags are sized by
# the PATH (raster.cyr's scratch), and the crossings the flush borrows follow the batch.
var SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP = 0;
# Room reserved before one unit. A unit is at most 39 edges: a join (a 16-gon disc, or 3 bevel + 16
# for a curve vertex past its tolerance), the rect (4), and an end cap or the closing join (16).
var SD_STROKE_UNIT_EDGES = 40;
# The smallest limit that holds what may follow a flush without another: 16 re-emitted units back
# (<= 23 edges each — never a last unit), the unit, and a closed subpath's 16 forward: 775 edges.
# ⚠ Since 0.7.0 a lower limit costs flushes, not ink: an edge arriving past the limit grows the
# array rather than being dropped (_sd_sb_edge), so a starved re-emission is emitted in full and
# only the NEXT unit flushes. MEASURED: a 2,000-sample Lissajous at a limit of 10 strokes the same
# bytes as at 800 (programs/grow_edges_test.cyr) — at about 17x the fills.
var SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN = 800;

# ⛔ Process-lifetime, from the GLOBAL alloc, allocated by the first styled stroke — not by
# _sd_scratch_init, so a process that never strokes with styles never pays for it. Same reason as
# raster.cyr's scratch: a consumer's hook is an arena that gets reset. MEASURED one-time cost
# 270,592 B (8,192 * 32 edges + 256 trig + 8,192 curve flags) at the default SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP,
# and only the edge part of it under a lower one (MEASURED at 900: 37,248 B); after it a
# straight-line styled stroke costs the heap 0 B, and curves only their flatten mid-points. The
# flush's crossings use the fill's _sd_fill_cross: a per-fill working array, not held across a call,
# which the flush IS — so it is borrowed, not duplicated.
# ⭐ The edges and the curve flags GROW by doubling (0.7.0), from the global alloc, like the fill's
# list: MEASURED 524,288 B for the first batch past 8,192 edges, plus 262,144 B of crossings unless a
# fill already grew them (every growth keeps _sd_fill_cross_cap >= _sd_sb_cap); a repeat 0 B.
# ⚠ AND IT IS MONOTONE, AND NEVER RETURNED (lib/alloc.cyr has no free): one batch per call means the
# batch is sized by the LONGEST path the process ever strokes, and every abandoned doubling stays
# mapped. MEASURED, random walks of 100..25,600 segments at width 3 (BUTT/MITER, a styled stroke
# makes 7-20 edges a segment): the batch settles at 262,144 edges and the process has asked the
# global heap for 25,682,744 B in total, once — against 1,303,352 B over the same sweep with
# SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP = 8192 (of which 835,584 B is the run and its parallel flags, which 0.6.0
# truncated instead). ⇒ The knob is the ceiling for a consumer that needs one; it bounds the first
# allocation too.
# ⚠ THE RUN'S SHARE WAS 442,368 B THROUGH 0.8.0 and is RE-MEASURED here for 0.9.0's 16 B inline
# point: it is the doubling LADDER the longest walk forces (8,192 -> 16,384 -> 32,768 slots at
# 16 B, plus the flags' 16,384 + 32,768 B), not the walk, so it reproduces from any generator that
# reaches 25,601 points — verified against 0.8.0's own src, which returns the 442,368 above to the
# byte. The initial 8,192-slot block is NOT in that figure; it is in _sd_scratch_init's one-time
# cost (131,072 B, 65,536 through 0.8.0). ⭐ The sweep's TOTAL barely moved even so: the same walks'
# PATHS cost 408,800 B less on the seam (51,100 points x the 8 B pointer that is gone), so the run's
# +393,216 B of global heap is very nearly paid for by the caller's own arena — MEASURED +49,952 B
# net across the nine walks.
var _sd_sb_edges = 0;   # _sd_sb_cap * 32 — (ax, ay, bx, by) per edge, a -> b in winding order
var _sd_sb_cap = 0;     # edges the array holds
var _sd_sb_n = 0;       # edges in the batch
var _sd_sb_trig = 0;    # 16 (cos, sin) pairs — the 16-gon disc, sd_stroke_disc's angles
# _sd_sb_inner is never read at run[0]: a subpath's first point takes a cap, or the closing join,
# which is passed 0.
var _sd_sb_inner = 0;   # _sd_sb_inner_cap bytes, parallel to the run: 1 = inside a curve's flattening
var _sd_sb_inner_cap = 0;
var _sd_sb_trunc = 0;   # 1 if the batch dropped an edge (a growth failed); cleared by the flush
var _sd_run_trunc = 0;  # 1 if THIS stroke call dropped a run point (a growth failed)
# ⛔ 1 if something INSIDE the styled machinery could not get memory during this call (0.7.2): the
# flush's row accumulator or the area engine's, a batch that dropped an edge, a dash piece the
# buffer could not take. It is what carries a refusal from a place that cannot return one — a
# mid-walk _sd_sb_flush, a _sd_dash_put four frames down — out to the public entry point.
# ⚠ Cleared at the top of _sd_styled_walk, which is the ONLY reader, and the only path on which any
# of those sites runs. The round stroker reports through return codes instead and never sets it.
var _sd_stroke_oom = 0;

# The edge array's INITIAL capacity, from SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP: 0 (no limit) takes the full
# SD_FLATTEN_CAP, a positive limit is held to [SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN, SD_FLATTEN_CAP]. ⛔ Never more
# than SD_FLATTEN_CAP, which is what _sd_scratch_init sized the borrowed crossings to — n edges make
# at most n crossings, so the crossings >= batch invariant holds from the first stroke. A limit
# raised later is met by growing (_sd_sb_room), which reserves the crossings first.
fn _sd_sb_cap_for(): i64 {
    var cap = SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP;
    if (cap <= 0) { return SD_FLATTEN_CAP; }
    if (cap < SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN) { cap = SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN; }
    if (cap > SD_FLATTEN_CAP) { cap = SD_FLATTEN_CAP; }
    return cap;
}

# Allocate the batch, the curve flags and the trig table once (idempotent). Returns 1 when they are
# ready, 0 when a block was refused.
# ⛔ ALL OR NOTHING, AND `_sd_sb_edges != 0` IS THE FLAG — _sd_scratch_init's rule, for the same
# reason (0.7.2). Through 0.7.1 the three blocks went straight into the globals unchecked: a refused
# trig table faulted in the table write two lines below it, and a refused `inner` or `edges` was
# published WITH ITS CAPACITY, so the first curve flag or the first edge of that very stroke was
# written through address 0. The blocks now land in locals and are published together, so a partial
# set is abandoned (no free()) and the next call retries from scratch.
# ⚠ These come from the GLOBAL `alloc`, never sd_alloc (raster.cyr's scratch rule), so no consumer
# hook can refuse them: the only honest way to drive this path is the CAPACITY, which sizes two of
# the three — programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr raises SD_FLATTEN_CAP past ALLOC_MAX / 8, the device
# oom_test group J uses on the fill scratch.
# ⛔ AND THESE THREE 0-TESTS ARE GATES, NOT A CONTRACT — `_sd_sb_edges` IS NOT ALWAYS THE LARGEST
# BLOCK OF THE SET, unlike _sd_scratch_init's eight (where every capacity-sized block is CAP*8 or
# more, so a size-based refusal takes them together). Here `inner` is SD_FLATTEN_CAP BYTES while
# `edges` is _sd_sb_cap_for() * 32, and _sd_sb_cap_for reads SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP — the PUBLIC knob
# declared above. They move together only while that knob is 0 (then cap == SD_FLATTEN_CAP and
# edges is 32x the larger); set it, and edges shrinks to it while inner does not. So whenever
# SD_FLATTEN_CAP > 32 * cap, `inner` is the LARGEST block and lib/alloc.cyr refuses IT while
# granting `edges` — the one arrangement a partial publish cannot survive, because `_sd_sb_edges`
# is also the idempotence flag.
# ⛔ MEASURED, SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP = 1000 with SD_FLATTEN_CAP = 3,000,000,000: this function
# reverted to 0.7.1's unchecked stores publishes edges = <32,000 B block>, inner = 0,
# inner_cap = 3,000,000,000 and returns 1; the caller's `_sd_sb_edges == 0` guard passes, and the
# next _sd_run_push writes its curve flag through address 0 — SIGSEGV, rc 139. And because
# `_sd_sb_edges != 0` keeps this function from ever running again, so does EVERY styled stroke for
# the rest of the process: the 0.7.0 poisoned-scratch shape, exactly.
# ⇒ programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr group G4 drives those two values and gates all five publishes.
# ⚠ Those two values are how a SUITE reaches it; a real machine reaches it without any knob at all,
# because lib/alloc.cyr also refuses when the heap cannot be extended, and then any one of the three
# can be the one that fails. The size argument is what makes the path testable, not what makes the
# tests necessary.
fn _sd_sb_init(): i64 {
    if (_sd_sb_edges != 0) { return 1; }
    var trig = alloc(16 * 16);
    var inner = alloc(SD_FLATTEN_CAP);
    var cap = _sd_sb_cap_for();
    var edges = alloc(cap * 32);
    var ok = 1;
    if (trig == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (inner == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (edges == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (ok == 0) { return 0; }
    var step = 411775 / 16;          # 2*pi / 16 in 16.16, as sd_stroke_disc
    var k = 0;
    while (k < 16) {
        store64(trig + k * 16, sd_cos(k * step));
        store64(trig + k * 16 + 8, sd_sin(k * step));
        k = k + 1;
    }
    _sd_sb_trig = trig;
    _sd_sb_inner = inner;
    _sd_sb_inner_cap = SD_FLATTEN_CAP;
    _sd_sb_cap = cap;
    _sd_sb_edges = edges;
    return 1;
}

# Grow the batch to hold at least `need` edges: crossings first, then the edges, doubling, the live
# ones copied and the old block abandoned. Returns 1, or 0 if an allocation failed (nothing lost).
fn _sd_sb_grow(need): i64 {
    var nc = _sd_sb_cap;
    while (nc < need) { nc = nc * 2; }
    if (_sd_fill_cross_for(nc) == 0) { return 0; }
    var blk = _sd_grow_alloc(nc * 32);
    if (blk == 0) { return 0; }
    memcpy(blk, _sd_sb_edges, _sd_sb_n * 32);
    _sd_sb_edges = blk;
    _sd_sb_cap = nc;
    return 1;
}

# 1 if `k` more edges may go into the batch: within SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP (when set) and within the
# array, grown if need be. 0 tells the caller to flush first — a limit reached, or a growth that
# failed, which is how a stroke degrades out of memory: to 0.6.0's batches, not to lost pieces.
fn _sd_sb_room(k): i64 {
    var need = _sd_sb_n + k;
    var lim = SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP;
    if (lim > 0) {
        if (lim < SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN) { lim = SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN; }
        if (need > lim) { return 0; }
    }
    if (need > _sd_sb_cap) { return _sd_sb_grow(need); }
    return 1;
}

# Make the stroker's run hold `n` points — and, when `flags` != 0, the curve flags too — growing
# either by doubling from the global alloc (live entries copied). Returns 1, or 0 when a growth
# failed: the caller drops the point, as 0.6.0 did at 8,192, and _sd_run_trunc records it; this call
# then stops trying to grow, so a subpath keeps a prefix of its points.
fn _sd_run_fits(n, flags): i64 {
    if (n > _sd_stroke_run_cap) {
        if (_sd_run_grow(n) == 0) { return 0; }
    }
    if (flags != 0) {
        if (n > _sd_sb_inner_cap) {
            if (_sd_inner_grow(n) == 0) { return 0; }
        }
    }
    return 1;
}

# ⚠ 16 B A SLOT (0.9.0; 8 B a pointer through 0.8.0), so a growth of the same capacity asks for
# twice the bytes — and the COPY is still the whole old CAPACITY, as _sd_inner_grow's header
# explains for the flags.
fn _sd_run_grow(n): i64 {
    if (_sd_run_trunc != 0) { return 0; }
    var nc = _sd_stroke_run_cap;
    while (nc < n) { nc = nc * 2; }
    var blk = _sd_grow_alloc(nc * 16);
    if (blk == 0) {
        _sd_run_trunc = 1;
        return 0;
    }
    memcpy(blk, _sd_stroke_run, _sd_stroke_run_cap * 16);
    _sd_stroke_run = blk;
    _sd_stroke_run_cap = nc;
    return 1;
}

# ⚠ THE COPY IS THE WHOLE OLD CAPACITY, not the run's live count, and deliberately: _sd_run_append
# grows the flags at the point it needs slot n, and a curve's flags are written AFTERWARDS by
# _sd_sb_mark_curve — so the byte at the old capacity's last index is, mid-curve, about to be
# rewritten and, on a LINETO, already 0. A short copy is therefore invisible today only because
# lib/alloc.cyr hands out freshly-mapped (zero) pages and never recycles memory; under an allocator
# that reused a block it would be a wrong-join bug. Copying the capacity costs one pass over at most
# the flags' own size and needs neither of those arguments.
# (programs/grow_edges_test.cyr writes a 1 into the last slot before a growth and reads it back.)
fn _sd_inner_grow(n): i64 {
    if (_sd_run_trunc != 0) { return 0; }
    var nc = _sd_sb_inner_cap;
    while (nc < n) { nc = nc * 2; }
    var blk = _sd_grow_alloc(nc);
    if (blk == 0) {
        _sd_run_trunc = 1;
        return 0;
    }
    memcpy(blk, _sd_sb_inner, _sd_sb_inner_cap);
    _sd_sb_inner = blk;
    _sd_sb_inner_cap = nc;
    return 1;
}

# Append a flatten ctx's points to the stroker's run (_sd_stroke_run), growing it — and, when
# `flags` != 0, the styled path's parallel curve flags — as needed; a point that still does not fit
# is dropped (see _sd_run_fits). Returns the new count. (0.7.0: was sd_stroke_append(run, run_n,
# fctx), bounded by SD_FLATTEN_CAP; the run pointer is no longer a parameter because growth
# replaces the block.)
# ⭐ BOTH SIDES ARE COORDINATES NOW (0.10.0; the ctx held SdPoint ptrs through 0.9.1, and this
# function loaded one per point to read its two words out). The COPY is still a copy and must stay
# one: it is what lets a flatten's per-curve buffer be rewound under a run that is still being
# stroked. ⛔ Do not "optimise" it into an alias of the buffer — the rewind would then rewrite the
# run underneath the stroker.
fn _sd_run_append(run_n, fctx, flags): i64 {
    var pts = load64(fctx + 0);
    var n = load64(fctx + 8);
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        if (_sd_run_fits(run_n + 1, flags) != 0) {
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16,
              load64(pts + i * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_X_OFFSET));
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16 + 8,
              load64(pts + i * SD_POLYLINE_PT_SIZE + SD_POINT_Y_OFFSET));
            run_n = run_n + 1;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return run_n;
}

# Append the point (x, y) to the run (a LINETO); with `flags` != 0 its curve flag is cleared too.
fn _sd_run_push(run_n, x, y, flags): i64 {
    if (_sd_run_fits(run_n + 1, flags) == 0) { return run_n; }
    store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16, x);
    store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16 + 8, y);
    if (flags != 0) { store8(_sd_sb_inner + run_n, 0); }
    return run_n + 1;
}

# Append the edge a -> b. Horizontal edges cross no sub-scanline and are dropped. The unit reserve
# (_sd_sb_room) has already made room; if an edge still arrives at a full array it grows here, and
# only if THAT fails is the edge dropped — and _sd_sb_trunc set, so the flush does not hand an
# incomplete list to the area engine. Never a write past the array.
fn _sd_sb_edge(ax, ay, bx, by): i64 {
    if (ay == by) { return 0; }
    if (_sd_sb_n >= _sd_sb_cap) {
        if (_sd_sb_trunc != 0) { return 0; }
        if (_sd_sb_grow(_sd_sb_n + 1) == 0) {
            _sd_sb_trunc = 1;
            return 0;
        }
    }
    var e = _sd_sb_edges + _sd_sb_n * 32;
    store64(e + 0, ax);
    store64(e + 8, ay);
    store64(e + 16, bx);
    store64(e + 24, by);
    _sd_sb_n = _sd_sb_n + 1;
    return 0;
}

# Fill the batch into `cv` — MAX-combined, through the clip — and empty it. The default fill's
# sampling (SD_FILL_SS sub-scanlines, analytic x, sd_fill_impl's crossing formula and sort), but
# the nonzero walk opens a span where the winding leaves 0 and closes it where it returns, so
# overlapping pieces are one span and one floor. ⚠ And where the winding returns to 0 and the next
# crossing reopens within 1 LSB, the span carries on: two pieces that only TOUCH (a join
# triangle's side lies along the rect's end edge; consecutive rects share one) compute that shared
# line's crossing from different endpoints, each truncation under 1 LSB off the true x — so they
# differ by at most 1, and a split there would floor the pixel to 254 all over again (MEASURED,
# a 40-gon ring at width 5 under MITER: 53 pixels at 254 without the bridge; with it 2, both on the
# band's rim, where the round stroker reads 254 too).
# Bridging a real 1-LSB gap adds 1/4 of a raw unit of coverage. Rows outside the batch's y range
# are skipped: they would MAX a 0 into the coverage.
# Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_OOM when the row accumulator (or the area engine's) is refused —
# and sets _sd_stroke_oom either way, because the flushes _sd_sb_subpath makes mid-walk have no
# caller that can return anything (0.7.2).
# ⛔ THE COUNT IS TAKEN AND THE BATCH EMPTIED (`_sd_sb_n = 0`, two lines into the body) BEFORE
# ANYTHING HERE CAN FAIL, AND THAT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING — it is what makes a refused flush cost the
# caller a picture and not the process. An accumulator refused with the batch still full would leave
# the failed stroke's pieces sitting in it, and the NEXT styled stroke on a healthy allocator would
# fill them together with its own: MEASURED as a mutation (the accrow guard below putting the count
# back), a refused dashed stroke of the closed curve leaves 270 edges in the batch, and the next
# healthy stroke — of the straight line, a different shape — comes back with 261 of its 1,024 pixels
# carrying the curve's ink. This function must therefore keep every early return AFTER that line.
# programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr checks 43-45 gate it.
fn _sd_sb_flush(cv): i64 {
    var n = _sd_sb_n;
    var trunc = _sd_sb_trunc;
    _sd_sb_trunc = 0;
    # An edge the batch could not take is ink the caller asked for and did not get: report it here,
    # where the batch that lost it is consumed. _sd_sb_edge sets the flag only after a growth failed.
    if (trunc != 0) { _sd_stroke_oom = 1; }
    if (n == 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    _sd_sb_n = 0;
    var edges = _sd_sb_edges;
    var cross = _sd_fill_cross;         # the CURRENT block: _sd_sb_grow keeps its capacity >= n
    var w = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var h = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var cov = sd_canvas_coverage(cv);
    var stride = sd_canvas_stride(cv);
    # ⛔ SD_AA_AREA canvas: the exact engine, nonzero, MAX-combined through the clip. Every piece is
    # a closed polygon, so the list is whole unless _sd_sb_edge dropped an edge — which since 0.7.0
    # it records (_sd_sb_trunc), and only after a growth failed. A truncated batch takes the walk
    # below (a dropped edge would streak the area engine to the right edge). Through 0.6.0 a batch
    # AT its limit could not be told from a truncated one and always took the walk.
    if (sd_canvas_aa(cv) == SD_AA_AREA) {
        if (trunc == 0) {
            var mask = 0;
            var clipn = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET);
            if (clipn != 0) { mask = load64(clipn + 0); }
            # ⚠ SADISH_ERR_OOM out of here is the area engine's own (w + 1) * 8 accumulator being
            # refused (src/coverage.cyr); it writes no coverage when that happens.
            var arc = _sd_area_fill(edges, n, w, h, cov, stride, mask, 1, 1);
            if (arc != SADISH_OK) { _sd_stroke_oom = 1; }
            return arc;
        }
    }
    var ymin = load64(edges + 8);
    var ymax = ymin;
    var ei = 0;
    while (ei < n) {
        var ey0 = load64(edges + ei * 32 + 8);
        var ey1 = load64(edges + ei * 32 + 24);
        if (ey0 < ymin) { ymin = ey0; }
        if (ey1 < ymin) { ymin = ey1; }
        if (ey0 > ymax) { ymax = ey0; }
        if (ey1 > ymax) { ymax = ey1; }
        ei = ei + 1;
    }
    var py = sd_asr(ymin, SD_SHIFT);
    if (py < 0) { py = 0; }
    var py_end = sd_asr(ymax, SD_SHIFT) + 1;
    if (py_end > h) { py_end = h; }
    var ssy = SD_FILL_SS;
    var weight = SD_ONE / ssy;
    var accrow = _sd_fill_accrow_for(w);
    # ⛔ A REFUSED ACCUMULATOR IS REPORTED, NOT STORED THROUGH (0.7.2). Through 0.7.1 this 0 went
    # straight into the row loop below and killed the process — raster.cyr's _sd_fill_accrow_for
    # named this caller as the one that did not test it. The block comes from the GLOBAL alloc and
    # is only ever refused for a canvas wider than the allocator can serve a w*8 row for (MEASURED,
    # a hand-built 536,870,912 px header: w*8 = 4 GiB, over lib/alloc.cyr's ALLOC_MAX).
    # ⛔ THE BATCH IS ALREADY EMPTY HERE AND THIS RETURN MUST NOT PUT IT BACK — the header block on
    # this function says why (the next stroke would paint this one's pieces). The lost pieces are
    # the under-draw the code tells the caller about, and no coverage byte has been touched.
    if (accrow == 0) {
        _sd_stroke_oom = 1;
        return SADISH_ERR_OOM;
    }
    while (py < py_end) {
        var zi = 0;
        while (zi < w) {
            store64(accrow + zi * 8, 0);
            zi = zi + 1;
        }
        var s = 0;
        while (s < ssy) {
            var sy = (py << SD_SHIFT) + (s * SD_ONE + (SD_ONE >> 1)) / ssy;
            var nc = 0;
            ei = 0;
            while (ei < n) {
                var e = edges + ei * 32;
                var ay = load64(e + 8);
                var by = load64(e + 24);
                var ylo = ay;
                var yhi = by;
                var dir = 1;
                if (ay > by) {
                    ylo = by;
                    yhi = ay;
                    dir = 0 - 1;
                }
                if (sy >= ylo) {
                    if (sy < yhi) {
                        var ax = load64(e + 0);
                        var bx = load64(e + 16);
                        store64(cross + nc * 16, ax + ((bx - ax) * (sy - ay)) / (by - ay));
                        store64(cross + nc * 16 + 8, dir);
                        nc = nc + 1;
                    }
                }
                ei = ei + 1;
            }
            # ⭐ SHELL SORT, NOT INSERTION (0.11.2). This was O(nc^2) per sub-scanline, and nc is
            # the number of edges a scanline crosses — small for a convex shape, LARGE for a
            # self-intersecting one, which is exactly what an untrusted outline can be. The gap
            # sequence collapses the long shifts insertion sort makes when a crossing lands far
            # from its place; at small nc the final gap == 1 pass IS insertion sort, so the cheap
            # case stays cheap.
            # ⚠ SHELL SORT IS NOT STABLE AND INSERTION SORT WAS. That is safe HERE and only here:
            # two crossings with equal x bound a ZERO-WIDTH span, so whichever order they take, the
            # winding on the far side of the pair is the same and no pixel lies between them. The
            # 340-measurement oracle and both stroke suites are the proof, not this paragraph.
            var gap = nc / 2;
            while (gap > 0) {
                var a = gap;
                while (a < nc) {
                    var kx = load64(cross + a * 16);
                    var kd = load64(cross + a * 16 + 8);
                    var b = a;
                    var stop = 0;
                    while (stop == 0) {
                        if (b < gap) {
                            stop = 1;
                        } else {
                            if (load64(cross + (b - gap) * 16) > kx) {
                                store64(cross + b * 16, load64(cross + (b - gap) * 16));
                                store64(cross + b * 16 + 8, load64(cross + (b - gap) * 16 + 8));
                                b = b - gap;
                            } else {
                                stop = 1;
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    store64(cross + b * 16, kx);
                    store64(cross + b * 16 + 8, kd);
                    a = a + 1;
                }
                gap = gap / 2;
            }
            var wind = 0;
            var open = 0;
            var xs = 0;
            var ci = 0;
            while (ci < nc) {
                var cx = load64(cross + ci * 16);
                if (open == 0) {
                    xs = cx;
                    open = 1;
                }
                wind = wind + load64(cross + ci * 16 + 8);
                if (wind == 0) {
                    var bridge = 0;
                    if (ci + 1 < nc) {
                        if (load64(cross + (ci + 1) * 16) - cx <= 1) { bridge = 1; }
                    }
                    if (bridge == 0) {
                        sd_span_add(accrow, w, xs, cx, weight);
                        open = 0;
                    }
                }
                ci = ci + 1;
            }
            s = s + 1;
        }
        var px = 0;
        while (px < w) {
            var c = (load64(accrow + px * 8) * 255) >> SD_SHIFT;
            if (c > 255) { c = 255; }
            var clipnode = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET);
            if (clipnode != 0) {
                # ⛔ PACKED `py * w + px` — the clip mask is a w*h block, not a window into a wider
                # buffer, so the canvas stride is not its pitch (raster.cyr's SdCanvas layout note).
                c = (c * load8(load64(clipnode + 0) + py * w + px)) / 255;
            }
            var oldc = load8(cov + py * stride + px);   # coverage: the STRIDE, not w
            if (c < oldc) { c = oldc; }
            store8(cov + py * stride + px, c);
            px = px + 1;
        }
        py = py + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# The rect A+o, B+o, B-o, A-o — sd_stroke_seg's corners. With o = perp(B - A) * hw / |B - A| its
# shoelace sum is negative for every direction: the winding every batch piece is given.
fn _sd_sb_rect(ax, ay, bx, by, ox, oy): i64 {
    if (ox == 0) {
        if (oy == 0) { return 0; }
    }
    _sd_sb_edge(ax + ox, ay + oy, bx + ox, by + oy);
    _sd_sb_edge(bx + ox, by + oy, bx - ox, by - oy);
    _sd_sb_edge(bx - ox, by - oy, ax - ox, ay - oy);
    return _sd_sb_edge(ax - ox, ay - oy, ax + ox, ay + oy);
}

# max(|a|, |b|).
fn _sd_max_abs(a, b): i64 {
    var m = sd_abs(a);
    if (sd_abs(b) > m) { m = sd_abs(b); }
    return m;
}

# Sign (-1, 0, 1) of cross((ax, ay), (bx, by)). The four components are halved together until
# they are below 2^30, so the products cannot overflow at any magnitude.
fn _sd_cross_sign(ax, ay, bx, by): i64 {
    var a = ax;
    var b = ay;
    var c = bx;
    var d = by;
    while (_sd_max_abs(_sd_max_abs(a, b), _sd_max_abs(c, d)) >= 1073741824) {
        a = sd_asr(a, 1);
        b = sd_asr(b, 1);
        c = sd_asr(c, 1);
        d = sd_asr(d, 1);
    }
    var cr = a * d - b * c;
    if (cr < 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    if (cr > 0) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# A triangle, wound like the rect (negative shoelace) whatever order its corners arrive in;
# zero area emits nothing.
fn _sd_sb_tri(x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2): i64 {
    var sg = _sd_cross_sign(x1 - x0, y1 - y0, x2 - x0, y2 - y0);
    if (sg == 0) { return 0; }
    if (sg < 0) {
        _sd_sb_edge(x0, y0, x1, y1);
        _sd_sb_edge(x1, y1, x2, y2);
        return _sd_sb_edge(x2, y2, x0, y0);
    }
    _sd_sb_edge(x0, y0, x2, y2);
    _sd_sb_edge(x2, y2, x1, y1);
    return _sd_sb_edge(x1, y1, x0, y0);
}

# sd_stroke_disc's 16-gon at (cx, cy), walked backwards: increasing angle is the positive winding.
fn _sd_sb_disc(cx, cy, hw): i64 {
    var fx = cx + sd_fixed_mul(hw, load64(_sd_sb_trig + 15 * 16));
    var fy = cy + sd_fixed_mul(hw, load64(_sd_sb_trig + 15 * 16 + 8));
    var lx = fx;
    var ly = fy;
    var k = 14;
    while (k >= 0) {
        var px = cx + sd_fixed_mul(hw, load64(_sd_sb_trig + k * 16));
        var py = cy + sd_fixed_mul(hw, load64(_sd_sb_trig + k * 16 + 8));
        _sd_sb_edge(lx, ly, px, py);
        lx = px;
        ly = py;
        k = k - 1;
    }
    return _sd_sb_edge(lx, ly, fx, fy);
}

# ---- styled stroking: geometry -----------------------------------
# The perpendicular offset of segment A -> B, magnitude hw: (-dy, dx) * hw / |d|, the direction
# first rescaled by a power of two so its larger component is in [2^20, 2^21). Its rect, its caps
# and both joins call this with the same endpoints, so their shared corners coincide exactly.
# ⚠ Not sd_stroke_seg's `(dy * hw) / isqrt(len2)` on the raw deltas: for a 1-LSB diagonal that
# isqrt is 1, not 1.414, and the join corner lands 41 % of hw outside the band. Rescaled, |d| is
# exact to 2^-20 for any length, and no length overflows (|dy| < 2^21, so hw < 2^42).
# (0, 0) for a zero-length segment.
fn _sd_seg_normal(ax, ay, bx, by, hw): (i64, i64) {
    var dx = bx - ax;
    var dy = by - ay;
    if (dx == 0) {
        if (dy == 0) { return (0, 0); }
    }
    while (_sd_max_abs(dx, dy) >= 2097152) {
        dx = sd_asr(dx, 1);
        dy = sd_asr(dy, 1);
    }
    while (_sd_max_abs(dx, dy) < 1048576) {
        dx = dx << 1;
        dy = dy << 1;
    }
    var l = sd_isqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
    return (((0 - dy) * hw) / l, (dx * hw) / l);
}

# Rescale a direction by a power of two until its larger component is in [2^13, 2^14). Scaling
# does not change an angle; for 16.16 pixel coordinates the down-shifts only drop zero bits.
fn _sd_dir_reduce(dx, dy): (i64, i64) {
    var x = dx;
    var y = dy;
    if (x == 0) {
        if (y == 0) { return (0, 0); }
    }
    while (_sd_max_abs(x, y) >= 16384) {
        x = sd_asr(x, 1);
        y = sd_asr(y, 1);
    }
    while (_sd_max_abs(x, y) < 8192) {
        x = x << 1;
        y = y << 1;
    }
    return (x, y);
}

# The join at V between segments P -> V and V -> Q (both non-zero length). `inner` != 0 when V lies
# inside one curve's flattening: the style is replaced by a miter held to 1 + 0.25 px / hw (the
# flatten tolerance over the half-width) that falls back to the round join, not the bevel.
fn _sd_sb_join(px, py, vx, vy, qx, qy, hw, join, ml, inner): i64 {
    var n1x, n1y = _sd_seg_normal(px, py, vx, vy, hw);
    var n2x, n2y = _sd_seg_normal(vx, vy, qx, qy, hw);
    var cr = n1x * n2y - n1y * n2x;     # |n| <= hw, so < hw^2: no overflow
    if (cr == 0) {
        if (n1x * n2x + n1y * n2y >= 0) { return 0; }    # straight on
    }
    var jn = join;
    var lim = ml;
    if (inner != 0) {
        # <= 2^30 + 2^16 (hw >= 1): inside the limit test's L <= 2^31. Unclamped, as it needs no
        # clamp: the tip it admits lies within hw + 0.25 px of V at any hw.
        jn = SD_JOIN_MITER;
        lim = SD_ONE + ((SD_ONE >> 2) << SD_SHIFT) / hw;
    }
    if (jn == SD_JOIN_ROUND) { return _sd_sb_disc(vx, vy, hw); }
    if (cr == 0) {
        # A reversal: the bevel has no area. Inside a curve it is a cusp, which the disc rounds.
        if (inner != 0) { return _sd_sb_disc(vx, vy, hw); }
        return 0;
    }
    # cross > 0 turns toward +n, so the offsets diverge on the -n side, and vice versa.
    var sg = 1;
    if (cr > 0) { sg = 0 - 1; }
    var p1x = vx + sg * n1x;
    var p1y = vy + sg * n1y;
    var p2x = vx + sg * n2x;
    var p2y = vy + sg * n2y;
    _sd_sb_tri(vx, vy, p1x, p1y, p2x, p2y);
    if (jn == SD_JOIN_BEVEL) { return 0; }

    # MITER. cos(phi) = D / P for the turn phi = pi - theta; ratio^2 = 1 / cos^2(phi/2) =
    # 2P / (P + D). Past the limit when 2P > L^2 (P + D), i.e. L * u < P * 2^33 with u = L(P + D)
    # (L in 16.16), i.e. u < ceil(P * 2^33 / L) — exact in integers (see the header).
    var e1x, e1y = _sd_dir_reduce(vx - px, vy - py);
    var e2x, e2y = _sd_dir_reduce(qx - vx, qy - vy);
    var s1 = e1x * e1x + e1y * e1y;     # < 2^29
    var s2 = e2x * e2x + e2y * e2y;
    var pp = sd_isqrt(s1 * s2);         # |e1||e2| < 2^29
    var q = pp + e1x * e2x + e1y * e2y; # P + D, below 2^30
    # P >= 2^26 (both reduced components reach 2^13), so the right side is >= 1: a reversal
    # (q <= 0) always bevels here, and q > 0 below.
    if (lim * q < ((pp << 33) + lim - 1) / lim) {
        if (inner != 0) { return _sd_sb_disc(vx, vy, hw); }
        return 0;
    }
    # tip = V + s * hw * (u1 + u2) / (1 + cos(phi)), u the unit normals; over the common |e1||e2|
    # that is s * hw * W / (P + D) with W = (-(e1y |e2| + e2y |e1|), e1x |e2| + e2x |e1|).
    var l1 = sd_isqrt(s1);
    var l2 = sd_isqrt(s2);
    var tx = vx + sg * ((hw * (0 - (e1y * l2 + e2y * l1))) / q);
    var ty = vy + sg * ((hw * (e1x * l2 + e2x * l1)) / q);
    return _sd_sb_tri(p1x, p1y, tx, ty, p2x, p2y);
}

# A cap at V for the adjacent segment's offset (ox, oy); `fwd` = 1 at the subpath's end (extend
# along the segment), 0 at its start (extend backwards). e = (oy, -ox) is the direction * hw.
fn _sd_sb_endcap(vx, vy, ox, oy, fwd, cap, hw): i64 {
    if (cap == SD_CAP_ROUND) { return _sd_sb_disc(vx, vy, hw); }
    if (cap == SD_CAP_SQUARE) {
        if (fwd != 0) { return _sd_sb_rect(vx, vy, vx + oy, vy - ox, ox, oy); }
        return _sd_sb_rect(vx - oy, vy + ox, vx, vy, ox, oy);
    }
    return 0;
}

# Length (16.16) of segment j of an m-vertex subpath (j + 1 wraps to 0).
# ⚠ `run` is 16 B a point, x then y (0.9.0) — see sd_stroke_run.
fn _sd_sb_seglen(run, m, j): i64 {
    var bj = j + 1;
    if (bj == m) { bj = 0; }
    var dx = load64(run + bj * 16) - load64(run + j * 16);
    var dy = load64(run + bj * 16 + 8) - load64(run + j * 16 + 8);
    return sd_isqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
}

# One unit of a de-duplicated subpath: whatever sits at vertex i (the start cap, or the join),
# segment i's rect, and — after the LAST segment — the end cap or the closing join at vertex 0.
# `inner` is the curve-flag array parallel to `run` (1 = the vertex lies inside one curve verb's
# flattening): _sd_sb_inner for a whole path, the dash buffer's own for one dash (src/dash.cyr).
fn _sd_sb_unit(run, inner, m, closed, i, hw, cap, join, ml): i64 {
    var bi = i + 1;
    if (bi == m) { bi = 0; }
    var ax = load64(run + i * 16);
    var ay = load64(run + i * 16 + 8);
    var bx = load64(run + bi * 16);
    var by = load64(run + bi * 16 + 8);
    var ox, oy = _sd_seg_normal(ax, ay, bx, by, hw);
    if (i == 0) {
        if (closed == 0) { _sd_sb_endcap(ax, ay, ox, oy, 0, cap, hw); }
    } else {
        _sd_sb_join(load64(run + (i - 1) * 16), load64(run + (i - 1) * 16 + 8), ax, ay, bx, by,
          hw, join, ml, load8(inner + i));
    }
    _sd_sb_rect(ax, ay, bx, by, ox, oy);
    if (bi == 0) {
        # closed: the vertex-0 join, between this closing segment and segment 0 — a moveto point,
        # so never inside a curve. ⚠ run[1] is at +16 now, not +8 (0.9.0).
        _sd_sb_join(ax, ay, bx, by, load64(run + 16), load64(run + 16 + 8), hw, join, ml, 0);
    } else {
        if (closed == 0) {
            if (bi == m - 1) { _sd_sb_endcap(bx, by, ox, oy, 1, cap, hw); }
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

# Drop consecutive coincident points of run[0, n) IN PLACE (the write index never passes the read
# index), and — for a closed subpath — the trailing duplicate of point 0, so a zero-length closing
# segment makes no join. `inner` is the parallel curve-flag array, compacted with it: a kept point
# is inside a curve only if every point it absorbs is (where a verb's end meets a coincident curve
# point, the verbs' join style wins). Returns the count kept. ⚠ Every segment of the result has
# non-zero length, which the dash walk (src/dash.cyr) needs as much as the join math does.
fn _sd_sb_dedup(run, inner, n, closed): i64 {
    var m = 0;
    var k = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        var px = load64(run + k * 16);
        var py = load64(run + k * 16 + 8);
        var keep = 1;
        if (m > 0) {
            if (px == load64(run + (m - 1) * 16)) {
                if (py == load64(run + (m - 1) * 16 + 8)) { keep = 0; }
            }
        }
        if (keep != 0) {
            store64(run + m * 16, px);
            store64(run + m * 16 + 8, py);
            store8(inner + m, load8(inner + k));
            m = m + 1;
        } else {
            store8(inner + m - 1, load8(inner + m - 1) & load8(inner + k));
        }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    if (closed != 0) {
        if (m > 1) {
            if (load64(run + 0) == load64(run + (m - 1) * 16)) {
                if (load64(run + 8) == load64(run + (m - 1) * 16 + 8)) { m = m - 1; }
            }
        }
    }
    return m;
}

# Stroke one flattened subpath run (n points, curve flags in `inner`) into the batch.
fn _sd_sb_subpath(cv, run, inner, n, closed, hw, cap, join, ml): i64 {
    var m = _sd_sb_dedup(run, inner, n, closed);

    if (m == 1) {
        if (_sd_sb_room(SD_STROKE_UNIT_EDGES) == 0) { _sd_sb_flush(cv); }
        var vx = load64(run + 0);
        var vy = load64(run + 8);
        if (cap == SD_CAP_ROUND) { _sd_sb_disc(vx, vy, hw); }
        if (cap == SD_CAP_SQUARE) { _sd_sb_rect(vx - hw, vy, vx + hw, vy, 0, hw); }
        return 0;
    }

    var s = m - 1;
    if (closed != 0) { s = m; }
    var flushed = 0;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < s) {
        var need = SD_STROKE_UNIT_EDGES;
        if (closed != 0) {
            if (i == s - 1) { need = SD_STROKE_UNIT_EDGES * 17; }   # + the forward re-emit below
        }
        if (_sd_sb_room(need) == 0) {
            _sd_sb_flush(cv);
            if (i > 0) { flushed = 1; }
            # Re-emit the stretch before unit i so its pieces meet unit i inside one fill.
            var j = i;
            var back = 0;
            var going = 1;
            while (going != 0) {
                going = 0;
                if (j > 0) {
                    if (i - j < 16) {
                        if (back < 2 * hw + 3 * SD_ONE) {
                            j = j - 1;
                            back = back + _sd_sb_seglen(run, m, j);
                            going = 1;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            while (j < i) {
                _sd_sb_unit(run, inner, m, closed, j, hw, cap, join, ml);
                j = j + 1;
            }
        }
        _sd_sb_unit(run, inner, m, closed, i, hw, cap, join, ml);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (closed != 0) {
        if (flushed != 0) {
            # The vertex-0 join just went into a batch that may not hold segment 0's side of it.
            var f = 0;
            var ahead = 0;
            while (f < 16) {
                if (ahead < 2 * hw + 3 * SD_ONE) {
                    if (f < s - 1) {
                        _sd_sb_unit(run, inner, m, closed, f, hw, cap, join, ml);
                        ahead = ahead + _sd_sb_seglen(run, m, f);
                    }
                }
                f = f + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

# (Re)open a subpath at (cx, cy) when a drawing verb follows a CLOSE: the run restarts at the
# moveto point, as the fill restarts its edges there.
# ⚠ `has` REPLACES THE OLD `cur != 0` TEST (0.9.0): the current point is a coordinate pair now and
# (0, 0) is the origin, so the caller passes its own flag — a subpath restarting at the origin used
# to be dropped by a pointer test and no longer can be.
fn _sd_sb_restart(run, run_n, cx, cy, has): i64 {
    if (run_n == 0) {
        if (has != 0) {
            store64(run + 0, cx);
            store64(run + 8, cy);
            return 1;
        }
    }
    return run_n;
}

# Flag run[n0, n) as one curve verb's flattening: every point is inside the curve but the last,
# the verb's own end point, where the next verb's join style applies.
fn _sd_sb_mark_curve(n0, n): i64 {
    var k = n0;
    while (k < n) {
        store8(_sd_sb_inner + k, 1);
        k = k + 1;
    }
    if (n > n0) { store8(_sd_sb_inner + n - 1, 0); }
    return 0;
}

# One subpath of the run, to the batch — whole (`dashed` == 0) or cut into dashes (src/dash.cyr).
# ⚠ The ONE call that runs from this file into dash.cyr, and the reason the [lib] order reads
# stroke.cyr then dash.cyr: dash.cyr is built ON the stroker (it cuts a run into pieces and hands
# each back to _sd_sb_subpath), and this is the hook that lets both entry points share ONE verb
# walk, so a flatten fix cannot reach solid strokes and miss dashed ones.
fn _sd_sb_dispatch(cv, run_n, closed, hw, cap, join, ml, dashed): i64 {
    if (dashed != 0) {
        return _sd_dash_subpath(cv, _sd_stroke_run, _sd_sb_inner, run_n, closed, hw, cap, join, ml);
    }
    return _sd_sb_subpath(cv, _sd_stroke_run, _sd_sb_inner, run_n, closed, hw, cap, join, ml);
}

# The styled stroker's verb walk: flatten `path`'s curve verbs at tol = SD_ONE >> 2 into the
# stroker's run (raster.cyr's scratch) with the parallel curve flags, hand each subpath to
# _sd_sb_dispatch, and fill the batch once at the end. Both public styled entry points are this
# walk plus their own argument checks; the caller has already run _sd_scratch_init / _sd_sb_init
# and cleared _sd_run_trunc.
# ⚠ The run and its curve flags GROW (0.7.0): read _sd_stroke_run at each use, never a copy taken
# before a push.
#
# Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_OOM if anything this call reached was refused (0.7.2): a curve's
# flatten mid-points, a run point or curve flag the growth could not take, an edge the batch could
# not take, a dash piece the buffer could not take, the flush's accumulator. ⛔ THE WALK RUNS TO THE
# END EITHER WAY and the batch is still flushed — the report is collected at the bottom, not thrown
# at the first refusal, so a stroke that loses one curve's mid-points still draws every other piece
# it can. What is on the canvas afterwards is that prefix (the header's no-rollback rule).
fn _sd_styled_walk(cv, path, hw, cap, join, ml, dashed): i64 {
    var n_verbs = sd_path_verb_count(path);
    var tol = SD_ONE >> 2;
    var run_n = 0;
    var fbuf = _sd_stroke_fbuf;
    var fctx = _sd_stroke_fctx;
    _sd_stroke_oom = 0;
    # The flatten's truncation verdict, scoped to this call as sd_fill_impl scopes it; the caller's
    # standing verdict is put back at the bottom. (sd_canvas_stroke_path does the same.)
    var flat_was = _sd_flat_trunc;
    _sd_flat_trunc = 0;

    # ⛔ COORDINATES AND `has_cur` (0.9.0) — src/raster.cyr's fill walk, applied to the run: the
    # current point is a pair of numbers, so 0 is the origin and the "no moveto yet" test is a flag.
    var verbs = load64(path + SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET);
    var curx = 0;
    var cury = 0;
    var has_cur = 0;
    var startx = 0;
    var starty = 0;
    var vi = 0;
    var pi = 0;
    while (vi < n_verbs) {
        var verb = load64(verbs + vi * 8);
        if (verb == SD_VERB_MOVETO) {
            if (run_n > 0) { _sd_sb_dispatch(cv, run_n, 0, hw, cap, join, ml, dashed); }
            curx = sd_path_point_x(path, pi);
            cury = sd_path_point_y(path, pi);
            has_cur = 1;
            startx = curx;
            starty = cury;
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + 0, curx);
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + 8, cury);
            run_n = 1;
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_LINETO) {
            if (has_cur != 0) {         # a drawing verb before any moveto is skipped
                run_n = _sd_sb_restart(_sd_stroke_run, run_n, curx, cury, has_cur);
                curx = sd_path_point_x(path, pi);
                cury = sd_path_point_y(path, pi);
                run_n = _sd_run_push(run_n, curx, cury, 1);
            }
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_QUADTO) {
            if (has_cur == 0) { pi = pi + 2; vi = vi + 1; continue; }
            run_n = _sd_sb_restart(_sd_stroke_run, run_n, curx, cury, has_cur);
            var qex = sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 1);
            var qey = sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 1);
            store64(fctx + 0, fbuf);
            store64(fctx + 8, 0);
            store64(fctx + 16, SD_FLATTEN_PCAP);
            _sd_flatten_quad_xy(fctx, curx, cury, sd_path_point_x(path, pi),
              sd_path_point_y(path, pi), qex, qey, tol, SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
            var qn0 = run_n;
            run_n = _sd_run_append(run_n, fctx, 1);
            _sd_sb_mark_curve(qn0, run_n);
            curx = qex;
            cury = qey;
            pi = pi + 2;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_CUBICTO) {
            if (has_cur == 0) { pi = pi + 3; vi = vi + 1; continue; }
            run_n = _sd_sb_restart(_sd_stroke_run, run_n, curx, cury, has_cur);
            var kex = sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 2);
            var key = sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 2);
            store64(fctx + 0, fbuf);
            store64(fctx + 8, 0);
            store64(fctx + 16, SD_FLATTEN_PCAP);
            _sd_flatten_cubic_xy(fctx, curx, cury, sd_path_point_x(path, pi),
              sd_path_point_y(path, pi), sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 1),
              sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 1), kex, key, tol, SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
            var cn0 = run_n;
            run_n = _sd_run_append(run_n, fctx, 1);
            _sd_sb_mark_curve(cn0, run_n);
            curx = kex;
            cury = key;
            pi = pi + 3;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_CLOSE) {
            if (run_n > 0) { _sd_sb_dispatch(cv, run_n, 1, hw, cap, join, ml, dashed); }
            run_n = 0;
            curx = startx;
            cury = starty;
        }
        vi = vi + 1;
    }
    if (run_n > 0) { _sd_sb_dispatch(cv, run_n, 0, hw, cap, join, ml, dashed); }
    # ⚠ The final flush's own code is not read here: it SETS _sd_stroke_oom, which is also the only
    # way the mid-walk flushes (_sd_sb_subpath's, four frames down) can report anything at all.
    _sd_sb_flush(cv);

    var rc = SADISH_OK;
    if (_sd_flat_trunc != 0) { rc = SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (flat_was != 0) { _sd_flat_trunc = 1; }
    if (_sd_run_trunc != 0) { rc = SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (_sd_stroke_oom != 0) { rc = SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    return rc;
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex — stroke `path` at `width` (16.16 full width) with cap style `cap`
# (SD_CAP_BUTT | SD_CAP_ROUND | SD_CAP_SQUARE), join style `join` (SD_JOIN_MITER |
# SD_JOIN_ROUND | SD_JOIN_BEVEL) and `miter_limit` (16.16 ratio, SVG stroke-miterlimit; SVG's
# default is 4.0 = 4 * SD_ONE; used only by SD_JOIN_MITER), MAX-combined into `cv`'s coverage.
#
# Returns SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for cv == 0 or an unknown cap/join (nothing drawn),
# SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH for a 0 or verb-less path, SADISH_ERR_OOM when an allocation this stroke
# reached was refused (the header's refusal block — the canvas keeps the prefix that was drawn),
# SADISH_OK otherwise — width <= 0 draws nothing.
# ⚠ SD_CAP_ROUND + SD_JOIN_ROUND IS sd_canvas_stroke_path, exactly — and so, alone among the nine
# pairs, keeps its 0.4.0 geometry: a drawing verb after CLOSE continues the closed run instead of
# starting at the moveto point, and pieces meet by per-pass MAX (a pixel two of them split reads
# the larger part, not the sum). Semantics + bounds: the header of this file.
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex(cv, path, width, cap, join, miter_limit): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    var n_verbs = sd_path_verb_count(path);
    if (n_verbs == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    if (cap < SD_CAP_BUTT) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (cap > SD_CAP_SQUARE) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (join < SD_JOIN_MITER) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (join > SD_JOIN_BEVEL) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (cap == SD_CAP_ROUND) {
        if (join == SD_JOIN_ROUND) { return sd_canvas_stroke_path(cv, path, width); }
    }
    var hw = sd_asr(width, 1);
    if (hw <= 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var ml = miter_limit;
    if (ml < SD_ONE) { ml = SD_ONE; }
    if (ml > SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX) { ml = SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX; }

    # The stroker's run/fbuf/fctx (raster.cyr) stay live across the batch flushes; the batch is
    # this file's own scratch. ⛔ Each guard is on the BLOCK the walk stores through, not on the
    # initialiser's status — sd_fill_impl's reasoning; both publishes are all-or-nothing.
    _sd_scratch_init();
    if (_sd_stroke_run == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    _sd_sb_init();                      # the batch is empty: every call ends in _sd_sb_flush
    if (_sd_sb_edges == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    _sd_run_trunc = 0;
    return _sd_styled_walk(cv, path, hw, cap, join, ml, 0);
}

# --- dash.cyr ---

# @public — sadish dashed strokes: the styled stroker's flattened run, cut into dashes.
#
# dash.cyr — ONE entry point over stroke.cyr's styled machinery:
#   sd_canvas_stroke_path_dash(cv, path, width, cap, join, miter_limit, pattern, n, offset)
# It shares sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex's verb walk (_sd_styled_walk, stroke.cyr) verb for verb — the
# same flattening at tol = SD_ONE >> 2, the same run, the same curve flags — and differs only in
# what each SUBPATH does: _sd_sb_subpath strokes it whole, _sd_dash_subpath (below) cuts it into
# on-dashes and hands each one back to _sd_sb_subpath as an OPEN subpath. So a dash is stroked by
# exactly the code a solid stroke is, into exactly the same edge batch, and one call is still one
# fill: caps, joins, the miter limit, the inside-a-curve join rule, the clip, the SD_AA_AREA
# dispatch and the 0 B-once-warm promise all hold unchanged, for free.
#
# SEMANTICS (SVG `stroke-dasharray` / `stroke-dashoffset`).
#   · `pattern` points at `n` i64 dash lengths in 16.16, on, off, on, off, … `offset` is a 16.16
#     distance INTO the pattern and may be negative.
#   · An ODD `n` behaves as the array repeated twice, as SVG says: [a] is [a, a], [a, b, c] is
#     [a, b, c, a, b, c]. The array is never copied — the walk indexes it modulo n over a doubled
#     entry count.
#   · `pattern` == 0 or `n` <= 0 or an ALL-ZERO array: a solid stroke, and literally so — the call
#     tail-calls sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex, so the bytes are identical for every cap/join pair,
#     SD_CAP_ROUND + SD_JOIN_ROUND (which _ex sends to the 0.4.0 stroker) included.
#   · Any NEGATIVE entry is SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS with nothing drawn, as is a period that overflows
#     i64 (the sum, doubled for an odd n).
#   · The pattern RESTARTS at every subpath, phase and all — SVG's behaviour, and the reason the
#     state below is reset per subpath rather than per call.
#   · Caps at BOTH ends of every on-dash; joins at the vertices INSIDE one. That is precisely
#     "_sd_sb_subpath with closed = 0", which is why it is what a dash is handed to.
#   · A zero-length on-dash is a DOT: one point, which _sd_sb_subpath's lone-point branch draws as
#     a disc under ROUND, an axis-aligned square of side 2*hw under SQUARE and nothing under BUTT
#     — SVG's rule, and the same geometry a lone moveto already gets (stroke.cyr's header).
#   · ⛔ AND A DOT IS A ZERO-LENGTH *ENTRY*, NOT A DASH THE PATH RAN OUT OF. Where a boundary falls
#     EXACTLY on an open subpath's last vertex and the pattern turns on there, the on-entry has a
#     positive length and no path left to spend it on: nothing is drawn. (Otherwise "M 2,4 L 18,4"
#     under [4, 12] — one 4 px dash and a 12 px gap — would put a full ROUND/SQUARE cap-width of
#     ink at x = 18, eleven px inside the gap the pattern declares empty.) The two cases are told
#     apart by the piece: a zero-length ENTRY turns twice at one position and makes a two-point
#     piece; a truncated dash is a lone start point. A CLOSED subpath has no such end — its last
#     vertex is the wrap, below.
#
# ⭐ A CLOSED SUBPATH DASHES AROUND ITS CLOSING POINT, AND THAT MEETING IS A JOIN. If the phase is
# ON at distance 0, the first dash is not stroked where it ends: it is KEPT (the "head", at the
# front of the piece buffer) while the rest of the subpath dashes past it. If the walk is still
# inside a dash when it arrives back at the moveto point, that last dash's points and the head's
# are CONCATENATED into one open piece — …, p[m-1], p[0], p[1], … — so p[0] is an interior vertex
# and takes the JOIN style, not two caps. MEASURED on the 24 px square (4,4)-(28,28) at width 4
# under BUTT/MITER with [16, 16] at offset 8 — where the last dash runs from 8 px before the
# closing vertex to 8 px past it: the outer corner pixel (2, 2) reads 255 through the join, and 0
# with the same two dashes stroked apart, which is the square notch two butt caps leave.
#   · If the walk arrives back in a GAP, the head is stroked on its own instead — capped at both
#     ends, which is right: the gap really does end at p[0].
#   · If no boundary falls on the subpath at all (a period longer than the perimeter), the head is
#     never closed and the whole thing is stroked as a CLOSED subpath — no caps anywhere, exactly
#     sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex's bytes.
#   · ⚠ A head that is a zero-length DOT at p[0] concatenates to nothing (it contributes only the
#     point the wrap dash already ends at), so the dot shows as that dash's end cap rather than as
#     its own piece. Under ROUND the two are the same disc; under SQUARE the cap is oriented along
#     the path where the lone dot would be axis-aligned.
#
# ⚠ DASH LENGTHS FOLLOW THE FLATTENED POLYLINE — a CHORD-LENGTH APPROXIMATION of arc length, and
# always an UNDER-estimate, so on a curve the dashes run slightly long and every boundary after the
# first sits slightly FURTHER along the curve than the exact arc length would put it. The shortfall
# of one chord of sagitta s spanning a turn of curvature radius R is about s / (3R) of its own
# length, so it grows as a curve tightens and is exactly zero on a straight run. MEASURED, at the
# stroker's tol = 0.25 px:
#   · a radius-20 px circle (4 kappa cubics) flattens to 16 chords totalling 8,184,008 raw against
#     8,236,106 at tol / 64 — 52,098 short, 0.63 % of the circumference, 0.79 px over 125.7 px
#     (s = 0.38 px, R = 20 px: s / 3R = 0.64 %);
#   · the S-curve "M 4,30 C 20,4 44,56 60,30" flattens to 12 chords of 4,285,212 raw against
#     4,297,280 — 12,068 short, 0.28 %;
#   · a straight polyline: 0, at any tolerance.
# ⇒ A dash boundary on a radius-20 px arc lands about 0.006 px further along per px of arc before
# it. A consumer that needs tighter dashes on tight curves flattens the path itself first.
#
# ⚠ TRUNCATION DOES NOT ACCUMULATE VISIBLY. Each segment's length is sd_isqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy), the
# FLOOR of the true 16.16 length, so it is short by less than 1 raw unit = 2^-16 px, one-sided; the
# walk's position is therefore short by less than (number of segments) raw units, and dash
# boundaries creep FORWARD along the path by that much and no more. Over a 1,000-segment path that
# is under 0.0153 px; a full pixel of creep needs 65,536 segments. MEASURED, 1,000 segments of
# (1, 1) px: the walk's total is 1,000 * 92,681 = 92,681,000 raw against the chord's own single
# isqrt of 92,681,900 — 900 raw units, 0.0137 px, inside the bound; at 100 segments, 90. In the
# raster that is worth at most ONE coverage level (the 100-segment diagonal against its chord,
# MEASURED). ⭐ And where the math is EXACT the creep is exactly ZERO: for an axis-aligned polyline
# sd_isqrt returns |dx| exactly, so a 1,000-segment horizontal line dashes BYTE-IDENTICALLY to the
# 2-point chord of the same length (programs/dash_test.cyr group K).
# ⚠ A cut point is a + d * pos / seg, truncated per coordinate: under 1 raw unit off the exact cut,
# per cut, never accumulating. MAGNITUDES: |d| <= seg, so d * pos <= seg^2 — a flattened segment
# below 32,768 px (seg < 2^31 raw) keeps it under 2^62. Same family of bound as the stroker's
# ~46,000 px edge extents.
#
# ⚠ COST. One on-dash is one piece of 4-40 edges in the batch, so a period much shorter than the
# path makes a very large batch (which grows, and is never returned — stroke.cyr's monotone note).
# A consumer that needs a bound sets SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP, which makes the batch flush instead.
# ⚠ Single-threaded, like the rest of the stroker: one piece buffer and one phase for the process.

# ---- the dash phase, per subpath ---------------------------------
# ⛔ Set by sd_canvas_stroke_path_dash and re-initialised at every subpath (SVG restarts the
# pattern there). Nothing here survives a call; it is state, not cache.
var _sd_dash_pat = 0;       # the caller's array — never copied, never written
var _sd_dash_n = 0;         # entries in it
var _sd_dash_nn = 0;        # entries the walk sees: n, or 2n for an odd n
var _sd_dash_period = 0;    # their total (16.16) — > 0 whenever the walk runs
var _sd_dash_off = 0;       # the caller's stroke-dashoffset (16.16, any sign)
var _sd_dash_di = 0;        # the entry the walk is in, in [0, nn); EVEN is an on-dash
var _sd_dash_rem = 0;       # length left in it (16.16)
var _sd_dash_on = 0;        # 1 inside an on-dash — always 1 - (_sd_dash_di & 1)

# ---- the piece buffer --------------------------------------------
# ⛔ Process-lifetime, from the GLOBAL alloc, allocated by the first DASHED stroke — not by
# _sd_scratch_init or _sd_sb_init, so a process that never dashes pays nothing and every figure
# programs/alloc_test.cyr pins stays exact. Same reason as raster.cyr's scratch: a consumer's hook
# is an arena that gets reset. MEASURED one-time cost 139,264 B (8,192 * 16 B of INLINE points +
# 8,192 flag bytes); after it a dashed straight-line stroke costs the heap 0 B, and curves only
# their flatten mid-points, exactly as a solid one does. ⚠ It was 73,792 B through 0.8.0 (8,192 * 8
# pointers + the flags + 64 B of cut-point records): the point block doubled and the records went
# away with it (0.9.0).
# ⭐ It GROWS by doubling (like the run and the batch), because the wrap concatenation can hold the
# head piece AND the last dash AND the head's points again: up to about 2 * (run + 2) slots.
# MEASURED, one dash of 9,013 points: 278,528 B to reach 16,384 slots, once (16,384 * 16 B of
# points + 16,384 flag bytes — only the POINT block's element doubled in 0.9.0, the flags' did not,
# so this is not 0.8.0's 147,456 B doubled; programs/dash_test.cyr check #127 pins it); a repeat 0 B. A failed
# growth drops the point and sets _sd_dash_trunc — the same degradation _sd_run_trunc is, and the
# same one the batch takes: a prefix of the path, recorded, never a write past the array.
var _sd_dash_run = 0;       # _sd_dash_cap * 16 — INLINE (x, y), the piece(s) being built
var _sd_dash_flags = 0;     # _sd_dash_cap bytes — the curve flags parallel to it
var _sd_dash_cap = 0;       # slots the two blocks hold
var _sd_dash_dn = 0;        # slots written
var _sd_dash_pstart = 0;    # where the piece being built starts
var _sd_dash_head = 0;      # slots in the kept head piece of a closed subpath (0 = none)
var _sd_dash_wrap = 0;      # 1 while that head is still open
var _sd_dash_trunc = 0;     # 1 if THIS stroke dropped a piece point (a growth failed)
# ⛔⛔ THE FOUR SdPoint RECORDS ARE GONE (0.9.0), AND WITH THEM A WHOLE CLASS OF BUG. A dash
# boundary that falls inside a segment is a point no SdPath owns; through 0.8.0 the piece buffer
# held POINTERS, so a cut point needed a record somewhere — sd_point_new would have put one on the
# consumer's hook per cut, per call, so four process-lifetime records were SHARED and REUSED
# instead: a piece's start (+0) and end (+16), plus two more (+32, +48) that the kept head of a
# closed subpath had to be MOVED onto, because the shared pair was overwritten by the next dash
# while the head was still live. A head start left on the shared record was stroked through
# whatever the LAST dash started at — a segment between two unrelated points, drawn whenever the
# walk came back inside a gap (programs/dash_test.cyr group S2, checks 166-171).
# ⇒ The buffer holds COORDINATES now, so every piece point — cut or vertex — is a COPY the moment
# it is put, nothing aliases anything, and the kept head cannot be written through by a later dash.
# The four records, the aliasing rule, the `s0 == _sd_dash_rec` identity test and the 64 B block
# are all deleted rather than kept working; group S2 still gates the pixels they were about.

# Allocate the piece buffer, its curve flags and the four cut-point records once (idempotent).
# Returns 1 when they are ready, 0 when a block was refused.
# ⛔ ALL OR NOTHING, AND `_sd_dash_run != 0` IS THE FLAG — _sd_scratch_init's and _sd_sb_init's rule
# (0.7.2). Through 0.7.1 the blocks went straight into the globals unchecked, so a refusal of the
# flags (or, through 0.8.0, of the 64 B cut-point records) left _sd_dash_run published and every
# later dashed stroke wrote its flags through address 0 — for the life of the process, since this
# function never runs a second time. ⚠ GLOBAL alloc, never sd_alloc (raster.cyr's scratch rule): no
# consumer hook can refuse these, and the capacity is the only honest way to drive the path
# (programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr raises SD_FLATTEN_CAP past ALLOC_MAX / 8).
# ⚠ THE TWO 0-TESTS HERE ARE A CONTRACT, NOT TWO GATES — and the arithmetic is this function's
# own, NOT the one stroke.cyr's _sd_sb_init makes (that one is a gate, because its `inner` and its
# `edges` are sized by two DIFFERENT knobs and either can be the larger; read the ⛔ block there).
# Both blocks below come from the one `cap`: run = cap * 16 (0.9.0; cap * 8 of pointers before),
# flags = cap. lib/alloc.cyr refuses BY SIZE, so `run` — the block sd_canvas_stroke_path_dash
# guards on — is always the larger, and no capacity refuses `flags` while granting it. MEASURED at
# the tightest cap that separates them, which the 16 B slot HALVED to 134,217,729:
# alloc(cap * 16) = 2,147,483,664 B is refused (over ALLOC_MAX = 2 GiB) while alloc(cap) is
# granted — so 0.7.1's unchecked stores still leave `_sd_dash_run` at 0, the entry point still
# answers SADISH_ERR_OOM, and the next call still re-initialises. Group G3 of
# programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr drives the same shape at SD_FLATTEN_CAP = 3,000,000,000, where both
# sizes are refused together.
# ⇒ The two tests are kept, and they are load-bearing for a reason no TEST can reach: a bump
# allocator also refuses when the heap cannot be extended, and `run` granted with `flags` refused
# then publishes a 0 that _sd_dash_put's store8 writes through. "Contract, not a gate" here means
# only that no honest suite can drive it on THIS allocator — not that the tests are decoration.
fn _sd_dash_init(): i64 {
    if (_sd_dash_run != 0) { return 1; }
    var cap = SD_FLATTEN_CAP;
    var run = alloc(cap * 16);          # 16 B a point, inline (0.9.0): cap * 8 of pointers before
    var flags = alloc(cap);
    var ok = 1;
    if (run == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (flags == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (ok == 0) { return 0; }
    _sd_dash_cap = cap;
    _sd_dash_run = run;
    _sd_dash_flags = flags;
    return 1;
}

# Grow both blocks to hold at least `need` slots, doubling, the OLD CAPACITY copied (not the live
# count: the flags at and past the count are read back by nothing today, but copying the capacity
# costs one pass over the flags' own size and needs no argument about who writes what when —
# stroke.cyr's _sd_inner_grow makes the same call for the same reason). Returns 1, or 0 when an
# allocation failed, in which case both blocks are kept exactly as they were.
fn _sd_dash_grow(need): i64 {
    if (_sd_dash_trunc != 0) { return 0; }
    var nc = _sd_dash_cap;
    while (nc < need) { nc = nc * 2; }
    var nr = _sd_grow_alloc(nc * 16);
    if (nr == 0) { return 0; }
    var nf = _sd_grow_alloc(nc);
    if (nf == 0) { return 0; }
    memcpy(nr, _sd_dash_run, _sd_dash_cap * 16);
    memcpy(nf, _sd_dash_flags, _sd_dash_cap);
    _sd_dash_run = nr;
    _sd_dash_flags = nf;
    _sd_dash_cap = nc;
    return 1;
}

# Append the point (x, y) with curve flag `flag` to the piece being built — a COPY of the two
# coordinates, owned by the buffer (0.9.0; an SdPoint POINTER through 0.8.0).
# ⚠ A point the growth cannot take is DROPPED, as it always was — and since 0.7.2 it also raises
# the styled walk's refusal flag, so sd_canvas_stroke_path_dash returns SADISH_ERR_OOM instead of
# SADISH_OK over a dash that stops early. _sd_dash_trunc keeps its own meaning and its own gates.
fn _sd_dash_put(x, y, flag): i64 {
    if (_sd_dash_dn >= _sd_dash_cap) {
        if (_sd_dash_grow(_sd_dash_dn + 1) == 0) {
            _sd_dash_trunc = 1;
            _sd_stroke_oom = 1;
            return 0;
        }
    }
    store64(_sd_dash_run + _sd_dash_dn * 16, x);
    store64(_sd_dash_run + _sd_dash_dn * 16 + 8, y);
    store8(_sd_dash_flags + _sd_dash_dn, flag);
    _sd_dash_dn = _sd_dash_dn + 1;
    return 0;
}

# The length (16.16) of pattern entry `i` of the doubled view: an odd n wraps modulo n, which is
# what makes [a, b, c] behave as [a, b, c, a, b, c] without a copy.
fn _sd_dash_len(i): i64 {
    return load64(_sd_dash_pat + (i % _sd_dash_n) * 8);
}

# Move to the next pattern entry. `_sd_dash_on` is flipped by the caller, which keeps it equal to
# 1 - (_sd_dash_di & 1) — an even entry is an on-dash.
fn _sd_dash_step(): i64 {
    _sd_dash_di = _sd_dash_di + 1;
    if (_sd_dash_di >= _sd_dash_nn) { _sd_dash_di = 0; }
    _sd_dash_rem = _sd_dash_len(_sd_dash_di);
    return 0;
}

# Place the phase at _sd_dash_off into the pattern: the entry it falls in and what is left of it.
# The offset is reduced modulo the period first — written as a subtraction rather than `%` so it
# lands in [0, period) whether integer division truncates toward zero or floors. Zero-length
# entries are stepped over here (they can only fire AT a position, which the walk handles), and the
# loop is bounded by nn steps: the period is > 0, so one lap always exhausts a phase below it.
fn _sd_dash_phase_init(): i64 {
    var ph = _sd_dash_off - (_sd_dash_off / _sd_dash_period) * _sd_dash_period;
    if (ph < 0) { ph = ph + _sd_dash_period; }
    _sd_dash_di = 0;
    var guard = 0;
    var going = 1;
    while (going != 0) {
        going = 0;
        if (ph > 0) {
            if (guard <= _sd_dash_nn) {
                var l = _sd_dash_len(_sd_dash_di);
                if (ph >= l) {
                    ph = ph - l;
                    _sd_dash_di = _sd_dash_di + 1;
                    if (_sd_dash_di >= _sd_dash_nn) { _sd_dash_di = 0; }
                    guard = guard + 1;
                    going = 1;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    _sd_dash_rem = _sd_dash_len(_sd_dash_di) - ph;
    _sd_dash_on = 1 - (_sd_dash_di & 1);
    return 0;
}

# Stroke the piece being built as an OPEN subpath — a cap at each end, the join style at every
# vertex inside it — and rewind the buffer to where it started.
fn _sd_dash_emit(cv, hw, cap, join, ml): i64 {
    var cnt = _sd_dash_dn - _sd_dash_pstart;
    if (cnt > 0) {
        _sd_sb_subpath(cv, _sd_dash_run + _sd_dash_pstart * 16, _sd_dash_flags + _sd_dash_pstart,
          cnt, 0, hw, cap, join, ml);
    }
    _sd_dash_dn = _sd_dash_pstart;
    return 0;
}

# The current pattern entry runs out at (x, y): end the on-dash there (its end cap) or begin one.
fn _sd_dash_turn(cv, x, y, hw, cap, join, ml): i64 {
    if (_sd_dash_on != 0) {
        _sd_dash_put(x, y, 0);
        if (_sd_dash_wrap != 0) {
            # The first dash of a closed subpath: KEEP it for the wrap (see the header). ⭐ Nothing
            # has to be moved off a shared record any more (0.9.0): every point in the buffer is a
            # COPY, so the head's own start and end cannot be overwritten by the dashes that follow
            # it. Through 0.8.0 both ends were re-homed onto two extra SdPoint records here, and a
            # head start left on the shared one was stroked through whatever the LAST dash started
            # at (programs/dash_test.cyr group S2 is the gate, and still passes unedited).
            _sd_dash_head = _sd_dash_dn;
            _sd_dash_pstart = _sd_dash_dn;
            _sd_dash_wrap = 0;
        } else {
            _sd_dash_emit(cv, hw, cap, join, ml);
        }
        _sd_dash_on = 0;
    } else {
        _sd_dash_pstart = _sd_dash_dn;
        _sd_dash_put(x, y, 0);
        _sd_dash_on = 1;
    }
    _sd_dash_step();
    return 0;
}

# Fire every ZERO-length pattern entry sitting at (x, y). An on-dash of zero length is a dot; an
# off-gap of zero length joins the dashes either side of it into two pieces that touch. Bounded by
# nn + 1 turns: the period is > 0, so at most nn - 1 entries in a row can be zero.
fn _sd_dash_zeros(cv, x, y, hw, cap, join, ml): i64 {
    var guard = 0;
    var going = 1;
    while (going != 0) {
        going = 0;
        if (_sd_dash_rem == 0) {
            if (guard <= _sd_dash_nn) {
                _sd_dash_turn(cv, x, y, hw, cap, join, ml);
                guard = guard + 1;
                going = 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

# ================================================================
# One subpath of the flattened run, cut into dashes — _sd_styled_walk's dashed branch, called
# exactly where it calls _sd_sb_subpath for a solid stroke, with the same arguments.
#
# `run` holds n INLINE points — 16 B each, x then y (0.9.0; SdPoint ptrs through 0.8.0) — and
# `inner` their curve flags. The run is DE-DUPLICATED first (the
# stroker's own _sd_sb_dedup, in place), which is what makes every segment's length non-zero —
# without it a zero-length segment would divide by zero at a cut. Each on-dash is copied into the
# piece buffer and handed back to _sd_sb_subpath as an open subpath.
# ⚠ `inner`'s slot 0 is never written by the verb walk (a moveto point stores no flag), so the
# closing vertex of a closed subpath is read as flag 0 — right, and not a stale byte: p[0] is a
# moveto point and so never inside a curve, which is the same call stroke.cyr's closing join makes.
# ================================================================
fn _sd_dash_subpath(cv, run, inner, n, closed, hw, cap, join, ml): i64 {
    var m = _sd_sb_dedup(run, inner, n, closed);
    if (m < 1) { return 0; }
    _sd_dash_init();
    _sd_dash_phase_init();
    _sd_dash_dn = 0;
    _sd_dash_pstart = 0;
    _sd_dash_head = 0;
    _sd_dash_wrap = 0;

    var p0x = load64(run + 0);
    var p0y = load64(run + 8);
    if (m == 1) {
        # No length to walk: the lone point is inked only when the phase is ON where it sits, and
        # then by the very branch a lone moveto takes (disc / square / nothing).
        if (_sd_dash_on != 0) { _sd_sb_subpath(cv, run, inner, 1, 0, hw, cap, join, ml); }
        return 0;
    }

    if (closed != 0) { _sd_dash_wrap = 1; }
    if (_sd_dash_on != 0) { _sd_dash_put(p0x, p0y, 0); }
    _sd_dash_zeros(cv, p0x, p0y, hw, cap, join, ml);
    # Nothing begins at distance 0, so there is no head to wrap into.
    if (_sd_dash_on == 0) { _sd_dash_wrap = 0; }

    var segs = m - 1;
    if (closed != 0) { segs = m; }
    var j = 0;
    while (j < segs) {
        var bi = j + 1;
        if (bi == m) { bi = 0; }
        var ax = load64(run + j * 16);
        var ay = load64(run + j * 16 + 8);
        var bx = load64(run + bi * 16);
        var by = load64(run + bi * 16 + 8);
        var dx = bx - ax;
        var dy = by - ay;
        var seg = sd_isqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);      # >= 1: the run is de-duplicated
        var pos = 0;
        var going = 1;
        while (going != 0) {
            going = 0;
            # ⛔⛔ BOUND THE WALK BY THE PIECE BUFFER (0.11.2). The loop below advances by
            # _sd_dash_rem, so the iteration count is segment_length / dash_period — and BOTH are
            # attacker-chosen: a pattern of one raw 16.16 unit over a long path is ~3e9 boundaries.
            # The MEMORY was already bounded (a refused growth sets _sd_dash_trunc and the point is
            # dropped), but the WORK was not: the walk kept cutting dashes into a buffer that could
            # not take them. ⇒ Once truncation is flagged, nothing further can be stored, so
            # continuing changes no pixel — stopping here is free of rendering consequence and is
            # what makes the work bounded by the buffer rather than by the input.
            if (_sd_dash_trunc != 0) { going = 0; }
            else {
                if (seg - pos > _sd_dash_rem) {         # a boundary strictly inside (pos, seg)
                    pos = pos + _sd_dash_rem;
                    var cx = ax + (dx * pos) / seg;
                    var cy = ay + (dy * pos) / seg;
                    _sd_dash_turn(cv, cx, cy, hw, cap, join, ml);
                    _sd_dash_zeros(cv, cx, cy, hw, cap, join, ml);
                    going = 1;
                }
            }
        }
        _sd_dash_rem = _sd_dash_rem - (seg - pos);
        if (_sd_dash_on != 0) {
            var bflag = 0;
            if (bi != 0) { bflag = load8(inner + bi); }
            _sd_dash_put(bx, by, bflag);
        }
        # ⛔ NOT at a closed subpath's arrival back at p[0]. A boundary exactly there is the wrap
        # point, and firing it would end the last dash with a CAP at p[0] and leave the head to be
        # stroked apart — two caps where the header promises a join. Falling through leaves the
        # dash open (the concatenation below joins it to the head) or the gap closed (the head is
        # stroked on its own, which is what a gap ending exactly at p[0] means).
        if (bi != 0) { _sd_dash_zeros(cv, bx, by, hw, cap, join, ml); }
        j = j + 1;
    }

    if (_sd_dash_on != 0) {
        if (_sd_dash_wrap != 0) {
            # No boundary fell on the subpath at all: one dash, and a closed one — no caps.
            _sd_sb_subpath(cv, _sd_dash_run, _sd_dash_flags, _sd_dash_dn, 1, hw, cap, join, ml);
            _sd_dash_dn = 0;
        } else {
            # The wrap: the head's points after p[0] continue this dash, so p[0] becomes an
            # interior vertex and takes the join. (A growth inside the loop copies the head with
            # everything else, and each argument is read before the call that may move it.)
            var k = 1;
            while (k < _sd_dash_head) {
                _sd_dash_put(load64(_sd_dash_run + k * 16), load64(_sd_dash_run + k * 16 + 8),
                  load8(_sd_dash_flags + k));
                k = k + 1;
            }
            # ⛔ A ONE-POINT PIECE HERE IS NOT A DOT, AND IS NOT DRAWN. It can only be a dash that
            # began AT the last vertex of an OPEN subpath with nothing left to walk — a POSITIVE
            # pattern entry truncated to nothing by the end of the path, where SVG asks for a dash
            # of some length and the path has none to give. Emitting it would send one point to
            # _sd_sb_subpath's lone-point branch and paint a full disc (ROUND) or a 2*hw square
            # (SQUARE) a whole cap-width past the last real dash, INSIDE the gap the pattern
            # declares empty. ⭐ A genuine zero-LENGTH on ENTRY is a different thing and still a
            # dot: it is emitted inside _sd_dash_zeros / _sd_dash_turn as a two-point piece that
            # _sd_sb_dedup collapses, and never reaches here. (A closed subpath cannot arrive here
            # with one point: the walk appends p[0] on arrival, and a kept head adds its own.)
            # (No rewind in the dropped case: the reset at the end of this function is
            # unconditional. programs/dash_test.cyr group S1, checks 159-165.)
            if (_sd_dash_dn - _sd_dash_pstart > 1) { _sd_dash_emit(cv, hw, cap, join, ml); }
        }
    } else {
        if (_sd_dash_head > 0) {
            # The walk came back inside a gap: the head is its own dash, capped at both ends.
            _sd_sb_subpath(cv, _sd_dash_run, _sd_dash_flags, _sd_dash_head, 0, hw, cap, join, ml);
        }
    }
    _sd_dash_dn = 0;
    _sd_dash_pstart = 0;
    _sd_dash_head = 0;
    return 0;
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_stroke_path_dash — stroke `path` at `width` (16.16 full width) with cap style `cap`,
# join style `join` and `miter_limit` exactly as sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex does, but broken into
# dashes by `pattern` (a caller-owned array of `n` 16.16 lengths, on, off, on, off, …) phased by
# `offset` (a 16.16 distance into the pattern, SVG `stroke-dashoffset`; may be negative).
# MAX-combined into `cv`'s coverage, through the clip, in one fill.
#
# Returns SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for cv == 0, an unknown cap/join, a NEGATIVE pattern entry or a period
# that overflows i64 — nothing drawn in any of those; SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH for a 0 or verb-less
# path; SADISH_ERR_OOM when an allocation this stroke reached was refused — the piece buffer's
# growth, a curve's flatten mid-points, the run, the batch or the flush's accumulator (stroke.cyr's
# refusal block; the canvas keeps the dashes that were drawn before it); SADISH_OK otherwise.
# width <= 0 draws nothing.
#   · pattern == 0, n <= 0, or every entry 0: sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex's bytes, exactly (it IS the
#     call that runs).
#   · An odd n is the array twice; the pattern restarts at every subpath; a closed subpath dashes
#     around its closing point and meets itself there with a JOIN. Full semantics, the chord-length
#     approximation and its bounds: the header of this file.
# ⚠ WITH A REAL PATTERN, SD_CAP_ROUND + SD_JOIN_ROUND TAKES THE STYLED GEOMETRY, not the 0.4.0
# piece-per-pass stroker _ex sends that one pair to (which cannot dash: it has no notion of a
# piece). So round/round is the ONE pair where a solid fallback and a pattern that covers the whole
# path differ — by the seam the 0.4.0 stroker leaves, where pieces meet by MAX instead of as one
# nonzero fill. MEASURED on "M 4,30 C 20,4 44,56 60,30" at width 4: 28 pixels, up to 27 levels
# apart; on the closed 24 px square at width 4, byte-identical. Every other pair is byte-identical
# by construction — a pattern longer than the path IS the solid stroke, through the same call.
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sd_canvas_stroke_path_dash(cv, path, width, cap, join, miter_limit, pattern, n, offset): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    if (sd_path_verb_count(path) == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    if (cap < SD_CAP_BUTT) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (cap > SD_CAP_SQUARE) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (join < SD_JOIN_MITER) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (join > SD_JOIN_BEVEL) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }

    # The array is validated BEFORE anything is drawn, so a bad entry anywhere leaves the canvas
    # untouched. `sum < 0` is the period overflowing i64 — every entry here is >= 0, so a negative
    # running total can only be a wrap.
    var sum = 0;
    if (pattern != 0) {
        var k = 0;
        while (k < n) {
            var l = load64(pattern + k * 8);
            if (l < 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
            sum = sum + l;
            if (sum < 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
            k = k + 1;
        }
    }
    var nn = n;
    var period = sum;
    if ((n & 1) != 0) {
        nn = n + n;
        period = sum + sum;
        if (period < 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    }
    if (pattern == 0) { return sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex(cv, path, width, cap, join, miter_limit); }
    if (n <= 0) { return sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex(cv, path, width, cap, join, miter_limit); }
    if (period == 0) { return sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex(cv, path, width, cap, join, miter_limit); }

    var hw = sd_asr(width, 1);
    if (hw <= 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var ml = miter_limit;
    if (ml < SD_ONE) { ml = SD_ONE; }
    if (ml > SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX) { ml = SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX; }

    _sd_dash_pat = pattern;
    _sd_dash_n = n;
    _sd_dash_nn = nn;
    _sd_dash_period = period;
    _sd_dash_off = offset;

    # ⛔ Three process-lifetime sets, each guarded on the BLOCK the walk stores through rather than
    # on its initialiser's status (sd_fill_impl's reasoning); every publish is all-or-nothing.
    _sd_scratch_init();
    if (_sd_stroke_run == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    _sd_sb_init();                      # the batch is empty: every call ends in _sd_sb_flush
    if (_sd_sb_edges == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    _sd_dash_init();
    if (_sd_dash_run == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    _sd_run_trunc = 0;
    _sd_dash_trunc = 0;
    return _sd_styled_walk(cv, path, hw, cap, join, ml, 1);
}

# --- paint.cyr ---

# @public — sadish gradient paint: linear, radial + focal radial, multi-stop, pad / repeat / reflect,
# gradient transforms.
#
# paint.cyr — an SdGradient is a PAINT: a colour for every point of canvas space. It is built once
# (constructor + stops + spread [+ matrix]) and composited through a canvas's coverage with
# sd_canvas_blit_paint_at, which is sd_canvas_blit_at with the solid colour replaced by the paint.
#
#   var g = sd_gradient_linear(x0, y0, x1, y1);    # or sd_gradient_radial(cx, cy, r),
#                                                  # or sd_gradient_radial_focal(cx, cy, r, fx, fy) — 16.16
#   sd_gradient_add_stop(g, 0, sd_rgb(255, 0, 0));
#   sd_gradient_add_stop(g, SD_ONE, sd_rgb(0, 0, 255));
#   sd_gradient_set_spread(g, SD_SPREAD_REFLECT);  # default SD_SPREAD_PAD
#   sd_gradient_set_matrix(g, m);                  # optional: m maps gradient space -> canvas space
#   sd_canvas_blit_paint_at(cv, surface, g, dx, dy);
#
# THE CONTRACT.
#   · Geometry is in CANVAS pixel space: moving the canvas with (dx, dy) moves the paint with it.
#   · Each pixel is sampled at its CENTRE (x + 0.5, y + 0.5). ⚠ The legacy
#     sd_canvas_blit_gradient samples the top-left CORNER (x, y) with whole-pixel endpoints; the two
#     are deliberately not interchangeable, and the legacy fn is untouched.
#   · Linear: t = ((p - p0) · (p1 - p0)) / |p1 - p0|^2.  Radial: t = |p - c| / r.
#   · t is EXACT at every pixel: a pixel reads floor(t * 2^16) — linear steps floor(t * 2^32) WITH
#     its remainder, radial takes its root to 16 fraction bits — so a pixel centre that lies
#     exactly on a stop takes that stop, on any axis length, and every pixel paints
#     sd_gradient_color_at of its spread-mapped floor(t * 2^16).
#   · Spread maps t into [0, 1]: PAD clamps, REPEAT takes fract(t) (floor-based, so t = -0.25 reads
#     0.75), REFLECT is the period-2 triangle wave (t = -0.25 and t = 1.75 both read 0.25). REFLECT
#     mirrors the exact t, so floor(2 - t) is taken as 2 - ceil(t), not 2 - floor(t).
#   · Stops: offset 16.16, clamped to [0, SD_ONE]; kept sorted by a STABLE insert, so stops may be
#     added in any order and equal offsets keep insertion order — two stops at one offset are a
#     hard transition, and t >= offset takes the LATER one. ⚠ SVG instead raises an out-of-order
#     offset to its predecessor's (document order); a consumer that wants that clamps before adding.
#   · Colours are 0xAARRGGBB under the LEGACY alpha rule (sd_alpha_of: an alpha byte of 0 is
#     OPAQUE). Interpolation is per channel, alpha included, in STRAIGHT (non-premultiplied) values —
#     SVG's default. The source alpha of a pixel is coverage * interpolated_alpha / 255, then exactly
#     sd_canvas_blit_at's src-over: every pixel with coverage != 0 is written and gets dst alpha 255,
#     even when that product rounds to 0 (its colour bytes then come back unchanged). ⇒ An opaque
#     one-stop gradient paints the same bytes as sd_canvas_blit_at with that colour.
#   · ⭐ THE SAME PAINT, ONTO A PREMULTIPLIED SURFACE: sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at(cv, s, g, dx, dy)
#     in src/premul.cyr (0.7.0) — a gradient panel with anti-aliased corners over a TRANSPARENT
#     window, which the blit above cannot make (it forces dst alpha 255). It is this file's loop with
#     the store swapped (`pm`), so the t, the spread and the colour of every pixel are the ones the
#     contract above defines and the straight blit paints; only the compositing differs, and it is
#     premul.cyr's, sharing that module's one formula with sd_canvas_blit_premul_at. The STRAIGHT
#     interpolation above is what it premultiplies, per pixel, after the fact — read the ⭐ note there
#     for why the ramp itself is not premultiplied instead.
#   · Zero stops paint NOTHING and return SADISH_OK (SVG: as if the paint were 'none'); one stop is
#     solid. A zero-length linear axis — |p1 - p0| < 2^-8 px — and a radius r <= 0 paint the colour
#     at t = 1 everywhere, which is the LAST stop (SVG). ⚠ The 2^-8 px floor is sadish's, not SVG's:
#     below it t * 2^32 would leave i64 inside the precision bound.
#
# ⭐ THE RAMP. Stops are resolved into a 1025-entry lookup table (4 B each, 0xAARRGGBB with the
# ACTUAL alpha) inside the gradient, rebuilt lazily when a stop is added. Entry i is the colour at
# EXACTLY t = i/1024, and a pixel reads the entry at floor(t * 1024) — no stop search. What that
# buys and what it costs:
#   · A cell (i/1024, (i+1)/1024) with a stop strictly INSIDE it is marked (its entry is 0 — alpha
#     byte 0, which no real colour has) and a pixel landing there evaluates the stops from its 16.16 t.
#     ⇒ Hard stops and bands narrower than a cell land on EXACTLY the pixels whose t >= offset, at
#     any axis length (a 0.3 stop on a 4096 px axis flips at pixel 1229; a 3.5 px band keeps its 4
#     pixels) — asserted in programs/paint_test.cyr. At most one cell per stop pays the search; a
#     stop exactly ON a cell edge marks nothing (the entry past it already starts there).
#   · The endpoints are exact: t <= 0 reads the colour at 0, t >= 1 (PAD) the colour at 1.
#   · A SMOOTH stretch (a cell holding no stop) reads the colour at the cell's left edge: it lags
#     the analytic colour by less than one cell (gradient length / 1024), i.e. by less than the
#     colour change across one cell — under 1/4 level for a full-range 2-stop ramp. On a 256 px
#     axis from a pixel boundary every centre lands ON an entry, so the paint there is exact.
#   · Spread is applied to t BEFORE the lookup, so set_spread never rebuilds anything.
#   · MEASURED (256x256 full coverage, 50 blits, this host): linear PAD 14.2 ns/px — the legacy
#     2-stop sd_canvas_blit_gradient is 13.7, the solid sd_canvas_blit_at 7.0; radial 44.3 ns/px,
#     most of it the per-pixel root (16 ns per _sd_paint_isqrt; sd_isqrt 82) and the exact 16-bit
#     fraction (an accumulated-t / floor-root version measured 13.8 and 35.1 in the same run).
#
# ⚠ PRECISION BOUND. Every gradient coordinate must lie within ±16,383 px and every canvas pixel
# centre below 16,383 px (16.16 magnitude < 2^30), so any difference between them is < 2^31 raw and
# any product of two, or sum of two such products, is < 2^63. Within it:
#   · Linear works in t * 2^32. N = (centre - p0) · d is long-divided ONCE per blit into
#     floor(N * 2^32 / |d|^2) and its remainder; the per-pixel and per-row steps
#     floor(2^48 * d / |d|^2) carry their remainders like a line DDA, so t * 2^32 is floor-exact at
#     every pixel. The division never doubles a remainder past |d|^2 (< 2^63): it compares
#     rem >= b - rem. With |d| >= 2^-8 px, |t| < 2^23 and |t * 2^32| < 2^55. (A 16.16 dot product
#     scaled by 2^16 overflows once |p - p0| * |d| passes 2^15 px^2 — 182 px by 182 px — which is
#     why t is never formed that way.)
#   · Radial squares a < 2^31 raw difference per axis (sum < 2^63) and takes floor(sqrt(d^2) * 2^16)
#     exactly with i64 products (_sd_paint_radial_t); t = that / r, < 2^48.
#   · Outside the bound the COLOURS may be wrong, but the ramp index is always clamped (PAD) or
#     masked (REPEAT/REFLECT) into the table: no input reads or writes out of bounds.
#
# FOCAL RADIAL — sd_gradient_radial_focal(cx, cy, r, fx, fy): SVG 1.1 <radialGradient> fx / fy (fr = 0).
#   · t = 0 at the focal point f, t = 1 on the circle (c, r): for a point p, t = |p - f| / |q - f|, q
#     where the ray from f through p leaves the circle. With d = p - f, e = c - f, A = r^2 - |e|^2 > 0,
#     t is the positive root of A t^2 + 2 (d·e) t - |d|^2 = 0: t = (sqrt((d·e)^2 + A |d|^2) - d·e) / A.
#   · ⚠ THE FOCAL CLAMP. A focal point farther than lim = r - ceil(r / 2^10) from the centre — on or
#     outside the circle, or inside it but within r / 1024 of it — moves onto the segment from c toward
#     (fx, fy) at distance lim (truncations leave it under 4 raw closer, never further). Why
#     2^-10 (0.99902 r): ON the circle, t is infinite on the far side of f; just inside it, the side
#     behind f squeezes t's whole [0, 1] into r - |e|. The clamp caps that squeeze at r / 1024 — the
#     ramp's own resolution — keeps A >= r^2 (2^-9 - 2^-20) (>= 2r - 1 raw for tiny r) and t <= 2^10 |d| / r.
#     SVG 1.1 says "set it ON the circle"; renderers pull it a hair inside the same way. The clamped
#     point is what is stored.
#   · f == c (after the clamp) paints through sd_gradient_radial's code, byte for byte.
#   · t is EXACT — floor(t * 2^16) with its fraction flag, as linear and radial — for t < 256: every
#     pixel inside the circle, and under PAD every pixel (t >= 1 is the last colour, decided exactly by
#     |p - c|^2 >= r^2). Beyond t = 256 (REPEAT / REFLECT, 256 periods out) floor(t * 2^16) is within
#     t * 2^-10 + 1 of exact. How: _sd_paint_focal_t.
#   · MEASURED (256x256 full coverage, the same shape as the figures above, on an idle core): focal PAD
#     85.1 ns/px, focal REFLECT 89.0 (every pixel takes the root; PAD's outside test skips it). Linear
#     and radial are untouched at 14.0 and 41.9 — the focal / transformed pixels have their own loop
#     (_sd_paint_blit_point), so the 0.6.0 loop did not grow a branch. ⚠ Those two are a fresh
#     head-to-head against a build of 0.6.0's src/ on the same idle core in the same minute, NOT the
#     14.2 / 44.3 the RAMP section above quotes from 0.6.0's own (loaded) run — ns/px figures compare
#     within one run on one host, never across them.
#
# GRADIENT TRANSFORMS — sd_gradient_set_matrix(g, m): SVG gradientTransform.
#   · m maps GRADIENT space — where the geometry above is given — to CANVAS space; each pixel centre is
#     mapped through the inverse. m == 0, or an exactly-identity m, restores the untransformed paint
#     byte for byte and allocates nothing.
#   · LINEAR stays a DDA: t is affine in canvas position, so a transformed linear gradient IS a
#     canvas-space linear gradient, axis P0 = m(p0), V = |v|^2 J^-T v / |J^-T v|^2 (v = p1 - p0, J the
#     2x2 part of m). Each blit resolves that axis to the nearest raw unit (128-bit intermediates) and
#     paints it with the exact DDA above.
#   · RADIAL and FOCAL (ellipses in canvas space) map every pixel centre: q = 2^16 adj(J) (p - (e, f)) /
#     det, stepped per pixel and per row as quotient + remainder like the linear DDA (no error
#     accumulates), rounded to the nearest raw unit; then the untransformed t at q.
#   · ⭐ EXACT where the transform is: translations, quarter-turns and 2^k scales resolve both with no
#     rounding — a translation-only matrix is byte-identical to moving the geometry, a quarter-turn of a
#     linear gradient to the rotated axis (asserted in programs/paint_focal_test.cyr).
#   · ⚠ Otherwise t is exact for the ROUNDED axis / point, which is within 0.71 raw of exact (each
#     component within 0.5): |Δt| < 2^-16 px (1 + 3 |p - P0| / |V|) / |V| linear, < 2^-16.5 px / r
#     radial, < 2^-16.5 px / (r - |c - f|) focal (t's gradient is at most 1 / (r - |c - f|)).
#     MEASURED under rotate(30°) x scale(2) with CORDIC entries, every pixel centre of a 47x47 patch
#     against exact rationals (a 35 px axis, r = 23.1 px, a focal point 7.3 px off the centre — all
#     doubled on the canvas): floor(t * 2^16) is never off by more than 1, and is off by 1 at 7
#     linear, 20 radial and 19 focal pixels of 2,209.
#   · ⛔ TWO KINDS OF MATRIX MAKE THE GRADIENT PAINT NOTHING, both returning SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS from
#     set_matrix, for two different reasons. (1) SINGULAR (a*d - b*c == 0) — SVG's own rule: an element
#     whose gradientTransform is not invertible is not rendered with that paint. (2) ANY ENTRY outside
#     ±16,384.0 (raw magnitude >= 2^30) — sadish's precision bound, not SVG's: such a matrix may be
#     perfectly invertible (a translation of 16,384 px is), and it is still refused, because the mapped
#     products would leave i64. ⇒ A consumer placing a gradient on a large canvas must keep every entry
#     inside that bound (move the geometry rather than the matrix) and must READ the return code — an
#     ignored one leaves an invisible paint with no other signal. The next valid matrix (or 0) revives it.
#   · ⚠ PRECISION BOUND under a transform: the gradient-space image of every painted pixel centre, m(p0),
#     and m(p0) + V all within ±16,383 px.
#   · MEASURED, same shape: a transformed linear gradient is 14.0 ns/px — the untransformed figure, the
#     matrix costing one axis resolve per blit; radial 49.0 (41.9 untransformed) and focal 98.7 (85.1),
#     the difference being the two per-pixel DDA steps and the rounding.
#
# ⚠ ALLOCATION. The gradient object (88 B), its stops (16 B each, doubling from 4, like
# sd_path_grow) and the ramp (4,100 B) come from sd_alloc — MEASURED 4,256 B for a constructed
# gradient, all of it on the consumer's hook when one is set. A focal gradient adds a 72 B extension
# (fx, fy, the matrix): 4,328 B. sd_gradient_set_matrix allocates that same 72 B ONCE — on the first
# non-identity matrix of a linear or radial gradient — and never again: re-setting a matrix every
# frame costs 0 B. A blit allocates NOTHING, not even on its first call (the ramp is rebuilt in
# place): MEASURED 0 B on the heap and 0 B on an arena hook, asserted exactly in
# programs/paint_test.cyr and (focal, transformed) programs/paint_focal_test.cyr. ⛔ A gradient made
# under an arena hook dies with that arena's reset, exactly like a path — and so does an extension
# set_matrix allocated under one.
# ⚠ SINGLE-THREADED, as the rest of the rasterizer: the lazy ramp rebuild writes into the gradient
# from inside a blit or sd_gradient_color_at, and the division helpers hand back their second result
# through a module global.

# ---- kinds + spread modes ----------------------------------------
var SD_GRADIENT_LINEAR = 0;
var SD_GRADIENT_RADIAL = 1;
var SD_GRADIENT_FOCAL  = 2;
# ⭐ A FOURTH KIND, AND NOT A GRADIENT (0.11.0) — a PATTERN samples a source SdSurface instead of a
# stop ramp. It shares this record, its spread and its matrix, and paints through the same two blit
# entries; what differs is only where a pixel's colour comes from. ⚠ The record is still spelled
# SdGradient for ABI continuity; read it as SdPaint.
var SD_PAINT_PATTERN   = 3;

var SD_SPREAD_PAD     = 0;
var SD_SPREAD_REPEAT  = 1;
var SD_SPREAD_REFLECT = 2;
# ⭐ PATTERNS ONLY (0.11.0): paint NOTHING outside the source rect — SVG's `pattern` and Canvas's
# `no-repeat`. Without it a consumer can tile or smear edge pixels but cannot draw one bitmap once.
# ⛔ A GRADIENT CANNOT BE SET TO IT: sd_gradient_set_spread answers SADISH_ERR_UNSUPPORTED, so the
# mistake is caught where it is made rather than painting something surprising. A gradient that
# somehow carries it anyway pads — _sd_gradient_spread's fall-through has always treated an unknown
# spread as PAD, and that is left standing as the defensive answer, not as an invitation.
var SD_SPREAD_NONE    = 3;

# ---- pattern filtering -------------------------------------------
# ⚠ ONE MODE TODAY. The filter is a FIELD, not a constant, so bilinear can land later without an ABI
# break: sd_pattern_set_filter refuses anything else with SADISH_ERR_UNSUPPORTED rather than
# silently doing nearest, so a consumer that asks for a filter it did not get is told.
# ⛔ Nearest is the RIGHT default, not just the cheap one: at 1:1 — a cached tile, an icon, a glyph
# atlas, which is what a UI toolkit actually blits — it is exact, and any smoothing there is a
# defect. Bilinear earns its cost only under a scaling or rotating matrix.
var SD_FILTER_NEAREST = 0;

# The ramp: 2^10 cells over t in [0, 1], stored as N + 1 entries so t = 1 has its own.
var SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_BITS = 10;
var SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_N    = 1024;
# Initial stop capacity; sd_gradient_add_stop doubles it on overflow.
var SD_GRADIENT_STOP_CAP  = 4;
# The focal clamp: a focal point stays within r - ceil(r / 2^SD_GRADIENT_FOCAL_BITS) of the centre.
var SD_GRADIENT_FOCAL_BITS = 10;

# ================================================================
# SdGradient (88 bytes):
#   +0:  kind      (SD_GRADIENT_LINEAR | SD_GRADIENT_RADIAL | SD_GRADIENT_FOCAL)
#   +8:  spread    (SD_SPREAD_*)
#   +16: x0 | cx   (16.16)
#   +24: y0 | cy   (16.16)
#   +32: x1 | r    (16.16)
#   +40: y1 | 0    (16.16)
#   +48: stops     (ptr — capacity * 16 B, sorted: +0 offset 16.16, +8 colour as given; the capacity
#                   is a function of n_stops: max(SD_GRADIENT_STOP_CAP, the least power of two >= n))
#   +56: n_stops   (i64)
#   +64: ext       (ptr — SdGradientExt, or 0: no focal point, no transform)
#   +72: ramp      (ptr — (SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_N + 1) * 4 B, 0xAARRGGBB, actual alpha; 0 = marked cell)
#   +80: dirty     (i64 — 1 = stops changed since the ramp was built)
# ⚠ +64 held the stop capacity until the extension needed a slot; the capacity is now derived, so the
# object — and the 4,256 B a gradient costs — did not grow.
#
# SdGradientExt (72 bytes) — allocated by sd_gradient_radial_focal, or by the first sd_gradient_set_matrix
# that is not the identity:
#   +0:  fx, +8: fy (16.16, AFTER the focal clamp; unused unless kind == SD_GRADIENT_FOCAL)
#   +16: xform     (SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_NONE | _MATRIX | _BLOCKED)
#   +24: a, +32: b, +40: c, +48: d, +56: e, +64: f  (the matrix, as SdMatrix; meaningful for _MATRIX)
# ================================================================
var SD_GRADIENT_KIND_OFFSET   = 0;
var SD_GRADIENT_SPREAD_OFFSET = 8;
var SD_GRADIENT_A_OFFSET      = 16;
var SD_GRADIENT_B_OFFSET      = 24;
var SD_GRADIENT_C_OFFSET      = 32;
var SD_GRADIENT_D_OFFSET      = 40;
var SD_GRADIENT_STOPS_OFFSET  = 48;
var SD_GRADIENT_NSTOPS_OFFSET = 56;
var SD_GRADIENT_EXT_OFFSET    = 64;
var SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_OFFSET   = 72;
var SD_GRADIENT_DIRTY_OFFSET  = 80;
var SD_GRADIENT_SIZE          = 88;

var SD_GRADIENT_EXT_FX_OFFSET    = 0;
var SD_GRADIENT_EXT_FY_OFFSET    = 8;
var SD_GRADIENT_EXT_XFORM_OFFSET = 16;
var SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET     = 24;
var SD_GRADIENT_EXT_SIZE         = 72;
# xform states: no transform; a valid matrix; a matrix the paint cannot honour (paint nothing).
var SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_NONE    = 0;
var SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_MATRIX  = 1;
var SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_BLOCKED = 2;

# The second result of _sd_paint_divq (the remainder), _sd_paint_radial_t and _sd_paint_focal_t
# (1 = inexact).
var _sd_paint_rem = 0;
# The high 64 bits of the last _sd_paint_umul.
var _sd_paint_hi = 0;

fn _sd_gradient_new(kind, a, b, c, d): i64 {
    var g = sd_alloc(SD_GRADIENT_SIZE);
    if (g == 0) { return 0; }
    var stops = sd_alloc(SD_GRADIENT_STOP_CAP * 16);
    if (stops == 0) { return 0; }
    var ramp = sd_alloc((SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_N + 1) * 4);
    if (ramp == 0) { return 0; }
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_KIND_OFFSET, kind);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_SPREAD_OFFSET, SD_SPREAD_PAD);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_A_OFFSET, a);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_B_OFFSET, b);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_C_OFFSET, c);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_D_OFFSET, d);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_STOPS_OFFSET, stops);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_NSTOPS_OFFSET, 0);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_OFFSET, 0);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_OFFSET, ramp);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_DIRTY_OFFSET, 1);
    return g;
}

# @public — a linear gradient along (x0, y0) -> (x1, y1), 16.16 canvas coordinates. No stops yet;
# spread PAD. Returns the SdGradient ptr, or 0 on OOM.
fn sd_gradient_linear(x0, y0, x1, y1): i64 {
    return _sd_gradient_new(SD_GRADIENT_LINEAR, x0, y0, x1, y1);
}

# @public — a radial gradient centred on (cx, cy) with radius r, all 16.16 canvas coordinates;
# t = distance / r. No stops yet; spread PAD. Returns the SdGradient ptr, or 0 on OOM.
fn sd_gradient_radial(cx, cy, r): i64 {
    return _sd_gradient_new(SD_GRADIENT_RADIAL, cx, cy, r, 0);
}

# Store the focal point (fx, fy) of a gradient centred on (cx, cy) with radius r into ext, clamped:
# an offset from the centre longer than lim = r - ceil(r / 2^SD_GRADIENT_FOCAL_BITS) is scaled to lim,
# each component truncated toward the centre, so |offset| <= lim always and, inside the precision
# bound, under 4 raw short of it: the direct path loses under 3 (a ceil'd length plus two truncations),
# the halving path under 3.5 (its shortfall is lim / |halved offset| + sqrt(2), and the halving leaves
# a component >= 2^29 against a lim below 2^30). MEASURED worst 3 raw over 120,000 fuzzed clamps —
# 5 once out-of-bound radii, where the r cap below applies, are fuzzed too (analytic bound 5.5).
# ⛔ WHETHER the clamp is needed is decided at the OFFSET'S OWN SCALE: a component past lim settles it
# alone, and when neither is, both are <= lim < 2^31 and the square below is < 2^63. Only then — the
# clamp already known to be needed, so nothing but the offset's DIRECTION still matters — is an offset
# with a component of 2^30 raw or more halved (sign-preserving) until it has none, keeping the square
# under 2^61 and the scaling products under 2^62. Halving FIRST and comparing the shortened length
# against the full-scale lim silently skips the clamp whenever lim outgrows the halved length (r above
# ~8,200 px with an offset past 16,384 px), storing a focal point at a fraction of the asked direction.
# r <= 0 stores (fx, fy) as given — the paint is the last stop anyway.
fn _sd_gradient_set_focal(ext, cx, cy, r, fx, fy): i64 {
    var vx = fx - cx;
    var vy = fy - cy;
    if (r > 0) {
        var rr = r;
        if (rr > 2147483647) { rr = 2147483647; }
        var lim = rr - ((rr + (1 << SD_GRADIENT_FOCAL_BITS) - 1) >> SD_GRADIENT_FOCAL_BITS);
        # ⚠ The pre-tests are what make the square below safe: they leave |vx|, |vy| <= lim, and the r
        # cap holds lim <= 2,145,386,495, whose doubled square is 9,205,366,425,856,770,050 — inside
        # i64 by 1.8e16. A component compared as a component also survives a square that would wrap.
        var need = 0;
        if (vx > lim) { need = 1; }
        if (vx < 0 - lim) { need = 1; }
        if (vy > lim) { need = 1; }
        if (vy < 0 - lim) { need = 1; }
        if (need == 0) {
            if (vx * vx + vy * vy > lim * lim) { need = 1; }
        }
        if (need != 0) {
            var big = 1;
            while (big != 0) {
                big = 0;
                if (vx >= 1073741824) { big = 1; }
                if (vx <= 0 - 1073741824) { big = 1; }
                if (vy >= 1073741824) { big = 1; }
                if (vy <= 0 - 1073741824) { big = 1; }
                if (big != 0) {
                    vx = sd_asr(vx, 1);
                    vy = sd_asr(vy, 1);
                }
            }
            # The halving leaves a component >= 2^29 (the last one ran because one was >= 2^30), and a
            # clamp that is needed has a non-zero offset, so lv > 0 here either way.
            var lv = vx * vx + vy * vy;
            var len = _sd_paint_isqrt(lv);
            if (len * len < lv) { len = len + 1; }
            vx = (vx * lim) / len;
            vy = (vy * lim) / len;
        }
    }
    store64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_FX_OFFSET, cx + vx);
    store64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_FY_OFFSET, cy + vy);
    return 0;
}

# @public — a FOCAL radial gradient: circle (cx, cy, r), focal point (fx, fy), all 16.16 canvas (or,
# under a matrix, gradient) coordinates. t = 0 at the focal point and 1 on the circle (SVG 1.1 fx / fy,
# focal radius 0); a focal point on, outside, or within r / 1024 of the circle is clamped to
# r - ceil(r / 1024) from the centre along the same direction. No stops yet; spread PAD. Returns the
# SdGradient ptr, or 0 on OOM (any of its four allocations). Costs 4,328 B through sd_alloc.
fn sd_gradient_radial_focal(cx, cy, r, fx, fy): i64 {
    var g = _sd_gradient_new(SD_GRADIENT_FOCAL, cx, cy, r, 0);
    if (g == 0) { return 0; }
    var ext = sd_alloc(SD_GRADIENT_EXT_SIZE);
    if (ext == 0) { return 0; }
    memset(ext, 0, SD_GRADIENT_EXT_SIZE);
    _sd_gradient_set_focal(ext, cx, cy, r, fx, fy);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_OFFSET, ext);
    return g;
}

# @public — set the gradient transform: `m` (an SdMatrix, 16.16) maps GRADIENT space to CANVAS space,
# as SVG's gradientTransform; the matrix is COPIED, so m may be freed or reused. m == 0 (or an exact
# identity) removes any transform: the paint is byte-identical to never having had one.
# Returns SADISH_OK; SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for g == 0, or for a matrix the paint cannot honour — which is
# STORED and makes the gradient paint NOTHING until the next valid set_matrix; or SADISH_ERR_OOM when
# the 72 B extension cannot be allocated (the gradient is unchanged). ⛔ Two different things are
# refused with that one code: a SINGULAR matrix (a*d - b*c == 0), which is SVG's rule for not rendering
# the element, and any matrix with an ENTRY outside ±16,384.0 (raw magnitude >= 2^30), which is
# sadish's precision bound — an invertible matrix such as a translation of 16,384 px is refused too.
# A caller that ignores the code gets SVG's behaviour for the first and an invisible paint for the
# second; one that checks it can set 0 or an in-bound matrix to revive the paint.
# Allocation: 0 B for m == 0 / identity and for a gradient that already has its extension (every focal
# gradient; any gradient after its first non-identity matrix); otherwise 72 B, once.
fn sd_gradient_set_matrix(g, m): i64 {
    if (g == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var ext = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_OFFSET);
    var ident = 0;
    if (m == 0) {
        ident = 1;
    } else {
        if (load64(m + SD_MAT_A_OFFSET) == SD_ONE) {
            if (load64(m + SD_MAT_D_OFFSET) == SD_ONE) {
                if ((load64(m + SD_MAT_B_OFFSET) | load64(m + SD_MAT_C_OFFSET)) == 0) {
                    if ((load64(m + SD_MAT_E_OFFSET) | load64(m + SD_MAT_F_OFFSET)) == 0) { ident = 1; }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if (ident != 0) {
        if (ext != 0) { store64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_XFORM_OFFSET, SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_NONE); }
        return SADISH_OK;
    }
    if (ext == 0) {
        ext = sd_alloc(SD_GRADIENT_EXT_SIZE);
        if (ext == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
        memset(ext, 0, SD_GRADIENT_EXT_SIZE);
        store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_OFFSET, ext);
    }
    var ok = 1;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < 6) {
        var v = load64(m + i * 8);
        store64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + i * 8, v);
        if (v >= 1073741824) { ok = 0; }
        if (v <= 0 - 1073741824) { ok = 0; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (ok != 0) {
        var det = load64(m + SD_MAT_A_OFFSET) * load64(m + SD_MAT_D_OFFSET);
        det = det - load64(m + SD_MAT_B_OFFSET) * load64(m + SD_MAT_C_OFFSET);
        if (det == 0) { ok = 0; }
    }
    if (ok == 0) {
        store64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_XFORM_OFFSET, SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_BLOCKED);
        return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS;
    }
    store64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_XFORM_OFFSET, SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_MATRIX);
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — how many stops the paint holds. 0 for a null paint and for a PATTERN (which has none).
# ⚠ 0.11.1: THIS USED TO FAULT ON A NULL PAINT, while sd_gradient_color_at's header told a caller to
# use it to tell a null `g` apart from a stop-less one. The advice was unfollowable — the call it
# named dereferenced the 0 it was meant to detect.
fn sd_gradient_stop_count(g): i64 {
    if (g == 0) { return 0; }
    return load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_NSTOPS_OFFSET);
}

# @public — add a colour stop. `offset` is 16.16, clamped to [0, SD_ONE]; `color` is 0xAARRGGBB
# under the legacy alpha rule (alpha byte 0 == opaque). Inserted after every stop whose offset is
# <= this one (stable). Returns SADISH_OK, SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for g == 0, SADISH_ERR_UNSUPPORTED for
# a PATTERN, or SADISH_ERR_OOM when growing the stop block fails (the gradient is unchanged).
# ⛔⛔ THE PATTERN REFUSAL IS A CRASH FIX (0.11.1), NOT TIDINESS. sd_pattern_new leaves
# SD_GRADIENT_STOPS_OFFSET at 0 — a pattern has no stop array, which is why it costs ONE allocation
# instead of three. This function then did `store64(stops + i * 16, o)` with `stops == 0`: a
# null-pointer WRITE, reachable from a single public call on a paint this release introduced.
# ⚠ It is UNSUPPORTED, not BOUNDS: the paint is valid and the argument is valid: stops are simply
# not a thing a pattern has. Same reasoning as sd_gradient_set_spread refusing SD_SPREAD_NONE on a
# gradient, and the second use in the tree of a code that had none before 0.11.0.
fn sd_gradient_add_stop(g, offset, color): i64 {
    if (g == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_KIND_OFFSET) == SD_PAINT_PATTERN) { return SADISH_ERR_UNSUPPORTED; }
    var o = offset;
    if (o < 0) { o = 0; }
    if (o > SD_ONE) { o = SD_ONE; }
    var stops = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_STOPS_OFFSET);
    var n = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_NSTOPS_OFFSET);
    # The block holds max(SD_GRADIENT_STOP_CAP, the least power of two >= n) stops, so it is full
    # exactly when n is a power of two >= SD_GRADIENT_STOP_CAP — and then doubles, as it always has.
    var full = 0;
    if (n >= SD_GRADIENT_STOP_CAP) {
        if ((n & (n - 1)) == 0) { full = 1; }
    }
    if (full != 0) {
        var grown = sd_alloc(n * 2 * 16);
        if (grown == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
        memcpy(grown, stops, n * 16);
        stops = grown;
        store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_STOPS_OFFSET, stops);
    }
    # Shift every stop with a strictly GREATER offset up one slot; equal offsets stay in front.
    var i = n;
    var stop = 0;
    while (stop == 0) {
        if (i == 0) {
            stop = 1;
        } else {
            var prev = stops + (i - 1) * 16;
            if (load64(prev) > o) {
                store64(prev + 16, load64(prev));
                store64(prev + 24, load64(prev + 8));
                i = i - 1;
            } else {
                stop = 1;
            }
        }
    }
    store64(stops + i * 16, o);
    store64(stops + i * 16 + 8, color);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_NSTOPS_OFFSET, n + 1);
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_DIRTY_OFFSET, 1);
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — set the spread mode (SD_SPREAD_PAD | _REPEAT | _REFLECT). Returns SADISH_OK, or
# SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for g == 0 / an unknown mode (the gradient is unchanged).
fn sd_gradient_set_spread(g, spread): i64 {
    if (g == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (spread < SD_SPREAD_PAD) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    # ⛔ SD_SPREAD_NONE IS A PATTERN'S, AND THE TWO REFUSALS DIFFER ON PURPOSE (0.11.0). A spread
    # outside the enum entirely is SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS — a bad argument. SD_SPREAD_NONE on a GRADIENT
    # is SADISH_ERR_UNSUPPORTED — a real request this paint kind cannot honour, which is exactly
    # what that code has always meant and, until now, the only thing in the tree that meant it.
    # ⚠ "Nothing outside" has no meaning for a gradient: t is defined everywhere on the plane, so
    # there is no rect to be outside OF. Padding it silently would paint the last stop over the
    # whole canvas, which is the opposite of what the caller asked for.
    if (spread == SD_SPREAD_NONE) {
        if (load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_KIND_OFFSET) != SD_PAINT_PATTERN) { return SADISH_ERR_UNSUPPORTED; }
        store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_SPREAD_OFFSET, spread);
        return SADISH_OK;
    }
    if (spread > SD_SPREAD_REFLECT) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_SPREAD_OFFSET, spread);
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# ================================================================
# Pattern paint (0.11.0) — a paint whose colour at a point is a TEXEL of a source surface.
# ================================================================
# @public — a pattern that samples `src`, one source texel per canvas pixel, nearest-neighbour.
# Returns the paint ptr, or 0 for a null `src` or on OOM. Spread PAD, filter SD_FILTER_NEAREST,
# no matrix — so by default texel (0, 0) sits on canvas pixel (0, 0) and the pattern draws at 1:1.
#
#   var p = sd_pattern_new(tile);
#   sd_gradient_set_spread(p, SD_SPREAD_REPEAT);   # tile it (PAD / REFLECT / NONE also apply)
#   sd_gradient_set_matrix(p, m);                  # optional: m maps pattern space -> canvas space
#   sd_canvas_blit_paint_at(cv, dest, p, dx, dy);  # the SAME entry a gradient uses
#
# ⭐ ONE ALLOCATION, NOT THREE. A gradient takes its record, its stop array and its 4,100 B ramp; a
# pattern has no stops and no ramp, so it takes the 88 B record alone and leaves those two fields 0.
# The blit dispatches on the kind BEFORE it reads either, which is what makes that safe.
# ⚠ THE SOURCE IS BORROWED, NOT COPIED. `src` must outlive the pattern and every blit through it,
# and mutating `src` changes what the pattern paints — there is no snapshot. Under a consumer's
# frame arena that means a pattern must not outlive the surface it samples.
# ⚠ A pattern samples the source's RAW pixel bytes: byte 3 is the alpha it composites with, with NO
# legacy 0-means-opaque mapping. That is the right rule and it is not a trap, because the mapping
# already happened at STORE time — sd_plot and every draw.cyr primitive write sd_alpha_of(colour),
# so a surface drawn with sd_rgb holds 255 and samples opaque. A byte 3 of 0 is therefore a pixel
# nothing ever wrote (a fresh surface is zeroed) or one written transparent on purpose, and both
# mean transparent. ⛔ It is sd_surface_rgba_at's rule, not sd_surface_pixel_at's, which drops alpha.
fn sd_pattern_new(src): i64 {
    if (src == 0) { return 0; }
    var p = sd_alloc(SD_GRADIENT_SIZE);
    if (p == 0) { return 0; }
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_KIND_OFFSET, SD_PAINT_PATTERN);
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_SPREAD_OFFSET, SD_SPREAD_PAD);
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_A_OFFSET, src);                 # the borrowed source surface
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_B_OFFSET, SD_FILTER_NEAREST);   # the filter field
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_C_OFFSET, 0);
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_D_OFFSET, 0);
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_STOPS_OFFSET, 0);               # ⛔ no stops, no ramp — see the header
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_NSTOPS_OFFSET, 0);
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_OFFSET, 0);
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_OFFSET, 0);
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_DIRTY_OFFSET, 0);
    return p;
}

# @public — 1 if `p` is a pattern, else 0. Safe on 0 and on a gradient.
fn sd_paint_is_pattern(p): i64 {
    if (p == 0) { return 0; }
    if (load64(p + SD_GRADIENT_KIND_OFFSET) == SD_PAINT_PATTERN) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# @public — the surface a pattern samples, or 0 for a null paint or a gradient.
fn sd_pattern_source(p): i64 {
    if (sd_paint_is_pattern(p) == 0) { return 0; }
    return load64(p + SD_GRADIENT_A_OFFSET);
}

# @public — the pattern's filter, or 0 for a null paint or a gradient (which is also
# SD_FILTER_NEAREST — tell them apart with sd_paint_is_pattern, never by the value).
fn sd_pattern_filter(p): i64 {
    if (sd_paint_is_pattern(p) == 0) { return 0; }
    return load64(p + SD_GRADIENT_B_OFFSET);
}

# @public — choose the pattern's filter. SADISH_OK, SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null paint or a
# gradient, or SADISH_ERR_UNSUPPORTED for a filter this release does not implement.
# ⚠ Refusing is the point: a consumer that asks for bilinear and is silently given nearest has no
# way to find out. See SD_FILTER_NEAREST.
fn sd_pattern_set_filter(p, f): i64 {
    if (p == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (load64(p + SD_GRADIENT_KIND_OFFSET) != SD_PAINT_PATTERN) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (f != SD_FILTER_NEAREST) { return SADISH_ERR_UNSUPPORTED; }
    store64(p + SD_GRADIENT_B_OFFSET, f);
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# Map texel index `i` onto [0, n) under `spread`, n > 0. Returns the index, or -1 meaning "paint
# nothing here" (SD_SPREAD_NONE outside the rect).
# ⚠ FLOOR SEMANTICS ON NEGATIVES, matching the gradient spreads exactly: REPEAT sends -1 to n - 1
# (not to 0), and REFLECT is the period-2n triangle, so -1 reads texel 0 and n reads texel n - 1.
# A gradient's t = -0.25 reads 0.75 under REPEAT for the same reason; the two are the same rule at
# different scales, which is what lets a consumer reason about a pattern the way it already reasons
# about a gradient.
fn _sd_pattern_wrap(i, n, spread): i64 {
    if (i >= 0) {
        if (i < n) { return i; }
    }
    if (spread == SD_SPREAD_NONE) { return 0 - 1; }
    if (spread == SD_SPREAD_REPEAT) {
        var m = i % n;
        if (m < 0) { m = m + n; }
        return m;
    }
    if (spread == SD_SPREAD_REFLECT) {
        var period = n + n;
        var v = i % period;
        if (v < 0) { v = v + period; }
        if (v < n) { return v; }
        return period - 1 - v;
    }
    # PAD, and the defensive answer for any spread not named above.
    if (i < 0) { return 0; }
    return n - 1;
}

# The colour of `n` sorted stops at t (16.16, [0, SD_ONE]), given k = the LAST stop whose offset is
# <= t (0 when t lies before every stop): stop k's colour before the first stop and from the last
# one on, otherwise the per-channel interpolation to stop k + 1, rounded to nearest. 0xAARRGGBB with
# the actual alpha.
fn _sd_gradient_lerp(stops, n, k, t): i64 {
    var lo = stops + k * 16;
    var c = load64(lo + 8);
    var e = sd_rgba(sd_color_r(c), sd_color_g(c), sd_color_b(c), sd_alpha_of(c));
    if (t < load64(lo)) { return e; }
    if (k + 1 >= n) { return e; }
    # load64(lo) <= t < offset[k+1], so the span is > 0.
    var hi = lo + 16;
    var c1 = load64(hi + 8);
    var span = load64(hi) - load64(lo);
    var w1 = t - load64(lo);
    var w0 = span - w1;
    var half = span >> 1;
    var r = (sd_color_r(c) * w0 + sd_color_r(c1) * w1 + half) / span;
    var gg = (sd_color_g(c) * w0 + sd_color_g(c1) * w1 + half) / span;
    var b = (sd_color_b(c) * w0 + sd_color_b(c1) * w1 + half) / span;
    var a = (sd_alpha_of(c) * w0 + sd_alpha_of(c1) * w1 + half) / span;
    return sd_rgba(r, gg, b, a);
}

# The colour at t (16.16, [0, SD_ONE]) by a stop search — for t inside a marked ramp cell.
fn _sd_gradient_eval(g, t): i64 {
    var stops = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_STOPS_OFFSET);
    var n = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_NSTOPS_OFFSET);
    var k = 0;
    var adv = 0;
    while (adv == 0) {
        if (k + 1 >= n) {
            adv = 1;
        } else {
            if (load64(stops + (k + 1) * 16) <= t) { k = k + 1; } else { adv = 1; }
        }
    }
    return _sd_gradient_lerp(stops, n, k, t);
}

# Resolve the sorted stops into the ramp: entry i = the colour at t = i * 64 (16.16), or 0 (alpha
# byte 0: no real entry has it) when a stop lies strictly inside (i * 64, (i + 1) * 64). Requires
# n_stops >= 1. Entry 1024 is never marked: no offset exceeds SD_ONE.
fn _sd_gradient_build_ramp(g): i64 {
    var stops = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_STOPS_OFFSET);
    var n = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_NSTOPS_OFFSET);
    var ramp = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_OFFSET);
    var cell = SD_ONE >> SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_BITS;
    var k = 0;                       # the LAST stop whose offset <= t (0 while t is before them all)
    var i = 0;
    while (i <= SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_N) {
        var t = i * cell;
        var adv = 0;
        while (adv == 0) {
            if (k + 1 >= n) {
                adv = 1;
            } else {
                if (load64(stops + (k + 1) * 16) <= t) { k = k + 1; } else { adv = 1; }
            }
        }
        var e = _sd_gradient_lerp(stops, n, k, t);
        # The first stop with an offset > t: k + 1, or k itself while t is before every stop.
        var nxt = k + 1;
        if (load64(stops + k * 16) > t) { nxt = k; }
        if (nxt < n) {
            if (load64(stops + nxt * 16) < t + cell) { e = 0; }
        }
        store32(ramp + i * 4, e);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    store64(g + SD_GRADIENT_DIRTY_OFFSET, 0);
    return 0;
}

# Map t = floor(true_t * one) (`one` a power of two) into [0, one] under `spread`, still a floor.
# `frac` is 1 when true_t * one is not an integer. Two's-complement `&` IS the floor-based modulo, so
# negative t needs no special case; REFLECT mirrors through the ceiling, floor(2 - x) = 2 - ceil(x).
fn _sd_gradient_spread(spread, t, frac, one): i64 {
    if (spread == SD_SPREAD_REPEAT) { return t & (one - 1); }
    if (spread == SD_SPREAD_REFLECT) {
        var v = t & (one * 2 - 1);
        var c = v + frac;
        if (c > one) { v = one * 2 - c; }
        return v;
    }
    if (t < 0) { return 0; }
    if (t > one) { return one; }
    return t;
}

# @public — the colour the paint has at parameter `t` (16.16, any value; spread applied), as
# 0xAARRGGBB — exactly what a blit paints for a pixel whose t is `t`: the ramp entry, or the stop
# evaluation in a cell that holds a stop. Byte 3 is the ACTUAL interpolated alpha, 1..255: it is
# never 0, because no stop can express 0 (legacy rule) and interpolation stays between its stops —
# so the value also reads back unchanged through sd_alpha_of. Read it with sd_color_a. Returns 0
# for g == 0 or a gradient with no stops; a caller tells that apart from a colour by
# sd_gradient_stop_count (0), never by the value.
fn sd_gradient_color_at(g, t): i64 {
    if (g == 0) { return 0; }
    if (load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_NSTOPS_OFFSET) == 0) { return 0; }
    if (load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_DIRTY_OFFSET) != 0) { _sd_gradient_build_ramp(g); }
    var u = _sd_gradient_spread(load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_SPREAD_OFFSET), t, 0, SD_ONE);
    var ramp = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_OFFSET);
    var e = load32(ramp + (u >> (SD_SHIFT - SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_BITS)) * 4);
    if (e == 0) { e = _sd_gradient_eval(g, u); }
    return e;
}

# floor(n / b) for b > 0 — Cyrius `/` truncates TOWARD ZERO, so a negative numerator needs the
# correction below. This is what the pattern sampler's per-pixel coordinate reduces to: MEASURED
# over 6,138 (value, divisor) pairs, sd_asr(_sd_paint_divq(n, b, 16), 16) == _sd_paint_floordiv(n, b)
# for every one. The 16-bit long division was computing sixteen bits it then threw away.
fn _sd_paint_floordiv(n, b): i64 {
    var q = n / b;
    if (n < 0) {
        if (q * b != n) { q = q - 1; }
    }
    return q;
}

# floor(n * 2^k / b) for b > 0, by restoring binary long division; the remainder, in [0, b), is left
# in _sd_paint_rem. Exact, and no intermediate exceeds b (< 2^63) — a remainder is doubled only when
# that stays below b — as long as the quotient itself fits (the direct product would not).
fn _sd_paint_divq(n, b, k): i64 {
    var neg = 0;
    var rem = n;
    if (rem < 0) { rem = 0 - rem; neg = 1; }
    var q = rem / b;
    rem = rem - q * b;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < k) {
        q = q << 1;
        if (rem >= b - rem) {
            rem = rem - (b - rem);
            q = q + 1;
        } else {
            rem = rem + rem;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (neg != 0) {
        q = 0 - q;
        if (rem != 0) {
            q = q - 1;
            rem = b - rem;
        }
    }
    _sd_paint_rem = rem;
    return q;
}

# floor(sqrt(n)) — the same value as sd_isqrt, which starts Newton at n itself (~log2(n)/2 halving
# divisions before it converges). This one starts at 2^ceil(bits/2), within 2x of the root, so it
# converges in a handful of divisions: it runs once per covered pixel of a radial blit.
fn _sd_paint_isqrt(n): i64 {
    if (n <= 0) { return 0; }
    var v = n;
    var bits = 1;
    if (v >= 4294967296) { v = v >> 32; bits = bits + 32; }
    if (v >= 65536) { v = v >> 16; bits = bits + 16; }
    if (v >= 256) { v = v >> 8; bits = bits + 8; }
    if (v >= 16) { v = v >> 4; bits = bits + 4; }
    if (v >= 4) { v = v >> 2; bits = bits + 2; }
    if (v >= 2) { bits = bits + 1; }
    var x = 1 << ((bits + 1) >> 1);   # n < 2^bits  ⇒  sqrt(n) < x
    var y = (x + n / x) >> 1;
    while (y < x) {
        x = y;
        y = (x + n / x) >> 1;
    }
    return x;
}

# floor(sqrt(d2) * 2^16 / r) for 0 <= d2 < 2^63 and r > 0 — a radial pixel's t in 16.16, EXACT.
# _sd_paint_rem is left 1 when sqrt(d2) * 2^16 / r is not an integer, else 0.
# The root is taken to 16 fraction bits, R = floor(sqrt(d2 * 2^32)), and t = R / r (a floor of a
# floor is the floor). Below 2^31, d2 * 2^32 fits and R is its root. Above it, with
# s = floor(sqrt(d2)) and m = d2 - s^2, the fraction bits are f = floor(m * 2^16 / (sqrt(d2) + s)),
# which is floor(m * 2^16 / 2s) or one less: the two quotients differ by < 2^16 / (2s + 1), under 1
# for any s >= 32,768, and here s >= 46,340. (s * 2^16 + f)^2 <= d2 * 2^32 decides it, as
# f^2 <= ((m * 2^16) mod 2s) * 2^16 — i64 throughout, where the direct square needs ~96 bits.
# (m == 2s makes the quotient 65536 with remainder 0, which that test takes down to 65535.)
fn _sd_paint_radial_t(d2, r): i64 {
    var root = 0;
    var inexact = 0;
    if (d2 < 2147483648) {
        var big = d2 << 32;
        root = _sd_paint_isqrt(big);
        if (root * root != big) { inexact = 1; }
    } else {
        var s = _sd_paint_isqrt(d2);
        var num = (d2 - s * s) << 16;
        var f = num / (2 * s);
        if (f * f > ((num - f * 2 * s) << 16)) { f = f - 1; }
        root = (s << 16) + f;
        if (num != 0) { inexact = 1; }
    }
    var t = root / r;
    if (t * r != root) { inexact = 1; }
    _sd_paint_rem = inexact;
    return t;
}

# ---- wide arithmetic for the focal root and the transformed axis ----------------------------------
# The bit length of v: 0 for v <= 0, else floor(log2(v)) + 1.
fn _sd_paint_bitlen(v): i64 {
    if (v <= 0) { return 0; }
    var x = v;
    var n = 1;
    if (x >= 4294967296) { x = x >> 32; n = n + 32; }
    if (x >= 65536) { x = x >> 16; n = n + 16; }
    if (x >= 256) { x = x >> 8; n = n + 8; }
    if (x >= 16) { x = x >> 4; n = n + 4; }
    if (x >= 4) { x = x >> 2; n = n + 2; }
    if (x >= 2) { n = n + 1; }
    return n;
}

# a * b for 0 <= a, b < 2^63 as 128 bits: returns the LOW 64 bits (a bit pattern — negative as an i64
# when bit 63 is set) and leaves the high 64 in _sd_paint_hi. By 32-bit halves: a0 * b0 < 2^64 wraps
# into a bit pattern the logical >> reads back; every other partial product is < 2^63.
fn _sd_paint_umul(a, b): i64 {
    var a0 = a & 4294967295;
    var a1 = a >> 32;
    var b0 = b & 4294967295;
    var b1 = b >> 32;
    var p00 = a0 * b0;
    var p01 = a0 * b1;
    var p10 = a1 * b0;
    var mid = (p00 >> 32) + (p01 & 4294967295) + (p10 & 4294967295);
    _sd_paint_hi = a1 * b1 + (p01 >> 32) + (p10 >> 32) + (mid >> 32);
    return (p00 & 4294967295) | ((mid & 4294967295) << 32);
}

# x < y with both read as UNSIGNED 64-bit patterns (the low halves of 128-bit values).
fn _sd_paint_ult(x, y): i64 {
    if ((x ^ (1 << 63)) < (y ^ (1 << 63))) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# The bit length of the non-negative 128-bit value hi:lo.
fn _sd_paint_bitlen128(hi, lo): i64 {
    if (hi != 0) { return 64 + _sd_paint_bitlen(hi); }
    if (lo < 0) { return 64; }
    return _sd_paint_bitlen(lo);
}

# The low 64 bits of hi:lo >> s for 0 < s <= 127, or of lo << -s for s <= 0 (then hi must be 0).
fn _sd_paint_shr128(hi, lo, s): i64 {
    if (s <= 0) { return lo << (0 - s); }
    if (s >= 64) { return hi >> (s - 64); }
    return (lo >> s) | (hi << (64 - s));
}

# ---- focal radial ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-blit constants of the focal root (single-threaded, like _sd_paint_rem): e = c - f, and A = r^2 - |e|^2
# (> 0) twice over — normalized, A = fan * 2^fsn with fan in [2^30, 2^31) (fsn < 0 is an exact left
# shift), and as the divisor fad = A >> fsd < 2^31 (fsd = 0 keeps a small A exact).
var _sd_paint_fex = 0;
var _sd_paint_fey = 0;
var _sd_paint_fa = 0;
var _sd_paint_fan = 0;
var _sd_paint_fsn = 0;
var _sd_paint_fad = 0;
var _sd_paint_fsd = 0;

fn _sd_paint_focal_setup(fex, fey, fa): i64 {
    _sd_paint_fex = fex;
    _sd_paint_fey = fey;
    _sd_paint_fa = fa;
    var na = _sd_paint_bitlen(fa);
    _sd_paint_fsn = na - 31;
    _sd_paint_fad = fa;
    _sd_paint_fsd = 0;
    if (na >= 31) {
        _sd_paint_fan = fa >> (na - 31);
        _sd_paint_fad = _sd_paint_fan;
        _sd_paint_fsd = na - 31;
    } else {
        _sd_paint_fan = fa << (31 - na);
    }
    return 0;
}

# The sign (-1, 0, 1) of h(k) = A k^2 + 2^17 b k - 2^32 l, for 0 <= k < 2^25, EXACTLY, in 128 bits:
# A k^2 < 2^112, |b| k 2^17 < 2^105, l 2^32 < 2^95 — every sum < 2^113, so each high half is a
# non-negative i64.
fn _sd_paint_focal_sign(k, b, l): i64 {
    var plo = _sd_paint_umul(_sd_paint_fa, k * k);
    var phi = _sd_paint_hi;
    var bm = b;
    if (bm < 0) { bm = 0 - bm; }
    var mlo = _sd_paint_umul(bm, k << 17);
    var mhi = _sd_paint_hi;
    var nlo = l << 32;
    var nhi = l >> 32;
    if (b >= 0) {
        var slo = plo + mlo;
        phi = phi + mhi;
        if (_sd_paint_ult(slo, plo) != 0) { phi = phi + 1; }
        plo = slo;
    } else {
        var tlo = nlo + mlo;
        nhi = nhi + mhi;
        if (_sd_paint_ult(tlo, nlo) != 0) { nhi = nhi + 1; }
        nlo = tlo;
    }
    if (phi > nhi) { return 1; }
    if (phi < nhi) { return 0 - 1; }
    if (plo == nlo) { return 0; }
    if (_sd_paint_ult(plo, nlo) != 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    return 1;
}

# ⭐ floor(t * 2^16) of a focal gradient at d = (dx, dy) = p - f (raw), with _sd_paint_rem = 1 when
# t * 2^16 is not an integer; the per-blit constants from _sd_paint_focal_setup. With l = |d|^2 and
# b = d·e (both exact i64 inside the bound), t * 2^16 is the positive root of
#     h(T) = A T^2 + 2^17 b T - 2^32 l
# (the quadratic above scaled by T = t * 2^16), so floor(t * 2^16) = the largest T with h(T) <= 0 —
# but h needs ~113 bits and its direct root ~158, so the root is taken in two steps:
#   1. ESTIMATE t * 2^24 in i64, as a mantissa and an exponent. S = sqrt(b^2 + A l) comes from those
#      two products rebuilt from 31-bit normalized factors (exact shifts left, truncating shifts
#      right: each factor >= 2^30 loses < 2^-30), aligned to one even exponent and rooted to 31 bits.
#      Then t = l / (b + S) when b >= 0 and t = (S - b) / A when b < 0 — the form with no
#      cancellation — as one 62-bit / 31-bit hardware division, shifted into place. Relative error
#      < 2^-28 (four 2^-30 truncations, the root's and the divisor's), plus < 2 units of 2^-24 from the
#      floors. Nothing overflows for any d within the bound; outside it, and for t * 2^24 >= 2^62,
#      t * 2^16 saturates at 2^54 — never a trap (every divisor is checked > 0 first).
#   2. DECIDE. With w = t8 / 2^26 + 3 > the estimate's error, a fraction (in 2^-24) at least w away
#      from both integers of 2^-16 settles floor(t * 2^16) and inexact. Otherwise the true value is
#      within 1 of k = round(t * 2^16), and the sign of h(k), exact in 128 bits (_sd_paint_focal_sign),
#      decides: h(k) > 0 -> k - 1, inexact; == 0 -> k, exact; < 0 -> k, inexact. ~2.4 % of pixels
#      with t < 1 take this step (6 of 256 fractions).
# ⇒ EXACT for t < 256 (t * 2^24 < 2^32, where w <= 69 < 128). Past it the estimate alone is used:
# floor(t * 2^16) within t * 2^-10 + 1, flagged inexact. MEASURED in a Python model of this code over
# 2.75M random and adversarial cases (focal on / just inside / at the centre, pixels on the circle,
# behind the focal point, within 4 raw of it, radii 2^1 .. 2^30 raw): 0 mismatches; the worst
# estimate error was 0.35 w. programs/paint_focal_test.cyr checks it against an independent bignum
# root.
fn _sd_paint_focal_t(dx, dy): i64 {
    var l = dx * dx + dy * dy;
    if (l == 0) {
        _sd_paint_rem = 0;
        return 0;
    }
    var sat = 18014398509481984;   # 2^54: t * 2^16 saturates at t = 2^38 (only past the bound)
    _sd_paint_rem = 1;
    if (l < 0) { return sat; }
    var b = dx * _sd_paint_fex + dy * _sd_paint_fey;
    var bm = b;
    if (bm < 0) { bm = 0 - bm; }
    if (bm < 0) { return sat; }
    # q * 2^ex ~ b^2 + A l, q in [2^60, 2^62).
    var nl = _sd_paint_bitlen(l);
    var l1 = 0;
    if (nl >= 31) { l1 = l >> (nl - 31); } else { l1 = l << (31 - nl); }
    var e2 = _sd_paint_fsn + nl - 31;
    var ex = e2;
    var e1 = 0;
    var t1 = 0;
    if (bm != 0) {
        var nb = _sd_paint_bitlen(bm);
        var b1 = 0;
        if (nb >= 31) { b1 = bm >> (nb - 31); } else { b1 = bm << (31 - nb); }
        t1 = b1 * b1;
        e1 = 2 * (nb - 31);
        if (e1 > ex) { ex = e1; }
    }
    ex = ex + (ex & 1);
    var q = 0;
    if (ex - e2 < 63) { q = (_sd_paint_fan * l1) >> (ex - e2); }
    if (bm != 0) {
        if (ex - e1 < 63) { q = q + (t1 >> (ex - e1)); }
    }
    if (q >= 4611686018427387904) {
        q = q >> 2;
        ex = ex + 2;
    }
    if (q < 1152921504606846976) {
        q = q << 2;
        ex = ex - 2;
    }
    var root = _sd_paint_isqrt(q);   # S ~ root * 2^hs, root in [2^30, 2^31)
    var hs = sd_asr(ex, 1);
    if (hs > 31) { return sat; }
    var quot = 0;
    var z = 0;
    if (b >= 0) {
        # t * 2^24 = l 2^24 / (b + S)
        var den = 0;
        var xd = 0;
        if (hs >= 0) {
            den = b + (root << hs);
        } else {
            if (b >= (1 << (62 + hs))) { return sat; }
            den = (b << (0 - hs)) + root;
            xd = hs;
        }
        if (den <= 0) { return sat; }
        var lsh = 62 - nl;
        var ln = 0;
        if (nl == 63) { ln = l >> 1; } else { ln = l << lsh; }
        var nd = _sd_paint_bitlen(den);
        quot = ln / (den >> (nd - 31));
        z = 24 - xd - lsh - (nd - 31);
    } else {
        # t * 2^24 = (S + |b|) 2^24 / A
        var num = 0;
        var xn = 0;
        if (hs >= 0) {
            num = bm + (root << hs);
        } else {
            if (bm >= (1 << (62 + hs))) { return sat; }
            num = (bm << (0 - hs)) + root;
            xn = hs;
        }
        if (num <= 0) { return sat; }
        var nn = _sd_paint_bitlen(num);
        var nsh = 62 - nn;
        var nnum = 0;
        if (nn == 63) { nnum = num >> 1; } else { nnum = num << nsh; }
        quot = nnum / _sd_paint_fad;
        z = 24 + xn - nsh - _sd_paint_fsd;
    }
    var t8 = 0;
    if (z >= 0) {
        if (z >= 62) { return sat; }
        if (quot >= (1 << (62 - z))) { return sat; }
        t8 = quot << z;
    } else {
        if (z > 0 - 63) { t8 = quot >> (0 - z); }
    }
    if (t8 >= 4294967296) { return t8 >> 8; }
    var fr = t8 & 255;
    var w = (t8 >> 26) + 3;
    if (fr >= w) {
        if (fr + w <= 255) { return t8 >> 8; }
    }
    var k = (t8 + 128) >> 8;
    var sg = _sd_paint_focal_sign(k, b, l);
    if (sg > 0) { return k - 1; }
    if (sg == 0) { _sd_paint_rem = 0; }
    return k;
}

# ---- transformed linear axis ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The canvas-space axis of the linear gradient (x0, y0) + (vx, vy) under ext's matrix (a, b, c, d, e, f):
# P0 = m(x0, y0) and V = |v|^2 J^-T v / |J^-T v|^2, each component rounded to the nearest raw unit, into
# _sd_paint_ax / _ay / _avx / _avy. With w = adj(J)^T v (raw^2), V = |v|^2 det w / (2^16 |w|^2): the
# ratio |v|^2 |det| / |w|^2 of two 128-bit values is taken to 60 fraction bits from their 62-bit
# mantissas (relative error < 2^-58), times |w| in 128 bits, shifted down with rounding — so V is within
# 2^-26 raw of exact before the final rounding, and an axis with an exact raw answer gets it.
# Requires det != 0, entries within ±2^30 raw, |v|^2 >= 2^16 (sd_gradient_set_matrix / the blit ensure it).
var _sd_paint_ax = 0;
var _sd_paint_ay = 0;
var _sd_paint_avx = 0;
var _sd_paint_avy = 0;

# One component: sign(w) sign(det) round(ratio * |w| / 2^s).
fn _sd_paint_axis_comp(ratio, w, det, s): i64 {
    if (s < 1) { return 0; }
    if (s > 126) { return 0; }
    var wm = w;
    if (wm < 0) { wm = 0 - wm; }
    var lo = _sd_paint_umul(ratio, wm);
    var hi = _sd_paint_hi;
    var hlo = 0;
    if (s <= 64) {
        hlo = 1 << (s - 1);
    } else {
        hi = hi + (1 << (s - 65));
    }
    var slo = lo + hlo;
    if (_sd_paint_ult(slo, lo) != 0) { hi = hi + 1; }
    var v = _sd_paint_shr128(hi, slo, s);
    if ((w < 0) != (det < 0)) { v = 0 - v; }
    return v;
}

fn _sd_paint_axis(ext, x0, y0, vx, vy): i64 {
    var a = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET);
    var b = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 8);
    var c = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 16);
    var d = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 24);
    var e = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 32);
    var f = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 40);
    _sd_paint_ax = sd_asr(a * x0 + c * y0 + 32768, 16) + e;
    _sd_paint_ay = sd_asr(b * x0 + d * y0 + 32768, 16) + f;
    var det = a * d - b * c;
    var wx = d * vx - b * vy;
    var wy = a * vy - c * vx;
    var dm = det;
    if (dm < 0) { dm = 0 - dm; }
    var nlo = _sd_paint_umul(vx * vx + vy * vy, dm);
    var nhi = _sd_paint_hi;
    var wxm = wx;
    if (wxm < 0) { wxm = 0 - wxm; }
    var wym = wy;
    if (wym < 0) { wym = 0 - wym; }
    var xlo = _sd_paint_umul(wxm, wxm);
    var xhi = _sd_paint_hi;
    var ylo = _sd_paint_umul(wym, wym);
    var whi = xhi + _sd_paint_hi;
    var wlo = xlo + ylo;
    if (_sd_paint_ult(wlo, xlo) != 0) { whi = whi + 1; }
    _sd_paint_avx = 0;
    _sd_paint_avy = 0;
    if ((whi | wlo) == 0) { return 0; }
    var sn = _sd_paint_bitlen128(nhi, nlo) - 62;
    var sw = _sd_paint_bitlen128(whi, wlo) - 62;
    var ratio = _sd_paint_divq(_sd_paint_shr128(nhi, nlo, sn), _sd_paint_shr128(whi, wlo, sw), 60);
    var s = 76 - sn + sw;
    _sd_paint_avx = _sd_paint_axis_comp(ratio, wx, det, s);
    _sd_paint_avy = _sd_paint_axis_comp(ratio, wy, det, s);
    return 0;
}

# ================================================================
# _sd_paint_blit_run — THE gradient blit: composite the canvas coverage onto `surface` with the
# canvas's (0,0) at surface pixel (dx, dy), the source colour of each pixel coming from gradient `g`
# sampled at the pixel's centre in CANVAS space. Clipped to the surface on every side; rows addressed
# by sd_surface_stride. Returns SADISH_OK (also for a gradient with no stops, or one whose matrix is
# blocked, which paint nothing), or SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null cv / surface / g.
# A linear gradient under a matrix is resolved to its canvas-space axis here and painted by the same
# loop; a focal point off the centre, or a matrix on a radial / focal gradient, gives every pixel its
# own gradient-space point — _sd_paint_blit_point's loop. The loop below is 0.6.0's, with `pm` added.
#
# ⭐ `pm` PICKS THE COMPOSITOR, AND NOTHING ELSE (0.7.0). Everything above the store — the linear
# DDA and its remainders, the radial and focal roots, the transformed point stepping, the spread, the
# ramp lookup and the marked-cell stop search — is ONE implementation, run identically either way, so
# no pixel can take a different t or a different colour in one blit than in the other:
#   pm == 0  sd_canvas_blit_paint_at: source alpha = coverage * paint_alpha / 255 (rounded DOWN to a
#            byte), then sd_canvas_blit_at's straight src-over — every pixel with coverage != 0 is
#            written and gets dst alpha 255, even where that product rounds to 0.
#   pm != 0  sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at (src/premul.cyr): the pixel's straight colour is
#            premultiplied at its own interpolated alpha and composited src-over a PREMULTIPLIED
#            destination, coverage and alpha kept apart, the dst alpha composited rather than forced.
# ⛔ Do not specialize one branch without the other, and do not lift the store out of the loop into
# a per-pixel helper for BOTH: pm == 0's four stores are 0.6.0's bytes verbatim, and paint_test /
# paint_focal_test pass on them unedited. The premultiplied store is one call because its arithmetic
# belongs to premul.cyr, which owns the formula and shares it with sd_canvas_blit_premul_at.
# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_blit_paint_at for a PATTERN (0.11.0): every covered pixel takes one source texel,
# nearest-neighbour. Clip, stride, coverage and BOTH stores are _sd_paint_blit_run's, `pm` included
# and meaning exactly the same thing — only the colour's origin differs, so a pattern and a gradient
# composite identically and a one-texel pattern paints the bytes sd_canvas_blit_at would.
#
# ⭐ TWO PATHS, AND THE COMMON ONE IS FREE. Unmapped, paint space IS canvas space, so the texel for
# canvas pixel (x, y) is (x, y) before spread — no arithmetic per pixel at all, which is the 1:1
# tile blit a UI toolkit actually does. Mapped, each pixel centre is carried back through the
# inverse of the caller's matrix: q = adj(J) (p - (e, f)) / det, in 16.16, by _sd_paint_divq — the
# same exact long division the radial path uses, evaluated per pixel rather than DDA-stepped.
# ⚠ WHY PER PIXEL AND NOT A DDA. The radial loop carries quotient+remainder because a gradient's t
# feeds a 1,025-entry ramp where one LSB can change the colour. A NEAREST sample discards all 16
# fraction bits, so sub-LSB drift can only matter for a pixel centre landing exactly on a texel
# boundary — and the division here is floor-exact, so even that pixel takes the texel the algebra
# says it does. The DDA would buy no accuracy; it is not omitted for simplicity alone.
# ⚠ PRECISION BOUND, inherited: every matrix entry < 2^30 (sd_gradient_set_matrix enforces it) and
# every pixel centre < 16,383 px, so each product below is < 2^60 and their difference < 2^61.
fn _sd_pattern_blit(cv, surface, g, dx, dy, pm): i64 {
    var ext = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_OFFSET);
    var xform = SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_NONE;
    if (ext != 0) { xform = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_XFORM_OFFSET); }
    if (xform == SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_BLOCKED) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var src = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_A_OFFSET);
    if (src == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var pw = sd_surface_width(src);
    var ph = sd_surface_height(src);
    # A 0-area source paints nothing and is not an error — the gradient's "zero stops" answer.
    if (pw <= 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    if (ph <= 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var ppx = sd_surface_pixels(src);
    var ppitch = sd_surface_stride(src);
    var spread = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_SPREAD_OFFSET);

    var cw = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var ch = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var covbuf = sd_canvas_coverage(cv);
    var covstride = sd_canvas_stride(cv);
    var sw = sd_surface_width(surface);
    var sh = sd_surface_height(surface);
    var spx = sd_surface_pixels(surface);
    var pitch = sd_surface_stride(surface);
    var x0 = 0;
    if (dx < 0) { x0 = 0 - dx; }
    var y0 = 0;
    if (dy < 0) { y0 = 0 - dy; }
    var x1 = cw;
    if (dx + x1 > sw) { x1 = sw - dx; }
    var y1 = ch;
    if (dy + y1 > sh) { y1 = sh - dy; }

    var mapped = 0;
    var ma = 0;
    var mb = 0;
    var mc = 0;
    var md = 0;
    var me = 0;
    var mf = 0;
    var mdet = 0;
    if (xform == SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_MATRIX) {
        ma = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET);
        mb = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 8);
        mc = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 16);
        md = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 24);
        me = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 32);
        mf = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 40);
        mdet = ma * md - mb * mc;
        if (mdet == 0) { return SADISH_OK; }   # set_matrix already refuses this; belt and braces
        if (mdet < 0) {
            # adj(J) / det == (-adj(J)) / |det|: negate the 2x2 so _sd_paint_divq's divisor is > 0.
            mdet = 0 - mdet;
            ma = 0 - ma;
            mb = 0 - mb;
            mc = 0 - mc;
            md = 0 - md;
        }
        mapped = 1;
    }

    # ⭐⭐ THE PER-PIXEL DIVISION IS GONE (0.11.2) — this loop ran TWO 16-iteration long divisions
    # per pixel, and MEASURED at 256x256 that made a mapped pattern blit 9.9x an unmapped one
    # (13.7 ms against 1.39). Two facts collapse it:
    #   1. sd_asr(_sd_paint_divq(N, mdet, 16), 16) IS floor(N / mdet) — the sixteen fraction bits
    #      were computed and then shifted away. Nearest sampling never wanted them.
    #   2. N is LINEAR in x: N(x+1, y) - N(x, y) = md * SD_ONE, a per-blit constant. So the
    #      quotient can be carried like the linear gradient's DDA — quotient plus remainder over
    #      mdet — and a pixel step is two adds and a compare instead of a division.
    # ⚠ THE DDA ADVANCES ON EVERY PIXEL, COVERED OR NOT, where the old code computed nothing for an
    # uncovered one. That is the trade: a few adds on skipped pixels against a long division on
    # painted ones. It is the right side of the trade at any coverage a pattern is worth using for,
    # and it is stated so nobody re-derives it as a regression.
    # ⛔ EXACTNESS IS THE REQUIREMENT, NOT THE GOAL. Every texel index must be the one the old
    # arithmetic produced — programs/pattern_test.cyr group D is the gate, and the 340-measurement
    # oracle diffs to zero.
    var dnq = 0;
    var dnr = 0;
    var dmq = 0;
    var dmr = 0;
    if (mapped != 0) {
        var dnx = md * SD_ONE;              # N(x+1) - N(x)
        var dmx = 0 - mb * SD_ONE;          # M(x+1) - M(x)
        dnq = _sd_paint_floordiv(dnx, mdet);
        dnr = dnx - dnq * mdet;             # in [0, mdet)
        dmq = _sd_paint_floordiv(dmx, mdet);
        dmr = dmx - dmq * mdet;
    }

    var y = y0;
    while (y < y1) {
        # Seed the row. Two divisions per ROW replace two per PIXEL.
        var nq = 0;
        var nr = 0;
        var mq = 0;
        var mr = 0;
        if (mapped != 0) {
            var ex0 = (x0 << SD_SHIFT) + (SD_ONE >> 1) - me;
            var ey0 = (y << SD_SHIFT) + (SD_ONE >> 1) - mf;
            var n0 = md * ex0 - mc * ey0;
            var m0 = ma * ey0 - mb * ex0;
            nq = _sd_paint_floordiv(n0, mdet);
            nr = n0 - nq * mdet;
            mq = _sd_paint_floordiv(m0, mdet);
            mr = m0 - mq * mdet;
        }
        var x = x0;
        while (x < x1) {
            var a = load8(covbuf + y * covstride + x);
            if (a != 0) {
                var tx = x;
                var ty = y;
                # ⛔ NO sd_asr HERE ANY MORE, and that is not a regression of the 0.11.0 fix — the
                # shift it guarded no longer exists. nq / mq ARE the floored texel indices, negative
                # ones included, because _sd_paint_floordiv floors rather than truncating. The
                # negative-index gate (pattern_test group D) is what proves it.
                if (mapped != 0) {
                    tx = nq;
                    ty = mq;
                }
                var sx = _sd_pattern_wrap(tx, pw, spread);
                var sy = _sd_pattern_wrap(ty, ph, spread);
                # ⛔ -1 is SD_SPREAD_NONE outside the rect: the pixel is SKIPPED, not written
                # transparent. The straight store below forces dst alpha 255, so writing a
                # transparent source there would still stamp an opaque pixel.
                if (sx >= 0) {
                    if (sy >= 0) {
                        var e = load32(ppx + sy * ppitch + sx * 4);
                        var off = (y + dy) * pitch + (x + dx) * 4;
                        if (pm != 0) {
                            _sd_premul_paint_px(spx + off, covbuf + y * covstride + x, e);
                        } else {
                            var sa = a;
                            var pa = (e >> 24) & 255;
                            if (pa != 255) { sa = (a * pa) / 255; }
                            var sr = (e >> 16) & 255;
                            var sg = (e >> 8) & 255;
                            var sb = e & 255;
                            if (sa == 255) {
                                store8(spx + off, sb);
                                store8(spx + off + 1, sg);
                                store8(spx + off + 2, sr);
                                store8(spx + off + 3, 255);
                            } else {
                                var ia = 255 - sa;
                                store8(spx + off, (sb * sa + load8(spx + off) * ia) / 255);
                                store8(spx + off + 1, (sg * sa + load8(spx + off + 1) * ia) / 255);
                                store8(spx + off + 2, (sr * sa + load8(spx + off + 2) * ia) / 255);
                                store8(spx + off + 3, 255);
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            if (mapped != 0) {
                nq = nq + dnq;
                nr = nr + dnr;
                if (nr >= mdet) { nr = nr - mdet; nq = nq + 1; }
                mq = mq + dmq;
                mr = mr + dmr;
                if (mr >= mdet) { mr = mr - mdet; mq = mq + 1; }
            }
            x = x + 1;
        }
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

fn _sd_paint_blit_run(cv, surface, g, dx, dy, pm): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (surface == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (g == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    # ⛔ THE KIND IS READ BEFORE THE STOPS AND BEFORE THE RAMP (0.11.0), and it has to be: a pattern
    # has neither, so the "no stops paints nothing" line below would reject every pattern and
    # _sd_gradient_build_ramp would run on a 0 ramp pointer.
    if (load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_KIND_OFFSET) == SD_PAINT_PATTERN) {
        return _sd_pattern_blit(cv, surface, g, dx, dy, pm);
    }
    if (load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_NSTOPS_OFFSET) == 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var ext = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_OFFSET);
    var xform = SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_NONE;
    if (ext != 0) { xform = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_XFORM_OFFSET); }
    if (xform == SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_BLOCKED) { return SADISH_OK; }
    if (load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_DIRTY_OFFSET) != 0) { _sd_gradient_build_ramp(g); }
    var ramp = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_OFFSET);
    var spread = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_SPREAD_OFFSET);
    var cw = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var ch = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var covbuf = sd_canvas_coverage(cv);
    var covstride = sd_canvas_stride(cv);
    var sw = sd_surface_width(surface);
    var sh = sd_surface_height(surface);
    var spx = sd_surface_pixels(surface);
    var pitch = sd_surface_stride(surface);

    # Clip the canvas rect [dx, dx+cw) x [dy, dy+ch) to the surface (canvas coordinates).
    var x0 = 0;
    if (dx < 0) { x0 = 0 - dx; }
    var y0 = 0;
    if (dy < 0) { y0 = 0 - dy; }
    var x1 = cw;
    if (dx + x1 > sw) { x1 = sw - dx; }
    var y1 = ch;
    if (dy + y1 > sh) { y1 = sh - dy; }

    # mode: 0 linear, 1 radial, 2 solid (the degenerate cases: the colour at t = 1, the last stop).
    var mode = 2;
    var one32 = 1 << 32;
    var gx = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_A_OFFSET);
    var gy = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_B_OFFSET);
    var rad = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_C_OFFSET);
    # Linear DDA, all in t * 2^32 = quotient + remainder / |d|^2: (qx, rx) per pixel step, (qy, ry)
    # per row, (rq, rr) at the row's first pixel; cx / cy = l2 - rx / l2 - ry, so carrying a
    # remainder never forms a sum >= l2.
    var l2 = 0;
    var qx = 0;
    var rx = 0;
    var cx = 0;
    var qy = 0;
    var ry = 0;
    var cy = 0;
    var rq = 0;
    var rr = 0;
    if (load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_KIND_OFFSET) == SD_GRADIENT_LINEAR) {
        var ddx = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_C_OFFSET) - gx;
        var ddy = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_D_OFFSET) - gy;
        l2 = ddx * ddx + ddy * ddy;
        if (xform == SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_MATRIX) {
            # The canvas-space axis. Degenerate if either axis is: |v| or |V| below 2^-8 px.
            if (l2 >= 65536) {
                _sd_paint_axis(ext, gx, gy, ddx, ddy);
                gx = _sd_paint_ax;
                gy = _sd_paint_ay;
                ddx = _sd_paint_avx;
                ddy = _sd_paint_avy;
                l2 = ddx * ddx + ddy * ddy;
            }
        }
        if (l2 >= 65536) {
            mode = 0;
            qx = _sd_paint_divq(ddx, l2, 48);
            rx = _sd_paint_rem;
            cx = l2 - rx;
            qy = _sd_paint_divq(ddy, l2, 48);
            ry = _sd_paint_rem;
            cy = l2 - ry;
            var ex = (x0 << SD_SHIFT) + (SD_ONE >> 1) - gx;
            var ey = (y0 << SD_SHIFT) + (SD_ONE >> 1) - gy;
            rq = _sd_paint_divq(ex * ddx + ey * ddy, l2, 32);
            rr = _sd_paint_rem;
        }
    } else {
        if (rad > 0) {
            mode = 1;
            var focal = _sd_paint_focal_of(g, ext, gx, gy, rad);
            if (focal != 0) { return _sd_paint_blit_point(cv, surface, g, dx, dy, 1, pm); }
            if (xform == SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_MATRIX) { return _sd_paint_blit_point(cv, surface, g, dx, dy, 0, pm); }
        }
    }
    var solid = load32(ramp + SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_N * 4);

    var y = y0;
    while (y < y1) {
        var tq = rq;
        var tr = rr;
        var ey2 = 0;
        if (mode == 1) {
            var eyr = (y << SD_SHIFT) + (SD_ONE >> 1) - gy;
            ey2 = eyr * eyr;
        }
        var x = x0;
        while (x < x1) {
            var a = load8(covbuf + y * covstride + x);
            if (a != 0) {
                var e = solid;
                if (mode == 0) {
                    var u = tq;
                    if (spread == SD_SPREAD_PAD) {
                        if (u < 0) { u = 0; }
                        if (u > one32) { u = one32; }
                    } else {
                        var fr = 0;
                        if (tr != 0) { fr = 1; }
                        u = _sd_gradient_spread(spread, tq, fr, one32);
                    }
                    e = load32(ramp + (u >> 22) * 4);
                    if (e == 0) { e = _sd_gradient_eval(g, u >> 16); }
                }
                if (mode == 1) {
                    var exr = (x << SD_SHIFT) + (SD_ONE >> 1) - gx;
                    var t16 = _sd_paint_radial_t(exr * exr + ey2, rad);
                    var u16 = _sd_gradient_spread(spread, t16, _sd_paint_rem, SD_ONE);
                    e = load32(ramp + (u16 >> 6) * 4);
                    if (e == 0) { e = _sd_gradient_eval(g, u16); }
                }
                var off = (y + dy) * pitch + (x + dx) * 4;
                if (pm != 0) {
                    _sd_premul_paint_px(spx + off, covbuf + y * covstride + x, e);
                } else {
                    var sa = a;
                    var pa = (e >> 24) & 255;
                    if (pa != 255) { sa = (a * pa) / 255; }
                    var sr = (e >> 16) & 255;
                    var sg = (e >> 8) & 255;
                    var sb = e & 255;
                    if (sa == 255) {
                        store8(spx + off, sb);
                        store8(spx + off + 1, sg);
                        store8(spx + off + 2, sr);
                        store8(spx + off + 3, 255);
                    } else {
                        var ia = 255 - sa;
                        store8(spx + off, (sb * sa + load8(spx + off) * ia) / 255);
                        store8(spx + off + 1, (sg * sa + load8(spx + off + 1) * ia) / 255);
                        store8(spx + off + 2, (sr * sa + load8(spx + off + 2) * ia) / 255);
                        store8(spx + off + 3, 255);
                    }
                }
            }
            if (tr >= cx) {
                tr = tr - cx;
                tq = tq + qx + 1;
            } else {
                tr = tr + rx;
                tq = tq + qx;
            }
            x = x + 1;
        }
        if (rr >= cy) {
            rr = rr - cy;
            rq = rq + qy + 1;
        } else {
            rr = rr + ry;
            rq = rq + qy;
        }
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# 1 when radial-kind `g` (r = rad > 0) paints through the focal root — kind SD_GRADIENT_FOCAL with its
# (clamped) focal point off the centre — after loading the per-blit focal constants; else 0 (a focal
# point ON the centre is sd_gradient_radial). A <= 0 cannot happen for a clamped point inside the
# bound; past it the focal point is ignored rather than dividing by it.
fn _sd_paint_focal_of(g, ext, gx, gy, rad): i64 {
    if (load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_KIND_OFFSET) != SD_GRADIENT_FOCAL) { return 0; }
    var fex = gx - load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_FX_OFFSET);
    var fey = gy - load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_FY_OFFSET);
    if ((fex | fey) == 0) { return 0; }
    var fa = rad * rad - fex * fex - fey * fey;
    if (fa <= 0) { return 0; }
    _sd_paint_focal_setup(fex, fey, fa);
    return 1;
}

# sd_canvas_blit_paint_at for a radial or focal gradient (r > 0) whose pixels each need their own
# gradient-space point q: the pixel centre, or under the matrix q = 2^16 adj(J) (p - (e, f)) / det held
# as quotient + remainder / det — (mxq, mxr) / (myq, myr) at the row's first pixel, (pxq, pxr) / (pyq,
# pyr) per pixel step, (rxq, rxr) / (ryq, ryr) per row, the *c = det - remainder carries exactly as
# the linear DDA's — rounded to nearest where the remainder reaches mh = ceil(det / 2). `focal` = 1:
# t from the focal root (constants already loaded by _sd_paint_focal_of), under PAD t >= 1 decided
# by |q - c|^2 >= r^2; 0: the radial t. Clip, stride, spread, ramp and both stores are the loop
# above's, `pm` included and meaning exactly the same thing there.
fn _sd_paint_blit_point(cv, surface, g, dx, dy, focal, pm): i64 {
    var ramp = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_RAMP_OFFSET);
    var spread = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_SPREAD_OFFSET);
    var ext = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_OFFSET);
    var cw = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var ch = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var covbuf = sd_canvas_coverage(cv);
    var covstride = sd_canvas_stride(cv);
    var sw = sd_surface_width(surface);
    var sh = sd_surface_height(surface);
    var spx = sd_surface_pixels(surface);
    var pitch = sd_surface_stride(surface);
    var x0 = 0;
    if (dx < 0) { x0 = 0 - dx; }
    var y0 = 0;
    if (dy < 0) { y0 = 0 - dy; }
    var x1 = cw;
    if (dx + x1 > sw) { x1 = sw - dx; }
    var y1 = ch;
    if (dy + y1 > sh) { y1 = sh - dy; }

    var gx = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_A_OFFSET);
    var gy = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_B_OFFSET);
    var rad = load64(g + SD_GRADIENT_C_OFFSET);
    var rad2 = rad * rad;
    var ffx = 0;
    var ffy = 0;
    if (focal != 0) {
        ffx = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_FX_OFFSET);
        ffy = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_FY_OFFSET);
    }
    var mapped = 0;
    var mh = 0;
    var mxq = 0;
    var mxr = 0;
    var myq = 0;
    var myr = 0;
    var pxq = 0;
    var pxr = 0;
    var pxc = 0;
    var pyq = 0;
    var pyr = 0;
    var pyc = 0;
    var rxq = 0;
    var rxr = 0;
    var rxc = 0;
    var ryq = 0;
    var ryr = 0;
    var ryc = 0;
    if (ext != 0) {
        if (load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_XFORM_OFFSET) == SD_GRADIENT_XFORM_MATRIX) { mapped = 1; }
    }
    if (mapped != 0) {
        var ma = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET);
        var mb = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 8);
        var mc = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 16);
        var md = load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 24);
        var mdet = ma * md - mb * mc;
        if (mdet < 0) {
            # adj(J) / det == (-adj(J)) / |det|: negate the 2x2 so the divisor is positive.
            mdet = 0 - mdet;
            ma = 0 - ma;
            mb = 0 - mb;
            mc = 0 - mc;
            md = 0 - md;
        }
        mh = mdet - (mdet >> 1);
        var px0 = (x0 << SD_SHIFT) + (SD_ONE >> 1) - load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 32);
        var py0 = (y0 << SD_SHIFT) + (SD_ONE >> 1) - load64(ext + SD_GRADIENT_EXT_M_OFFSET + 40);
        mxq = _sd_paint_divq(md * px0 - mc * py0, mdet, 16);
        mxr = _sd_paint_rem;
        myq = _sd_paint_divq(ma * py0 - mb * px0, mdet, 16);
        myr = _sd_paint_rem;
        pxq = _sd_paint_divq(md, mdet, 32);
        pxr = _sd_paint_rem;
        pxc = mdet - pxr;
        pyq = _sd_paint_divq(0 - mb, mdet, 32);
        pyr = _sd_paint_rem;
        pyc = mdet - pyr;
        rxq = _sd_paint_divq(0 - mc, mdet, 32);
        rxr = _sd_paint_rem;
        rxc = mdet - rxr;
        ryq = _sd_paint_divq(ma, mdet, 32);
        ryr = _sd_paint_rem;
        ryc = mdet - ryr;
    }

    var y = y0;
    while (y < y1) {
        var txq = mxq;
        var txr = mxr;
        var tyq = myq;
        var tyr = myr;
        var x = x0;
        while (x < x1) {
            var a = load8(covbuf + y * covstride + x);
            if (a != 0) {
                var qpx = (x << SD_SHIFT) + (SD_ONE >> 1);
                var qpy = (y << SD_SHIFT) + (SD_ONE >> 1);
                if (mapped != 0) {
                    qpx = txq;
                    if (txr >= mh) { qpx = qpx + 1; }
                    qpy = tyq;
                    if (tyr >= mh) { qpy = qpy + 1; }
                }
                var ocx = qpx - gx;
                var ocy = qpy - gy;
                var t16 = SD_ONE;
                _sd_paint_rem = 0;
                if (focal == 0) {
                    t16 = _sd_paint_radial_t(ocx * ocx + ocy * ocy, rad);
                } else {
                    var inside = 1;
                    if (spread == SD_SPREAD_PAD) {
                        if (ocx * ocx + ocy * ocy >= rad2) { inside = 0; }
                    }
                    if (inside != 0) { t16 = _sd_paint_focal_t(qpx - ffx, qpy - ffy); }
                }
                var u16 = _sd_gradient_spread(spread, t16, _sd_paint_rem, SD_ONE);
                var e = load32(ramp + (u16 >> 6) * 4);
                if (e == 0) { e = _sd_gradient_eval(g, u16); }
                var off = (y + dy) * pitch + (x + dx) * 4;
                if (pm != 0) {
                    _sd_premul_paint_px(spx + off, covbuf + y * covstride + x, e);
                } else {
                    var sa = a;
                    var pa = (e >> 24) & 255;
                    if (pa != 255) { sa = (a * pa) / 255; }
                    var sr = (e >> 16) & 255;
                    var sg = (e >> 8) & 255;
                    var sb = e & 255;
                    if (sa == 255) {
                        store8(spx + off, sb);
                        store8(spx + off + 1, sg);
                        store8(spx + off + 2, sr);
                        store8(spx + off + 3, 255);
                    } else {
                        var ia = 255 - sa;
                        store8(spx + off, (sb * sa + load8(spx + off) * ia) / 255);
                        store8(spx + off + 1, (sg * sa + load8(spx + off + 1) * ia) / 255);
                        store8(spx + off + 2, (sr * sa + load8(spx + off + 2) * ia) / 255);
                        store8(spx + off + 3, 255);
                    }
                }
            }
            if (mapped != 0) {
                if (txr >= pxc) {
                    txr = txr - pxc;
                    txq = txq + pxq + 1;
                } else {
                    txr = txr + pxr;
                    txq = txq + pxq;
                }
                if (tyr >= pyc) {
                    tyr = tyr - pyc;
                    tyq = tyq + pyq + 1;
                } else {
                    tyr = tyr + pyr;
                    tyq = tyq + pyq;
                }
            }
            x = x + 1;
        }
        if (mapped != 0) {
            if (mxr >= rxc) {
                mxr = mxr - rxc;
                mxq = mxq + rxq + 1;
            } else {
                mxr = mxr + rxr;
                mxq = mxq + rxq;
            }
            if (myr >= ryc) {
                myr = myr - ryc;
                myq = myq + ryq + 1;
            } else {
                myr = myr + ryr;
                myq = myq + ryq;
            }
        }
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — composite the canvas coverage onto `surface` through the gradient paint `g`, the canvas's
# (0,0) at surface pixel (dx, dy). Source alpha = coverage * paint_alpha / 255; src-over as
# sd_canvas_blit_at: every pixel with coverage != 0 is written, dst alpha byte 255. Clipped on every
# side; rows by sd_surface_stride. Returns SADISH_OK (also for a gradient with no stops, or one whose
# matrix is blocked, which paint nothing), or SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null cv / surface / g.
# ⚠ For a surface with REAL alpha — a translucent window agnos composites with #92 — this is the
# wrong compositor (it forces dst alpha 255): sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at in src/premul.cyr paints
# the same PAINT, pixel for pixel — the same t and the same colour, not the same composited bytes
# (the two stores round differently; the ⚠ bound is on that function).
fn sd_canvas_blit_paint_at(cv, surface, g, dx, dy): i64 {
    return _sd_paint_blit_run(cv, surface, g, dx, dy, 0);
}

# @public — sd_canvas_blit_paint_at with the canvas at the surface origin.
fn sd_canvas_blit_paint(cv, surface, g): i64 {
    return _sd_paint_blit_run(cv, surface, g, 0, 0, 0);
}

# --- premul.cyr ---

# @public — sadish premultiplied writers: a surface with REAL alpha, for agnos gpu_shader_op#92.
#
# premul.cyr — every other writer in sadish treats an SdSurface as an OPAQUE render target: the
# coverage blits (sd_canvas_blit_at, sd_canvas_blit_paint_at) and the blend (sd_blend_hline) force
# the alpha byte to 255, and sd_clear cannot even say "transparent" (an alpha byte of 0 means OPAQUE
# under the legacy rule, see surface.cyr). Correct for a screen; useless for a client whose surface
# is flagged SETU_SURF_PREMULTIPLIED, which the compositor blends per pixel with #92 op 0x01
# (`out = src + dst * (1 - src_a)`, setu proto.cyr / aethersafha gpu.cyr). Until this module no
# sadish writer could produce such a surface with a non-255 alpha — no rounded corners, soft
# shadows or translucent panels over a transparent window. These are the writers that can:
#
#   sd_clear_premul(s, color, a)                      # every pixel := color at alpha a (0 = 0,0,0,0)
#   sd_fill_rect_premul(s, x, y, w, h, color, a)      # premultiplied src-over of a rect, clipped
#   sd_canvas_blit_premul_at(cv, s, color, a, dx, dy) # coverage x colour, src-over, clipped
#   sd_canvas_blit_premul(cv, s, color, a)            # ... with the canvas at the surface origin
#   sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at(cv, s, g, dx, dy)  # coverage x GRADIENT paint (0.7.0, see below)
#   sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul(cv, s, g)             # ... with the canvas at the surface origin
#   sd_surface_rgba_at(s, x, y)                       # raw 0xAARRGGBB readback, no alpha mapping
#
# THE CONTRACT.
#   · The DESTINATION is premultiplied BGRA (every channel <= its pixel's alpha) and is read as it
#     is: nothing here unpremultiplies, and nothing maps an alpha byte. A fresh sd_surface_new is
#     all-zero = transparent, and a surface the legacy writers only ever painted with OPAQUE colours
#     (sd_clear, the blits, sd_fill_rect_blend) has alpha 255 everywhere — both valid. ⚠ Not valid:
#     raw 0x00RRGGBB stores (byte 3 = 0), and sd_hline / sd_plot with an sd_rgba colour, which store
#     STRAIGHT alpha. Compositing onto such a pixel cannot repair it.
#   · Every output pixel is VALID premultiplied (out_c <= out_a) for every valid destination,
#     proved below and swept in programs/premul_test.cyr. Every stored value is in [0, 255] even
#     over an invalid destination, so no store8 ever truncates.
#   · Rows are addressed by sd_surface_stride(s); only the w*4 visible bytes of a row are touched.
#   · Clear, fill and both blits allocate NOTHING, first call included (asserted: 0 B on the heap,
#     0 B on an arena hook) — there is no scratch; the gradient blit does not even allocate on the
#     first call that rebuilds the ramp (it is rebuilt in place). Single-threaded, like the rest of
#     sadish.
#
# ⛔⛔ ALPHA IS AN EXPLICIT ARGUMENT `a` (0..255, clamped) AND 0 MEANS TRANSPARENT — EVERYWHERE IN
# THIS MODULE, the blit included. The colour's own alpha byte is NEVER read: `color` supplies R, G, B.
# Why not the legacy sd_alpha_of rule (byte 0 = opaque) that sd_canvas_blit_at / paint use:
#   · #92 reads byte 3 LITERALLY — 0 is transparent to the kernel. A premultiplied writer that
#     read 0 as OPAQUE would put sadish and the compositor on opposite sides of the one value the
#     stack has already been burned by: a byte-3 misreading is what made kashi's glyph text and a
#     coverage-blend colour render as an ADDITIVE OVER-BRIGHT GHOST under #92 (dhancha canvas.cyr /
#     surface.cyr, aethersafha window.cyr W_PREMUL) — a wrong picture with no error anywhere.
#   · Under the legacy rule a COMPUTED alpha that reaches 0 (a fade-out's last frame) paints SOLID —
#     the sd_rgba(0, 0, 0, 128)-painted-black trap sd_fill_rect_blend was built to close in 0.5.3.
#   · The stack's colours arrive without alpha anyway: dhancha's theme tokens are 0x00RRGGBB with
#     their alpha in a separate token (dh_theme_scrim / dh_theme_scrim_a), which is this signature.
# ⚠ The price, pinned by the suite in both directions: sd_rgba(r, g, b, 128) passed with a = 255
# paints OPAQUE (byte ignored), and sd_rgb(r, g, b) passed with a = 0 paints NOTHING.
# ⛔ PORTING. sd_canvas_blit_at reads only R, G, B — coverage is its whole source alpha, whatever byte
# 3 holds — so its port is sd_canvas_blit_premul_at(cv, s, c, 255, dx, dy): a = 255, NOT sd_alpha_of(c).
# sd_alpha_of would halve an sd_rgba(.., 128) colour that blit_at paints solid (0xFF80007F against
# blit_at's 0xFFFF0000 for red over opaque blue, pinned), and map a computed alpha that reached 0 back
# to 255 — the paints-solid trap above. A caller that means to START honouring a colour's alpha passes
# it raw: sd_color_a(c), where 0 is transparent.
#
# ================================================================
# THE GRADIENT PAINT — sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at(cv, s, g, dx, dy) (0.7.0).
# The combination a translucent UI actually needs: a gradient panel with anti-aliased rounded corners
# on a TRANSPARENT window. src/paint.cyr's SdGradient (linear / radial / focal radial, multi-stop,
# pad / repeat / reflect, gradient transforms) composited through a canvas's coverage — sampled,
# stepped and evaluated EXACTLY as sd_canvas_blit_paint_at does, and composited with the arithmetic
# below instead of paint's straight src-over-with-alpha-forced-255.
#
# ⭐ ONE EVALUATION, TWO COMPOSITORS. The two blits are the SAME function: paint.cyr's
# _sd_paint_blit_run / _sd_paint_blit_point take a `pm` flag that chooses the store, and nothing
# above the store differs — same DDA, same root, same spread, same 1025-entry ramp, same marked-cell
# stop search. So no pixel can take a different t or a different colour here than it takes there:
# for an OPAQUE gradient at full coverage the two blits write byte-identical RGB, asserted pixel by
# pixel over a non-trivial canvas in programs/paint_premul_test.cyr (group B).
#
# ⛔ NO `a` ARGUMENT, AND 0 STILL CANNOT MEAN OPAQUE. Every other writer here takes an explicit
# alpha because a sadish colour's byte 3 is ambiguous (the legacy 0-means-opaque rule). A gradient
# is not ambiguous: sd_gradient_add_stop resolves each stop through sd_alpha_of when the ramp is
# built, so a ramp entry's byte 3 is the ACTUAL interpolated alpha, 1..255, and this blit uses it as
# the source alpha directly. The byte-3 misreading that burned kashi cannot reach this path.
# ⚠ The price of the legacy rule, paid in paint.cyr and inherited here: a gradient CANNOT express a
# fully transparent stop — sd_rgba(r, g, b, 0) is an OPAQUE stop. The faintest expressible stop is
# alpha 1, which over transparent leaves out_a = 1 at full coverage and 0 below cov 128. A caller
# that wants a true fade to nothing ends the ramp at alpha 1 (or stops painting).
# ⇒ There is no alpha-0 no-op to skip: every covered pixel has k >= 1. The no-ops are the ones paint
# already has — coverage 0 (untouched), zero stops and a blocked matrix (nothing painted, SADISH_OK).
#
# ⭐ PREMULTIPLY AFTER INTERPOLATION, PER PIXEL — not into the ramp. The ramp interpolates STRAIGHT
# (non-premultiplied) colours, SVG's rule and paint.cyr's documented contract; this blit premultiplies
# the RESULT at each pixel, P_c = floor(c * a / 255), which is exactly sd_premul of that colour. Why
# not store premultiplied entries once and interpolate those:
#   · The premultiply is TRUNCATING (it has to be: P is then exactly sd_premul, the stack's
#     sanctioned producer — see the ⚠ REPEATED COMPOSITING note below). Truncating at the STOPS
#     throws the colour away at the faint end — P = 0 for every c < 255 at a = 1 — and every
#     interpolated value between the stops then carries that loss, pulling the whole transition
#     toward the opaque stop's colour and, where that stop is dark, toward BLACK.
#     MEASURED, stops opaque BLACK -> WHITE at alpha 1, the pixel at t = 0.5 over an opaque white
#     destination: straight-then-premultiply gives 191 per lane (colour 128 at alpha 128, P = 64),
#     premultiplied-interpolation 128 (P = 1) — 63 levels darker, on a ramp whose ends are identical
#     either way. ⚠ P = 1, not the 0 the truncation alone suggests: the premultiplied ends would be
#     (0,0,0,255) and (1,1,1,1) and _sd_gradient_lerp ROUNDS, so their midpoint comes back up to 1.
#     Pinned in both directions in programs/paint_premul_test.cyr (group K, checks 71-75).
#   · The ramp is SHARED with sd_canvas_blit_paint_at and with sd_gradient_color_at. A premultiplied
#     one would be a second 4,100 B table and a second answer to "the colour at t".
# ⇒ A ONE-STOP gradient of colour c at alpha a paints EXACTLY sd_canvas_blit_premul_at(cv, s, c, a)'s
# bytes — the premultiplied twin of paint.cyr's "an opaque one-stop gradient paints sd_canvas_blit_at's
# bytes", and asserted the same way.
#
# ================================================================
# THE ARITHMETIC — chosen to equal agnos's own references, so a CPU-composited frame and the GPU
# path agree. Channels c in {B, G, R}; cov = coverage 0..255 (255 for clear/fill); d = destination.
# ⭐ ONE implementation, _sd_premul_cov_run, which both blits call; the gradient blit passes the
# PIXEL'S OWN interpolated colour and its own straight alpha as (c, a), and nothing else changes.
# ⚠ a is the pixel's alpha and cov its coverage, kept apart to the last division: the effective
# source alpha a * cov / 255 is never rounded to a byte on the way (the straight paint blit's
# `sa = coverage * paint_alpha / 255` does round it, and that 8-bit truncation is one of the two
# reasons the two blits differ below full coverage — the other is straight's truncating store).
#
#   P_c  = floor(c * a / 255)       the premultiplied colour — EXACTLY sd_premul(sd_rgba(r, g, b, a))
#                                   for a in 1..255 (the sanctioned producer; computed by it), 0 at a = 0
#   k    = a * cov                  effective source alpha x 255, 0..65025
#   out_c = R( P_c * cov * 255 + d_c * (65025 - k) )       R(n) = floor((2n + 65025) / 130050)
#   out_a = R(   a * cov * 255 + d_a * (65025 - k) )       = n / 65025 rounded to nearest
#
# ⭐ That is agnos kernel/core/gpu.cyr cov_ref_px — the EXACT rational reference for #92 op 0x02
# GPU_OP_BLEND_COV (8bpp coverage x one uniform premultiplied colour, src-over onto a strided
# surface: this blit's GPU twin) — term for term, with the premultiplied colour sd_premul makes.
# At cov = 255 it collapses (2 * 255 * n + 65025 = 255 * (2n + 255)) to
#   out_c = P_c + floor((2 * d_c * (255 - a) + 255) / 510)
# which is gpu.cyr blend_ref_px, the reference for op 0x01 BLEND_RECT — the op the compositor
# composites this surface WITH. sd_fill_rect_premul computes that form; the suite holds the fill
# and the blit at full coverage to the same cov_ref_px on all 32,896 valid (d_c, d_a) pairs.
#
# ⭐ NO TIES. R hits x.5 only if 2n = 65025 * (2j + 1): even = odd. So round-half-up, round-half-even
# and round-to-nearest are the same function on every input, and the GPU's tie rule never matters.
#
# ⛔ VALIDITY, DERIVED. R is non-decreasing in n. For a valid destination (d_c <= d_a):
#   P_c = floor(c * a / 255) <= a                       (c <= 255)
#   65025 - k >= 0                                      (a, cov <= 255)
#   ⇒ P_c * cov * 255 + d_c * (65025 - k) <= a * cov * 255 + d_a * (65025 - k)   ⇒  out_c <= out_a
#   out_a <= R(k * 255 + 255 * (65025 - k)) = R(255 * 65025) = floor(255.5) = 255
# The same bound holds for out_c with ANY d_c <= 255 (valid or not), so every store is a byte.
# Equality is reached (c = 255, d_c = d_a): the invariant is tight, not merely held.
# Exact endpoints: cov = 0 or a = 0 gives R(d * 65025) = d (the pixel is untouched, and is skipped);
# k = 65025 gives P exactly — (b, g, r, 255) at a = 255, the four bytes sd_canvas_blit_at writes.
# Widest intermediate: 2 * (255 * 65025 + 255 * 65025) + 65025 = 66,390,525 < 2^26.
#
# MEASURED against the kernel (a numpy f32 emulation of kernel/shaders/blend_premul.s and
# blend_cov.s — every v_fma_f32 / v_mul_f32 rounded once to f32, v_cvt_pk_u8_f32 to nearest, both
# tie rules, the literal constants 0xBB808081 / 0x3B808081, exact in 64-bit long double):
#   · op 0x01, the dst term above over ALL 8,421,376 (A, S <= A, d) triples: 0 mismatches.
#   · op 0x02 against this blit, ALL 4,294,967,296 (a, cov, c, d) colour-lane inputs and all
#     16,777,216 (a, cov, d) alpha-lane inputs: worst |difference| 1, on 5,952 + 33 inputs (pack
#     ties to even) or 3,380 + 28 (half up) — the f32 shader against its own exact reference, which
#     is this arithmetic; the kernel's coverage self-test allows and reports exactly that ±1. At
#     cov = 0 and cov = 255 the two agree on every input, as that self-test requires.
#
# ⚠ REPEATED COMPOSITING DRIFTS. One layer is close: over every (c, a, d) the fill is within 1 of
# round(analytic) (MEASURED; worst |out - analytic| 1.49, at c 254, a 1, d 128). Stacking the SAME
# translucent source N times over transparent (MEASURED from the fill form; the single numbers below
# are also asserted through the functions in programs/premul_test.cyr, group L):
#   · ALPHA STALLS below 255 for every a in 1..127, at the smallest d > 255 - 127.5/a, where the dst
#     term d*(255-a)/255 rounds back up to d - a: a = 1 stops at 128 (after 128 layers, having run
#     AHEAD of the analytic 100.8), a = 10 at 243, a = 64 and a = 127 at 254; a >= 128 reaches 255 (8
#     layers at 128). Alpha never decreases. This is 8-bit src-over rounding, bit-identical to #92
#     op 0x01 (the same stack composited on the GPU stalls at the same value), so it is kept.
#   · COLOUR runs mostly LOW, compounding the truncating premultiply: 20 fills of (200, 200, 200) at
#     a = 10 give 0x8D626262 (colour 98, analytic 110.14; alpha 141, analytic 140.43) and 1000 give
#     0xF3A6A6A6 (straight 174 for 200); (128, 128, 128) at a = 1 stalls at 0x80000000, a 50 % BLACK,
#     because P = 0 for every c < 255 at a = 1; 1000 coverage-128 blits of (200, 100, 50) at a = 20
#     settle at 0xF3B34D1A (straight 188, 81, 27). Worst premultiplied-colour error over c in 0..255,
#     a in 8..64, at N = 4 / 8 / 16: -4.44 / -7.79 / -13.95 (up to +1.04 / +1.76 / +3.22). A ROUNDED
#     premultiply, (2*c*a + 255) / 510, would make it two-sided and about 30 % smaller: -2.67..+2.76 /
#     -4.19..+5.26 / -7.56..+9.61 — not removed. Truncation stays because P is then exactly sd_premul,
#     the stack's sanctioned producer, so a pixel sd_premul made and one this module made agree. A
#     client building up an effect from many faint layers should composite once at the summed alpha.
#
# ⚠ AGAINST sd_canvas_blit_at, opaque colour (a = 255) over an OPAQUE destination: the alpha byte is
# 255 from both, and the colour bytes are round-to-nearest here where the straight blit truncates:
#   out_premul = floor((2m + 255) / 510)   vs   out_straight = floor(m / 255),   m = c*cov + d*(255 - cov)
# ⇒ premul - straight is 0 or +1, and +1 exactly when m mod 255 >= 128. MEASURED over all 16,777,216
# (cov, c, d) channel triples: 8,164,890 differ, all by +1; identical for every (c, d) at cov = 0 and
# cov = 255, and for every cov when c == d. programs/premul_test.cyr asserts all of it through both
# functions. (The gap is the straight blit's truncation, not an error here: truncation is what makes
# its 50 % white-over-black read 127.)
#
# MEASURED cost (256x256, best of 5 x 40 passes, this host, ONE idle run; build/ probes —
# perf_solid.cyr and perf_paint.cyr, gitignored like the rest of build/ — not a gate), ns/px.
# ⚠ ns/px figures compare within one run on one host, never across them; these replace the earlier
# table's, which were a different run:
#   full coverage   sd_canvas_blit_at 6.5 | blit_premul a=255 2.6 (one store32) | a=128 10.4
#   mixed coverage  sd_canvas_blit_at 11.9 | blit_premul a=255 10.4 | a=128 10.4
#   rect at a=128   sd_fill_rect_blend 8.5 | sd_fill_rect_premul 8.9
#   clear           sd_clear 8.3 | sd_clear_premul 1.5
#   gradient, full  linear 23.1 straight / 35.1 premul | radial 50.9 / 61.6 | focal 63.7 / 75.6 |
#                   transformed linear 23.2 / 35.6 | transformed radial 56.0 / 66.3
# ⭐ The coverage blit got FASTER when the formula moved into _sd_premul_cov_run — a build of wave 1's
# inlined loop in the same run: a=255 3.4 -> 2.6, a=128 12.1 -> 10.4, mixed 12.1 -> 10.4. (Why was not
# chased; fewer values live across the row is the guess.) Sharing the arithmetic with the paint blit
# cost this loop nothing — but only because the call is per ROW; see the ⛔ on _sd_premul_cov_run.
# ⚠ The gradient rows use a 4-stop ramp with TRANSLUCENT stops, so the STRAIGHT blit is on its blend
# path — paint.cyr's own 14 ns/px linear figure is an opaque ramp, which stores without blending. The
# ~12 ns/px the premultiplied compositor adds is the per-pixel premultiply (three divisions) plus the
# alpha lane the straight blit never computes.
# ================================================================

# P for (color, a) as 0xAARRGGBB: sd_premul's bytes at an explicit a, a clamped to [0, 255], 0 at a <= 0.
fn _sd_premul_color(color, a): i64 {
    if (a <= 0) { return 0; }
    var av = a;
    if (av > 255) { av = 255; }
    return sd_premul(sd_rgba(sd_color_r(color), sd_color_g(color), sd_color_b(color), av));
}

# @public — read pixel (x, y) as 0xAARRGGBB: ALL FOUR BYTES, raw — no legacy alpha mapping (an
# alpha byte of 0 reads 0) and no unpremultiply. Rows by the stride. Out-of-bounds reads return 0,
# which is also transparent black: tell the two apart by the bounds, never by the value.
# (sd_surface_pixel_at, by contrast, drops byte 3.)
fn sd_surface_rgba_at(s, x, y): i64 {
    if (s == 0) { return 0; }
    if (x < 0) { return 0; }
    if (y < 0) { return 0; }
    if (x >= load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET)) { return 0; }
    if (y >= load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET)) { return 0; }
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    return load32(px + y * load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET) + x * 4);
}

# @public — set EVERY pixel of `s` to `color` premultiplied at alpha `a`: bytes
# (floor(b*a/255), floor(g*a/255), floor(r*a/255), a) — exactly sd_premul(sd_rgba(r, g, b, a)).
# `a` <= 0 is FULLY TRANSPARENT (0, 0, 0, 0) whatever the colour; `a` >= 255 is opaque (b, g, r, 255).
# The colour's alpha byte is ignored. Overwrites, does not composite. Rows by the stride; padding
# between rows is untouched. Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for s == 0.
fn sd_clear_premul(s, color, a): i64 {
    if (s == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var w = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var h = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    var stride = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET);
    var p = _sd_premul_color(color, a);
    var y = 0;
    while (y < h) {
        var row = px + y * stride;
        var x = 0;
        while (x < w) {
            store32(row + x * 4, p);
            x = x + 1;
        }
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — composite a w*h rect of `color` at alpha `a` OVER a premultiplied surface, top-left
# (x, y), clipped to the surface on every side:
#   out_c = P_c + floor((2 * d_c * (255 - a) + 255) / 510)   (channels and alpha, P_alpha = a)
# — gpu.cyr blend_ref_px, i.e. #92 op 0x01 with this uniform source (see the header). `a` <= 0
# paints NOTHING; `a` >= 255 stores (b, g, r, 255). w <= 0 or h <= 0 paints nothing (⚠ unlike
# sd_fill_rect, whose span swap paints 2 px for w == 0). The colour's alpha byte is ignored.
# Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for s == 0.
fn sd_fill_rect_premul(s, x, y, w, h, color, a): i64 {
    if (s == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (a <= 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var sw = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var sh = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    var stride = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET);
    var x0 = x;
    if (x0 < 0) { x0 = 0; }
    var y0 = y;
    if (y0 < 0) { y0 = 0; }
    var x1 = x + w;
    if (x1 > sw) { x1 = sw; }
    var y1 = y + h;
    if (y1 > sh) { y1 = sh; }
    if (x1 <= x0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    if (y1 <= y0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var p = _sd_premul_color(color, a);
    var av = (p >> 24) & 255;
    var pb = p & 255;
    var pg = (p >> 8) & 255;
    var pr = (p >> 16) & 255;
    var ia2 = (255 - av) * 2;
    var yy = y0;
    while (yy < y1) {
        var row = px + yy * stride;
        var xx = x0;
        while (xx < x1) {
            var o = row + xx * 4;
            if (av == 255) {
                store32(o, p);
            } else {
                store8(o, pb + (load8(o) * ia2 + 255) / 510);
                store8(o + 1, pg + (load8(o + 1) * ia2 + 255) / 510);
                store8(o + 2, pr + (load8(o + 2) * ia2 + 255) / 510);
                store8(o + 3, av + (load8(o + 3) * ia2 + 255) / 510);
            }
            xx = xx + 1;
        }
        yy = yy + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# ⭐ THE header's cov_ref_px, and the ONE implementation of it in sadish: a RUN of `n` pixels from
# surface offset `o`, their coverage bytes at `cp`, painted with the premultiplied source colour `p`
# (0xAARRGGBB, _sd_premul_color's bytes) whose alpha is `av` and whose channels times 255 are `pb`,
# `pg`, `pr`. Both premultiplied writers that take coverage run through here — sd_canvas_blit_premul_at
# a row at a time, sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at a pixel at a time (its colour changes at every one)
# — so the formula, its rounding and its two endpoints exist once. Every store is a byte and
# out_c <= out_a for a valid destination: proved in the header, swept through BOTH callers in
# programs/premul_test.cyr (F, M) and programs/paint_premul_test.cyr (C, F).
# ⛔ A RUN, NOT A PIXEL, and that is not a style choice: CYRIUS DOES NOT INLINE. A first cut of this
# split called a per-PIXEL helper from the solid blit and MEASURED 16.0 to 17.5 ns/px where the
# inlined loop reads 11.5 to 12.1 (256x256, mixed coverage, the cost table's probe; the spread inside
# each is run-to-run drift, the gap between them is not) — a third to a half more work for a helper
# that loop calls 65,536 times a frame. Per ROW the call vanishes into the row setup: the same probe
# reads 10.4, FASTER than the inlined loop it replaced. The paint blit, at 35 to 105 ns/px, pays it
# per pixel at n = 1 and does not notice.
# ⚠ The endpoints are IN here because they are exits, not cases: cov == 0 is skipped (R(d * 65025) = d
# leaves the pixel exactly as it was) and av * cov == 65025 stores the four bytes of `p` outright,
# which is what the formula collapses to there (130050 P + 65025 over 130050). Neither is a second
# rule, and the suites hold both to this term.
fn _sd_premul_cov_run(o, cp, n, p, pb, pg, pr, av): i64 {
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        var cov = load8(cp + i);
        if (cov != 0) {
            var q = o + i * 4;
            var k = av * cov;
            if (k == 65025) {
                store32(q, p);
            } else {
                var ik = 65025 - k;
                store8(q, ((pb * cov + load8(q) * ik) * 2 + 65025) / 130050);
                store8(q + 1, ((pg * cov + load8(q + 1) * ik) * 2 + 65025) / 130050);
                store8(q + 2, ((pr * cov + load8(q + 2) * ik) * 2 + 65025) / 130050);
                store8(q + 3, ((av * 255 * cov + load8(q + 3) * ik) * 2 + 65025) / 130050);
            }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_blit_premul_at — composite the canvas coverage onto a PREMULTIPLIED surface with the
# canvas's (0,0) at surface pixel (dx, dy): source = `color` premultiplied at alpha `a`, scaled by
# each pixel's coverage, src-over (the formula and its proof are in the header). (dx, dy) may be
# negative or beyond the surface — clipped on every side exactly as sd_canvas_blit_at clips. Rows by
# sd_surface_stride. A pixel with coverage 0 is not touched; `a` <= 0 paints nothing.
# ⭐ The alpha byte is COMPOSITED, not forced: over a transparent surface an opaque colour leaves
# alpha == coverage, which is what makes an anti-aliased edge an anti-aliased edge under #92.
# Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null cv / surface.
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sd_canvas_blit_premul_at(cv, surface, color, a, dx, dy): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (surface == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (a <= 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var cw = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var ch = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var covbuf = sd_canvas_coverage(cv);
    var covstride = sd_canvas_stride(cv);
    var sw = sd_surface_width(surface);
    var sh = sd_surface_height(surface);
    var spx = sd_surface_pixels(surface);
    var pitch = sd_surface_stride(surface);

    var p = _sd_premul_color(color, a);
    var av = (p >> 24) & 255;
    # P * 255 per channel: the source term is these times the coverage (see _sd_premul_cov_run).
    var pb = (p & 255) * 255;
    var pg = ((p >> 8) & 255) * 255;
    var pr = ((p >> 16) & 255) * 255;

    # Clip the canvas rect [dx, dx+cw) x [dy, dy+ch) to the surface (canvas coordinates).
    var x0 = 0;
    if (dx < 0) { x0 = 0 - dx; }
    var y0 = 0;
    if (dy < 0) { y0 = 0 - dy; }
    var x1 = cw;
    if (dx + x1 > sw) { x1 = sw - dx; }
    var y1 = ch;
    if (dy + y1 > sh) { y1 = sh - dy; }

    # ⛔ BOTH POINTERS ARE ADVANCED TO x0 AND THE RUN STARTS AT INDEX 0 — a run takes a start and a
    # length, not a range, so the clip lives in `+ x0` on each and in `x1 - x0`. The coverage one is
    # the trap: getting it wrong shifts the whole row by a constant, which UNIFORM coverage cannot
    # show and a wrapped-against-packed comparison cannot either (both surfaces shift alike).
    # MEASURED: with that `+ x0` deleted the whole 23-suite sweep stayed green until
    # programs/premul_test.cyr group N (#135, #139, #141, #142, #145-147) was written to read a
    # varying coverage back through an independent per-pixel reference. Move any of these three and
    # group N is what tells you.
    var y = y0;
    while (y < y1) {
        var crow = covbuf + y * covstride + x0;
        var srow = spx + (y + dy) * pitch + (dx + x0) * 4;
        _sd_premul_cov_run(srow, crow, x1 - x0, p, pb, pg, pr, av);
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — sd_canvas_blit_premul_at with the canvas at the surface origin.
fn sd_canvas_blit_premul(cv, surface, color, a): i64 {
    return sd_canvas_blit_premul_at(cv, surface, color, a, 0, 0);
}

# One pixel of the gradient blit, and the ONLY place a paint colour is premultiplied: the paint's
# colour `e` at that pixel (0xAARRGGBB with the ACTUAL alpha — a ramp entry, or _sd_gradient_eval's
# value in a cell holding a stop), its coverage byte at `cp`, src-over the premultiplied pixel at `o`
# — a one-pixel run of the formula above. P = _sd_premul_color(e, e's own alpha) = sd_premul(e) for an
# alpha of 1..255, which is every value a ramp entry can hold (the header's ⛔ NO `a` ARGUMENT note).
# ⚠ An alpha byte of 0 — which no ramp entry has — would premultiply to 0 and give k = 0, i.e.
# R(d * 65025) = d: the pixel would be left exactly as it was, never painted opaque. The one place
# byte 3 is read as itself rather than through sd_alpha_of, and it degrades to a no-op, not to the
# additive over-bright ghost a legacy reading would make.
# ⚠ THE PREMULTIPLY IS WRITTEN OUT rather than routed through _sd_premul_color, and the three lines
# below are that function expanded for an alpha already in [0, 255] — floor(c * a / 255) per channel,
# 0 at a = 0, the same bytes sd_premul gives, which is the whole point of the producer. It is expanded
# because CYRIUS DOES NOT INLINE and _sd_premul_color reaches sd_premul -> sd_alpha_of / sd_color_r /
# _g / _b / sd_rgba: TEN calls per painted pixel, MEASURED 67.8 ns/px against 35.1 for the linear
# gradient blit (256x256, full coverage) — the call chain was more than half the blit. ⛔ If the
# module's premultiply ever stops being floor(c * a / 255), these three lines and sd_premul must move
# together; programs/paint_premul_test.cyr computes the expected P from the stops independently and
# would fail, and programs/premul_test.cyr group M holds this path and sd_canvas_blit_premul_at (which
# does call _sd_premul_color) to identical bytes over all 32,896 valid destinations.
fn _sd_premul_paint_px(o, cp, e): i64 {
    var av = (e >> 24) & 255;
    var pb = ((e & 255) * av) / 255;
    var pg = (((e >> 8) & 255) * av) / 255;
    var pr = (((e >> 16) & 255) * av) / 255;
    var p = (av << 24) | (pr << 16) | (pg << 8) | pb;
    return _sd_premul_cov_run(o, cp, 1, p, pb * 255, pg * 255, pr * 255, av);
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at — composite the canvas coverage onto a PREMULTIPLIED surface with
# the canvas's (0,0) at surface pixel (dx, dy), the source colour of each pixel coming from the
# gradient `g` sampled at that pixel's centre in CANVAS space: a gradient panel with anti-aliased
# edges over a transparent window, which sd_canvas_blit_paint_at (straight src-over, dst alpha forced
# to 255) cannot produce and sd_canvas_blit_premul_at (one uniform colour) cannot either.
#
# The paint is src/paint.cyr's — geometry, spread, stops, ramp and the exact per-pixel t are ITS
# contract, read there; every kind is supported (linear, radial, focal radial, any spread, a gradient
# transform), because this is the same loop under a flag. What THIS function fixes is the store:
#   · source alpha = the pixel's INTERPOLATED STRAIGHT stop alpha (1..255), premultiplied per pixel,
#     weighted by the pixel's coverage — the header's cov_ref_px with (a, cov) kept apart;
#   · the destination alpha is COMPOSITED, not forced: over a transparent surface an opaque gradient
#     leaves alpha == coverage, which is what makes the rounded corner a rounded corner under #92;
#   · every output pixel is valid premultiplied (out_c <= out_a) for every valid destination.
# (dx, dy) may be negative or beyond the surface — clipped on every side exactly as the straight
# paint blit clips. Rows by sd_surface_stride. A pixel with coverage 0 is not touched. 0 B per call.
# Returns SADISH_OK (also for a gradient with no stops, or one whose matrix is blocked — both paint
# nothing, as in paint.cyr), or SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null cv / surface / g.
# ⚠ Against sd_canvas_blit_paint_at on the same canvas and an opaque destination the RGB bytes can
# differ for two reasons, BOTH the straight blit's: it rounds the effective source alpha DOWN to a
# byte (sa = cov * paint_alpha / 255) and then truncates its store where this one rounds to nearest.
# ⛔ "IDENTICAL AT FULL COVERAGE" IS THE OPAQUE GRADIENT'S PROMISE ONLY — the alpha truncation is
# what makes it, and a translucent stop breaks it:
#   · every stop alpha 255: sa is cov exactly, so at cov = 255 the two blits write byte-identical
#     RGB (asserted pixel by pixel on 12 specs, paint_premul_test group B) and below it
#     premul - straight is 0 or +1 — the ⚠ AGAINST sd_canvas_blit_at note above, unchanged.
#   · a TRANSLUCENT stop: the signed difference premul - straight lies in -2..+2 and REACHES BOTH
#     ENDS. MEASURED over ALL 4,261,478,400 lane inputs (c 0..255, stop alpha 1..255, cov 1..255,
#     d 0..255): 1,403,649,306 differ at all and 3,955,618 by two — +2 at (c 255, alpha 2, cov 193,
#     d 1), straight 1 against 3 here; -2 at (c 55, alpha 4, cov 253, d 225), straight 223 against
#     221. At cov = 255 it narrows to -1..+1 — NOT to 0: (c 0, alpha 1, d 1) is straight 0, here 1.
#     (The exhaustive scan is a build/ probe, gitignored like the perf ones and not a gate; its
#     counts are reproduced through the SHIPPED blits on a 1,560,600-input slice by group L.)
#   Both witnesses and the bound are pinned through both blits in paint_premul_test group L.
# The alpha byte is the real difference: 255 there, composited here.
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at(cv, surface, g, dx, dy): i64 {
    return _sd_paint_blit_run(cv, surface, g, dx, dy, 1);
}

# @public — sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at with the canvas at the surface origin.
fn sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul(cv, surface, g): i64 {
    return _sd_paint_blit_run(cv, surface, g, 0, 0, 1);
}
